window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("backend-fundamentals", {"topics":[{"key":"backend-fundamentals/python","sources":["Official docs: Python (asyncio, concurrent.futures)","PEP 703 — Making the Global Interpreter Lock Optional in CPython","Official docs: Pydantic"],"why":"- **An AI service is glue.** It takes a request, builds a prompt, calls one or more models, validates the\n  structured answer, maybe hits a vector store and a tokenizer, and streams a result back.\n- **Each piece exists first, often only, in Python.** PyTorch, Hugging Face, sentence-transformers,\n  tiktoken, every provider's official SDK, the eval harnesses and the research team's notebooks. Another\n  language means reimplementing them, shelling out to Python anyway, or maintaining two stacks.\n- **\"Python is slow, use Go or Java\" fails on arithmetic.** A model call takes hundreds of milliseconds\n  to tens of seconds; the overhead a faster language would shave is microseconds to low milliseconds. The\n  hot loops (matrix multiplies, tokenization) already run in C, C++, Rust or CUDA under the Python API.\n- **\"Python cannot do concurrency because of the GIL\" fails too.** The GIL is released while a thread\n  blocks on a socket, and an event loop needs no threads to hold thousands of in-flight requests.\n- **What Python must do well is wait on many model calls at once**, and `asyncio` does exactly that. The\n  GIL is a real limit only for CPU-bound pure-Python loops, answered by a native library or a separate\n  process, not a different language.","how":"### Execution model and the GIL\n1. **CPython compiles source to bytecode and interprets it.**\n2. **The Global Interpreter Lock lets one thread execute bytecode at a time**, *because* the\n   reference-counting memory model is not thread-safe without it.\n3. **Threads still help for I/O.** The interpreter drops the GIL during blocking system calls, and\n   well-behaved C extensions (NumPy, PyTorch, hashing, compression) release it during long native work.\n4. **CPU-bound pure-Python code needs processes.** `multiprocessing` or `ProcessPoolExecutor` sidestep\n   the GIL by running separate interpreters.\n\n- **Free-threaded builds exist but are opt-in.** Python 3.13 added an experimental free-threaded build\n  (PEP 703) without the GIL, and 3.14 made it officially supported.\n- **Still plan as if the GIL is there**, because it is a separate build rather than the default and not\n  every C extension supports it yet.\n\n### asyncio: waiting without threads\n- **Coroutines.** An `async def` function is a coroutine; `await` hands control back to the event loop\n  until the awaited I/O completes.\n- **Cheap waiting.** One thread can hold hundreds of pending model calls, each costing a small object\n  rather than a thread stack.\n- **The LLM-backend shape.** Fan out calls, cap concurrency with a `Semaphore` to respect provider rate\n  limits, gather results.\n- **The one rule.** A blocking call inside a coroutine (a sync HTTP client, `time.sleep`, a heavy loop)\n  freezes the whole loop, because nothing else can run until it returns.\n\n| 500 requests, each awaiting a 4-second model call | Thread per request | Event loop |\n|---|---|---|\n| What is held | 500 threads, each with its own stack | 500 suspended coroutine objects |\n| Cost of waiting | OS context switches to do nothing | woken only when its socket has bytes |\n| Python work per request | well under a millisecond (payload build, reply parse) | same, so one loop is idle most of the time |\n\n### Picking the tool for each kind of work\nTwo questions decide it: does the work wait or compute, and does it hold the GIL?\n\n| Work | Holds the GIL? | Where it runs |\n|---|---|---|\n| Async I/O (httpx, async DB driver, provider async client) | No, it waits | `await` it on the loop |\n| Blocking I/O from a sync-only library | Released while blocked | `await asyncio.to_thread(fn, ...)` |\n| Native compute that releases the GIL (NumPy, PyTorch, hashing) | Released | a thread (`to_thread`) so the loop stays free |\n| Pure-Python CPU work (parsing, loops over dicts) | Held | `ProcessPoolExecutor`, or move it to a worker service |\n\n- **Threads are cheaper than processes** (shared memory, no pickling of arguments and results) but only\n  help when the GIL is released.\n- **Processes always parallelise but every call pays serialisation**, so they pay off only when the work\n  is much larger than its inputs and outputs.\n\n### Types and Pydantic\n- **Type hints are annotations; the interpreter does not enforce them.** Fine inside your code with\n  `mypy` checking it.\n- **Boundary data needs runtime validation**: an HTTP body, a model's JSON output, a config file.\n- **Pydantic validates and coerces at runtime and emits JSON Schema**, so one class is the API contract,\n  the structured-output schema you hand to an LLM, and the parsed result. This single-source-of-truth\n  role is why Pydantic became the lingua franca of AI services.\n\n### Packaging and environments\n- **One virtual environment and one lockfile per project.** `uv`, Poetry or plain `pip` with a\n  requirements lock all work.\n- **The characteristic pain is heavy native dependencies** (PyTorch with a specific CUDA build), which\n  is why AI services pin exact versions and ship in containers.\n\n### Where it sits in the stack\n- **Python:** a FastAPI service for the API, worker processes for long jobs, scripts and notebooks for\n  evals and data prep.\n- **TypeScript:** the product UI and sometimes a thin gateway.\n- **The boundary is an HTTP contract**, usually generated from the Python service's OpenAPI schema.","example":"### Fan-out with a concurrency cap and a GIL escape hatch\nFan out classification calls concurrently, validate each model answer at runtime, and push a CPU-bound\nstep out of the GIL-bound main process. The `Semaphore` is the rate-limit guard; the\n`ProcessPoolExecutor` is the GIL escape hatch.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor\nimport httpx\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ValidationError\n\nclass Verdict(BaseModel):\n    label: str\n    confidence: float\n\nSEM = asyncio.Semaphore(8)                      # at most 8 model calls in flight\n\nasync def classify(client: httpx.AsyncClient, text: str) -> Verdict | None:\n    async with SEM:                             # waits without holding a thread\n        r = await client.post(\"http://localhost:8000/v1/classify\", json={\"text\": text}, timeout=30)\n    try:\n        return Verdict.model_validate_json(r.content)  # runtime check; bad JSON is a ValidationError too\n    except ValidationError:\n        return None\n\ndef count_words(text: str) -> int:              # stand-in for CPU-heavy pure-Python work\n    return sum(1 for _ in text.split())\n\nasync def main(texts: list[str]):\n    async with httpx.AsyncClient() as client:   # one connection pool for the whole batch\n        verdicts = await asyncio.gather(*(classify(client, t) for t in texts))\n    loop = asyncio.get_running_loop()\n    with ProcessPoolExecutor() as pool:         # CPU-bound work runs in separate interpreters\n        counts = await asyncio.gather(*(loop.run_in_executor(pool, count_words, t) for t in texts))\n    return list(zip(verdicts, counts))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":                      # required when workers are spawned, not forked\n    print(asyncio.run(main([\"refund my order\", \"great product\"] * 50)))\n```\n\n### One blocking call freezes every other request\nRunnable with the standard library only. The ticker stands in for every other request on the loop.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, time\n\nasync def ticker(stop: asyncio.Event) -> int:\n    ticks = 0\n    while not stop.is_set():\n        await asyncio.sleep(0.01)      # a stand-in for every other request on the loop\n        ticks += 1\n    return ticks\n\nasync def handler(blocking: bool) -> None:\n    if blocking:\n        time.sleep(1)                  # sync call: the whole loop stops for 1 s\n    else:\n        await asyncio.sleep(1)         # async wait: the loop keeps serving others\n\nasync def main() -> None:\n    for blocking in (True, False):\n        stop = asyncio.Event()\n        t = asyncio.create_task(ticker(stop))\n        start = time.perf_counter()\n        await asyncio.gather(*(handler(blocking) for _ in range(5)))\n        stop.set()\n        print(f\"blocking={blocking}: {time.perf_counter() - start:.1f}s, ticks={await t}\")\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n# blocking=True:  5.0s, ticks=1    (five handlers ran one after another, nothing else ran)\n# blocking=False: 1.0s, ticks=~90  (five waits overlapped, the ticker kept running)\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: is Python the bottleneck?\nA support bot handles a request in four stages. Figures are illustrative orders of magnitude.\n\n| Stage | Time | Runs in |\n|---|---|---|\n| Parse body, build prompt | ~0.5 ms | Python |\n| Embed query + vector search | ~30 ms | network wait |\n| Model call | ~2,000 ms | network wait |\n| Validate JSON with Pydantic | ~0.2 ms | Python (Rust core) |\n\nPython executes under 1 ms of a ~2 s request, so the model dominates by three orders of magnitude.\nRewriting in Go would save well under 0.1% of latency; capping concurrency and streaming would matter far more.","patterns":["**One shared async client plus a semaphore.** When calling a provider at volume, create one `httpx.AsyncClient` (or the provider's async client) at startup and wrap calls in `asyncio.Semaphore(N)` sized below the rate limit. Monitor 429 rate, in-flight count and p95 latency.","**Watch event-loop lag.** Run staging with asyncio debug mode (`asyncio.run(main(), debug=True)` or `PYTHONASYNCIODEBUG=1`) so callbacks slower than `slow_callback_duration` are logged; in production sample a stuck worker with `py-spy dump --pid <pid>`. Alert on loop lag and CPU per worker.","**Offload by work type.** Use `asyncio.to_thread` for sync libraries and GIL-releasing native code, and a `ProcessPoolExecutor` or a separate queue worker for pure-Python CPU work. Monitor queue depth and worker CPU saturation.","**Structured concurrency for background work.** Use `asyncio.TaskGroup` (Python 3.11+) or keep a set of task references instead of bare `create_task`, so failures surface and tasks are not collected. Monitor unhandled-exception logs from tasks.","**Frozen lockfiles in the image.** Build containers with `uv sync --frozen` (or an equivalent locked install) and pin the PyTorch/CUDA build explicitly. Re-run the eval set on every dependency bump and track score drift."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    W[\"Work in a request\"] --> Q1{\"Waits on I/O?\"}\n    Q1 -->|\"async library\"| A[\"await on the event loop\"]\n    Q1 -->|\"sync-only library\"| T1[\"asyncio.to_thread\"]\n    Q1 -->|\"no, it computes\"| Q2{\"Releases the GIL?\"}\n    Q2 -->|\"yes: NumPy, PyTorch, hashing\"| T2[\"thread via to_thread\"]\n    Q2 -->|\"no: pure-Python loop\"| P[\"ProcessPoolExecutor or worker service\"]\n    A --> L[\"Loop stays responsive\"]\n    T1 --> L\n    T2 --> L\n    P --> L","caption":"Route each piece of work by two questions: does it wait or compute, and does it hold the GIL while computing?"},"interview":[{"q":"What exactly does the GIL prevent, and when is it released?","a":"- **Definition:** a lock that lets only one thread execute Python bytecode at a time, because CPython's\n  reference counting is not thread-safe without it.\n- **Released:** during blocking system calls (socket and file I/O) and by C extensions such as NumPy and\n  PyTorch during long native work, so I/O-bound and native-heavy threads still overlap.\n- **Not released:** in pure-Python CPU loops, which need separate processes or native code to parallelise."},{"q":"Throughput of an async FastAPI service drops to one request at a time while CPU is idle. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Suspect a blocking call on the loop:** a sync HTTP client, a sync DB driver, `time.sleep`, or a heavy\n  loop inside an `async def` handler.\n- **Confirm it:** enable asyncio debug mode in staging to log slow callbacks, or take a `py-spy dump` of\n  the worker and look for a frame stuck in a sync call.\n- **Fix:** switch to the async client, or wrap the sync call in `asyncio.to_thread`; re-measure p95 under\n  concurrent load."},{"q":"When would you choose threads, processes or asyncio for a piece of work?","a":"- **asyncio** for many concurrent network waits, such as hundreds of model calls, because each wait costs\n  a small coroutine object.\n- **Threads** for sync-only libraries and GIL-releasing native compute, because memory is shared and\n  nothing is pickled.\n- **Processes** for pure-Python CPU work, but only when the work is much larger than its inputs and\n  outputs, because every call pays serialisation."},{"q":"You must classify 10,000 documents through a provider limited to 500 requests per minute. How do you structure it?","a":"- **Concurrency cap:** an `asyncio.Semaphore` plus a client-side rate limiter keeps calls under the\n  limit; 10,000 at 500 per minute is at least 20 minutes, whatever the language.\n- **Resilience:** retry 429 and 5xx with exponential backoff and jitter, validate each answer with\n  Pydantic, and record failures for a re-run.\n- **Shape:** one shared async client for connection reuse; batch APIs, if the provider offers them, cut\n  cost and remove the rate-limit pressure entirely."},{"q":"Would you run the free-threaded CPython build in production today?","a":"- **Status:** experimental in 3.13 (PEP 703) and officially supported from 3.14, but a separate opt-in\n  build, not the default.\n- **Risk:** not every C extension supports it yet, so a single dependency can force the GIL back or\n  break.\n- **Answer:** only for a measured CPU-bound pure-Python workload whose full dependency tree supports it;\n  an I/O-bound LLM service gains little, because it was never GIL-bound."}],"tradeoffs":["**Ecosystem vs raw speed.** You get the entire ML ecosystem and the fastest path from notebook to service, but pay with a runtime slow for CPU-bound pure-Python code. It flips only when the service does heavy per-request computation in Python itself rather than in native libraries or a model.","**Cheap concurrency vs async discipline.** `asyncio` gives cheap concurrency for I/O-bound work, but every library on the request path must be async-aware; one blocking call undoes it, and mixing sync and async code is a constant tax.","**Dynamic typing vs safety.** It speeds experimentation, but production code needs `mypy` plus runtime validation at boundaries; skipping either shows up as type errors found by users instead of CI.","**Native dependencies: strength and weakness.** PyTorch and CUDA are the ecosystem's strength and its deployment weakness: images are large and version pinning is non-negotiable."],"pitfalls":["**Sync client inside `async def`.** Calling `requests` or a sync DB driver in an async handler; the symptom is throughput collapsing to one request at a time while CPU sits idle. Fix: an async client or `asyncio.to_thread`.","**Blaming the GIL for an I/O-bound service.** Profiling shows threads blocked on sockets; the fix is concurrency, not multiprocessing.","**Trusting type hints as validation.** A model returns `\"confidence\": \"high\"` and the code crashes three functions later instead of at the boundary. Fix: validate with Pydantic at the edge.","**Dropping the task from `asyncio.create_task(...)`.** The loop keeps only a weak reference, so the task can be garbage-collected mid-flight; background work (a log write, a cache fill) silently never completes. Keep a reference or use a `TaskGroup`.","**Unpinned dependencies in an AI service.** A transitive upgrade of a tokenizer or SDK changes outputs; the symptom is evals drifting with no code change. Fix: a lockfile and pinned images."],"when":"- **Default to Python** for anything touching models, tokenizers, embeddings, evals or data preparation,\n  and for the service layer that orchestrates them: the library support decides it.\n- **Keep CPU-bound hot paths** in native code or separate processes, and measure before assuming Python is\n  the bottleneck; in most LLM services the model call dominates by two or three orders of magnitude.\n- **Measure:** event-loop lag (asyncio's debug mode logs any callback longer than\n  `slow_callback_duration`, 100 ms by default), CPU utilisation per worker under load, and a profiler\n  sample (py-spy) showing Python frames versus waiting on sockets.\n- **Consider another language** for the product edge (TypeScript next to the UI) or a tiny, extremely\n  high-throughput proxy where microseconds matter.\n- **Interviews:** explain the GIL precisely (what it blocks, when it is released), why `asyncio` fits LLM\n  workloads, and why Pydantic sits at every boundary. The `python-for-ai` module covers each in detail.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes async service code, keeps blocking work off the event loop, validates every boundary with Pydantic, and pins the environment so evals stay reproducible.","architect":"Decides the language split: Python for the model core and data work, TypeScript or another language where the product or throughput argument wins, joined by a typed contract.","fde":"Lands inside a customer's existing Python (or non-Python) stack and must explain why the AI core stays Python and how it integrates with their Java or .NET services over HTTP."},"check":{"q":"A colleague argues the new LLM gateway should be written in Go because \"Python's GIL cannot handle\n2,000 concurrent requests\". How do you respond, and what would change your answer?","a":"The GIL serialises Python bytecode execution, not waiting. A gateway's work per request is\nbuilding a payload and awaiting a model call that takes seconds; with `asyncio`, one process holds\nthousands of pending awaits on a single thread, and the GIL is irrelevant while they wait on sockets.\nMultiple uvicorn workers add CPU headroom for serialisation. I would ask what CPU-bound work the\ngateway does per request; if it must tokenize or transform megabytes in pure Python per call, that is\nthe argument for native code or another language. I would also weigh the cost of a second language\nwhen the model, eval and SDK ecosystem is Python-first."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the GIL rarely limit an LLM-serving backend?","options":["Modern Python releases the GIL for all threads automatically","The service is I/O-bound, and the GIL is released while waiting on sockets, so asyncio or threads overlap the waits","Uvicorn runs each request in its own interpreter","LLM responses are small, so bytecode execution is negligible regardless of concurrency"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The GIL blocks concurrent execution of Python bytecode, not concurrent waiting. An LLM backend mostly awaits network I/O, during which the GIL is not held, so one process serves many requests."},{"q":"Which piece of work should leave the main Python process rather than run inside a coroutine?","options":["Awaiting 200 concurrent HTTP calls to a model provider","A NumPy matrix multiply over a batch of embeddings","A pure-Python loop that parses a 500 MB log file line by line","Reading configuration from environment variables at startup"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Pure-Python CPU work holds the GIL, so neither the event loop nor a thread can overlap it; only a separate process can. A long NumPy multiply also blocks the loop if run inline, but it releases the GIL, so a thread in the same process (`asyncio.to_thread`) suffices. Awaited HTTP calls are I/O-bound."},{"q":"What does a Pydantic model add that a type hint alone does not?","options":["It makes the interpreter reject wrongly typed arguments at call time","Runtime validation and coercion of external data plus JSON Schema generation","Faster attribute access through compiled slots","Automatic retries when validation fails"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Type hints are inert annotations checked only by tools like mypy. Pydantic validates data at runtime, which is what an HTTP body or a model's JSON output needs, and emits the schema you give the LLM."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/fastapi","sources":["Official docs: FastAPI","Official docs: Starlette","Official docs: Pydantic"],"why":"- **An AI endpoint is unusual HTTP.** A request may hold its connection open for ten seconds, stream\n  hundreds of chunks, carry large structured bodies both ways, and share expensive clients (a provider\n  connection pool, a vector store) across every request.\n- **WSGI was built for the opposite.** Flask and traditional Django dedicate one thread or process per\n  request. With 200 in-flight model calls of five seconds each, a WSGI server needs 200 workers that sit\n  idle on sockets; one ASGI event loop holds all 200 pending awaits, bounded by memory and the upstream.\n- **Contract drift.** Hand-written validation and docs diverge the week after launch; a client sends\n  `temperature: \"0.7\"` and the handler crashes deep in a provider call.\n- **One declaration, many uses.** FastAPI reads Pydantic types on the handler signature and derives\n  validation, a precise 422, response filtering and the OpenAPI schema, so docs cannot lie. The same class\n  is the JSON Schema for structured output, closing the loop between API and prompt contracts.\n- **The naive alternatives cost more.** Raw Starlette or aiohttp with hand validation reinvents those\n  layers; Flask-with-async-bolted-on keeps the WSGI mental model where it hurts most.","how":"### ASGI and the request path\n1. **Uvicorn accepts the connection** and hands an ASGI `scope` to the app.\n2. **Middleware runs** (CORS, auth, tracing).\n3. **The router matches** path and method.\n4. **FastAPI resolves the handler's dependencies.**\n5. **Path, query and body parameters are validated** against the declared types. A failure stops at the\n   boundary with a 422 and a field-level error list, before the handler body runs.\n6. **The handler runs**, and the return value is serialised through `response_model`.\n\n- **Caveat:** dependencies may already have executed when validation fails, so keep side effects out\n  of them.\n\n### `async def` versus `def` handlers\n| Handler | Runs on | Use when | Concurrency cap per worker |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `async def` | the event loop; every `await` yields | everything you call is async (httpx, async DB drivers, async SDK clients) | memory and the upstream's limits |\n| `def` | a bounded worker threadpool, so it cannot block the loop | you must call a blocking library | the threadpool (AnyIO's default limiter is 40 tokens) |\n\n- **The failure mode is the mix.** A sync SDK call inside `async def` blocks the loop, and every other\n  request on that worker waits.\n- **Same rule for dependencies.** A plain `def` dependency is also run in the threadpool.\n- **Capacity arithmetic.** If each `def` call holds a thread for 5 seconds, one worker serves at most\n  40 / 5 = 8 requests per second before requests queue for a thread. That is why the async path is the\n  default for model-calling endpoints.\n\n### Workers and processes\n- **One uvicorn process is one event loop on one core.**\n- **Run several worker processes per machine** (uvicorn `--workers`, gunicorn with uvicorn workers, or\n  one process per container behind the orchestrator) *because* JSON parsing, validation and\n  serialisation still use CPU, and one loop cannot use a second core.\n- **Workers share nothing**, so in-memory caches, rate-limit counters and \"which streams are open\" state\n  belong in Redis or the database, not in a module-level dict.\n\n### Pydantic as the contract\n- **Request:** declaring `body: ChatRequest` validates and parses the JSON body.\n- **Response:** `response_model=Answer` filters output to the declared fields, so a leaked internal\n  attribute never reaches a client, and documents it.\n- **Constraints:** `Field` constraints (`max_length`, `ge`, `le`) become both runtime checks and schema\n  metadata.\n\n### Dependency injection and lifespan\n- **`Depends`** lets a handler ask for a current user, a DB session or a shared HTTP client without\n  constructing it; dependencies compose and can be overridden in tests.\n- **`lifespan`** runs startup and shutdown code: build application-scoped resources there (one\n  `httpx.AsyncClient`, one model client) instead of recreating them per request.\n\n### Streaming\n- **`StreamingResponse` takes an async generator** and writes each yielded chunk as it arrives.\n- **`text/event-stream` framing gives server-sent events**, the standard way to stream tokens to browsers\n  and SDKs.\n- **A slow stream does not block others**, because each `yield` happens between awaits.\n\n### Background tasks are not a job queue\n- **`BackgroundTasks` runs a function after the response is sent, in the same process.** Fine for a few\n  hundred milliseconds of follow-up (an audit row, a metric).\n- **Wrong home for a two-minute summarisation**, *because* the work dies with the process on a deploy or\n  crash, nothing retries it, and it competes with live requests for the same loop or threadpool.\n- **Long or must-complete work** goes to a durable queue and a separate worker, with a `202` and a job\n  resource on the API side.\n\n### Compared with the alternatives\n| Framework | Strength | Fit |\n|---|---|---|\n| Flask | simple, sync-first | small internal tools; poor for long-lived streaming calls |\n| Django | ORM, admin, auth | when those matter more than async purity; async support is partial |\n| Starlette | the ASGI core | FastAPI without validation, DI and docs |\n| Litestar | comparable ASGI framework, different DI style | the reasoning in this lesson applies too |","example":"### Streaming chat endpoint\nOne shared upstream client created in `lifespan`, a validated request body, and an SSE stream from an\nasync generator. Every `await` inside `events()` releases the loop to other requests, which is the\nwhole point of the async design.\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom contextlib import asynccontextmanager\nimport httpx\nfrom fastapi import Depends, FastAPI, Request\nfrom fastapi.responses import StreamingResponse\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field\n\n@asynccontextmanager\nasync def lifespan(app: FastAPI):\n    app.state.http = httpx.AsyncClient(base_url=\"http://model-server:8000\", timeout=60)\n    yield                                          # the app serves requests here\n    await app.state.http.aclose()                  # shutdown: close the pool cleanly\n\napp = FastAPI(lifespan=lifespan)\n\nclass ChatRequest(BaseModel):\n    prompt: str = Field(min_length=1, max_length=8000)\n    temperature: float = Field(0.2, ge=0, le=2)   # validated and documented from one declaration\n\ndef http(request: Request) -> httpx.AsyncClient:   # dependency: one shared pool, not one per request\n    return request.app.state.http\n\n@app.post(\"/v1/chat\")\nasync def chat(body: ChatRequest, client: httpx.AsyncClient = Depends(http)):\n    async def events():\n        async with client.stream(\"POST\", \"/generate\", json=body.model_dump()) as r:\n            async for line in r.aiter_lines():     # each await yields the loop to other requests\n                if line:\n                    yield f\"data: {json.dumps({'token': line})}\\n\\n\"\n        yield \"data: [DONE]\\n\\n\"\n    return StreamingResponse(events(), media_type=\"text/event-stream\")\n```\n\n### Testing it without a model server\n`dependency_overrides` swaps the shared client for one backed by `httpx.MockTransport`, so the test\nexercises validation, the stream framing and the 422 path with no network.\n\n```python\nimport json\nimport httpx\nfrom fastapi.testclient import TestClient\nfrom main import app, http                         # the module above\n\ndef fake_http() -> httpx.AsyncClient:\n    def upstream(request: httpx.Request) -> httpx.Response:\n        assert json.loads(request.content)[\"prompt\"] == \"hi\"\n        return httpx.Response(200, text=\"hello\\nworld\\n\")\n    return httpx.AsyncClient(transport=httpx.MockTransport(upstream), base_url=\"http://test\")\n\napp.dependency_overrides[http] = fake_http        # swap the real pool for a fake upstream\n\ndef test_chat_streams_tokens():\n    with TestClient(app) as c:                     # the with-block runs lifespan\n        r = c.post(\"/v1/chat\", json={\"prompt\": \"hi\"})\n        assert r.status_code == 200\n        assert 'data: {\"token\": \"hello\"}' in r.text\n        assert r.text.endswith(\"data: [DONE]\\n\\n\")\n\ndef test_rejects_out_of_range_temperature():\n    with TestClient(app) as c:\n        r = c.post(\"/v1/chat\", json={\"prompt\": \"hi\", \"temperature\": 5})\n        assert r.status_code == 422                # stopped at the boundary, handler never runs\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: sizing one worker for 5-second model calls\nTarget: 30 requests per second of a model call that takes 5 seconds.\n\n| Design | Per-worker ceiling | Workers needed for 30 rps |\n|---|---|---|\n| `def` handler, default 40-thread limiter | 40 / 5 = 8 rps | 4, with threads mostly idle on sockets |\n| `def` handler, limiter raised to 200 | 200 / 5 = 40 rps | 1, but 200 threads of memory and switching |\n| `async def` with an async client | memory and upstream limits; ~150 awaits in flight | 1 for I/O; add workers only for CPU |\n\nBy Little's law, 30 rps × 5 s means ~150 requests in flight at once whatever the design; the question is\nonly whether each one costs a thread or a coroutine.","patterns":["**Shared clients in `lifespan`.** Create one `httpx.AsyncClient` per process at startup, with explicit `timeout` and `httpx.Limits(max_connections=...)`, inject it via `Depends`, and close it on shutdown. Monitor connection-pool waits and new TLS handshakes per second.","**A canary for loop lag.** Run a background coroutine that sleeps 100 ms and records how late it wakes; export that as a metric and alert on it. A health check whose latency tracks the model endpoint's is the same signal from outside.","**Workers sized to cores, state external.** Run roughly one uvicorn worker per core (`--workers`, or one process per container), keep rate-limit counters and caches in Redis, and monitor per-worker CPU. Uneven CPU across workers points to a hot CPU-bound handler.","**Durable jobs for long work.** For anything beyond a few hundred milliseconds or that must finish, return `202` with a job id and hand the work to a queue worker (Celery, RQ, Arq or a cloud queue). Monitor queue depth, job age and failure rate.","**Explicit `response_model` on every route.** Declare the response schema so internal fields are filtered, and publish the generated OpenAPI as the contract other teams codegen from. Diff the schema in CI to catch accidental breaking changes."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"Client request\"] --> U[\"Uvicorn: ASGI scope\"]\n    U --> M[\"Middleware: CORS, auth, tracing\"]\n    M --> R[\"Router: path and method\"]\n    R --> D[\"Resolve dependencies\"]\n    D --> V{\"Params valid?\"}\n    V -->|\"no\"| E[\"422 field errors\"]\n    V -->|\"yes\"| H{\"Handler type\"}\n    H -->|\"async def\"| L[\"Runs on event loop\"]\n    H -->|\"def\"| T[\"Runs in threadpool, cap 40\"]\n    L --> S[\"Serialise via response_model\"]\n    T --> S","caption":"Follow one request: validation can reject it before the handler, and the handler's type decides\nwhether it runs on the loop or in the threadpool."},"interview":[{"q":"What does FastAPI derive from the Pydantic types on a handler signature?","a":"- **Validation and parsing:** path, query and body values are checked and coerced before the handler\n  runs, and failures return a 422 with field-level errors.\n- **Response filtering:** `response_model` drops undeclared fields, so internal attributes do not leak.\n- **Documentation:** the OpenAPI schema comes from the same declarations, so docs and behaviour cannot\n  drift; the model can also be the structured-output schema given to an LLM."},{"q":"A streaming endpoint works locally but users see the whole answer at once in production. What do you check?","a":"- **Buffering in front of the app:** a reverse proxy or load balancer buffering responses holds chunks\n  until the end; disable response buffering for the route (in nginx, for example, the\n  `X-Accel-Buffering: no` response header).\n- **Compression middleware:** gzip can buffer small chunks; exclude `text/event-stream` from it.\n- **The generator itself:** make sure it yields per token and does not collect a list first, and that the\n  upstream call is a streaming call."},{"q":"Where would you run a two-minute document summarisation triggered by an API call?","a":"- **Not in `BackgroundTasks`:** it dies with the process on deploy or crash, nothing retries it, and it\n  competes with live requests.\n- **Durable queue plus worker:** return `202` with a job id, enqueue, and let a separate worker run it\n  with retries and idempotency.\n- **Client contract:** a `GET /jobs/{id}` status resource, or a webhook or SSE notification on completion."},{"q":"When would you choose `def` over `async def` for a handler?","a":"- **When the only client is blocking** (a sync SDK or DB driver) and switching is not possible yet:\n  `def` runs it in the threadpool so the loop stays free.\n- **The cost is a concurrency cap:** AnyIO's default 40 threads means 8 rps per worker at 5 s per call.\n- **Never mix:** a blocking call inside `async def` is worse than either option, because it stalls the\n  whole worker."},{"q":"How many uvicorn workers would you run, and what changes when you go from one to four?","a":"- **Sizing:** roughly one per core, because each worker is one event loop on one core and the CPU work is\n  parsing, validation and serialisation.\n- **What breaks:** any in-process state, such as rate-limit counters, caches or open-stream tracking,\n  now exists four times; move it to Redis or the database.\n- **Monitor:** per-worker CPU and loop lag; more workers do not help if the upstream is the bottleneck."}],"tradeoffs":["**Concurrency vs async discipline.** High concurrency for I/O-bound handlers, but every library on the hot path must be async or explicitly offloaded; one blocking call turns the advantage into a liability.","**Validation vs parsing cost.** Pydantic removes boilerplate and keeps docs honest, but adds per-request parsing; for very large bodies or extreme throughput that cost is measurable and worth profiling.","**Thin framework vs batteries.** No ORM, admin or auth framework keeps it flexible, but you assemble those pieces yourself; Django wins when a full-stack CRUD app is the actual job.","**I/O scaling vs CPU scaling.** Single-process asyncio scales I/O, not CPU; you still run several uvicorn workers per machine and design them to be stateless."],"pitfalls":["**Sync SDK or `requests` inside `async def`.** Symptom: latency on every endpoint of that worker grows with the sum of blocking upstream calls queued ahead of it. Fix: an async client or `def`.","**An `httpx.AsyncClient` per request.** Symptom: a new TLS handshake per call and socket exhaustion under load. Fix: one client in `lifespan`.","**Returning ORM objects or internal dicts without `response_model`.** Symptom: an internal field (a cost figure, a secret flag) appears in client responses.","**CPU-heavy work in the handler** (tokenizing megabytes, re-ranking in pure Python). Symptom: loop stalls visible as every endpoint slowing together. Fix: a thread, process or worker.","**Long jobs through `BackgroundTasks`.** Symptom: work silently vanishes on every deploy or pod restart, with no error logged because the request already returned 200.","**State in module-level variables.** Symptom: behaviour depends on which worker served the request (a rate limit that allows N times the configured rate with N workers)."],"when":"- **Use when** building the HTTP layer of a Python AI service: model gateways, RAG endpoints, agent\n  runners, anything that awaits a model and benefits from streaming and typed contracts.\n- **Prefer Django** when the application is mostly conventional CRUD with an ORM and admin, and Flask only\n  for tiny sync tools.\n- **Measure:** event-loop lag (schedule a no-op every 100 ms and record how late it fires), health-check\n  latency under load (it should stay flat), threadpool saturation for `def` handlers, and upstream\n  connection reuse.\n- **Day-to-day skill:** keep the request path fully async, design Pydantic models that double as\n  structured-output schemas, and wire shared clients through dependencies.\n- **Interviews:** probe whether you know why `def` and `async def` handlers behave differently. The\n  `fastapi-for-ai` module covers each mechanism in depth.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds endpoints with Pydantic contracts, shared async clients via `Depends`, and SSE streaming, and keeps anything blocking out of the event loop.","architect":"Decides that the AI service layer is ASGI and stateless, sets worker and concurrency limits per process, and treats the OpenAPI schema as the published contract for other teams.","fde":"Stands up a FastAPI service inside a customer's network, exposes `/docs` so their integrators can self-serve, and explains why blocking code in their middleware (a sync auth lookup) stalls every async handler behind it."},"check":{"q":"A FastAPI service has one `async def` endpoint that calls a provider through a synchronous SDK.\nUnder light load it is fine; under load every endpoint, including the health check, becomes slow.\nExplain the mechanism and two fixes with their trade-offs.","a":"The sync SDK call blocks the event loop thread for the full duration of the model request, so\nno other coroutine on that worker, including the health check, can run until it returns; under load\nrequests queue behind each other. Fix one: switch to the SDK's async client (or httpx) so the call\nyields to the loop; this is the right long-term fix but requires the whole path to be async. Fix two:\nchange the handler to plain `def` so FastAPI runs it in the threadpool; this unblocks the loop\nimmediately but caps concurrency at the threadpool size (AnyIO's default limiter is 40 threads,\ntunable) and adds thread overhead; at 5 seconds per call, 40 threads is only 8 requests per second per\nworker. A middle path is to keep the handler async and wrap just the sync call in\n`await asyncio.to_thread(...)`, with the same thread cap. Either way, add a metric for event-loop lag\nso a regression is visible, and treat a slow health check as the first sign of a blocked loop."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does FastAPI run a plain `def` handler in a threadpool but an `async def` handler on the loop?","options":["Threadpools are faster for JSON serialisation","A sync function would block the single event loop, so it is isolated in a thread while coroutines yield at each await","Pydantic validation only works in threads","The threadpool enables HTTP/2 multiplexing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The event loop is one thread; a blocking function on it stops every other request. Running sync handlers in threads protects the loop, while async handlers cooperate by yielding at awaits."},{"q":"What do you get from declaring the request body as a Pydantic model?","options":["Automatic caching of identical request bodies","Guaranteed backward compatibility when fields are renamed","Runtime validation with a 422 error, parsed typed access, and the matching OpenAPI schema from one declaration","Automatic retries of the upstream model call on validation failure"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The handler's type annotations drive validation, parsing and documentation together, so the docs and the behaviour cannot diverge. Caching, compatibility and retries are separate concerns."},{"q":"When is Django a better choice than FastAPI for a service that also calls an LLM?","options":["When the service must stream tokens to a browser","When the application is mostly CRUD with an ORM, admin and auth, and the LLM call is a small part of it","When the service needs OpenAPI documentation","When the provider SDK is async-only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"FastAPI's strengths are async I/O and typed contracts; Django's are batteries for conventional web apps. If the bulk of the work is CRUD and admin, Django's ecosystem outweighs async purity."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/node-js-typescript","sources":["Official docs: Node.js (event loop, streams, worker_threads)","Official docs: TypeScript handbook","Official docs: Zod"],"why":"- **The web is built in TypeScript.** React, Next.js and the SSR frameworks that render them, the\n  serverless and edge platforms that host them, and the Vercel AI SDK that streams model output into\n  components. AI products are mostly chat and streaming interfaces, so a product team cannot avoid it.\n- **Same language as the UI removes overhead.** Putting the model-facing request path next to the UI\n  removes a translation layer, a deployment and a second on-call rota.\n- **\"Everything in Python\" fails at the edge.** The frontend team does not write Python, SSR frameworks are\n  TypeScript, and edge runtimes are built around V8; where Python is offered, it runs through WebAssembly\n  with a restricted package set.\n- **\"Everything in TypeScript\" fails at the core.** Training, fine-tuning, eval harnesses, tokenizers,\n  vector and data tooling are Python-first. The honest outcome is two languages; the real question is\n  where the boundary sits and how its contract stays honest.\n- **Node fits the edge for a mechanical reason.** Its single-threaded, non-blocking event loop holds\n  thousands of concurrent streams cheaply: open upstream, relay chunks, apply backpressure, close. It is\n  bad at CPU-bound main-thread work, like Python, but has no NumPy or PyTorch to hand that work to.","how":"### The event loop\n- **One JavaScript thread.** libuv provides a thread pool for file, DNS and crypto work and a loop that\n  dispatches completed I/O callbacks.\n- **Waiting is cheap.** A handler awaiting a network call costs a promise, not a thread.\n- **The corollary.** Synchronous CPU work on the main thread (parsing a 50 MB JSON body, tokenizing a book\n  in pure JavaScript) stops every other request.\n- **Escape hatches.** `worker_threads` for CPU-bound tasks, and heavy compute kept in the Python core,\n  *because* nothing on the main thread can preempt a running function.\n\n### Streams and backpressure\n1. **Read upstream incrementally.** Node 18 and later ship `fetch` and the Web Streams API globally, so a\n   handler reads a model stream chunk by chunk and writes it to the client as it arrives.\n2. **Watch the write signal.** `res.write()` returns `false` once the response's internal buffer passes\n   its high-water mark: the client is reading slower than you write.\n3. **Honour it.** Stop pulling from upstream and wait for `drain`. That keeps memory flat when a slow\n   browser consumes a fast model; ignoring it is the classic way a streaming proxy runs out of memory.\n\n- **Web Streams express the same idea** through `desiredSize` and pull-based reads, and `pipeTo` handles\n  it for you.\n\n### Cancellation\n- **The problem.** When the user closes the tab, the upstream model keeps generating tokens you are billed\n  for.\n- **The signal.** Node reports the hang-up as a `close` event on the response before it finished writing.\n- **The wiring.** Connect that to an `AbortController` whose `signal` was passed to `fetch`, which tears\n  down the upstream connection.\n- **The caveat.** It saves money only if the upstream in turn stops generating when its client\n  disconnects, so check that both hops propagate cancellation.\n\n### TypeScript: static types, no runtime\n- **Types are erased at compile time.** Inside your code they catch mistakes; at the boundary they\n  guarantee nothing, *because* data arrives at runtime from a model, a webhook or an API that never saw\n  your types.\n- **Pair a runtime schema with inferred types.** One Zod (or similar) schema validates the LLM's JSON,\n  derives the TypeScript type, and converts to JSON Schema for the model's tool definition.\n- **The other half of the discipline:** `strict` mode and exhaustive switches on discriminated unions.\n\n### Where it fits in an AI system\n| Node/TypeScript | Python |\n|---|---|\n| chat UI and its streaming route handlers | model serving |\n| backend-for-frontend adding auth and rate limits in front of the Python service | RAG pipelines |\n| serverless functions and edge middleware | evals |\n| MCP servers exposing product data as tools | fine-tuning |\n| agent orchestration through the OpenAI, Anthropic and Vercel SDKs | batch data jobs |\n\n- **The boundary is an HTTP (often SSE) contract.** Generating the TypeScript client from the Python\n  service's OpenAPI schema is how the two sides stay in sync.","example":"### Streaming gateway with backpressure, cancellation and Zod\nA minimal Node gateway that relays a streaming response from the Python core, honours backpressure,\ncancels the upstream call when the client disconnects, and validates a model-produced tool call at\nruntime with a Zod schema that also provides the static type for free.\n\n```ts\nimport http from \"node:http\";\nimport { Readable } from \"node:stream\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst ToolCall = z.object({                       // runtime schema: TS types vanish at runtime\n  name: z.literal(\"lookup_order\"),\n  arguments: z.object({ orderId: z.string().regex(/^ord_[a-z0-9]+$/) }),\n});\ntype ToolCall = z.infer<typeof ToolCall>;        // static type derived from the runtime schema\n\nasync function* relay(upstream: Response): AsyncGenerator<string> {\n  const reader = upstream.body!.getReader();\n  const dec = new TextDecoder();\n  for (;;) {\n    const { value, done } = await reader.read();  // yields the event loop between chunks\n    if (done) return;\n    yield dec.decode(value, { stream: true });\n  }\n}\n\nhttp.createServer(async (req, res) => {\n  const ac = new AbortController();               // client hangs up -> cancel upstream generation\n  res.on(\"close\", () => { if (!res.writableFinished) ac.abort(); });\n  try {\n    const upstream = await fetch(\"http://python-core:8000/v1/chat\", {\n      method: \"POST\", headers: { \"content-type\": \"application/json\" }, signal: ac.signal,\n      body: Readable.toWeb(req) as ReadableStream, // forward the client body without buffering it\n      duplex: \"half\",\n    } as RequestInit);\n    res.writeHead(200, { \"content-type\": \"text/event-stream\" });\n    for await (const chunk of relay(upstream)) {\n      if (!res.write(chunk))                       // buffer past high-water mark: client is slow\n        await new Promise((r) => { res.once(\"drain\", r); res.once(\"close\", r); });\n    }\n    res.end();\n  } catch (e) { if (!ac.signal.aborted) res.destroy(e as Error); } // abort is expected, not an error\n}).listen(3000);\n\nconst parsed = ToolCall.safeParse(JSON.parse('{\"name\":\"lookup_order\",\"arguments\":{\"orderId\":\"ord_42x\"}}'));\nif (!parsed.success) console.error(parsed.error.issues);   // never trust model output structurally\n```\n\n### Measuring a blocked event loop, and the worker fix\nRunnable with Node alone (`node lag.mjs`). `perf_hooks.monitorEventLoopDelay` records how late the\nloop's timer fires, in nanoseconds.\n\n```js\nimport { monitorEventLoopDelay } from \"node:perf_hooks\";\nimport { Worker } from \"node:worker_threads\";\n\nconst h = monitorEventLoopDelay({ resolution: 10 });     // sample loop delay every 10 ms\nh.enable();\n\nconst busy = (ms) => { const end = Date.now() + ms; while (Date.now() < end); }; // sync CPU work\n\nasync function phase(name, work) {\n  h.reset();\n  await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 50));             // first sample after reset is a baseline\n  await work();\n  await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 200));            // let the histogram take samples\n  console.log(`${name}: max loop delay ${(h.max / 1e6).toFixed(0)} ms`); // histogram is in ns\n}\n\nawait phase(\"idle\", async () => {});\nawait phase(\"500 ms of CPU on the main thread\", async () => busy(500));\nawait phase(\"same 500 ms in a worker thread\", () => new Promise((resolve) => {\n  const src = `const end = Date.now() + 500; while (Date.now() < end);\n               require(\"node:worker_threads\").parentPort.postMessage(\"done\");`;\n  new Worker(src, { eval: true }).once(\"message\", resolve); // main loop stays free meanwhile\n}));\n// idle: ~11 ms   main thread: ~507 ms   worker thread: ~11 ms\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: why the gateway's memory climbed\nA gateway relays 500 concurrent chats. The model emits ~50 tokens per second; a slow mobile client\nreads ~10. Figures are illustrative.\n\n| Design | Per slow stream after 60 s | 500 streams |\n|---|---|---|\n| Ignore `res.write()` returning `false` | ~2,400 unsent tokens buffered and growing | memory climbs until the process dies |\n| Wait for `drain` before pulling more | about one high-water mark of buffer | flat; the upstream TCP window throttles the model stream |\n| Plus abort on `close` | buffer freed when the tab closes | upstream connections track client connections |","patterns":["**Alert on event-loop delay.** Export `monitorEventLoopDelay` percentiles (p99, max) as a metric every few seconds and reset the histogram; alert when p99 climbs. Rising lag across all routes points to sync CPU work on the main thread.","**Move CPU work to a worker pool.** Tokenizing, large JSON transforms or hashing go to `worker_threads` (often through a pool library such as Piscina) or to the Python core. Monitor pool queue length and task duration.","**Abort upstream on disconnect.** Pass an `AbortController` signal to every upstream `fetch` and abort on the response `close` event before it finished. Monitor the ratio of upstream to client connections, and token spend against tokens delivered.","**Validate every boundary with a schema.** Parse model output, webhooks and third-party responses with Zod `safeParse` and log `error.issues` with the prompt version. Track validation-failure rate per model and prompt.","**Generate the client from OpenAPI.** Run a generator (for example openapi-typescript) over the Python service's schema in CI and fail the build on a diff. That turns a renamed field into a compile error instead of a silent `undefined`."],"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant B as Browser\n    participant G as Node gateway\n    participant P as Python core\n    B->>G: POST /chat\n    G->>P: fetch with AbortController signal\n    P-->>G: stream chunk\n    G-->>B: res.write chunk\n    Note over G: write returns false, wait for drain\n    B-->>G: drain, client caught up\n    P-->>G: next chunk\n    B--xG: tab closed, close event\n    G--xP: ac.abort cancels upstream","caption":"The gateway relays chunks, pauses when the browser falls behind, and aborts the upstream call when the\nbrowser leaves."},"interview":[{"q":"Why does a TypeScript service still need runtime validation?","a":"- **Types are erased at compile time:** the emitted JavaScript has no knowledge of `ToolCall`, so a cast\n  only silences the compiler.\n- **Boundary data never saw your types:** model output, webhooks and third-party APIs can send anything.\n- **The fix is one schema for both:** Zod validates at runtime, `z.infer` derives the static type, and the\n  same schema can become the model's tool JSON Schema."},{"q":"A Node streaming gateway's memory grows steadily under load until it is OOM-killed. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Suspect ignored backpressure:** check whether the relay keeps writing after `res.write()` returns\n  `false` instead of waiting for `drain`.\n- **Suspect leaked upstreams:** compare upstream and client connection counts; more upstreams means\n  disconnects are not aborting `fetch`.\n- **Confirm with a heap snapshot** under a slow-client load test, then add the `drain` wait and the\n  `AbortController` wiring and re-run."},{"q":"When would you not put an AI feature's backend in Node?","a":"- **When it touches weights, tokenizers or eval datasets:** that tooling is Python-first, and ports lag.\n- **When per-request CPU work is heavy:** the single JavaScript thread blocks everyone, and Node has no\n  NumPy-class native ecosystem to offload to.\n- **When serverless limits bite:** long agent loops or big streams hit platform timeouts and body-size\n  limits."},{"q":"How would you keep a TypeScript frontend and a Python AI service in sync as both evolve?","a":"- **Single source of truth:** the Python service's Pydantic models generate its OpenAPI schema.\n- **Generated client:** CI generates TypeScript types from that schema and fails on an unreviewed diff,\n  so a rename breaks the build, not production.\n- **Compatibility rules:** additive changes only within a version, plus contract tests on the boundary."},{"q":"A user closes the tab halfway through a long answer. What should happen, and how do you verify it?","a":"- **Gateway:** the response `close` event fires before the write finished; abort the `AbortController`\n  passed to `fetch`, which closes the upstream connection.\n- **Core:** the Python service must notice the disconnect and stop generation, or the abort saves\n  nothing; both hops must propagate cancellation.\n- **Verify:** load-test with clients that disconnect early and compare tokens generated against tokens\n  delivered."}],"tradeoffs":["**One language vs a split ecosystem.** One language from browser to gateway cuts translation and team overhead, but the model, eval and data ecosystem stays in Python, so you pay for a cross-language contract instead.","**Cheap streaming vs CPU fragility.** The event loop makes streaming proxies cheap, but any CPU-bound step on the main thread blocks everyone; it flips when per-request compute grows and you need worker threads or Python.","**Static safety vs runtime blindness.** Types give refactoring safety but vanish at runtime, so every external boundary needs a schema library and the duplication that comes with it.","**Frictionless serverless vs platform limits.** Serverless and edge deployment is easy for Node, but long-running agent loops and large streaming responses hit platform timeouts and body-size limits a long-lived server never sees."],"pitfalls":["**Validating model output with a cast (`as ToolCall`).** Symptom: a runtime `undefined is not a function` far from the boundary that produced the bad data. Fix: `safeParse`.","**Buffering the whole model stream before writing.** Symptom: time-to-first-token equals total generation time, and memory spikes under concurrent chats.","**Not propagating client disconnects upstream.** Symptom: token spend exceeds what users received, and open upstream connections outnumber open client connections.","**Sync tokenization or large JSON transforms in a route handler.** Symptom: every concurrent request's latency rises together, visible as event-loop lag.","**A hand-written TypeScript client drifting from the Python API.** Symptom: a field rename on one side surfaces as silent `undefined` on the other. Fix: generate it from OpenAPI."],"when":"- **Use Node/TypeScript** for the layer facing the product: UI streaming routes, a backend-for-frontend,\n  serverless glue, MCP servers for product data, and lightweight agent orchestration where the SDKs are\n  excellent.\n- **Keep in Python:** inference, evaluation, fine-tuning and data pipelines.\n- **TypeScript-only is reasonable** if the team is entirely TypeScript and the AI work is API calls to a\n  provider, until evals or custom models appear.\n- **Measure:** event-loop lag (`perf_hooks.monitorEventLoopDelay` gives a histogram), heap and RSS under\n  many concurrent slow streams, and the ratio of upstream to client connections; generate the client from\n  OpenAPI rather than typing it by hand.\n- **Interviews:** explain why types are erased, how backpressure works in a streaming proxy, and where the\n  Python boundary belongs. The `node-ts-for-ai` module goes deep on each.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes streaming route handlers, validates model output with Zod, keeps CPU work off the main thread, and generates the client types from the Python service's OpenAPI schema.","architect":"Draws the TypeScript/Python boundary, chooses between a BFF and direct calls, and owns the contract-generation pipeline that keeps the two sides from drifting.","fde":"Meets customers whose product teams are TypeScript-only and must show how the AI core integrates through SSE and typed clients without forcing a Python rewrite on them."},"check":{"q":"A customer's TypeScript team wants to build the entire AI feature, including evals and a\nretrieval pipeline, in Node to avoid \"a second language\". What do you tell them, and where would you\ndraw the line?","a":"I would separate the edge from the core. The chat route, streaming, auth and the MCP tools can\nlive in TypeScript today with first-class SDKs, and that is where their team is strongest. Evals,\nembedding pipelines, reranking and any fine-tuning depend on Python-first libraries and the research\nworkflow; rewriting them in Node means maintaining ports of tools that move monthly. The line is\n\"anything that touches model weights, tokenizers or evaluation datasets is Python; anything that\ntouches the user is TypeScript\", with an OpenAPI-generated client as the contract so the second\nlanguage costs a build step rather than a translation team."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must a TypeScript service validate an LLM's JSON output with a runtime schema even though the response type is declared?","options":["Because JSON.parse returns `any` only in strict mode","TypeScript types are erased at compile time, so declared types guarantee nothing about data that arrives at runtime","Because LLM output is always a string and must be converted","Because Node's fetch strips type information from responses"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Static types describe what the compiler assumes, not what the network delivers. A Zod-style schema checks the bytes that actually arrived and derives the static type from the same definition."},{"q":"Which operation blocks every other request in a Node gateway?","options":["Awaiting a 5-second streaming response from the model provider","Writing chunks to 500 concurrent SSE clients","Synchronously parsing a 50 MB JSON document in the route handler","Reading a secret from an environment variable"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Node runs JavaScript on one thread; synchronous CPU work cannot be interrupted, so all pending callbacks wait. Awaited I/O and buffered writes yield to the loop between operations."},{"q":"In a mixed TypeScript/Python AI stack, which responsibility belongs in the Python core?","options":["The streaming chat route handler next to the React UI","Edge middleware that adds auth headers","The evaluation harness and embedding pipeline","An MCP server exposing the product's CRM records as tools"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Evals, embeddings and anything near model weights depend on Python-first tooling. Routes, middleware and MCP servers for product data are edge concerns where TypeScript's SDKs and team fit win."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/rest-graphql-api-design","sources":["Fielding, 2000 — Architectural Styles and the Design of Network-based Software Architectures","IETF RFC 9110 — HTTP Semantics","IETF RFC 9457 — Problem Details for HTTP APIs","Official docs: GraphQL specification"],"why":"- **A shipped interface is frozen.** Casual day-one choices cost forever: an action-style endpoint named\n  `/getUserOrders2`, an error returned as 200 with `{\"ok\": false}`, a delete that is not safe to retry.\n  API styles make those decisions by convention instead of by accident.\n- **REST leans on HTTP instead of tunnelling through it.** Safe, idempotent `GET` can be cached and\n  retried blind; idempotent `PUT` and `DELETE` survive a timeout retry without duplicates; meaningful\n  status codes let load balancers count 5xx and retry policies act on 429 and 503 without reading bodies.\n- **The naive alternative throws that away.** One `POST /api` endpoint with an `action` field: nothing\n  cacheable, nothing retry-safe by inspection, every request identical to observability.\n- **GraphQL solved a different pain.** It arose at Facebook (open-sourced in 2015): mobile clients needed\n  many shapes of the same data, and REST either over-fetched (one fat resource) or under-fetched (three\n  round trips per screen), with endpoints multiplying per screen. Client-authored queries over a typed\n  schema fix that, at the cost of HTTP's per-resource semantics, so neither style dominates.\n- **AI services sharpen the question.** Model calls are big `POST` bodies with streaming responses, and\n  tools exposed to an agent are easiest to describe as REST operations with an OpenAPI schema.","how":"### REST: resources and methods\n- **Model nouns, not verbs:** `/conversations/{id}/messages`, not `/sendMessage`. Nest collections and\n  items at most a level or two.\n\n| Method | Semantics (defined by HTTP) |\n|---|---|\n| `GET` | safe and idempotent; may be cached |\n| `PUT` | replaces a resource; idempotent |\n| `DELETE` | idempotent |\n| `PATCH` | applies a partial change |\n| `POST` | creates or triggers; *not* idempotent, which is why non-trivial APIs accept an `Idempotency-Key` header on it |\n\n- **Idempotency is about server state, not identical responses.** A second `DELETE` of the same job may\n  return `204` or `404`; both are idempotent, because the resource ends up gone either way. Pick one and\n  document it, since clients write retry logic against it.\n\n### REST: status codes and errors\nStatus codes are the first thing a client branches on.\n\n| Code | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `200`, `201 Created` | success; created |\n| `202 Accepted` | work continues after the response |\n| `204` | empty success |\n| `400` / `422` | invalid input |\n| `401` / `403` | identity / permission |\n| `404` | not found |\n| `409` | state conflict |\n| `429` | rate limited |\n| `5xx` | your fault |\n\n- **One error envelope everywhere.** Problem Details (RFC 9457) gives a standard JSON shape with `type`,\n  `title`, `status` and `detail`.\n\n### REST: concurrency and conditional requests\n1. **A `GET` returns an `ETag`**, a version tag for the representation.\n2. **The updater sends `If-Match: <etag>`** with its `PUT` or `PATCH`.\n3. **The server answers `412 Precondition Failed`** if someone changed the resource in between.\n\n- **Optimistic concurrency without a custom `version` field.** It stops two editors (or two agents) from\n  silently overwriting each other's changes to the same prompt template or config.\n- **Cheap polling.** The same tag sent as `If-None-Match` on a `GET` lets the server answer\n  `304 Not Modified` with no body.\n\n### REST: hypermedia and long-running work\n- **Fielding's definition also requires hypermedia** (responses carrying links to what the client can do\n  next, often called HATEOAS). Most production \"REST\" APIs skip it; the useful remnants are the `Location`\n  header, `next` links in paginated responses, and job resources that link to their result.\n- **Long-running AI work maps cleanly.** `POST /summaries` returns `202` with a `Location` to a job\n  resource that clients `GET` until it is `succeeded`, or the response streams as `text/event-stream`.\n- **Both keep proxies and timeouts happy**, *because* no single request has to live as long as the job.\n\n### GraphQL: schema, resolvers, one endpoint\n1. **A schema in SDL** declares types and fields and three roots: `Query`, `Mutation`, `Subscription`.\n2. **A client sends a query** naming exactly the fields it wants.\n3. **The server executes it** by calling a resolver per field, walking the tree.\n\n- **N+1 by default.** Resolvers run per field and per parent object, so 100 conversations each resolving\n  `lastMessage` naively issue 101 database queries. The standard fix is a per-request DataLoader that\n  batches and caches lookups.\n- **No HTTP caching by default.** Almost everything travels as `POST` to one endpoint, unless you send\n  queries (typically persisted ones) over `GET`.\n- **Errors ride with data.** Field errors return alongside partial data in an `errors` array,\n  conventionally with a 200, so clients must inspect the body. The GraphQL-over-HTTP spec lets servers\n  using `application/graphql-response+json` answer 4xx when a request fails before execution.\n- **Cost limits are mandatory**, *because* clients author queries and an unbounded nested query is a\n  denial-of-service vector. Enforce depth and cost limits.\n- **Subscriptions have no fixed transport.** Most servers use WebSockets (the `graphql-ws` protocol); SSE\n  is a common alternative.\n\n### GraphQL: evolving the contract\n- **No URL versions.** Add fields freely: clients receive only what they ask for, so additions break no one.\n- **Deprecate, then remove.** Mark old fields `@deprecated(reason: ...)` and remove them once field-level\n  usage telemetry shows zero queries selecting them.\n- **That telemetry is a quiet advantage.** REST cannot give it per field without extra work, which matters\n  for a fast-moving product schema.\n\n### The decision\n| Situation | Choose | Because |\n|---|---|---|\n| One product UI assembling screens from a rich domain, team iterates weekly | GraphQL | shape selection moves to the client |\n| Service-to-service calls | REST + OpenAPI | simple, cacheable, retry-safe |\n| Public APIs for integrators | REST + OpenAPI | the schema generates clients |\n| Anything cached by CDNs | REST | HTTP caches key on method and URL |\n| Anything exposed to an LLM as a tool | REST + OpenAPI | the schema generates tool definitions directly |\n\n- **Many teams run both:** GraphQL in the product's BFF, REST underneath and outward.","example":"### REST: a long-running summarisation job in FastAPI\n`POST` returns `202` with a `Location`, `GET` is safe to poll, `DELETE` is idempotent.\n\n```python\nfrom uuid import uuid4\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Response, status\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\n\napp = FastAPI()\nJOBS: dict[str, dict] = {}                          # stand-in for a database\n\nclass SummarizeRequest(BaseModel):\n    document_id: str\n    max_words: int = 200\n\n@app.post(\"/v1/summaries\", status_code=status.HTTP_202_ACCEPTED)\nasync def create_summary(body: SummarizeRequest, response: Response):\n    job_id = f\"sum_{uuid4().hex[:12]}\"\n    JOBS[job_id] = {\"id\": job_id, \"status\": \"queued\", \"input\": body.model_dump(), \"result\": None}\n    # enqueue to a worker here; the HTTP request returns immediately\n    response.headers[\"Location\"] = f\"/v1/summaries/{job_id}\"   # where to poll\n    return JOBS[job_id]\n\n@app.get(\"/v1/summaries/{job_id}\")                  # GET is safe and idempotent: poll freely\nasync def get_summary(job_id: str):\n    job = JOBS.get(job_id)\n    if job is None:\n        # FastAPI wraps this as {\"detail\": {...}}; a real service maps errors to RFC 9457 in a handler\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail={\"type\": \"not_found\", \"job_id\": job_id})\n    return job                                       # 200 whether queued or done; status is in the body\n\n@app.delete(\"/v1/summaries/{job_id}\", status_code=204)   # idempotent: a second DELETE is also 204\nasync def cancel_summary(job_id: str):\n    JOBS.pop(job_id, None)\n    return Response(status_code=204)\n```\n\n### GraphQL: the same domain\nFor a client that wants to pick fields and subscribe to updates.\n\n```graphql\nenum JobStatus { QUEUED RUNNING SUCCEEDED FAILED }\ntype Summary { id: ID!  status: JobStatus!  result: String }\ntype Query { summary(id: ID!): Summary }\ntype Mutation { createSummary(documentId: ID!, maxWords: Int = 200): Summary! }\ntype Subscription { summaryUpdated(id: ID!): Summary! }\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: two agents edit one prompt template\nAgent A and agent B both improve the same template within seconds.\n\n| Step | Without `If-Match` | With `ETag` / `If-Match` |\n|---|---|---|\n| A and B `GET` the template | both read version `\"v7\"` | both receive `ETag: \"v7\"` |\n| A sends `PUT` | saved as v8 | `If-Match: \"v7\"` matches, saved, new tag `\"v8\"` |\n| B sends `PUT` | silently overwrites A's change | `If-Match: \"v7\"` fails: `412 Precondition Failed` |\n| B recovers | nothing to recover; \"my change disappeared\" | re-`GET` v8, merge, retry with `If-Match: \"v8\"` |","patterns":["**One Problem Details envelope.** Register exception handlers that map every error to RFC 9457 JSON with the `application/problem+json` media type and a stable `type` URI per error kind. Monitor error counts by `type` and status, which turns dashboards into a list of named failure modes.","**Idempotency keys on `POST`.** Require an `Idempotency-Key` on creates and job submissions, store key plus request hash plus response for a fixed window (for example 24 hours), and replay the stored response on a repeat. Monitor replay hits and key reuse with a different body (return `409` or `422`).","**Job resources with polling hints.** For model work over a few seconds, return `202` plus `Location`, and add a `Retry-After` hint so clients poll at a sane interval. Monitor job age by status and the share of jobs stuck in `queued`.","**GraphQL guardrails.** Use persisted (allow-listed) queries for first-party clients, a per-request DataLoader for every list relation, and depth or complexity limits in the server. Monitor database queries per operation and rejected-query counts.","**Breaking-change gate in CI.** Generate the OpenAPI document on every build and diff it against the released one with a tool such as oasdiff; fail on removed fields or tightened types. Track the count of deliberate breaking changes per release."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"New API boundary\"] --> Q1{\"Called by an agent, partner or CDN?\"}\n    Q1 -->|\"yes\"| R[\"REST plus OpenAPI\"]\n    Q1 -->|\"no\"| Q2{\"Many client shapes over one domain?\"}\n    Q2 -->|\"no\"| R\n    Q2 -->|\"yes\"| G[\"GraphQL in the BFF\"]\n    G --> G1[\"DataLoader, depth and cost limits\"]\n    R --> Q3{\"Job longer than a few seconds?\"}\n    Q3 -->|\"yes\"| J[\"202 plus job resource, or SSE\"]\n    Q3 -->|\"no\"| D[\"Plain request and response\"]","caption":"Start from the client and the boundary, not from taste: most AI-service boundaries land on REST."},"interview":[{"q":"What does \"idempotent\" mean for an HTTP method, and why does it matter for retries?","a":"- **Definition:** repeating the request leaves the server in the same state as sending it once; the\n  responses may differ (a second `DELETE` may return `404`).\n- **Why it matters:** a client or proxy can retry an idempotent request after a timeout without creating\n  duplicates; `GET`, `PUT` and `DELETE` are idempotent, `POST` is not.\n- **Making `POST` safe:** accept an `Idempotency-Key` and replay the stored result for repeats."},{"q":"Two users report that their edits to a shared config keep disappearing. How do you diagnose and fix it?","a":"- **Diagnosis:** a read-modify-write race; logs show two `PUT`s within seconds from different users, the\n  later one built on a stale read.\n- **Fix:** return an `ETag` on `GET` and require `If-Match` on `PUT`/`PATCH`; the losing writer gets `412`\n  and must re-read and merge.\n- **UX:** surface the conflict to the user instead of silently retrying with the newer version."},{"q":"When would you not use GraphQL?","a":"- **Public or partner APIs:** integrators expect REST, CDN caching and per-endpoint rate limits, all\n  awkward when everything is `POST /graphql`.\n- **Agent tools and service-to-service calls:** OpenAPI operations map directly onto tool definitions and\n  generated clients.\n- **Few client shapes:** the resolver, DataLoader and cost-limit machinery only pays off when many\n  clients need different shapes."},{"q":"A GraphQL endpoint's p99 jumped after a new screen shipped. What do you look at?","a":"- **Resolver fan-out:** database queries per operation; a new list field without DataLoader shows as\n  N+1 (100 items, 101 queries).\n- **Query cost:** depth and estimated complexity of the new query, and whether a cost limit should reject\n  or paginate it.\n- **Fix and guard:** batch with DataLoader, persist the query, and alert on queries-per-operation."},{"q":"How would you design an endpoint for a model call that takes 30 seconds to 5 minutes?","a":"- **Submit:** `POST` with an `Idempotency-Key` returns `202 Accepted`, a `Location` to the job, and\n  optionally a `Retry-After` polling hint.\n- **Observe:** clients `GET` the job until a terminal `status`, or subscribe to SSE progress events.\n- **Finish:** the result lives at its own URL and is cacheable once terminal; `DELETE` cancels and is\n  idempotent."}],"tradeoffs":["**REST caching vs round trips.** REST gets HTTP caching, retry-safety by method and tooling for free, but a screen needing five resources costs five round trips; it flips to GraphQL when client-side shape variety dominates.","**GraphQL exact shapes vs operational load.** Clients get exact shapes and one evolving schema, but you give up HTTP caching, must defend against expensive queries and must batch resolvers. It usually flips back to REST for public APIs, where caching and per-endpoint limits matter (GitHub and Shopify are notable GraphQL exceptions).","**`202` jobs vs simplicity.** A job resource keeps requests short and proxy-friendly, but clients must poll or subscribe, which costs more code than one long request when jobs are reliably under a few seconds.","**Strict status codes vs up-front work.** A single error envelope and strict codes make clients simple, but force you to decide the code for every failure up front rather than leaking exceptions as 500s."],"pitfalls":["**Verbs in URLs and everything as `POST`.** Symptom: nothing can be cached or safely retried, and observability shows one undifferentiated endpoint.","**Errors with status 200 and a flag in the body.** Symptom: retry and alerting policies never fire, and SDKs treat failures as success.","**GraphQL resolvers without DataLoader.** Symptom: query count scales with list length in the database logs (the N+1 pattern).","**Holding a request open for a multi-minute AI job.** Symptom: intermittent 504s from a proxy whose idle timeout you never configured, and clients retrying work that already ran.","**Read-modify-write without `ETag`/`If-Match` or a version field.** Symptom: \"my change disappeared\" reports when two users or two agents edit the same resource within seconds.","**Unlimited GraphQL query depth and cost.** Symptom: one client's nested query pins the database while everything else times out."],"when":"- **Default to REST with an OpenAPI schema** for AI service endpoints, internal service-to-service calls\n  and anything an agent calls as a tool; the schema becomes the client, the docs and the tool definition.\n- **Reach for GraphQL** when a product team owns a UI that assembles many shapes from a shared domain and\n  is paying for it in round trips and endpoint sprawl.\n- **Measure** round trips per screen, cache hit rate and p99 latency before switching either way.\n- **Day to day:** name resources, choose status codes deliberately, and design the long-running-job\n  pattern.\n- **Interviews:** expect \"design the API for X\" questions testing whether you reach for idempotency,\n  `202` plus polling or streaming, and a consistent error shape without being prompted.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Designs resource paths and status codes, implements `202` plus polling or SSE for model calls, and keeps the OpenAPI schema accurate enough to generate clients and tool definitions.","architect":"Decides which style each boundary uses (GraphQL in the BFF, REST between services and outward), sets the error envelope and idempotency conventions, and reviews breaking changes.","fde":"Maps a customer's existing REST or GraphQL conventions onto the AI service's contract and explains why model endpoints need async job patterns their CRUD APIs never required."},"check":{"q":"You are designing the API for a document-analysis service whose jobs take 30 seconds to 5\nminutes and whose results are read by a web UI, a partner integration and an internal agent. Which\nstyle and request pattern do you choose, and why?","a":"REST with an OpenAPI schema, because three different clients including an agent need a\ncontract that generates clients and tool definitions, and partners expect it. `POST /analyses` returns\n`202 Accepted` with a `Location` and a job resource; clients `GET` it until `status` is terminal, or\nsubscribe to an SSE stream of progress events for the UI. The `POST` accepts an `Idempotency-Key` so a\npartner's retry after a timeout does not run the job twice. Results live at `/analyses/{id}/result`\nand are cacheable once terminal. I would not pick GraphQL here: the shapes are few, the clients are\nmostly machines, and long-running work plus caching favour HTTP semantics."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why return `202 Accepted` with a `Location` header for a five-minute AI job instead of holding the request open until it finishes?","options":["Because `200` cannot carry a JSON body larger than the proxy buffer","Proxies and clients have idle timeouts, and a short request plus a pollable job resource survives them and is retry-safe","Because `202` enables HTTP caching of the eventual result","Because FastAPI cannot await for more than 60 seconds"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Long-held connections hit proxy and client timeouts and make retries ambiguous. A job resource turns the work into state that can be polled, cached when terminal and cancelled."},{"q":"Why can an ordinary HTTP cache not store GraphQL responses by default?","options":["GraphQL responses are always compressed","The GraphQL spec forbids caching","Queries go as `POST` to one endpoint, so the URL does not identify the resource being read","GraphQL responses never include a `Content-Type` header"],"answer":2,"explanation":"HTTP caches key on method and URL. With every query a `POST` to `/graphql`, there is nothing to key on; persisted queries over `GET` are the workaround."},{"q":"A GraphQL list of 100 conversations resolving `lastMessage` issues 101 database queries. What is the standard fix?","options":["Switch the field to a REST endpoint","A per-request DataLoader that batches and caches the lookups","Increase the database connection pool to 101","Mark the field as `@deprecated`"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Resolvers run per parent object, so without batching each one queries alone. DataLoader collects the keys requested in one tick and issues a single batched query."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/api-and-sdk-integration","sources":["Official docs: httpx","Official docs: OpenAI Python SDK","Official docs: Anthropic Python SDK","Nygard, 2007 — Release It!"],"why":"- **Every remote system misbehaves on schedule.** A model provider, vector database, CRM or payment\n  processor returns 429 when you are busiest, 503 during its deploys, a half-finished stream when a load\n  balancer recycles, and a slightly different JSON shape after a release you did not read about.\n- **The naive version is `requests.post(url, json=payload)` everywhere.** No timeout (the `requests`\n  library applies none by default), so a hung provider pins a worker forever; no retry, so a transient\n  503 reaches users; raw exceptions, so callers cannot tell \"retry later\" from \"your request is invalid\".\n- **Scattered keys and silent incidents.** The API key is read in twelve places, so rotation is a treasure\n  hunt. The signature incident: every worker blocked on one vendor while the dashboard shows zero errors.\n- **\"The SDK handles it\" is half true.** Official SDKs retry 429 and 5xx with backoff, parse streams and\n  type responses, but their defaults are generic. Retries silently multiply latency in a slow outage, and\n  cost whenever an attempt the provider already processed is abandoned and resent.\n- **Defaults and upgrades bite.** Default timeouts in the major Python LLM SDKs are on the order of ten\n  minutes at the time of writing, so one stuck call holds a request for minutes, and an SDK upgrade can\n  change the API version it speaks. A layer must own those decisions explicitly.","how":"### Raw HTTP versus SDK\n| An SDK gives you | An SDK hides |\n|---|---|\n| auth headers and a base URL | the connection pool |\n| typed request and response models | the timeout values |\n| retry with exponential backoff on retryable statuses, honouring `Retry-After` | which calls it considers safe to retry |\n| SSE parsing into events; auto-pagination iterators | the mapping from HTTP status to exception class |\n| matching sync and async clients | |\n\n- **Read the constructor before shipping.** `timeout` and `max_retries` define your worst-case latency.\n\n### The adapter: one module owns the vendor\n- **Wrap each external system in one module** that exposes your own types and raises your own exceptions,\n  *because* vendor types and errors leak otherwise, and swapping or adding a provider becomes a rewrite.\n- **Classify every failure:**\n\n| Class | Examples | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| Retryable | 429, 5xx (most often 502/503/504), connection errors, timeouts, truncated streams | back off and retry |\n| Terminal | 400/422 (your request is wrong), 401/403 (credentials), 404, content-policy refusals | fail now |\n| Ambiguous | a timeout on a non-idempotent call; the request may have completed | retry only with an idempotency key |\n\n### Timeouts\n- **Set connect, read and total timeouts separately.**\n- **Read timeout is a gap, not a deadline.** In httpx and most clients it is the longest allowed gap\n  between received bytes. On a stream it bounds time-to-first-token and every inter-chunk gap.\n- **On a non-streaming model call** the server sends nothing until generation finishes, so the read\n  timeout effectively bounds generation time.\n- **A stream may run for minutes** while each chunk arrives within a second, so the read timeout is a\n  per-chunk budget.\n- **A total deadline needs a separate wrapper** (`asyncio.timeout` around the call). Tie it to what the\n  user-facing request can afford, not to what the provider allows.\n\n### Retries, idempotency and budgets\n- **Backoff with full jitter.** Sleep a uniform random time in `[0, min(cap, base × 2^n)]` and honour\n  `Retry-After` when present, *because* synchronized retries from many clients create a thundering herd.\n- **Retry only idempotent operations or ones carrying an idempotency key.** An LLM completion is usually\n  safe to re-run (it costs money, not correctness); a tool call that sends an email is not.\n- **Bound retries** per request and per time window so an outage does not triple your traffic.\n\n```\nworst-case latency = a·t + S        a = attempts, t = per-attempt timeout, S = total backoff sleep\noutage load on the vendor           up to a × normal traffic\n```\n\n- **The arithmetic is `a·t + S`**, and layering multiplies it: 3 adapter attempts around an SDK making 3\n  per call (its default of 2 retries) turn one user request into 9 upstream calls.\n- **Retry in exactly one layer** and switch the others off (`max_retries=0` on the SDK client).\n\n### Streams fail differently\n1. **A stream can return `200`, send half the answer and stop**: a load balancer recycled the connection,\n   or the provider hit an internal error mid-generation.\n2. **The status already said success**, so the only signal is the missing terminal event: a final\n   `finish_reason`, a stop event or a `[DONE]` sentinel, depending on the provider.\n3. **Treat a stream that ends without its terminal event as a retryable failure**, not a short answer.\n4. **Retrying a stream affects the user.** Tokens already shown cannot be unshown: restart and replace the\n   partial text, or surface the truncation and let the user retry.\n\n### Circuit breakers, fallbacks and connection reuse\n- **Circuit breaker.** After N consecutive failures, stop calling the vendor for a cool-down and fail\n  fast or route to a fallback model; this protects your latency and the vendor's recovery.\n- **One client per process**, so TLS handshakes and connections are reused; a client per request is a\n  common source of socket exhaustion.\n\n### Configuration, observability and tests\n- **Keys** come from a secret store or environment, never source.\n- **Log every call:** the vendor's request id, status, latency, attempt count and token usage, with a\n  correlation id.\n- **Pin** SDK versions and any API-version header.\n- **Test** happy paths with recorded responses and failure paths with injected 429/timeout/truncation;\n  run a small contract test against the live sandbox on a schedule.","example":"### A minimal adapter with a total deadline\nExplicit per-phase timeouts, a shared client, an error taxonomy, bounded retries with jitter that honour\n`Retry-After`, an idempotency key so a retried `POST` cannot double-execute, and one total deadline the\nretries must fit inside.\n\n- **Without the deadline:** three attempts that each hit the 60-second read timeout, plus backoff sleeps,\n  can hold the caller for over three minutes.\n- **With a 20-second budget:** the first attempt times out at the deadline, and the caller gets a\n  `TimeoutError` at 20 seconds that it can turn into a fallback or a clear error.\n- **A `Retry-After: 30` hint on a 429** cannot fit in the remaining budget, so the adapter fails\n  immediately instead of sleeping past the point where the user has given up.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, os, random\nimport httpx\n\nclass RetryableError(Exception):\n    def __init__(self, msg: str, retry_after: float | None = None):\n        super().__init__(msg); self.retry_after = retry_after\n\nclass TerminalError(Exception): ...\n\nTIMEOUT = httpx.Timeout(connect=5.0, read=60.0, write=10.0, pool=5.0)   # explicit, per phase\nclient = httpx.AsyncClient(base_url=\"https://api.example-llm.com\", timeout=TIMEOUT,\n                           headers={\"Authorization\": \"Bearer \" + os.environ[\"LLM_API_KEY\"]})\n\ndef classify(r: httpx.Response) -> None:\n    if r.status_code < 400:\n        return\n    if r.status_code == 429 or r.status_code >= 500:\n        ra = r.headers.get(\"retry-after\")\n        raise RetryableError(f\"HTTP {r.status_code}\", float(ra) if ra and ra.isdigit() else None)\n    raise TerminalError(f\"HTTP {r.status_code}: {r.text[:200]}\")   # 400/401/403/422: retrying won't help\n\nasync def complete(payload: dict, idem_key: str, attempts: int = 3, budget_s: float = 20.0) -> dict:\n    loop = asyncio.get_running_loop()\n    deadline = loop.time() + budget_s                  # total budget across ALL attempts and sleeps\n    async with asyncio.timeout_at(deadline):           # Python 3.11+; raises TimeoutError at the deadline\n        for n in range(attempts):\n            try:\n                r = await client.post(\"/v1/complete\", json=payload,\n                                      headers={\"Idempotency-Key\": idem_key})   # safe to retry\n                classify(r)\n                return r.json()\n            except (RetryableError, httpx.TimeoutException, httpx.TransportError) as e:\n                hinted = getattr(e, \"retry_after\", None)\n                delay = hinted if hinted else random.uniform(0, min(8.0, 2 ** n))  # full jitter\n                if n == attempts - 1 or loop.time() + delay >= deadline:\n                    raise                              # retrying cannot finish inside the budget\n                await asyncio.sleep(delay)\n    raise RuntimeError(\"unreachable\")\n```\n\n### A circuit breaker in front of the adapter\nRunnable with the standard library. After `threshold` consecutive failures calls fail fast; after the\ncool-down they probe again, and one failed probe re-opens it.\n\n```python\nimport time\n\nclass CircuitBreaker:\n    \"\"\"Open after `threshold` consecutive failures; after `cooldown_s`, let calls probe again.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, threshold: int = 5, cooldown_s: float = 30.0):\n        self.threshold, self.cooldown_s = threshold, cooldown_s\n        self.failures, self.opened_at = 0, None\n\n    def allow(self) -> bool:\n        if self.opened_at is None:\n            return True                                   # closed: normal traffic\n        return time.monotonic() - self.opened_at >= self.cooldown_s   # half-open after cool-down\n\n    def record(self, ok: bool) -> None:\n        if ok:\n            self.failures, self.opened_at = 0, None       # a success closes the breaker\n        else:\n            self.failures += 1\n            if self.failures >= self.threshold:           # also re-opens on a failed probe\n                self.opened_at = time.monotonic()\n\nb = CircuitBreaker(threshold=3, cooldown_s=0.2)\nfor _ in range(3):\n    b.record(False)\nprint(b.allow())            # False: open, calls fail fast\ntime.sleep(0.25)\nprint(b.allow())            # True: half-open, calls may probe again\nb.record(False)\nprint(b.allow())            # False: the probe failed, open again\ntime.sleep(0.25); b.record(True)\nprint(b.allow())            # True: closed\n```\n\nIn the service, check `allow()` before `complete(...)`, call `record(True)` on success and\n`record(False)` on retryable failures or `TimeoutError`, and route to a fallback model while it is open.\n\n### Worked scenario: retries in two layers during an outage\nA service at 100 user requests per second calls a provider through its SDK (default 2 retries, so 3\nattempts) wrapped in an adapter that also makes 3 attempts. The provider starts returning 503.\n\n| Quantity | Two layers retrying | Adapter only (`max_retries=0`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Upstream calls per user request | 3 × 3 = 9 | 3 |\n| Load sent to the struggling provider | ~900 calls/s | ~300 calls/s |\n| Worst case per request, ten-minute SDK timeout | up to 9 × 10 min of waiting, plus sleeps | capped by the 20 s total deadline |\n| What users see | requests hang, then fail | a clear error or fallback within 20 s |","patterns":["**Retry in one layer only.** Set `max_retries=0` and an explicit `timeout` on the SDK client, and own the policy in the adapter (or the reverse, but never both). Monitor a histogram of attempts per request and alert when the mean climbs above 1.","**A total deadline per user request.** Wrap every vendor call in `asyncio.timeout` sized from the user-facing budget, and pass the remaining budget down to nested calls. Monitor p99 latency including retries and the deadline-exceeded count.","**Breaker plus fallback model.** Open after N consecutive retryable failures, fail fast or route to a secondary provider or smaller model, and probe after a cool-down. Monitor breaker state changes and the share of traffic served by the fallback.","**Log the vendor's request id on every call.** Providers return a request-id response header (named differently per provider); log it with your correlation id, status, attempts and token usage. It is what the vendor's support team asks for during an incident.","**Fault-injected tests plus a scheduled sandbox contract test.** Mock the transport (for example `httpx.MockTransport` or the respx library) to inject 429s, timeouts and truncated streams in CI, and run a few live calls against the vendor sandbox on a schedule. Alert when the contract test fails."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"Caller\"] --> B{\"Breaker open?\"}\n    B -->|\"yes\"| F[\"Fail fast or fallback model\"]\n    B -->|\"no\"| A[\"Attempt with per-phase timeouts\"]\n    A --> K{\"Classify result\"}\n    K -->|\"2xx with terminal event\"| OK[\"Return your own type\"]\n    K -->|\"400, 401, 403, 422\"| T[\"Terminal error, no retry\"]\n    K -->|\"429, 5xx, timeout, truncated\"| R{\"Budget left and idempotent?\"}\n    R -->|\"yes\"| S[\"Sleep: Retry-After or full jitter\"]\n    S --> A\n    R -->|\"no\"| E[\"Raise, record breaker failure\"]","caption":"Each attempt is classified; only retryable failures loop, and only while the total budget allows."},"interview":[{"q":"What does the read timeout actually bound on a streaming model call?","a":"- **Definition:** the longest allowed gap between received bytes, not the whole response.\n- **On a stream:** it bounds time-to-first-token and each inter-chunk gap, so a healthy stream can run for\n  minutes without tripping it.\n- **Consequence:** you need a separate total deadline, such as `asyncio.timeout`, sized from the\n  user-facing budget."},{"q":"During a vendor incident your workers are all busy but error rates are zero. What is happening and how do you fix it?","a":"- **Diagnosis:** calls are hanging, not failing; there is no timeout or a multi-minute default, so\n  workers wait instead of erroring.\n- **Immediate fix:** explicit connect and read timeouts plus a total deadline, so failures surface fast.\n- **Structural fix:** a circuit breaker that fails fast or routes to a fallback, and alerts on in-flight\n  calls and latency, not only error rate."},{"q":"Which errors should never be retried, and which are ambiguous?","a":"- **Never:** 400/422, 401/403 and 404 are deterministic; a retry repeats the bad request and burns quota.\n- **Retry:** 429 (honour `Retry-After`), 502/503/504, connection errors and truncated streams.\n- **Ambiguous:** a timeout on a non-idempotent call may have succeeded; retry only with an idempotency key."},{"q":"Your adapter makes 3 attempts and the SDK has its default 2 retries. What happens at 100 requests per second during an outage?","a":"- **Multiplication:** 3 × 3 = 9 upstream calls per user request, about 900 calls per second at the\n  struggling vendor.\n- **Latency:** worst case grows as attempts times timeout plus sleeps in each layer.\n- **Fix:** retry in one layer (`max_retries=0` on the SDK), add jitter and a per-request retry budget."},{"q":"When would you bypass the official SDK and call the HTTP API directly?","a":"- **Missing features:** a new endpoint or parameter the SDK has not shipped yet.\n- **Control:** exact streaming behaviour, a custom transport, or an SDK that blocks the event loop.\n- **Cost:** you then own auth, retries, pagination and stream parsing yourself, so keep it behind the\n  same adapter interface."}],"tradeoffs":["**SDK convenience vs control.** An SDK saves weeks of client code and tracks API changes, but chooses retry and timeout defaults you must override and couples you to its release cadence; raw HTTP wins only for tiny surfaces or unsupported languages.","**Hidden failures vs amplified outages.** Automatic retries hide transient failures, but each costs money and latency and can amplify an outage; it flips when the operation is non-idempotent or the budget is tight.","**Adapter layer vs ceremony.** A thin adapter per vendor adds types and mapping, but makes provider swaps, fallbacks and tests cheap; for a single-vendor prototype it can feel like ceremony.","**Aggressive timeouts vs slow successes.** They protect your latency budget, but turn slow successes into failures and create ambiguous outcomes on non-idempotent calls."],"pitfalls":["**No timeout, or an inherited multi-minute default.** Symptom: worker pools drain during a vendor incident while error rates stay at zero.","**Retrying everything, including 400 and 401.** Symptom: the same invalid request hammers the vendor four times, with a rate-limit ban on top of the original bug.","**Retrying a non-idempotent `POST` after a timeout without an idempotency key.** Symptom: duplicate records, double charges or two emails sent.","**Retrying in two layers** (your wrapper and the SDK, or a gateway and the app). Symptom: vendor-side request counts several times your own during an incident.","**Treating a stream without its terminal event as complete.** Symptom: answers stop mid-sentence and parsers fail on truncated JSON with no error in the logs.","**A new client per request.** Symptom: a TLS handshake on every call and \"too many open files\" under load.","**Parsing vendor error bodies ad hoc in callers.** Symptom: a schema change in the error payload breaks twelve call sites at once."],"when":"- **Scale the adapter to blast radius.** A model provider on the request path gets timeouts, retries,\n  idempotency keys, a breaker and metrics; a nightly report API can live with a timeout and a retry.\n- **Use the official SDK** when one exists and you have read its timeout and retry settings.\n- **Drop to `httpx`** when the SDK lacks a feature, blocks the event loop, or you need exact control over\n  streaming.\n- **Measure** attempt counts, p99 latency including retries, and vendor request ids in logs.\n- **Interviews:** \"how do you call a third-party API in production\" is standard; walk timeouts, retry\n  policy, idempotency, error taxonomy and observability in that order.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds and tests the adapter: explicit timeouts, retry with jitter, idempotency keys, one shared client, and error mapping that callers can branch on.","architect":"Sets the integration standard (which errors retry, the retry budget, breaker thresholds, fallback routing) and decides where provider abstraction lives.","fde":"Integrates the AI service with a customer's internal APIs whose SDKs may be absent or unreliable, and negotiates sandbox access, rate limits and credentials for them."},"check":{"q":"Your service calls a model provider through its official SDK with defaults. During a provider\nincident, user requests take four minutes to fail and your token spend for the hour jumps well above\nnormal. Explain what happened and how you would configure the integration differently.","a":"The SDK retried each failing call (twice by default in the major LLM SDKs) with backoff, and its\nlong default timeout let each attempt wait minutes, so one user request became several long attempts.\nEach retry resent the full prompt, and attempts the provider had partly or fully processed before the\nconnection died or the client gave up are generally still billed, while users who saw failures retried\non top; that is the cost spike (exactly which failed requests are billed is provider-specific). I would set an explicit total timeout tied to the user-facing\nbudget, cap retries to one or two with jitter and honour `Retry-After`, add a circuit breaker that\nfails fast or routes to a fallback model after consecutive failures, and emit attempt-count and\nlatency metrics so the behaviour is visible. Idempotency keys would make the remaining retries safe\nif the call had side effects."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which failure should an integration layer not retry automatically?","options":["A 503 from the provider's load balancer","A connect timeout","A 422 saying a required field is missing","A 429 with a `Retry-After` header"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Retries help only when the next attempt can succeed. A validation error is deterministic, so retrying repeats the same bad request, wastes quota and delays the real fix."},{"q":"Why add an `Idempotency-Key` header when retrying a `POST` after a timeout?","options":["It makes the request faster on the second attempt","The first attempt may have completed, and the key lets the server return that result instead of executing again","It tells the server to skip authentication on retries","It disables the server's rate limiter for the retry"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A timeout is ambiguous: the server may have done the work. An idempotency key lets it recognise the repeat and return the stored outcome, preventing duplicate side effects."},{"q":"What is the main reason to keep one HTTP client per process rather than per request?","options":["Per-request clients cannot send custom headers","A single client guarantees ordered delivery of requests","Connection and TLS session reuse, plus one place to configure timeouts and limits","Multiple clients would share the same API key incorrectly"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each new client opens fresh connections and TLS handshakes, which cost latency and sockets. A shared client pools connections and centralises the configuration the whole service depends on."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility","sources":["IETF RFC 8594 — The Sunset HTTP Header Field","Official docs: Stripe API versioning","Official docs: Anthropic API versions","Winters, Manshreck & Wright, 2020 — Software Engineering at Google (Hyrum's Law)"],"why":"- **Clients are distributed in time as well as space.** A mobile app users never update, a partner\n  integration built by a contractor who left, an SDK pinned in a lockfile, an agent whose system prompt\n  describes your tool's arguments. Rename a field and each breaks silently at its own pace, with no\n  rollback.\n- **Hyrum's law.** With enough users, every observable behaviour of your interface becomes something\n  somebody depends on, documented or not.\n- **The three naive responses fail.** \"Never break anything\" freezes bad decisions and bloats the API\n  with single-client fields. \"Bump to `/v2` whenever we change something\" produces version sprawl: each\n  version is a fork to maintain, test and secure, and nothing forces clients to migrate.\n- **\"Be careful\" fails too**, the first time a well-meaning engineer makes a required response field\n  optional and a strict client starts throwing.\n- **The working discipline sits between.** Additive, compatible changes are the normal path so versions\n  are rare; an unavoidable break is versioned deliberately, deprecated with dates, measured, and removed\n  on schedule. AI services need it twice over, because a model or prompt change can alter outputs for the\n  same input, which is also a breaking change from the client's point of view.","how":"### What counts as breaking\n- **Breaking for clients:** removing or renaming a field or endpoint; changing a field's type or format\n  (integer to string, seconds to milliseconds); making an optional request field required; tightening\n  validation; changing a default; changing the meaning of a value; changing status codes or the error\n  envelope; changing authentication.\n- **Usually compatible:** adding an optional request field, adding a response field, adding an\n  endpoint, relaxing validation.\n- **Contested:** adding an enum value, which breaks clients that switch exhaustively. Document that\n  enums are open and clients must handle unknowns.\n\n### Tolerant readers\n- **Compatibility is a two-sided contract.** Clients ignore unknown response fields and treat enums as\n  open; servers accept missing optional fields and ignore unknown request fields unless the endpoint is\n  security-sensitive.\n- **Pydantic's default of ignoring extra fields is the tolerant choice.** `extra=\"forbid\"` is stricter\n  and turns a client's harmless new field into a 422, so choose it knowingly.\n- **Postel's principle, applied with judgement**, *because* total leniency hides bugs.\n\n### Versioning strategies\n| Strategy | Example | Gain | Cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| URL path | `/v1/...`, as OpenAI does | visible, routable, easy to test and cache | coarse: the whole API moves together |\n| Header | `Accept: application/vnd.acme.v2+json`, or a custom header such as Anthropic's `anthropic-version` date | stable URLs, per-request choice | harder to try in a browser, easy to forget in a proxy |\n| Pinned date per account | Stripe: pinned to the version current at the account's first call, overridable per request with `Stripe-Version` | maximum compatibility | a chain of server-side transformations renders new behaviour in old shapes |\n| Query parameter | `?version=2` | simple | mixes with filters and cache keys |\n| Evolution only | GraphQL: add fields, mark old ones `@deprecated`, remove after usage reaches zero | no versions at all | needs field-level usage telemetry |\n\n- **Keep one implementation and render versions at the edge as representations**, *because* two forked\n  code paths drift and the old one stops getting fixes.\n\n### Deprecation lifecycle\n1. **Announce** in docs and changelog.\n2. **Signal in-band.** On old-version responses emit `Sunset` (RFC 8594, an HTTP date) and a `Link` to\n   the migration guide, and mark the route deprecated in OpenAPI.\n3. **Add the deprecation header.** RFC 9745 (2025) adds a `Deprecation` header whose value is a\n   structured-field date (`@` followed by Unix seconds) and a `deprecation` link relation for the guide.\n4. **Measure** requests per version per client, so you know who is left.\n5. **Run brownouts**: short planned outages of the old version before removal.\n6. **Remove on the announced date.**\n\n### Catching breaks before they ship\nReview by eye misses breaks, so make them mechanical with two complementary CI checks.\n- **Schema diff (provider side).** Compare the OpenAPI document generated from the branch with the one\n  published from `main`; fail on anything in the breaking list (removed field, narrowed type, new\n  required input).\n- **Consumer-driven contract tests (Pact style).** Each client publishes the requests it sends and the\n  fields it reads, and the provider's build replays them. This catches Hyrum's-law dependencies the\n  schema never promised, such as a client relying on list order.\n\n### SDKs and client libraries\n- **An SDK has its own compatibility surface** on top of the wire API: method names, exception classes,\n  default timeouts and retries.\n- **Version it with semantic versioning**, so a breaking change to any of those is a major bump even when\n  the HTTP API did not change.\n- **Decouple the SDK version from the API version it speaks.** Pinning the API version inside each SDK\n  release is common, so upgrading the library is not silently also an API migration.\n\n### Data and schema changes: expand and contract\n1. **Expand:** add the new field and populate both, so old and new readers both work.\n2. **Migrate:** move every consumer to the new field, verified by telemetry rather than by asking.\n3. **Contract:** stop writing the old field, then remove it.\n\n- **The order matters** *because* at every point each deployed producer and consumer, old or new, still\n  sees a field it understands, which lets the two sides deploy independently.\n- **Same pattern** for message schemas and tool arguments.\n\n### AI-specific contracts\n- **Model versions retire.** Pin exact snapshot names and track provider deprecation notices.\n- **Prompts are contracts.** A revision that changes output format is a contract change for everything\n  downstream, so version prompts alongside code.\n- **Tool schemas are contracts.** A change can break an agent that learned the old arguments from its\n  prompt or few-shot examples.\n- **Embeddings are versioned data.** Re-embedding with a new model invalidates an index built with the\n  old one.","example":"### Two versioned routers over one implementation\nThe v2 shape renamed `summary` to `text` and added citations, a breaking change. So v1 is kept as a thin\nrepresentation with a `Sunset` header and an OpenAPI deprecation flag, not as a forked code path.\n\n```python\nfrom fastapi import APIRouter, FastAPI, Response\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ConfigDict\n\nclass SummaryV2(BaseModel):             # current shape: renamed field, structured citations\n    id: str\n    text: str\n    citations: list[dict]\n    model: str\n\nclass SummaryV1(BaseModel):             # frozen shape old clients depend on\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"ignore\")   # Pydantic's default, spelled out: unknown input dropped\n    id: str\n    summary: str                        # renamed to `text` in v2, hence the shim below\n\nasync def summarize(doc_id: str) -> SummaryV2:\n    return SummaryV2(id=\"sum_1\", text=\"...\", citations=[{\"doc\": doc_id, \"span\": [0, 40]}],\n                     model=\"summarizer-2026-03-01\")   # pinned model snapshot is part of the contract\n\nv1, v2 = APIRouter(prefix=\"/v1\"), APIRouter(prefix=\"/v2\")\n\n@v2.post(\"/summaries\", response_model=SummaryV2)\nasync def create_v2(doc_id: str):\n    return await summarize(doc_id)\n\n@v1.post(\"/summaries\", response_model=SummaryV1, deprecated=True)   # flagged in OpenAPI docs\nasync def create_v1(doc_id: str, response: Response):\n    s = await summarize(doc_id)                      # one implementation, two representations\n    response.headers[\"Deprecation\"] = \"@1767225600\"            # RFC 9745: deprecated since 2026-01-01\n    response.headers[\"Sunset\"] = \"Sat, 31 Oct 2026 00:00:00 GMT\"  # RFC 8594: removal date\n    response.headers[\"Link\"] = '</docs/migrate-v2>; rel=\"deprecation\"'\n    return SummaryV1(id=s.id, summary=s.text)        # render the old shape; drop fields v1 never promised\n\napp = FastAPI()\napp.include_router(v1)\napp.include_router(v2)\n```\n\n### A tolerant reader on the client side\nRunnable with the standard library. The client survives a new enum value, a new field and the\n`summary` to `text` rename during the expand phase.\n\n```python\nfrom enum import Enum\n\nclass Status(str, Enum):\n    QUEUED = \"queued\"\n    DONE = \"done\"\n    UNKNOWN = \"unknown\"\n\n    @classmethod\n    def _missing_(cls, value):          # open enum: a value added later maps to UNKNOWN\n        return cls.UNKNOWN\n\ndef read_job(payload: dict) -> dict:\n    return {\n        \"id\": payload[\"id\"],\n        \"status\": Status(payload.get(\"status\", \"unknown\")),\n        \"text\": payload.get(\"text\") or payload.get(\"summary\"),  # new name first, old as fallback\n    }                                    # unknown fields such as \"citations\" are simply ignored\n\nprint(read_job({\"id\": \"j1\", \"status\": \"done\", \"summary\": \"old shape\"}))\nprint(read_job({\"id\": \"j2\", \"status\": \"cancelled\", \"text\": \"new shape\", \"citations\": []}))\n# {'id': 'j1', 'status': <Status.DONE: 'done'>, 'text': 'old shape'}\n# {'id': 'j2', 'status': <Status.UNKNOWN: 'unknown'>, 'text': 'new shape'}\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: renaming a field with expand and contract\nRename `summary` to `text` on an endpoint with three integrators. Dates are illustrative.\n\n| Week | Server writes | Readers that work | Gate to move on |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0 (expand) | `summary` and `text` | old and new | deploy is additive, no gate |\n| 1-8 (migrate) | both; `Deprecation` and `Sunset` on responses | old and new | per-client telemetry: reads of `summary` |\n| 9 (brownout) | `text` only for two one-hour windows | new only | no errors from the remaining clients |\n| 10 (contract) | `text` only | new only | announced date reached, usage at zero |","patterns":["**Breaking-change gate in CI.** Generate the OpenAPI document on every build and diff it against the published one with a tool such as oasdiff (its `breaking` command), failing the build on removals, narrowed types or new required inputs. Track deliberate breaks per release.","**Consumer contracts before deploy.** With Pact, consumers publish contracts to a broker and the provider verifies them in CI; gate deploys with the broker's `can-i-deploy` check. Monitor contract verification failures per consumer.","**Version usage telemetry.** Tag every request metric with API version and client id, and build a \"who is still on v1\" dashboard before announcing a sunset. Alert when a deprecated version's traffic rises instead of falls.","**Scheduled brownouts.** Before removal, return `410 Gone` (or the error your docs promise) from the old version for short announced windows, with a body linking the migration guide. Watch support tickets and the error rate of remaining clients.","**Pin model snapshots and prompt versions.** Store the exact model snapshot name and prompt version in config, include them in responses or logs, and run the eval and format-parsing suite before changing either. Monitor downstream parse-failure rate after each change."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"Proposed change\"] --> Q{\"On the breaking list?\"}\n    Q -->|\"no\"| A[\"Ship additively\"]\n    Q -->|\"yes\"| X{\"Expand-contract possible?\"}\n    X -->|\"yes\"| E[\"Add new field, write both\"]\n    E --> M[\"Migrate consumers, verify by telemetry\"]\n    X -->|\"no\"| V[\"New version, one implementation\"]\n    V --> D[\"Deprecation and Sunset headers\"]\n    D --> M\n    M --> B[\"Brownouts\"]\n    B --> R[\"Remove old shape on the date\"]","caption":"Most changes take the additive path; only an unavoidable break enters the versioned deprecation\nlifecycle, and it leaves only when telemetry says nobody is left."},"interview":[{"q":"What is Hyrum's law, and how does it change how you evolve an API?","a":"- **Definition:** with enough users, every observable behaviour of an interface, documented or not,\n  becomes something somebody depends on.\n- **Consequence:** the schema is not the whole contract; list order, error wording and timing can all\n  be load-bearing for someone.\n- **Response:** consumer-driven contract tests and per-client telemetry reveal the dependencies the\n  schema never promised, before a change ships."},{"q":"A partner reports `KeyError` failures the day after your release, but your schema diff passed. How do you investigate?","a":"- **Find the change:** diff actual responses before and after the release for the partner's calls; an\n  undocumented field or a behaviour (a null instead of a missing key) may have changed.\n- **Mitigate first:** roll back, or restore the old field as an additive shim.\n- **Prevent:** add the partner's expectations as a consumer contract, and extend the schema diff to\n  cover nullability and format changes."},{"q":"Path, header or date-pinned versioning: how do you choose?","a":"- **Path** when the API is small or public and visibility, routing and caching matter more than\n  fine-grained change.\n- **Header or date pinning** when many small changes ship continuously and clients must opt in per\n  request or per account.\n- **The cost of pinning** is a growing chain of transformations rendering new behaviour in old shapes,\n  which you maintain and test forever."},{"q":"How do you decide when it is safe to remove a deprecated version?","a":"- **Telemetry, not hope:** requests per version per client must reach zero, or the remaining clients have\n  agreed dates.\n- **In-band warnings:** `Deprecation`, `Sunset` and a `Link` to the migration guide on every old-version\n  response for the whole window.\n- **Brownouts:** short planned outages flush out stragglers before the hard cutoff."},{"q":"Your provider retires the model snapshot behind a structured-extraction endpoint. How do you migrate without breaking clients?","a":"- **Treat it as a contract change:** output format and quality can shift for the same input.\n- **Pin and evaluate:** run the candidate snapshot through the eval and format-parsing suite, and adjust\n  the prompt version together with the model.\n- **Roll out gradually:** shadow or canary traffic, compare parse-failure and quality metrics, and keep\n  the response schema identical so clients see no change."}],"tradeoffs":["**Additive-only vs a clean API.** Every client keeps working with no migration, but the API accumulates legacy fields and awkward names; it flips to a versioned break when the old shape actively misleads new integrators.","**Path versioning vs fine-grained evolution.** Path versions are simple and visible but move the whole API at once; header or date pinning allows fine-grained evolution at the cost of transformation code and harder debugging.","**Per-account pinning vs server cost.** Stripe-style pinning gives clients indefinite stability, but every change adds a transformation layer the server must maintain and test forever.","**Strict validation vs tolerance.** `extra=\"forbid\"` catches client typos early but turns harmless additive changes by clients into outages; loosen it on public endpoints, keep it on internal ones."],"pitfalls":["**A field rename shipped as a \"small cleanup\".** Symptom: a partner integration fails days later with `KeyError` on a field you removed.","**Forking the codebase per version.** Symptom: a bug fixed in v2 still ships in v1, and a security patch reaches only one of them.","**Announcing a deprecation without measuring usage.** Symptom: v1 is removed on the date and the largest customer turns out to be still on it.","**Treating model or prompt updates as invisible.** Symptom: downstream parsers break because the new model stopped emitting the code fence the old one always produced.","**Clients switching exhaustively on an enum.** Symptom: a crash the day a new status value is added, even though no field changed."],"when":"- **Every change to a published interface:** review each diff of a request or response model against the\n  breaking list, and give prompt and model changes the same review.\n- **Introduce a version** only when a break is unavoidable, and then exactly one.\n- **Measure** requests per version per client, error rates after a release, and time-to-migrate for\n  deprecated versions.\n- **Day to day:** contract tests in CI that fail on breaking schema diffs, and release notes written for\n  integrators.\n- **Interviews:** list what breaks, pick a strategy with reasons, and describe a deprecation lifecycle\n  with dates and telemetry rather than hope.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Keeps changes additive, renders old versions as representations over one implementation, and wires contract tests that fail on breaking OpenAPI diffs.","architect":"Chooses the versioning strategy and deprecation policy, decides what is a published contract versus internal, and extends the policy to models, prompts and tool schemas.","fde":"Shields customer integrations from provider model deprecations and your own API changes by pinning versions and planning migrations with the customer's release calendar."},"check":{"q":"A product team wants to change the `confidence` field in your extraction API from a 0–100\ninteger to a 0–1 float, and to add a `reasons` array. How do you ship this without breaking the three\nexternal integrators on the endpoint?","a":"The `reasons` array is additive and ships immediately. The `confidence` change is breaking:\nsame name, different type and scale, so a client that thresholds at 80 would silently treat every\nresult as low confidence. Options: add a new field (`confidence_score` as a float), keep `confidence`\nas the integer, and deprecate it with a `Sunset` header and docs, removing it after telemetry shows\nzero use; or, if the API is already versioned, make the change in the next version and render the old\nshape from one implementation. I would avoid a bare `/v2` for one field. Either way, contract tests\nin CI should fail on the type change so it cannot ship by accident."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which change is backward compatible for a typical JSON API client?","options":["Renaming `summary` to `text` in the response","Adding an optional `citations` array to the response","Changing `created_at` from seconds to milliseconds","Making the optional `language` request field required"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Tolerant clients ignore fields they do not know, so additions are safe. Renames, unit changes and newly required inputs all change what existing clients send or read."},{"q":"Why does Stripe-style per-account date pinning cost more on the server than path versioning?","options":["Dates are harder to parse than path segments","The server must maintain a chain of transformations that renders current behaviour in every older pinned shape","Pinned accounts cannot use the SDKs","It requires a separate database per version"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each pinned version is a representation the server must keep producing from the current implementation, so every change adds a transformation to maintain and test indefinitely."},{"q":"What should happen before an old API version is removed?","options":["Nothing; the new version has been available for a month","Return 500 from the old version so clients notice","Announce a sunset date, emit deprecation headers, measure remaining usage per client, and run brownouts","Silently redirect old-version requests to the new one"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Removal is safe only when you know who is left and they have been warned in-band and out-of-band. Brownouts surface stragglers before the hard cutoff; silent redirects hide breaking shape changes."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/pagination-rate-limits-api-quotas","sources":["IETF RFC 6585 — Additional HTTP Status Codes","IETF RFC 9110 — HTTP Semantics","Winand, 2012 — SQL Performance Explained","Official docs: Stripe API"],"why":"- **The unbounded list is a bomb.** `GET /documents` for a tenant with two million rows: the database\n  materialises every row, the server serialises hundreds of megabytes of JSON, a 30-second timeout fires\n  mid-parse, and the client retries and doubles the load. Response size must be bounded by the server,\n  not by the client's good manners.\n- **The obvious fix, `?offset=1000&limit=50`, degrades.** Page 20,000 makes the engine produce and discard\n  a million rows, and a row inserted while the client is on page 3 shifts every later page, so items are\n  skipped or shown twice.\n- **Capacity is shared.** A connection pool, a CPU, a provider's tokens-per-minute allowance: one burst (a\n  retry storm, a batch script, a scraper) takes capacity from everyone and cascades into timeouts.\n  \"Just scale up\" fails: you cannot scale a provider's limit, bursts beat autoscaling, and unlimited\n  capacity is an unlimited bill.\n- **A limiter is also a signal.** `429` plus `Retry-After` tells a well-behaved client exactly when to\n  come back, which turns overload into orderly queueing instead of a pile-up.\n- **Rate limits say nothing about a month.** They protect the system *this minute*, not against a tenant\n  running at the limit for a month. When every call costs money, as every LLM call does, you need an\n  entitlement (how much, not how fast), or a free-tier batch job outspends its lifetime revenue unnoticed.","how":"### Pagination: offset, keyset, cursor\n| Style | Query shape | Cost per page | Under concurrent writes | Use for |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Offset | `ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 50 OFFSET 1000` | produces 1,050 rows and throws 1,000 away; grows linearly with depth | window drifts | small, static sets needing \"page 7 of 12\" |\n| Keyset (seek) | `WHERE (created_at, id) < (:last_ts, :last_id) ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC LIMIT 50` | roughly O(log n + page size), any depth | stable | large or moving lists |\n| Cursor | keyset values in an opaque token | same as keyset | stable | public APIs and agent tools |\n\n- **Keyset needs an index on `(created_at, id)`** so the engine seeks directly to the position. Inserts do\n  not shift the window *because* position is defined by a value rather than a count.\n- **The `id` tie-breaker is mandatory** because `created_at` is not unique; without it, rows sharing a\n  timestamp at a page boundary get skipped or repeated.\n\n### Cursor rules\n1. **Wrap the keyset values in an opaque token** (base64 JSON, optionally HMAC-signed) and return\n   `{\"data\": [...], \"next_cursor\": \"...\", \"has_more\": true}`.\n2. **Keep it opaque** *because* it is part of your contract: if clients can read it they will build it,\n   and you can never change the sort or the key again.\n3. **Bind the cursor to its query.** Include the sort and a hash of the filters, or sign it, so reuse\n   with different filters gets a clear 400 rather than a silently wrong page.\n4. **Fetch `limit + 1` rows** to compute `has_more` without a count.\n5. **Enforce a default and a maximum page size** on the server; clients will ask for 10,000.\n6. **Omit or estimate totals**, because `COUNT(*)` over a large table is a full scan on every page.\n\n- **As a consumer:** loop until `has_more` is false, and treat an invalid cursor as \"start over\".\n- **Agent tools need the same discipline:** one page per call with `has_more` surfaced to the model, or one\n  tool result eats the context window.\n\n### Rate limiting algorithms\n- **Token bucket:** capacity *b* refilled at *r* tokens per second; each request takes one token (or *n*\n  for token-metered APIs); empty means reject or wait. It allows a burst of *b* and a sustained rate of\n  *r*, what most APIs want *because* real clients are bursty and shedding a five-request burst from an\n  idle client helps nobody.\n- **Leaky bucket:** requests queue and drain at a fixed rate; smooth output at the cost of queueing delay.\n  Use it in front of a downstream that cannot absorb bursts at all.\n- **Fixed window:** `INCR limit:{key}:{minute}` with a TTL. One command, but a client can send a full\n  window's worth at 11:59:59 and again at 12:00:00, so the real peak is twice the nominal limit.\n- **Sliding window log:** every request timestamp in a sorted set, counting those in the trailing window;\n  exact, but memory grows with traffic.\n- **Sliding window counter:** weight the previous window's count by its overlap with the trailing window;\n  approximate, cheap, usually enough.\n- **Concurrency limit:** cap requests *in flight*. By Little's law, in-flight = arrival rate × duration, so\n  10 requests per second of 30-second streaming completions is about 300 open streams, each holding a\n  connection and a model-server slot. LLM gateways usually enforce both a rate and a max-in-flight per\n  tenant.\n\n### Token bucket trace\nTake *b* = 10 and *r* = 2 per second; an idle client fires 15 requests at t = 0.\n\n| Time | Tokens before | Requests | Outcome |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| t = 0 | 10 | 15 | first 10 pass and drain the bucket; next 5 are rejected (or wait) |\n| t = 1 s | 2 | 2 | 2 more pass |\n| any interval *T* | | | at most `b + r·T` admitted: burst *b*, long-run rate *r* |\n\n### Distributed enforcement and placement\n- **Counters must be atomic.** `INCR` followed by `EXPIRE` as two commands leaves a key without a TTL if\n  the process dies in between, so use a Lua script or `SET NX EX` plus `INCR`.\n- **Gateway** (nginx, Envoy, a cloud API gateway): coarse per-IP or per-key limits, no business context.\n- **Application level in Redis:** per tenant, per model and token-weighted.\n- **Provider dimensions.** Model providers limit requests per minute (RPM) and tokens per minute (TPM)\n  separately, often per model and per region. For LLM workloads TPM binds first *because* each request\n  carries thousands of tokens.\n- **Admission accounting.** Some providers debit the token budget at admission using your `max_tokens`\n  plus a prompt estimate, not tokens actually generated, so a generous `max_tokens` can produce 429s\n  while real usage is low. Check your provider's accounting rules.\n\n### Signalling limits\n- **Respond `429 Too Many Requests`** (RFC 6585) with `Retry-After` in seconds or as an HTTP date.\n- **Publish remaining budget** so clients throttle before they hit 429: `X-RateLimit-Limit`,\n  `X-RateLimit-Remaining`, `X-RateLimit-Reset` by convention.\n- **The standard is still a draft.** An IETF httpapi draft, still evolving, standardises `RateLimit` and\n  `RateLimit-Policy` fields; earlier drafts used `RateLimit-Limit`/`-Remaining`/`-Reset`.\n- **As a consumer:** honour `Retry-After`, back off exponentially with jitter, cap concurrency with a\n  semaphore, and keep a client-side token bucket sized to your known limit, so you queue locally instead\n  of burning retries.\n\n### Quotas: entitlement and cost\n- **Definition.** A cap on total consumption over a long window (day, month) or a budget (dollars, tokens,\n  jobs) per account, tenant or feature. Rate limit answers \"how fast\"; quota answers \"how much\".\n- **Enforcement.** A counter per `(tenant, period)` in Redis or the database.\n- **Reserve, then reconcile.** For token quotas, reserve an estimate at admission and reconcile from the\n  response's `usage` field, *because* real cost is known only after the call and a stream can fail\n  halfway.\n- **Soft vs hard.** Soft limits alert, degrade to a cheaper model, lower `max_tokens` or require approval;\n  hard limits reject.\n- **Distinguishable errors.** No universal status code exists for quota exhaustion. The error body must be\n  machine-distinguishable from a rate limit, because the client reaction differs: wait seconds versus\n  stop until the next period or upgrade.\n- **Both directions.** You consume a provider's quota and issue quotas to tenants; allocations against one\n  provider key must fit its limit, or you overbook with priorities and shed low-priority work on a 429.\n  That mapping is capacity planning, covered in the infra-capacity-economics and reliability modules.","example":"### Keyset endpoint with an opaque cursor, plus a client-side TPM bucket\nThe bucket is in-process; with several workers the same logic moves into Redis. `db` is an asyncpg pool\ncreated at startup.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, base64, json, time\nfrom datetime import datetime\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Query\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\ndef encode_cursor(created_at: datetime, id_: int) -> str:\n    return base64.urlsafe_b64encode(json.dumps([created_at.isoformat(), id_]).encode()).decode()\n\ndef decode_cursor(cursor: str) -> tuple[datetime, int]:\n    try:\n        ts, id_ = json.loads(base64.urlsafe_b64decode(cursor))\n        return datetime.fromisoformat(ts), int(id_)\n    except (ValueError, TypeError):\n        raise HTTPException(400, \"invalid cursor\")             # opaque: clients never build or parse it\n\n@app.get(\"/documents\")\nasync def list_documents(limit: int = Query(20, ge=1, le=100), cursor: str | None = None):\n    sql, args = \"SELECT id, created_at, title FROM documents\", []\n    if cursor:\n        last_ts, last_id = decode_cursor(cursor)\n        sql += \" WHERE (created_at, id) < ($1, $2)\"             # keyset: seek to position, never skip rows\n        args = [last_ts, last_id]\n    sql += f\" ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC LIMIT {limit + 1}\"  # +1 row answers has_more without COUNT(*)\n    rows = await db.fetch(sql, *args)                           # needs an index on (created_at, id)\n    page, has_more = rows[:limit], len(rows) > limit\n    next_cursor = encode_cursor(page[-1][\"created_at\"], page[-1][\"id\"]) if has_more else None\n    return {\"data\": [dict(r) for r in page], \"next_cursor\": next_cursor, \"has_more\": has_more}\n\nclass TokenBucket:\n    \"\"\"Client-side limiter: queue locally instead of collecting 429s from the provider.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, rate_per_sec: float, capacity: float):\n        self.rate, self.capacity = rate_per_sec, capacity\n        self.tokens, self.last = capacity, time.monotonic()\n    async def acquire(self, n: float) -> None:\n        if n > self.capacity:\n            raise ValueError(\"request larger than bucket capacity would wait forever\")\n        while True:\n            now = time.monotonic()\n            self.tokens = min(self.capacity, self.tokens + (now - self.last) * self.rate); self.last = now\n            if self.tokens >= n:\n                self.tokens -= n; return                        # no await between check and debit: safe in asyncio\n            await asyncio.sleep((n - self.tokens) / self.rate)  # sleep exactly the refill time we need\n\ntpm_bucket = TokenBucket(rate_per_sec=200_000 / 60, capacity=200_000)   # illustrative 200k TPM limit\n# before each model call: await tpm_bucket.acquire(prompt_token_estimate + max_tokens)\n```\n\n### Atomic fixed-window counter in Redis\nThe script runs atomically on the Redis server, so the key can never be left without its TTL. It uses\nthe redis-py client; `r.eval(script, numkeys, *keys_and_args)` is the standard call.\n\n```python\nimport time\nimport redis\n\nr = redis.Redis()\nFIXED_WINDOW = \"\"\"\nlocal n = redis.call('INCR', KEYS[1])\nif n == 1 then redis.call('EXPIRE', KEYS[1], ARGV[1]) end\nreturn n\n\"\"\"\n\ndef allow(tenant: str, limit: int = 100, window_s: int = 60) -> bool:\n    key = f\"rl:{tenant}:{int(time.time() // window_s)}\"     # one key per tenant per window\n    count = r.eval(FIXED_WINDOW, 1, key, window_s)          # INCR and EXPIRE as one atomic step\n    return count <= limit                                   # caller returns 429 + Retry-After if False\n```\n\nRemember the boundary burst: this admits up to 2 × `limit` in a two-second span across a window edge,\nso put a token bucket in front of anything fragile.\n\n### Worked scenario: a 1,000-document batch against a TPM limit\nProvider limit: 500 RPM and 200,000 TPM. Each request carries a ~20,000-token prompt.\n\n| Quantity | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Requests per minute allowed by TPM | 200,000 / 20,000 = 10 |\n| Requests per minute allowed by RPM | 500, irrelevant |\n| Total tokens in the batch | 1,000 × 20,000 = 20 million |\n| Best-case drain time | 20,000,000 / 200,000 = 100 minutes |\n| Without a client bucket | 429s within seconds, retries make it worse |","patterns":["**Client-side bucket per provider key and model.** Size it a little below the published TPM and RPM (for example 90%), charge prompt estimate plus `max_tokens`, and queue instead of calling. Monitor time waiting in the bucket and the 429 rate; both should stay low and flat.","**Atomic Redis limiter per tenant and model.** Implement the bucket or window as a Lua script keyed by tenant, model and window, and decide up front whether a Redis outage fails open or closed. Monitor limiter latency, Redis errors and rejections per tenant.","**Concurrency leases for streaming.** Cap in-flight streams per tenant with a counter or semaphore whose entries carry a TTL, so a crashed worker cannot leak slots. Monitor in-flight per tenant against the Little's-law estimate (rate × duration).","**Reserve-and-reconcile token quotas.** Debit an estimate at admission, correct it from `usage` when the response ends, and release it on failure. Alert tenants and on-call at a soft threshold (for example 80%) before the hard stop.","**Bounded pages everywhere, including agent tools.** Enforce a default and maximum `limit`, return `next_cursor` and `has_more`, and never return totals by `COUNT(*)` on hot paths. Monitor p95 page latency by depth and the largest page sizes requested."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Tenant request\"] --> G{\"Gateway: per-key rate OK?\"}\n    G -->|\"no\"| E1[\"429 plus Retry-After\"]\n    G -->|\"yes\"| Q{\"Tenant quota left this period?\"}\n    Q -->|\"no\"| E2[\"Quota error, distinct body\"]\n    Q -->|\"yes\"| C{\"In-flight below cap?\"}\n    C -->|\"no\"| E1\n    C -->|\"yes\"| B[\"Client-side TPM bucket\"]\n    B --> P[\"Provider call, reserve then reconcile tokens\"]\n    P --> L[\"Page of results, limit plus has_more\"]","caption":"A request passes three bounds in order of time scale before any work runs, and the response itself is\nbounded by a page size."},"interview":[{"q":"Why does offset pagination show duplicates and gaps while the list is being written to?","a":"- **Position is a count:** `OFFSET 1000` means \"skip 1,000 rows\", so a row inserted above the client's\n  position shifts every later page by one and an item is shown twice.\n- **Deletes do the reverse:** a removed row shifts items up, and one is skipped.\n- **Keyset fixes it** because position is a value (`(created_at, id)` of the last row seen), and the `id`\n  tie-breaker keeps boundaries exact when timestamps collide."},{"q":"Design the rate limiter for a multi-tenant LLM gateway.","a":"- **Dimensions:** per tenant and per model, enforcing requests per minute, tokens per minute\n  (prompt estimate plus `max_tokens`) and max in-flight streams.\n- **Mechanism:** a token bucket as an atomic Lua script in Redis, coarse per-IP limits at the gateway,\n  and a client-side bucket in front of the provider so tenant allocations fit its limit.\n- **Signals and failure:** `429` with `Retry-After` and remaining-budget headers, plus a decision on fail\n  open or closed when Redis is down."},{"q":"Your service gets 429s from the provider, but the usage dashboard shows you at 30% of TPM. What do you check?","a":"- **Admission accounting:** some providers debit `max_tokens` plus a prompt estimate at admission, so a\n  generous `max_tokens` consumes budget that is never used.\n- **Dimension and scope:** RPM, a per-model or per-region limit, or another service sharing the same key.\n- **Fix:** right-size `max_tokens`, add a client-side bucket using the provider's accounting, and split\n  keys or projects per workload."},{"q":"When do you need a concurrency limit in addition to a rate limit?","a":"- **When requests are long:** by Little's law in-flight = rate × duration, so 10 requests per second of\n  30-second streams is about 300 open streams at any moment.\n- **What saturates:** connections and model-server slots, not the per-minute counter.\n- **So** cap in-flight per tenant with TTL leases, and size the cap from the expected rate and duration."},{"q":"Rate limit or quota: which error should the client see, and why does it matter?","a":"- **Rate limit:** `429` with `Retry-After`; the right reaction is to wait seconds and retry.\n- **Quota:** a machine-distinguishable error body (no universal status code exists), because the right\n  reaction is to stop until the next period, degrade or upgrade.\n- **Mixing them up** makes clients retry into an exhausted quota, or give up on a momentary burst."}],"tradeoffs":["**Keyset/cursor vs page numbers.** They cost the same at page 1 and page 10,000 and stay stable under writes, but cannot jump to page *N* or sort by an arbitrary unindexed column; offset wins when the set is small, static and the UI genuinely needs page numbers.","**Token bucket bursts vs smoothing.** Admitting bursts keeps latency low for bursty, light clients, but a burst of size *b* still lands on the downstream at once; when it has no buffer, a leaky bucket's smoothing is worth its queueing delay.","**Gateway vs application enforcement.** The gateway is uniform and cheap but cannot see token counts or tenant tier; application-level limiting can, at the price of a Redis round trip per request and one more component that must stay highly available.","**Hard vs soft quotas.** Hard quotas protect the bill and fairness, but stopping a paying customer dead at 100% creates support load; soft limits with graceful degradation win when the SLA matters more than the marginal cost.","**Exact vs approximate windows.** A sliding-window log is fair and precise but its memory grows with traffic; the sliding counter is cheaper and good enough unless the limit itself is contractual."],"pitfalls":["**Offset pagination on a growing table.** Symptom: page latency climbs with page number, and users report duplicated or missing items while the list is being written to.","**A non-unique sort column without a tie-breaker.** Symptom: rows vanish or repeat exactly at page boundaries when many rows share a timestamp.","**A fixed-window counter guarding a fragile downstream.** Symptom: overload incidents clustered around minute boundaries, at twice the nominal limit.","**Retrying 429 immediately or without jitter.** Symptom: a retry storm in which the 429 rate climbs instead of clearing, sometimes ending with the provider disabling the key.","**Counting requests when the provider counts tokens.** Symptom: 429s at a small fraction of the request limit, because each request carries a 40k-token prompt.","**Charging quota only after the response.** Symptom: tenants exceed their cap by one burst of concurrent requests, and failed streaming calls are never accounted for.","**Rate-limiting long streams without a concurrency cap.** Symptom: the model server or connection pool saturates while every tenant is under its requests-per-minute limit.","**A readable cursor.** Symptom: integrations break after a release that changed the sort order, because clients had been hand-building cursors."],"when":"- **Pagination:** default to cursor pagination for any list that can grow or is ordered by time; keep\n  offset for small admin tables.\n- **Rate limits:** every endpoint fronting a scarce resource, per tenant or key, with a token bucket in\n  Redis at the application layer and coarse limits at the gateway; put a client-side bucket in front of\n  every provider whose limits you know.\n- **Quotas:** as soon as a call has a cost, which for LLM products is day one.\n- **Measure** the 429 rate per tenant, time waiting in client limiters, quota utilisation, and page\n  latency by depth.\n- **Interviews:** \"design a rate limiter\" is constant; compare token bucket with fixed and sliding\n  windows, handle atomicity in Redis, choose response headers, and separate rate from quota.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements keyset endpoints and opaque cursors, the Redis limiter script, the client-side TPM bucket, and the 429 and `Retry-After` handling inside every SDK wrapper.","architect":"Chooses the limit dimensions (RPM, TPM, concurrency), the tenant quota model and how it overbooks provider capacity, and where enforcement lives (gateway versus service).","fde":"Translates a customer's expected load into provider limits, requests quota increases ahead of launch, and designs the \"you have hit your quota\" experience and dashboards their admins will see."},"check":{"q":"Your model provider allows 500 requests and 200,000 tokens per minute on the model you use. Your\nagent sends prompts of roughly 20,000 tokens. A customer submits 1,000 documents at once. What happens,\nand how do you redesign?","a":"TPM binds: 200,000 / 20,000 is 10 requests per minute, so the 500 RPM figure is irrelevant and\nthe burst produces 429s within seconds. The batch is 20 million tokens, so 100 minutes at the very\nbest. Redesign: queue the batch and drain it through a client-side token bucket sized to TPM (counting\nthe prompt estimate plus `max_tokens`), bound concurrency, honour `Retry-After` with jittered backoff,\ngive each tenant a quota so one customer cannot consume the whole budget, shrink the prompt (retrieval\ninstead of whole documents; prompt caching cuts cost and latency, and on some providers cached tokens\nalso stop counting against the input-token limit), and ask for a quota\nincrease or spread load across regions or providers before launch. If the provider offers an\nasynchronous batch endpoint with separate limits, route offline jobs there. Then watch the 429 rate and\nqueue wait time."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does keyset pagination stay fast at page 10,000 while offset pagination slows down?","options":["Keyset pages are cached in Redis after the first request","The index seek starts at the last-seen key, so each page costs about the page size rather than the offset","Keyset queries return fewer columns, so less data is transferred","The database compresses rows that have already been paginated"],"answer":1,"explanation":"OFFSET forces the engine to produce and discard every earlier row in sort order, so cost grows with depth. A keyset predicate on an indexed column lets the engine jump straight to the position, so the cost is proportional to the page, not the depth."},{"q":"A fixed-window limiter allows 100 requests per minute. What is the most a client can push through in a two-second span that straddles the minute boundary?","options":["100, because the limit is enforced per minute","About 3, because 100 per minute is under two per second","200: the last 100 of one window and the first 100 of the next","Unbounded, because the counter resets to zero"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The counter is per calendar window, not per trailing minute, so a client can exhaust one window at its end and the next at its start. Sliding-window and token-bucket limiters remove that boundary burst."},{"q":"A tenant's plan allows 60 requests per minute and 100,000 requests per month. They send a steady 30 requests per minute for three days. Which bound stops them?","options":["The rate limit, because 30 per minute sustained for hours exceeds 60 per minute","The monthly quota, which 30 per minute exhausts in a little over two days","Neither, because they never exceed 60 per minute","Both at once, on the third day"],"answer":1,"explanation":"30 per minute is 43,200 per day, so 100,000 is used up after about 2.3 days without ever exceeding the per-minute rate. Rate limits bound speed; quotas bound total consumption over the period."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/webhook-signature-verification-and-replay-protection","sources":["IETF RFC 2104 — HMAC: Keyed-Hashing for Message Authentication","Official docs: Stripe webhooks","Official docs: Standard Webhooks specification","Official docs: Slack API (verifying requests)"],"why":"- **The endpoint is public.** `POST /webhooks/payments` is reachable from every machine on the internet.\n  Trust the body and an attacker sends `{\"type\": \"payment.succeeded\", \"customer\": \"them\"}` and gets the\n  product for free.\n- **AI systems raise the stakes.** An agent platform receiving Slack events will execute instructions from\n  anyone who can craft a request: prompt injection with a delivery mechanism. A pipeline waiting for a\n  \"batch job finished\" event will ingest a forged result.\n- **Plain HTTP does not identify the sender.** TLS authenticates your server to the sender, not the\n  sender to you.\n- **The naive defences fail.** A secret URL is a bearer token that leaks through access logs, proxies,\n  browser history and support-ticket screenshots, and rotating it touches every integration. IP\n  allowlists break on large, changing cloud ranges and ingress that rewrites source addresses. A\n  `\"source\": \"billing\"` field is written by whoever sends the JSON.\n- **What remains is a MAC.** Mutual TLS would settle it, but few SaaS providers offer it for webhooks. A\n  shared secret used to compute a MAC over the exact bytes is cheap, verifiable offline, and proves the\n  producer held the secret.\n- **Signing stops forgery, not replay.** A captured delivery is still correctly signed next week, so\n  \"credit 100 units\" can be resent at will. Providers also redeliver on any non-2xx or timeout, so\n  duplicates are normal operation. A signed timestamp bounds the window, an event id bounds duplicates\n  inside it, and an idempotent handler makes the rest harmless.","how":"### What the provider signs\n- **Each endpoint gets its own secret.** Stripe calls it a signing secret; GitHub a webhook secret you\n  type into the settings page.\n- **On delivery** the provider computes `HMAC-SHA256(secret, signed_content)` and sends the result in a\n  header. Schemes differ in what `signed_content` is:\n\n| Provider or scheme | Signed content | Headers |\n|---|---|---|\n| Stripe | `\"{timestamp}.{raw_body}\"` | `Stripe-Signature: t=<unix>,v1=<hex>` |\n| Standard Webhooks (Svix and several vendors) | `\"{msg_id}.{timestamp}.{raw_body}\"` | `webhook-id`, `webhook-timestamp`, `webhook-signature` |\n| GitHub | the body alone, no timestamp | `X-Hub-Signature-256: sha256=<hex>`, plus a separate `X-GitHub-Delivery` id |\n| Slack | `\"v0:{timestamp}:{raw_body}\"` | `X-Slack-Signature` |\n\n- **The common core:** an HMAC with a per-endpoint secret over raw bytes.\n- **An HMAC, not a bare hash**, *because* a hash of public bytes can be computed by anyone.\n- **In a header, not the body**, *because* the body is the thing being authenticated.\n- **Some providers sign asymmetrically** with a published public key, which lets many parties verify\n  without any of them being able to forge.\n\n### Verifying, in order\n1. **Read the raw body bytes before any framework parses them.** Re-serialising parsed JSON changes\n   whitespace, key order, float formatting and unicode escaping, so the MAC will not match. Cap the body\n   size at the edge first, since an unauthenticated sender can otherwise make you buffer and hash\n   arbitrarily large payloads.\n2. **Parse the header** into a timestamp and one or more signatures; reject anything malformed.\n3. **Check `|now − timestamp| ≤ tolerance`.** A few minutes (Stripe's libraries default to 300 seconds),\n   not zero, *because* clocks drift and deliveries queue.\n4. **Recompute the MAC and compare with `hmac.compare_digest`, never `==`.** An ordinary comparison\n   returns at the first differing byte, so response time leaks how many leading bytes of a guess were\n   right; a constant-time compare removes that side channel.\n5. **Accept if *any* supplied signature matches**, because during secret rotation providers send one\n   signature per active secret.\n6. **Only now parse the JSON** and dispatch on the event type.\n\n### Replay protection\n- **The timestamp helps only because it is signed.** An attacker who edits it breaks the MAC.\n- **No signed timestamp, no replay bound.** An unsigned timestamp header, or none at all (GitHub's\n  scheme), means dedupe by delivery id is the only replay defence, and its retention must be long.\n- **A signed window is still enough for a double credit.** It turns \"valid forever\" into \"valid for five\n  minutes\", so also deduplicate by event id: `SET webhook:seen:{id} 1 NX EX <ttl>` in Redis, or an insert\n  into a `processed_events(event_id UNIQUE)` table.\n- **Retention covers the retry horizon (often days)**, not just the tolerance window, *because*\n  legitimate retries arrive later with the same id.\n- **Check whether retries are re-signed** with a fresh timestamp; if not, the tolerance must cover the\n  provider's retry schedule too.\n\n| Dedupe store | Guarantee | Failure mode |\n|---|---|---|\n| Uniqueness insert in the same DB transaction as the side effect | both commit or neither; a crash between \"seen\" and \"credited\" cannot lose an event | a write per event |\n| Redis flag set before the work | one cheap command | can mark an event seen that then fails: the exactly-once limitation |\n\n- **The cure for the remainder:** an idempotent handler plus periodic reconciliation against the\n  provider's API.\n\n### Acknowledge fast, process later, trust the API over the payload\n- **Providers are impatient.** They time out deliveries after seconds to tens of seconds, retry any\n  non-2xx with backoff over hours or days, and may disable an endpoint that keeps failing.\n- **So:** verify, dedupe, enqueue the raw event, return 200, and let a worker do the real work.\n- **Deliveries are unordered and payloads can be stale** (a `completed` can arrive before `started`).\n  For anything that moves money or grants access, fetch the current object from the provider's\n  authenticated API by id and act on that.\n- **\"Thin\" webhooks** that carry only ids make this pattern the default.\n\n### Secrets\n- **Store it in your secret manager, per endpoint, never in code.**\n- **It is symmetric:** anyone holding it can forge deliveries, so never share one secret across services\n  or environments.\n- **Rotate with an overlap window** during which both old and new secrets are accepted.","example":"### FastAPI receiver for a Stripe-style scheme\nRaw-body HMAC, timestamp tolerance, multiple signatures for rotation, constant-time compare, Redis\ndedupe keyed by event id with retention longer than the tolerance, and a queue hand-off so the provider\ngets its 200 in milliseconds. Test it with three fixtures: a recorded good delivery, the same body with\none byte changed, and the same delivery twice.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, hmac, json, os, time\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Request\nimport redis.asyncio as redis\n\napp = FastAPI()\nSECRET = os.environ[\"WEBHOOK_SIGNING_SECRET\"].encode()   # per-endpoint secret from the secret manager\nTOLERANCE_S = 300                                        # clock skew + delivery delay we accept\nSEEN_TTL_S = 3 * 24 * 3600                               # must cover the provider's retry horizon\nr = redis.from_url(os.environ[\"REDIS_URL\"])\n\ndef verify(raw: bytes, header: str) -> None:\n    # Stripe-style header: \"t=<unix>,v1=<hex>[,v1=<hex>]\"; two v1 values appear during secret rotation\n    fields = [p.split(\"=\", 1) for p in header.split(\",\") if \"=\" in p]\n    ts = next((v for k, v in fields if k == \"t\"), None)\n    sigs = [v for k, v in fields if k == \"v1\"]\n    if ts is None or not ts.isdigit() or not sigs:\n        raise HTTPException(400, \"malformed signature header\")\n    if abs(time.time() - int(ts)) > TOLERANCE_S:\n        raise HTTPException(400, \"timestamp outside tolerance\")        # bounds replay to a short window\n    expected = hmac.new(SECRET, f\"{ts}.\".encode() + raw, hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()\n    # constant-time compare on bytes: compare_digest raises TypeError on non-ASCII str input\n    if not any(hmac.compare_digest(expected.encode(), s.encode()) for s in sigs):\n        raise HTTPException(400, \"bad signature\")\n\n@app.post(\"/webhooks/provider\")\nasync def receive(request: Request):\n    raw = await request.body()                     # the exact bytes that were signed, never re-serialised JSON\n    verify(raw, request.headers.get(\"Provider-Signature\", \"\"))\n    event_id = json.loads(raw).get(\"id\")\n    if not event_id:\n        raise HTTPException(400, \"missing event id\")\n    key = f\"webhook:seen:{event_id}\"\n    if not await r.set(key, 1, nx=True, ex=SEEN_TTL_S):\n        return {\"status\": \"duplicate\"}             # 2xx: a retry or a replay, either way do nothing again\n    try:\n        await r.lpush(\"webhook:inbox\", raw)        # ack fast; a worker fetches the object by id and acts\n    except Exception:\n        await r.delete(key)                        # un-mark so the provider's retry is not dropped\n        raise\n    return {\"status\": \"queued\"}\n```\n\n### The fixtures, runnable with the standard library\nA self-contained version of the same checks, run against a good delivery, a tampered body, a retry, a\nreplay a week later, and a delivery signed during secret rotation.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, hmac\n\ndef sign(secret: bytes, ts: int, raw: bytes) -> str:\n    return hmac.new(secret, f\"{ts}.\".encode() + raw, hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()\n\ndef check(secrets: list[bytes], raw: bytes, header: str, seen: set, now: float, tol: int = 300) -> str:\n    fields = [p.split(\"=\", 1) for p in header.split(\",\") if \"=\" in p]\n    ts = next((v for k, v in fields if k == \"t\"), None)\n    sigs = [v for k, v in fields if k == \"v1\"]\n    if ts is None or not ts.isdigit() or not sigs:\n        return \"400 malformed\"\n    if abs(now - int(ts)) > tol:\n        return \"400 stale\"\n    ok = any(hmac.compare_digest(sign(s, int(ts), raw).encode(), sig.encode())\n             for s in secrets for sig in sigs)            # any active secret, any supplied signature\n    if not ok:\n        return \"400 bad signature\"\n    if raw in seen:                                       # stand-in for dedupe by event id\n        return \"200 duplicate\"\n    seen.add(raw)\n    return \"200 queued\"\n\nold, new = b\"whsec_old\", b\"whsec_new\"\nbody, now, seen = b'{\"id\":\"evt_1\",\"type\":\"payment.succeeded\"}', 1_760_000_000, set()\ngood = f\"t={now},v1={sign(old, now, body)}\"\nprint(check([old], body, good, seen, now))                         # 200 queued\nprint(check([old], body.replace(b\"1\", b\"2\"), good, seen, now))     # 400 bad signature (one byte changed)\nprint(check([old], body, good, seen, now + 60))                    # 200 duplicate (retry or replay)\nprint(check([old], body, good, seen, now + 7 * 86400))             # 400 stale (replayed a week later)\nrot = f\"t={now},v1={sign(old, now, body)},v1={sign(new, now, body)}\"\nprint(check([new], body, rot, set(), now))                         # 200 queued (rotation overlap)\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: which check stops which attack\nTolerance 300 seconds, dedupe retention 3 days, provider retries for up to a few days.\n\n| Incoming request | Signature | Timestamp window | Event-id dedupe | Result |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Forged body, no secret | fails | | | 400 |\n| Real delivery, one byte edited | fails | | | 400 |\n| Real delivery replayed after a week | valid | fails | | 400 |\n| Real delivery replayed after 2 minutes | valid | passes | seen | 2xx, no side effect |\n| Provider retry after a timeout | valid (re-signed) | passes | seen | 2xx, no side effect |\n| Retry after 4 days, beyond retention | valid | passes | expired | processed again: only an idempotent handler saves you |","patterns":["**Verify in a dedicated dependency on raw bytes.** Read `await request.body()` before any JSON parsing, cap the body size at the proxy, and use the provider's official verification helper when one exists. Monitor the verification-failure rate per provider; anything above zero is an attack or a misconfiguration.","**Transactional dedupe for money and access.** Insert into `processed_events(event_id UNIQUE)` with `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (event_id) DO NOTHING` inside the same transaction as the side effect, and skip when no row was inserted. Monitor the duplicate rate; a spike usually means slow acks.","**Ack-then-queue with a dead-letter queue.** Return 200 after verify, dedupe and enqueue, and let workers retry with a dead-letter queue for poison events. Monitor processing lag, DLQ depth and provider-side delivery failures in the provider's dashboard.","**Overlapped secret rotation.** Keep a list of active secrets per endpoint in the secret manager, accept a match from any of them, roll the provider to the new secret, then retire the old one after the overlap. Watch for a burst of signature failures during the switch.","**Scheduled reconciliation.** Periodically list recent objects from the provider's API and compare with what you processed, to catch dropped or expired-dedupe events. Track the count of discrepancies found per run."],"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant P as Provider\n    participant W as Webhook endpoint\n    participant R as Redis or DB\n    participant Q as Queue worker\n    P->>W: POST raw body plus signature header\n    Note over W: cap size, check timestamp, HMAC, constant-time compare\n    W->>R: SET seen id NX with TTL\n    R-->>W: new, or already seen\n    W->>Q: enqueue raw event\n    W-->>P: 200 within milliseconds\n    Q->>P: GET current object by id\n    Note over Q: idempotent side effect","caption":"Verification and dedupe happen before any work, the provider gets its 200 at once, and the worker acts\non the provider's current object rather than the payload."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is the timestamp included inside the signed content rather than sent as its own header?","a":"- **Binding:** if the timestamp is part of the MAC input, an attacker who changes it to look fresh breaks\n  the signature.\n- **Unsigned is useless:** a separate, unsigned timestamp can be rewritten on a replayed request, so it\n  bounds nothing.\n- **Without one** (GitHub's scheme), deduplication by delivery id with long retention is the only replay\n  defence."},{"q":"After a deploy, every webhook signature fails, but the secret did not change. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Suspect the bytes:** new body-parsing middleware, a gateway transformation or a compression layer is\n  changing the body before verification.\n- **Confirm:** log a hash and length of the raw body at the edge and in the handler for one delivery, and\n  compare with the provider's dashboard.\n- **Also check** that the header name and the secret's environment variable still match after the deploy,\n  and that the server clock has not drifted outside the tolerance."},{"q":"Redis `SET NX` or a unique constraint in the database: which do you use for webhook dedupe?","a":"- **Redis** is one cheap command with a TTL, fine for low-stakes events, but it is not transactional with\n  the side effect and can mark an event seen that then fails.\n- **A unique insert in the same transaction** as the side effect costs a write per event and guarantees\n  both commit or neither.\n- **For money or access**, use the transaction, plus an idempotent handler and reconciliation for what\n  slips past retention."},{"q":"How long should dedupe records live, and what happens if they expire too early?","a":"- **Retention covers the provider's retry horizon**, often days, not the few-minute tolerance window,\n  because legitimate retries carry the same id later.\n- **Too short:** a late retry is processed again, producing double side effects unless the handler is\n  idempotent.\n- **Size it** from the provider's documented retry schedule, and monitor the age of duplicates you see."},{"q":"Why acknowledge immediately and process in a worker instead of doing the work in the handler?","a":"- **Provider timeouts:** deliveries time out after seconds to tens of seconds, and a slow handler causes\n  retries, duplicates and possibly a disabled endpoint.\n- **Ordering and freshness:** the worker can fetch the current object by id, which handles out-of-order\n  and stale payloads.\n- **Cost:** a 200 no longer means \"done\", so you need queue monitoring, a dead-letter queue and\n  reconciliation."}],"tradeoffs":["**Shared-secret HMAC vs asymmetric signatures.** HMAC is simple, fast and verifiable offline, but every holder can forge; asymmetric signatures win when many parties must verify or the verifier must never be able to sign.","**Tight vs wide tolerance.** A tight window shrinks replay exposure, but clock skew and queued deliveries cause false rejections and more retries; widen it when the provider does not re-sign retries and lean on id deduplication instead.","**Redis TTL vs a unique constraint.** Redis dedupe is one cheap command but not transactional with your side effect; a unique constraint in the same transaction costs a write per event and buys a real effectively-once guarantee.","**Immediate ack vs visible completion.** Acknowledging immediately frees you from provider timeouts, but a 200 no longer means \"done\", so monitoring, dead-lettering and reconciliation move to the queue.","**Fetching the object vs API cost.** Fetching the authoritative object on every event defeats stale and out-of-order payloads, but adds an API call per event, subject to the provider's rate limits."],"pitfalls":["**Verifying over re-serialised JSON.** Symptom: signatures fail only for some payloads (non-ASCII text, floats, nested key order) while every local test passes.","**Comparing signatures with `==`.** There is no functional symptom, which is exactly the danger; it surfaces only in a pentest or security review.","**Checking the signature but neither timestamp nor event id.** Symptom: double side effects (two credits, two emails) whenever the provider retries, and silent replayability.","**A gateway, body-parsing middleware or compression layer rewriting the body.** Symptom: every signature fails after a deployment that changed nothing in your code.","**Doing the work inline and returning 200 at the end.** Symptom: provider-side timeouts, escalating retries and duplicate processing under load, sometimes ending with the endpoint disabled.","**Assuming ordered delivery.** Symptom: a record stuck in `started` because a late `started` event overwrote `completed`.","**Rotating the secret without an overlap.** Symptom: a burst of 400s and missed events during the switch.","**Logging full headers and bodies.** Symptom: signatures, tokens and customer data sit in the log platform for anyone with search access."],"when":"- **Every inbound webhook, without exception**, including your own services' events whenever they cross a\n  trust boundary; the cost is a few lines, the failure mode is an attacker driving your business logic.\n- **If a sender cannot sign**, require at least a secret header, and prefer polling the provider's API for\n  state over trusting pushes.\n- **Measure** the verification-failure rate (anything above zero is an attack or a misconfiguration), the\n  duplicate rate, the distribution of timestamp age, and processing lag.\n- **Interviews:** \"how would you secure a webhook endpoint\" is standard; walk raw-body HMAC, constant-time\n  compare, timestamp window, id dedupe, idempotent handler, ack-then-queue and fetching the authoritative\n  object, and say why each exists.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the verification middleware on raw bytes, the dedupe and queue hand-off, and the three fixtures (good, tampered, duplicated) that keep it honest in CI.","architect":"Defines the trust boundary for inbound events: which may trigger side effects, where the idempotency store lives, the retry horizon, and who owns and rotates each secret.","fde":"Wires customer systems (Slack, GitHub, Salesforce, an in-house ERP) to the agent, checks each one's signing scheme, and agrees how secrets are exchanged and rotated inside the customer's security process."},"check":{"q":"A colleague says the billing webhook is already secure: it lives at a long random URL, the\nhandler checks that the JSON contains `\"source\": \"billing\"`, and the site is HTTPS-only. Explain what\neach measure misses and what you would implement instead.","a":"The random URL is a bearer secret that leaks through access logs, proxies and screenshots and\ncannot be rotated without touching every integration. The `source` field is part of the body the\nattacker writes. HTTPS encrypts in transit and proves your identity to the sender; it says nothing\nabout who sent the request. Instead: verify an HMAC-SHA256 over the timestamp and the raw body using a\nper-endpoint secret from the secret manager, compare in constant time, reject timestamps older than a\nfew minutes, deduplicate on event id with retention covering the provider's retry schedule, make the\nhandler idempotent, return 200 quickly and process from a queue, and for anything that grants value,\nfetch the current object from the billing API by id before acting."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must the HMAC be computed over the raw request bytes rather than over the parsed JSON object?","options":["Parsing JSON is too slow to do before authentication","Serialisation is not canonical, so re-encoded JSON can differ byte for byte from what the provider signed","Raw bytes include the headers, which are part of the signature","JSON objects cannot be hashed because they are unordered"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The provider signed a specific byte sequence. Whitespace, key order, escaping and number formatting can all change on a parse-and-dump round trip, producing a different MAC even though the data is \"the same\"."},{"q":"A delivery arrives with a valid signature and a timestamp two minutes old, but its event id is already in your seen-set. What should the handler do?","options":["Return 400 so the provider stops sending it","Process it again, because the signature proves it is genuine","Return 2xx without running the side effect again","Return 500 so the provider retries later with a fresh timestamp"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Whether it is a provider retry or an attacker's replay, the right action is the same: acknowledge and do nothing. A non-2xx would only trigger more retries of an event you have already handled."},{"q":"What does a constant-time comparison such as `hmac.compare_digest` protect against that `==` does not?","options":["SHA-256 hash collisions","Length-extension attacks on the hash","A timing side channel where early exit reveals how many leading bytes of the guess were correct","Replay of a previously valid signature"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Ordinary string comparison stops at the first mismatch, so response time correlates with the number of correct leading bytes. Length-extension is already prevented by HMAC's construction, and replay is handled by timestamps and ids, not by the comparison."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/database-design","sources":["Codd, 1970 — A Relational Model of Data for Large Shared Data Banks","Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Official docs: PostgreSQL"],"why":"- **Data outlives code.** Code is rewritten every year; the data it produced is still there a decade\n  later. A layout shaped by whatever the first endpoint needed breaks in three ways.\n- **Copies drift (update anomaly).** The same fact stored in several rows (a user's email copied into\n  every order) disagrees after the first update that misses one. Application discipline cannot fix it,\n  because the next engineer does not know the copies exist.\n- **Invariants in code get bypassed.** \"A message always belongs to exactly one conversation\" is violated\n  by the one script, migration or manual fix that skips the code, leaving orphans that crash reads months later.\n- **Fast queries become scans.** What was fast at ten thousand rows is a table scan at ten million, and by\n  then the layout is load-bearing for a dozen services. \"Store JSON blobs and figure it out later\" just\n  postpones all three to when the data is largest and the authors are gone.\n- **AI systems raise the stakes.** Conversation history, tool-call logs, embeddings and evaluation results\n  are the product's memory, queried by humans, dashboards and retrieval code long after the request that\n  wrote them. Design decides up front which facts exist, who owns each, which rules the database enforces\n  and which access paths must be cheap.","how":"### Entities, keys and relationships\n1. **Name the nouns** the business reasons about: tenant, user, conversation, message, tool call, run,\n   document, chunk.\n2. **State relationships with cardinality:** a conversation has many messages, a message has zero or more\n   tool calls, a document has many chunks.\n3. **Give each entity a table and a primary key.** A **surrogate key** (bigint identity or UUID) is the\n   default *because* natural keys (email, external IDs) change, get reused, or turn out not to be unique.\n4. **Prefer time-ordered UUIDs.** Use UUIDv7 over random UUIDv4 for primary keys, because a B-tree on\n   random keys inserts into random pages, which defeats cache locality and bloats the index as it grows.\n5. **Turn relationships into foreign keys** the database enforces, so an orphan cannot exist even when a\n   script bypasses the application.\n\n### Normalization: one fact, one place\nA table describes one kind of thing, and every non-key column depends on the whole key and nothing else.\n\n| Form | Rule | Example fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1NF | No repeating groups or lists packed into a column | `tags = \"a,b,c\"` becomes a `tags` table or an array column with proper indexing |\n| 2NF | With a composite key, no column depends on only part of it | In a `(conversation_id, user_id)` membership table, `user_name` depends on `user_id` alone and belongs in `users` |\n| 3NF | No non-key column depends on another non-key column | If `messages` stores `user_id` and `user_email`, the email belongs in `users`; otherwise changing an email rewrites every message row |\n\n- **Normalize by default** *because* writes are where consistency is decided; a normalized schema makes\n  the correct write the only possible write.\n- **The cost is joins on read.** A `conversations.last_message_at` column duplicated from `messages` makes\n  the inbox query cheap, but must be maintained in the same transaction as the insert.\n- **Denormalize on measurement.** Do it when a specific query is measured to be slow, and write down the\n  invariant you are now responsible for.\n\n### Constraints are invariants the database guarantees\n- **The cheapest tests you will write:** `NOT NULL`, `UNIQUE`, `CHECK (tokens_used >= 0)`, foreign keys\n  with explicit `ON DELETE` behaviour, and enum-like `CHECK (role IN ('user','assistant','tool'))`. They\n  run on every write path, including the ones you forgot.\n- **Partial unique indexes** encode \"unique among live rows\":\n  `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... ON documents (tenant_id, external_id) WHERE deleted_at IS NULL`; a plain\n  `UNIQUE` constraint cannot take a `WHERE`, and code alone gets this wrong under concurrent inserts.\n\n### Types carry meaning\n| Use | Never | Because |\n|---|---|---|\n| `timestamptz` | bare `timestamp` | `timestamptz` stores an absolute instant (UTC internally); `timestamp` stores a wall-clock reading whose meaning depends on the writer's assumed zone, so rows from servers in different zones cannot be compared |\n| `numeric` for money and token prices | `float` | binary floats cannot represent 0.1 exactly, and rounding errors accumulate in invoices |\n| `jsonb` for semi-structured payloads (raw model responses, tool arguments) | jsonb for fields you filter or join on | pull those fields into a real column with a real index |\n\n- **Version your JSON.** Store a `schema_version` next to any jsonb you will parse later.\n\n### Multi-tenancy and deletion from day one\n- **Tenant on every row.** Every tenant-owned row carries `tenant_id`, every tenant-scoped index leads\n  with it, and every query filters on it. PostgreSQL row-level security can enforce the filter when you do\n  not trust every code path.\n- **Plain FKs allow cross-tenant links.** A foreign key on `conversation_id` still lets a message carry\n  tenant A's id while pointing at tenant B's conversation, because nothing compares the two columns.\n- **Composite FK fix.** Make the parent's `(tenant_id, id)` unique and have the child reference that pair,\n  so the database itself refuses a cross-tenant link.\n- **Decide deletion before the first user asks.** A `deleted_at` soft-delete column keeps audit history\n  but every query must exclude it. Hard deletes with `ON DELETE CASCADE` are simpler but irreversible.\n- **One clear owner.** Retention and right-to-erasure requests are much easier when data is owned by one\n  clear parent row.\n\n### Indexes follow queries\n- **An index is a sorted copy of some columns.** It makes one access path cheap and every write slightly\n  more expensive.\n- **Ask the right question.** Not \"which columns are important\" but which `WHERE` and `ORDER BY` patterns\n  run often.\n- **Column order matters.** `(conversation_id, created_at)` serves \"messages of this conversation in\n  order\" in one range scan; the same columns reversed do not.\n- **Equality first, range or sort last,** *because* the B-tree sorts by the first column, then the second\n  within ties. Equality on the leading column narrows to one contiguous slice already ordered by the next.\n  Put the range column first and one conversation's entries scatter across the whole range.\n- **Index foreign-key columns.** Joins and cascading deletes look rows up by them, and PostgreSQL does not\n  create one automatically on the referencing side.\n\n### Evolving the schema\nThe schema changes while old code still runs, so every change must work with both versions for the length\nof a deploy. That is the **expand/contract** rule:\n\n1. Add the new column or table.\n2. Write to both.\n3. Backfill in batches.\n4. Move reads.\n5. Only then drop the old one. A one-step column rename breaks every instance still on the previous release.\n\n| Operation | Cost |\n|---|---|\n| Add a column with a constant default (since PostgreSQL 11) | does not rewrite existing rows |\n| Change a column's type | generally rewrites the table |\n| `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` | builds without blocking writes, but slower, and a failure leaves an invalid index to drop |\n\nReview every migration for which locks it takes on a large, hot table.","example":"### A schema for an LLM application\nNote the tenant column on every row (and the composite foreign key that keeps a message in its\nconversation's tenant), the constraints that encode rules, and the jsonb for raw payloads next to the\nextracted columns (`model`, token counts) that queries actually filter and aggregate on.\n\nThe composite index serves the one hot query (load a conversation's messages in order), and the plain\nindex on the foreign key is what cascading deletes need.\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE conversations (\n  id           uuid PRIMARY KEY,                 -- UUIDv7 generated by the app\n  tenant_id    uuid NOT NULL,\n  user_id      uuid NOT NULL,\n  title        text,\n  created_at   timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),\n  deleted_at   timestamptz,\n  UNIQUE (tenant_id, id)                         -- target for tenant-consistent FKs\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE messages (\n  id              bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n  tenant_id       uuid NOT NULL,\n  conversation_id uuid NOT NULL,\n  FOREIGN KEY (tenant_id, conversation_id)       -- a message cannot point into another tenant\n    REFERENCES conversations (tenant_id, id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  role            text NOT NULL CHECK (role IN ('system','user','assistant','tool')),\n  content         text NOT NULL,\n  model           text,\n  input_tokens    integer CHECK (input_tokens >= 0),\n  output_tokens   integer CHECK (output_tokens >= 0),\n  raw_response    jsonb,                     -- provider payload, never filtered on directly\n  schema_version  smallint NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,\n  created_at      timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()\n);\n\n-- the hot path: \"messages of one conversation, oldest first\"\nCREATE INDEX messages_by_conversation ON messages (conversation_id, created_at);\n-- the tenant-scoped dashboard query: spend per tenant per day\nCREATE INDEX messages_by_tenant_time ON messages (tenant_id, created_at);\n\nCREATE TABLE tool_calls (\n  id            bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n  tenant_id     uuid NOT NULL,                -- child tables carry the tenant too\n  message_id    bigint NOT NULL REFERENCES messages(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  tool_name     text NOT NULL,\n  arguments     jsonb NOT NULL,\n  status        text NOT NULL CHECK (status IN ('pending','succeeded','failed')),\n  idempotency_key text,\n  UNIQUE (tenant_id, idempotency_key)        -- a retried call cannot be recorded twice\n);\n-- FK lookup for joins and for ON DELETE CASCADE from messages\nCREATE INDEX tool_calls_by_message ON tool_calls (message_id);\n```\n\n### Runnable: the database refuses bad writes\nStandard-library SQLite, so it runs anywhere. It shows the composite foreign key rejecting a cross-tenant\nlink, a `CHECK` rejecting a bad role, and the composite index serving the ordered read without a sort.\n\n```python\nimport sqlite3\ndb = sqlite3.connect(\":memory:\")\ndb.execute(\"PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON\")            # SQLite enforces FKs only when asked\ndb.executescript(\"\"\"\nCREATE TABLE conversations (\n  id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, tenant_id TEXT NOT NULL,\n  UNIQUE (tenant_id, id));\nCREATE TABLE messages (\n  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, tenant_id TEXT NOT NULL, conversation_id TEXT NOT NULL,\n  role TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (role IN ('system','user','assistant','tool')),\n  created_at TEXT NOT NULL,\n  FOREIGN KEY (tenant_id, conversation_id) REFERENCES conversations (tenant_id, id));\nCREATE INDEX messages_by_conversation ON messages (conversation_id, created_at);\n\"\"\")\ndb.execute(\"INSERT INTO conversations VALUES ('c1', 'tenantA')\")\ndb.execute(\"INSERT INTO messages VALUES (1, 'tenantA', 'c1', 'user', '2025-01-01')\")\n\nfor bad in [(2, 'tenantB', 'c1', 'user', '2025-01-01'),       # cross-tenant link\n            (3, 'tenantA', 'c1', 'robot', '2025-01-01')]:     # role not allowed\n    try:\n        db.execute(\"INSERT INTO messages VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?)\", bad)\n    except sqlite3.IntegrityError as e:\n        print(\"rejected:\", bad[0], e)\n\nplan = db.execute(\"EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN SELECT * FROM messages \"\n                  \"WHERE conversation_id = 'c1' ORDER BY created_at\").fetchall()\nprint(plan[-1][-1])   # SEARCH messages USING INDEX messages_by_conversation (conversation_id=?)\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: renaming a column with zero downtime\nThe team wants `messages.content` renamed to `body` on a large, hot table while three app versions roll out.\n\n| Step | Deploy | What runs |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Expand | migration | add nullable `body` column (no table rewrite) |\n| 2. Dual write | app v2 | writes `content` and `body`; still reads `content` |\n| 3. Backfill | job | copy `content` into `body` in small batches by primary-key range |\n| 4. Move reads | app v3 | reads `body`, still writes both |\n| 5. Contract | app v4 + migration | stop writing `content`, then drop it |\n\nA one-step `ALTER TABLE ... RENAME COLUMN` would have broken every v1 instance still serving traffic.","patterns":["**Lock-safe migrations.** For any migration on a hot table, set `SET lock_timeout = '5s'` in the migration session so a blocked `ALTER` fails fast instead of queueing every write behind it, and build indexes with `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY`. Monitor lock waits in `pg_stat_activity` and drop invalid indexes.","**Add constraints without a long lock.** On large tables add a `CHECK` or foreign key as `NOT VALID`, then run `ALTER TABLE ... VALIDATE CONSTRAINT` separately; new writes are checked immediately and validation does not block them. Monitor validation duration.","**Row-level security for tenancy.** `ALTER TABLE messages ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` plus a policy on `tenant_id = current_setting('app.tenant_id')::uuid`, set per request. Table owners bypass policies unless you also `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY`. Monitor with a CI test that tries a cross-tenant read.","**Index review from statistics.** Periodically list indexes with `idx_scan = 0` in `pg_stat_user_indexes` (candidates to drop) and slow statements from `pg_stat_statements` (candidates for a composite index). Monitor write latency on tables you add indexes to.","**Keep big payloads out of the hot table.** Full prompts and document text go in a side table or object storage keyed by message id, so the hot table's working set fits in cache. Monitor table size with `pg_total_relation_size` and cache hit ratio."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    N[\"Name entities and cardinality\"] --> K[\"Tables with surrogate keys\"]\n    K --> C[\"FKs, CHECK, NOT NULL, UNIQUE\"]\n    C --> NF[\"Normalize to 3NF\"]\n    NF --> Q[\"List hot WHERE and ORDER BY\"]\n    Q --> I[\"Composite indexes, equality first\"]\n    I --> M{\"Read measured slow?\"}\n    M -->|\"yes\"| D[\"Denormalize one column, same transaction\"]\n    M -->|\"no\"| KEEP[\"Keep normalized\"]\n    D --> E[\"Ship via expand/contract migration\"]\n    KEEP --> E","caption":"Read it top to bottom: design starts from entities and invariants, indexes come from queries, and\ndenormalization is a measured exception, not a default."},"interview":[{"q":"Why default to surrogate keys, and why UUIDv7 rather than UUIDv4?","a":"- **Surrogate keys are stable.** Natural keys like email or external IDs change, get reused or turn out\n  not unique, so a bigint identity or UUID is the primary key and the natural key gets its own unique constraint.\n- **UUIDv7 is time-ordered.** New keys land at the right edge of the B-tree, while random UUIDv4 inserts\n  into random pages, hurting cache locality and bloating the index as the table grows."},{"q":"How would you stop a message from pointing at another tenant's conversation?","a":"- **A plain FK is not enough,** because nothing compares the child's `tenant_id` with the parent's.\n- **Use a composite key:** make `(tenant_id, id)` unique on conversations and reference that pair from\n  messages, so the database rejects a cross-tenant link on every write path.\n- **Add defence in depth** with row-level security on `tenant_id` and tenant-leading indexes so every query\n  is scoped and cheap."},{"q":"The \"load conversation\" query is slow even though both columns are indexed. What do you check?","a":"- **Run `EXPLAIN ANALYZE`** and look for a sort node or bitmap combination of two single-column indexes.\n- **Fix the index shape:** one composite index with the equality column first and the sort column last,\n  `(conversation_id, created_at)`, returns rows already ordered in one range scan.\n- **Then check write cost,** dropping single-column indexes the composite one makes redundant."},{"q":"When would you choose jsonb or denormalization over a normalized design?","a":"- **jsonb for payloads you do not query:** raw provider responses and tool arguments, with a\n  `schema_version`, and extracted columns for anything filtered or joined.\n- **Denormalize after measurement:** a single column like `last_message_at` for a hot inbox query,\n  maintained in the same transaction as the insert, with the invariant written down.\n- **Avoid both** when several teams query the data in ways you cannot predict; normalized tables keep it\n  correct for unknown readers."},{"q":"How do you change a schema on a large production table without downtime?","a":"- **Expand/contract:** add the new column, dual-write, backfill in batches, move reads, then drop the old\n  column a release later, so every running version stays compatible.\n- **Watch locks:** adding a column with a constant default is cheap since PostgreSQL 11, a type change\n  generally rewrites the table, and indexes go in with `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY`.\n- **Fail fast:** set a short lock timeout in migration sessions so a blocked DDL does not queue all writes."}],"tradeoffs":["**Normalization vs joins.** One fact in one place costs joins; flip to denormalization only when a specific read path is measured slow and its duplicate can be maintained transactionally.","**Surrogate keys vs natural identifiers.** Surrogate keys are stable and cheap to join, but you still need a unique constraint on the natural identifier or duplicates silently appear.","**UUID vs bigint keys.** UUIDs can be generated client-side and merged across systems, but random UUIDs cost index locality and 16 bytes per reference versus 8 for bigint; time-ordered UUIDs recover most of the locality.","**`jsonb` flexibility vs queryability.** It absorbs schema changes without migrations, but every field later filtered without a column and index becomes a sequential scan, and nothing enforces its shape.","**Read speed vs write cost.** Every index speeds one read pattern and taxes every insert and update; on write-heavy tables such as event logs, each extra index is a measurable throughput cost.","**Constraint checks vs trust.** Foreign keys and `CHECK`s cost a lookup per write but catch bugs at the only point that sees every path; dropping them \"for speed\" trades microseconds for untrustworthy data."],"pitfalls":["**Omitting `tenant_id` from child tables.** Relying on joins to the parent for scoping; the symptom is a query that returns another tenant's rows the first time someone forgets the join.","**Using `float` for money or token prices.** Symptom: invoices off by a cent and tests that compare with tolerances.","**Bare `timestamp` columns.** Symptom: rows written before and after a server or container time-zone change are hours apart in meaning but adjacent in value, and \"yesterday\" reports disagree.","**Indexing columns rather than queries.** Symptom: ten single-column indexes, slow writes, and the hot query still sorting because no composite index matches its `WHERE` and `ORDER BY`.","**Entity-attribute-value tables to avoid migrations.** \"One table of key/value pairs for everything\"; the symptom is five-way self-joins to rebuild one object and no constraints on anything.","**Multi-megabyte payloads in the hottest table.** Full prompts or document text; the symptom is bloated tables, slow vacuums and a cache that no longer holds the working set.","**One-step rename or type change on a large table.** Symptom: errors from instances on the previous release, or a migration holding a lock for minutes while every write queues behind it.","**Designing deletion last.** Symptom: a right-to-erasure request takes a week because data is scattered across tables with no ownership chain."],"when":"- **Use when:** data will be read by anything other than the code that wrote it: dashboards, evaluations,\n  audits, other services, or future you.\n- **Prototype exception:** for a throwaway prototype a single jsonb column is fine; the moment a second\n  query pattern appears, extract real columns.\n- **Measure before denormalizing:** run `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` on the slow query and check the write rate of\n  the table you would duplicate into.\n- **Day to day:** migration reviews (\"why is there no index for the query this endpoint runs?\", \"what\n  happens to messages when a conversation is deleted?\").\n- **Interviews:** expect a product description and a request to draw tables, keys and indexes, then defend\n  each constraint and say which query each index serves.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the migration, picks types and constraints, and adds the composite index that matches the query the new endpoint runs, verified with `EXPLAIN`.","architect":"Owns the ownership model: which service owns which tables, how tenancy is enforced, the retention and deletion policy, and when a read model may be denormalized.","fde":"Maps the customer's existing schema and identifiers onto the product's model, finds the missing tenant or ownership keys, and plans how their history will be imported and later erased."},"check":{"q":"A chat product stores each conversation as one jsonb document containing all its messages. Reads\nare fast today. What breaks as it grows, and how would you restructure it?","a":"Appending a message rewrites the whole document, so write cost grows with conversation length and\nlong conversations create bloat and lock contention. You cannot index or constrain individual messages,\nso \"all messages by this tool across tenants\" or \"token spend per day\" become full scans that parse JSON.\nDeleting one message or one user's data means rewriting documents. The fix is a `messages` table with\n`tenant_id`, `conversation_id`, `role`, token counts and `created_at`, a composite index on\n`(conversation_id, created_at)` for the conversation view, and a second on `(tenant_id, created_at)` for\nreporting; keep the raw provider response in a jsonb column on the message row, where it is not filtered\non. If the inbox view needs `last_message_at`, denormalize that single column and maintain it in the same\ntransaction as the insert."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is a composite index on (conversation_id, created_at) better than two single-column indexes for loading a conversation's messages in order?","options":["Composite indexes are stored in memory while single-column indexes are on disk","One range scan over the composite index returns the rows already in the right order, while separate indexes require combining results and then sorting","Single-column indexes cannot be used together in the same query","The composite index takes less space than two single-column indexes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The planner can walk the composite index from the first matching conversation_id in created_at order, so no sort step is needed. With single-column indexes the planner must either fetch the conversation's rows and sort them, or combine indexes in a bitmap scan, which also loses order and needs a separate sort."},{"q":"A team drops foreign keys \"to make inserts faster\". Which failure is this most likely to cause?","options":["Queries become slower because the planner can no longer use joins","The database refuses to create indexes on the former foreign-key columns","Orphan rows appear whenever any script or bug deletes a parent without its children, and reads fail later","Transactions can no longer be rolled back on those tables"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A foreign key is checked on every write path, including the ones the application code does not cover. Without it, orphans accumulate silently and surface as missing-join bugs or crashes much later."},{"q":"When is denormalizing a column such as conversations.last_message_at the right call?","options":["Whenever a join would otherwise be required, because joins are always expensive","Never, because it violates third normal form","When a specific frequent query is measured to be slow and the duplicate can be updated in the same transaction as the source row","Only when the database does not support indexes on the child table"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Denormalization buys read speed by taking on a consistency obligation. It is justified by measurement and made safe by maintaining the copy transactionally; doing it by default reintroduces update anomalies."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/sql","sources":["Codd, 1970 — A Relational Model of Data for Large Shared Data Banks","Official docs: PostgreSQL","Winand — Use The Index, Luke"],"why":"- **Navigational databases coupled code to layout.** Programs walked pointers from record to record, so\n  every program encoded the physical layout, and changing the layout meant changing every program.\n- **Codd separated what from how.** Data became relations (tables of tuples) and queries became algebra\n  over them. SQL is the practical language from that, and the planner turns the *what* into a *how* at run\n  time using statistics about the actual data.\n- **The naive loop is N+1.** Fetch conversations, then each one's messages, then count tokens per message.\n  Each round trip is a network hop (hundreds of microseconds to milliseconds), so 1,000 conversations cost\n  1,001 queries and between half a second and several seconds of wall time.\n- **The loop also hides the question.** The database never sees the whole request, so it cannot pick a hash\n  join or use an index to do the work in one pass. One query with a join and an aggregate is one scan.\n- **AI backends are heavy SQL users.** Per-tenant token spend, latency percentiles per model, the last N\n  messages of a conversation, eval results joined to prompt versions, and hybrid retrieval mixing vector\n  similarity with a metadata filter are each one query when written well, a slow fan-out when not.","how":"### Logical evaluation order\nA `SELECT` is written in one order and evaluated in another:\n\n1. `FROM` and `JOIN`: build the row set.\n2. `WHERE`: filter rows.\n3. `GROUP BY`: collapse into groups.\n4. `HAVING`: filter groups.\n5. `SELECT`: compute output columns, including window functions.\n6. `DISTINCT`.\n7. `ORDER BY`.\n8. `LIMIT`/`OFFSET`.\n\nThis explains the classic confusions:\n- **No `SELECT` alias in `WHERE`,** because `WHERE` runs first.\n- **`HAVING` exists** because aggregates do not exist yet when `WHERE` runs.\n- **Window functions see filtered, grouped rows** because they run during `SELECT`.\n\n### Joins are set operations, not loops\n- **Inner join** keeps rows that match on both sides.\n- **Left join** keeps every left row and fills the right side with NULLs when nothing matches; that is how\n  you find conversations with no messages (`WHERE m.id IS NULL`).\n- **`EXISTS`/`NOT EXISTS`** express \"has at least one\" without duplicating rows the way a join would.\n\nThe engine picks the physical algorithm; you make the good ones possible with indexes and accurate statistics:\n\n| Algorithm | Chosen when |\n|---|---|\n| **Nested loop** | one side is tiny, or has an index on the join key |\n| **Hash join** | both sides are large and unsorted |\n| **Merge join** | both sides are already sorted on the key |\n\n### Aggregates and window functions\n- **`GROUP BY` collapses rows.**\n- **A window function computes per row without collapsing:**\n  `SUM(...) OVER (PARTITION BY tenant_id ORDER BY day)` gives running totals, rankings and \"latest row per\n  group\" in a single pass.\n- **PostgreSQL idiom for \"newest message per conversation\":** `DISTINCT ON (conversation_id) ... ORDER BY\n  conversation_id, created_at DESC`.\n\n### How the planner decides\n`EXPLAIN ANALYZE` shows the tree the planner chose, with real row counts and timings. Cost estimates come\nfrom table statistics (row counts, value distributions gathered by `ANALYZE`).\n\n| Scan | What it does | Cheapest when |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sequential | read everything | most rows qualify |\n| Index | walk a B-tree to matching rows | few rows qualify |\n| Bitmap | collect matching pages from one or more indexes, then read them in physical order | in between |\n| Index-only | answer from the index alone, skipping the table | the index holds every column read, e.g. via `INCLUDE (input_tokens)` |\n\n- **Sargable predicates.** A predicate can use an index when the indexed column appears bare on one side:\n  `created_at >= $1` can; `date(created_at) = $1` cannot, because the index stores `created_at`, not its date.\n- **Prefix `LIKE`.** `LIKE 'abc%'` can use a B-tree, but in PostgreSQL only if the index uses the `C`\n  collation or the `text_pattern_ops` operator class. A default index under a locale such as\n  `en_US.UTF-8` is not used for `LIKE`.\n- **Infix `LIKE`.** `LIKE '%abc%'` cannot use a B-tree at all and needs a trigram (`pg_trgm`) index.\n- **Index-only scans need vacuum.** They work only for pages the visibility map marks all-visible, one more\n  reason vacuum health shows up in query latency.\n- **Statistics go stale.** A table that grew 100× since the last `ANALYZE` gets bad plans; autovacuum\n  usually re-analyzes, but can lag after a bulk load.\n\n### NULL is not a value\nSQL uses three-valued logic: a comparison with NULL is *unknown*, and `WHERE` keeps only rows where the\npredicate is true.\n\n- **`WHERE model <> 'gpt-x'`** silently drops every row whose `model` is NULL.\n- **`x = NULL` never matches;** use `IS NULL` or `IS DISTINCT FROM`.\n- **`NOT IN (subquery)` returns nothing** once the subquery yields a single NULL, because\n  `a NOT IN (b, NULL)` expands to `a <> b AND a <> NULL`, which can never be true.\n- **Arithmetic propagates NULL:** `input_tokens + output_tokens` is NULL if either side is.\n- **`SUM` skips NULLs,** so a row with a missing count vanishes from a spend total instead of erroring.\n- **`COUNT(*)` counts rows; `COUNT(col)` counts non-NULL values.** The difference is a common source of\n  reports that disagree.\n\n### Parameters, not string formatting\n- **Parameters travel separately.** Placeholders (`$1`, `%s`, `?` depending on the driver) are sent apart\n  from the SQL text.\n- **The only reliable injection defence,** *because* the database never parses user input as SQL. It also\n  lets the server reuse a prepared plan.\n- **Interpolation is a vulnerability** regardless of how well you escape.\n\n### Pagination and the OFFSET trap\n- **`LIMIT 20 OFFSET 100000` still reads and discards 100,000 rows;** cost grows linearly with page depth.\n- **Keyset pagination** (`WHERE (created_at, id) < ($last_ts, $last_id) ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC\n  LIMIT 20`) seeks directly to the next page using the index and costs the same on page 1 and page 5,000.\n\n### Where the ORM fits\n- **Convenient for CRUD, dangerous for reporting.** Lazy-loaded relationships are the usual source of\n  N+1, and complex aggregations are often clearer as SQL.\n- **Working rule:** let the ORM handle simple writes and reads by key; write (and `EXPLAIN`) the hot or\n  analytical queries by hand.","example":"### Two queries an LLM backend runs constantly\n- **Spend report:** per-tenant spend with a running total, one pass, a window function over the grouped rows.\n- **Inbox view:** \"newest message per conversation\" via a `LATERAL` subquery (the `DISTINCT ON` form above\n  is the alternative), with a keyset cursor so page depth does not change the cost.\n- **Scaling note:** each page still evaluates the lateral lookup for every conversation the user owns.\n  With thousands of conversations, the next step is a denormalized `conversations.last_message_at` with an\n  index on `(tenant_id, user_id, last_message_at, id)`, so the cursor seeks straight to the page.\n\n```sql\n-- 1. Daily token spend per tenant for the last 7 days, with a running total.\n--    No tenant filter, so a (tenant_id, created_at) index gives no simple range scan;\n--    an index on created_at (or a BRIN index on an append-only table) serves this.\n--    Day boundaries follow the session TimeZone setting.\nSELECT tenant_id,\n       date_trunc('day', created_at) AS day,\n       SUM(COALESCE(input_tokens, 0) + COALESCE(output_tokens, 0))        AS tokens,\n       SUM(SUM(COALESCE(input_tokens, 0) + COALESCE(output_tokens, 0)))   -- window over groups\n         OVER (PARTITION BY tenant_id ORDER BY date_trunc('day', created_at)) AS running_tokens\nFROM   messages\nWHERE  created_at >= now() - interval '7 days'      -- sargable: bare column vs constant\n  AND  role = 'assistant'\nGROUP  BY tenant_id, date_trunc('day', created_at)\nORDER  BY tenant_id, day;\n\n-- 2. Inbox: the newest message of each conversation for one user, 20 at a time.\n--    Keyset pagination: pass the last row's (created_at, id) back instead of an OFFSET.\nSELECT c.id, c.title, m.content, m.created_at\nFROM   conversations c\nJOIN   LATERAL (\n         SELECT content, created_at, id\n         FROM   messages\n         WHERE  conversation_id = c.id\n         ORDER  BY created_at DESC, id DESC           -- id breaks timestamp ties\n         LIMIT  1                                     -- hits (conversation_id, created_at)\n       ) m ON true\nWHERE  c.tenant_id = $1 AND c.user_id = $2 AND c.deleted_at IS NULL\n  AND  (m.created_at, m.id) < ($3, $4)                -- previous page's last row; first page: ('infinity', 0)\nORDER  BY m.created_at DESC, m.id DESC\nLIMIT  20;\n```\n\nRun each with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` and check:\n1. **The scan on `messages` is an index scan,** not a sequential scan.\n2. **Estimated rows are within an order of magnitude of actual.** A large mismatch means stale statistics\n   or a predicate the planner cannot estimate.\n\n- **The `id` tie-breaker matters** in both the `ORDER BY` and the cursor: two messages with the same\n  `created_at` would otherwise have no defined order, and a page boundary between them could skip or\n  repeat a row.\n- **The `COALESCE` calls** stop a single row with a NULL count from dropping out of the total.\n\n### Runnable: NULL silently changes answers\nStandard-library SQLite follows the same three-valued logic as PostgreSQL here.\n\n```python\nimport sqlite3\ndb = sqlite3.connect(\":memory:\")\ndb.executescript(\"\"\"\nCREATE TABLE messages (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, model TEXT, input_tokens INTEGER);\nINSERT INTO messages (model, input_tokens) VALUES ('gpt-x', 10), ('other', 20), (NULL, 30), ('other', NULL);\nCREATE TABLE blocked (model TEXT);\nINSERT INTO blocked VALUES ('gpt-x'), (NULL);\n\"\"\")\nq = lambda sql: db.execute(sql).fetchall()\nprint(q(\"SELECT COUNT(*) FROM messages WHERE model <> 'gpt-x'\"))          # [(2,)]  NULL row dropped\nprint(q(\"SELECT COUNT(*), COUNT(input_tokens), SUM(input_tokens) FROM messages\"))  # [(4, 3, 60)]\nprint(q(\"SELECT COUNT(*) FROM messages WHERE model NOT IN (SELECT model FROM blocked)\"))  # [(0,)]\nprint(q(\"\"\"SELECT COUNT(*) FROM messages m\n           WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM blocked b WHERE b.model = m.model)\"\"\"))   # [(3,)]\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: what N+1 costs an inbox endpoint\nAn endpoint lists 1,000 conversations and lazily loads each one's latest message (illustrative round-trip\ntimes, not a benchmark).\n\n| Approach | Queries | At 0.5 ms per round trip | At 3 ms per round trip |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| ORM loop (N+1) | 1,001 | ~0.5 s | ~3 s |\n| One `LATERAL` join | 1 | one round trip plus query time | one round trip plus query time |\n\nEach of the 1,001 queries looks fast in the log; the sum is the latency. The join lets the planner use\n`(conversation_id, created_at)` once per conversation inside a single statement.","patterns":["**Rank by total time, not mean.** Enable the `pg_stat_statements` extension and sort by `total_exec_time` weekly; a 2 ms query run a million times outranks a 5 s nightly report. Monitor the top ten after each release.","**Log slow plans automatically.** Load the `auto_explain` module with `auto_explain.log_min_duration` set (for example 500ms) so production plans of slow queries land in the logs. Monitor for sequential scans on large tables and estimate-versus-actual row gaps.","**Cap runaway queries.** Set `statement_timeout` per role or per transaction (for example a few seconds for the API role, longer for reporting), so one bad plan cannot pin connections. Monitor cancelled statements by route.","**Keyset pagination everywhere users scroll.** Return an opaque cursor encoding `(created_at, id)` and back it with an index ending in those columns. Monitor p95 latency by page depth; it should be flat.","**Detect N+1 in tests.** Count queries per request in integration tests (for example SQLAlchemy's `before_cursor_execute` event) and fail when a list endpoint's count grows with result size."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    Q[\"SQL text plus parameters\"] --> P[\"Parse and rewrite\"]\n    P --> PL[\"Cost-based planner\"]\n    ST[(\"Table statistics from ANALYZE\")] --> PL\n    IX[(\"Indexes\")] --> PL\n    PL --> S{\"Few rows qualify?\"}\n    S -->|\"yes, sargable\"| IS[\"Index or index-only scan\"]\n    S -->|\"no\"| SS[\"Sequential or bitmap scan\"]\n    IS --> J[\"Join: nested loop, hash or merge\"]\n    SS --> J\n    J --> A[\"Group, window, sort, limit\"]\n    A --> R[\"Result rows\"]","caption":"Follow a query from text to rows: the planner, fed by statistics, picks scans and joins; stale statistics\nor a non-sargable predicate is where it goes wrong."},"interview":[{"q":"Why can't you use a SELECT alias in WHERE, but you can in ORDER BY?","a":"- **Logical order:** `FROM`/`JOIN`, `WHERE`, `GROUP BY`, `HAVING`, `SELECT`, `DISTINCT`, `ORDER BY`, `LIMIT`.\n- **So `WHERE` runs before the alias exists,** while `ORDER BY` runs after `SELECT` has computed it. The\n  same order explains why aggregates need `HAVING` rather than `WHERE`."},{"q":"A query with NOT IN suddenly returns zero rows after a data import. What happened?","a":"- **The subquery now yields a NULL.** `a NOT IN (b, NULL)` becomes `a <> b AND a <> NULL`, which is never\n  true under three-valued logic, so every row is dropped.\n- **Fix:** rewrite with `NOT EXISTS`, which compares row by row and ignores the NULL, or filter NULLs out of\n  the subquery explicitly."},{"q":"When would you split one big join into two queries?","a":"- **When the join multiplies rows.** One conversation × many messages × many tool calls can produce a huge\n  intermediate result shipped over the wire.\n- **Two targeted queries** (conversations with messages, then tool calls for those message ids) move less\n  data. Keep the count of queries constant, not proportional to rows, so it does not become N+1."},{"q":"Users report that page 200 of a list takes seconds while page 1 is instant. Diagnose and fix.","a":"- **Cause:** `LIMIT/OFFSET` reads and discards every earlier row, so cost grows linearly with depth.\n- **Fix:** keyset pagination on `(created_at, id)` with a matching index; every page is one index seek.\n- **Trade-off:** no jumping to an arbitrary page number, so confirm users scroll rather than jump."},{"q":"How would you find the queries worth optimizing in a production PostgreSQL?","a":"- **Use `pg_stat_statements` sorted by total time,** because a fast query called millions of times often\n  costs more than a slow rare one.\n- **Then read the plan** with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` on production-sized data, checking scan types and\n  estimated versus actual rows; a gap of 10× or more points to stale statistics."}],"tradeoffs":["**Declarative vs control.** The planner adapts to data growth, but you give up control of the algorithm; when it guesses wrong you fix statistics or indexes, not the query's \"steps\".","**One big query vs several.** One joined query minimizes round trips but can produce a huge intermediate result; when a join multiplies rows (one conversation × many messages × many tool calls), two targeted queries are cheaper.","**Index speed vs write cost.** Indexes make predicates cheap but each slows writes and uses memory; an hourly reporting query rarely justifies an index every insert pays for.","**ORM convenience vs visibility.** ORMs speed CRUD and keep types in sync but hide the SQL; once a query touches more than two tables or an aggregate, hand-written SQL is usually faster and clearer.","**Keyset vs OFFSET.** Keyset is constant-cost but cannot jump to \"page 37\"; OFFSET can, at linear cost, so pick by whether users actually jump or just scroll.","**CTE readability vs fences.** Since PostgreSQL 12 the planner can inline a CTE referenced once, so readability no longer forces materialization, but `MATERIALIZED` is there when you need the optimisation fence."],"pitfalls":["**The N+1 loop hidden behind an ORM relationship.** Symptom: hundreds of identical small queries in the log per request, each fast, together slow.","**Wrapping an indexed column in a function** (`lower(email) = $1`, `date(created_at) = $1`). Symptom: a sequential scan in `EXPLAIN` despite an index. Rewrite as a range or add an expression index.","**Mutable expression indexes.** The expression must be immutable, so on a `timestamptz` column index `((created_at AT TIME ZONE 'UTC')::date)`, not `date(created_at)`, which depends on the session time zone.","**`NOT IN (subquery)` where the subquery can return NULL.** Symptom: silently zero rows. Use `NOT EXISTS`.","**`SELECT *` in application code.** Symptom: a column added for a migration doubles network payload and breaks positional unpacking in a client.","**Deep `OFFSET` pagination.** Symptom: page one in milliseconds, page two hundred in seconds, database CPU climbing as users scroll.","**Building SQL with f-strings or template literals.** Symptom: a security finding, or a query that breaks on the first title containing an apostrophe.","**Trusting plans from a small dev database.** Symptom: instant in staging, a sequential scan over 50 million rows in production, because the planner's choice flips with size."],"when":"- **Use when:** the question is relational: filtering, joining, aggregating or ranking rows across entities.\n- **Write by hand:** anything that joins more than two tables, aggregates, or runs on a hot path; let the\n  ORM handle simple key lookups and inserts.\n- **Measure before shipping:** run `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` against production-sized data and confirm scan types\n  and row estimates.\n- **Measure after shipping:** watch `pg_stat_statements` for the queries with the highest total time, not\n  the highest mean.\n- **Interviews:** expect to write a query with a join, a `GROUP BY` and a window function, explain why a\n  predicate cannot use an index, and diagnose an N+1 from a description of symptoms.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the spend and inbox queries, replaces ORM loops with joins, and reads `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` to confirm each hot query uses the index it was designed for.","architect":"Decides which questions are answered by the transactional database versus a warehouse or search index, and sets the rule that hot-path SQL is reviewed with its plan.","fde":"Writes the one-off queries that prove value in a customer's data, and reads their slow-query logs to find the N+1 or missing index behind a complaint about latency."},"check":{"q":"A dashboard query filtering `messages` by `tenant_id` and the last 24 hours takes 8 seconds in\nproduction but 20 ms in staging. There is an index on `(tenant_id, created_at)`. What do you check and why?","a":"First run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` in production to see whether the index is used at all. If the\nplan shows a sequential scan, the predicate is probably not sargable (a function wrapped around\n`created_at`, or a type mismatch that makes PostgreSQL cast the column rather than the parameter, such as\nan `integer` column compared with a `numeric` parameter) or the statistics are stale after rapid growth,\nso `ANALYZE messages` and compare. If the\nindex is used but the query is still slow, look at actual versus estimated rows: one huge tenant can make\nthe range scan return millions of rows, in which case the aggregate itself is the cost and a pre-aggregated\ndaily table is the fix. Staging is fast simply because it is small; the planner's choice flips with data\nvolume, which is why plans must be checked against production-sized data."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can WHERE date(created_at) = '2026-10-20' not use a B-tree index on created_at?","options":["Date comparisons are never indexable in PostgreSQL","The index stores created_at values, not the result of date(), so the planner cannot seek on the function's output","The literal must be cast to timestamptz before the index can be used","B-tree indexes only support equality on integer columns"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The index is ordered by the raw column. Applying a function hides the column from the planner; rewrite as a range (`created_at >= '2026-10-20' AND created_at < '2026-10-21'`) or create an index on an immutable expression such as `((created_at AT TIME ZONE 'UTC')::date)`."},{"q":"An endpoint loads 500 conversations and then each conversation's last message via an ORM relationship. Which fix addresses the root cause?","options":["Add an index on messages.content to speed up each lookup","Increase the connection pool size so the 500 queries run in parallel","Replace the per-row lookups with one query that joins or uses a lateral subquery to fetch the latest message per conversation","Cache the conversation list in Redis so the loop runs less often"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The cost is 501 round trips, not slow individual queries. A single joined or lateral query lets the database do the work in one pass; more connections or a cache only hides the pattern."},{"q":"What is the main advantage of keyset pagination over LIMIT/OFFSET?","options":["It returns rows in a stable order even without an ORDER BY clause","It allows jumping directly to any page number","It seeks to the next page through the index, so deep pages cost the same as the first page","It avoids the need for an index on the sort column"],"answer":2,"explanation":"OFFSET reads and discards every preceding row, so cost grows with page depth. A keyset cursor uses the index to start exactly where the previous page ended; the trade is losing random page jumps."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/nosql","sources":["DeCandia et al., 2007 — Dynamo: Amazon's Highly Available Key-value Store","Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Official docs: Amazon DynamoDB","Official docs: MongoDB"],"why":"- **One machine has a ceiling.** A relational database answers any SQL question, but only within one\n  machine's disk, memory and write throughput. Past that you must partition.\n- **Partitioning breaks the casual operations.** Once rows live on different machines, a join is a network\n  operation and a transaction is a distributed commit: possible, but slow and fragile.\n- **Restrict the model, gain scale.** If every operation touches one partition identified by a key, each\n  machine serves its partition independently, nodes are added without coordination, and a failure affects\n  only its share of keys.\n- **Hand-sharding SQL is worse.** It pushes joins and transactions into application code where every\n  engineer must know the scheme, and keeps the schema rigid across nodes that cannot migrate atomically.\n  NoSQL makes the constraint explicit: declare a partition key; cross-partition work is unsupported or\n  clearly expensive.\n- **Some stores exist for the model, not scale.** Documents let items carry different fields and nest\n  read-together data (event payloads, tool outputs whose shape varies by type); graphs make multi-hop\n  traversals cheap that would be chains of self-joins. \"NoSQL\" is a bundle of answers, so first ask which\n  problem you have. The cost: unplanned queries are full scans or impossible, and \"schemaless\" means the\n  schema lives in your code, unenforced.","how":"### The families\n| Family | Examples | Model | Best at |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Key-value** | Redis, DynamoDB used simply, Riak | get and put a value by key | the simplest and fastest; no queries other than by key |\n| **Document** | MongoDB, Couchbase, DynamoDB with nested attributes | JSON-like documents with nested fields | indexing and querying fields inside documents, within a collection |\n| **Wide-column** | Cassandra, ScyllaDB, Bigtable | rows grouped by partition key, ordered by clustering columns | very high write rates and time-ordered reads within a key |\n| **Graph** | Neo4j | nodes and edges | traversals (\"friends of friends\") |\n\nSearch engines and vector databases are specialised stores that behave like document stores with a\ndifferent index at the core; they are covered in their own lessons.\n\n### Partitioning by key\n1. **Route by key.** The store hashes the partition key to pick a node (or a key range maps to a node).\n2. **One lookup at any size.** Every operation carrying the key goes to one node and costs one lookup\n   regardless of total data size, which is the whole point.\n3. **No key, fan out.** Operations without the key must hit every node, so ask: \"which key will every hot\n   query have in hand?\"\n\n| Store | Key that routes |\n|---|---|\n| DynamoDB | partition key plus an optional sort key |\n| Cassandra | partition key plus clustering columns |\n| MongoDB | shard key |\n\n### Replication and consistency\n- **N replicas per partition** for durability. Writes are acknowledged after W replicas accept; reads\n  consult R replicas.\n- **Strict quorum: W + R > N.** The read set must overlap the write set, so a read reaches at least one\n  replica holding the latest acknowledged write.\n- **Edge cases weaken it:** sloppy quorums that accept writes on stand-in nodes, concurrent writes resolved\n  by last-write-wins, and writes that failed on some replicas but were not rolled back.\n- **W = 1 is fastest** and allows a read a moment later to see the old value: **eventual consistency**.\n- **Per-operation choice.** Cassandra consistency levels and DynamoDB's `ConsistentRead`; strongly\n  consistent reads cost more and may fail when a replica is down.\n- **CAP in practice:** during a partition, a store either refuses some requests or serves possibly-stale data.\n\n### Modelling: access patterns first\nRelational design starts from entities and normalizes; NoSQL starts from the list of queries and shapes\ndata so each is a single-partition read. Two decisions dominate:\n\n1. **Embed or reference.**\n   - Embed child data when it is read together and bounded in size (a message with its tool calls).\n   - Reference by id when the child set is unbounded or shared (all messages of a conversation that may\n     run to thousands).\n   - Documents have hard size limits (16 MB in MongoDB, 400 KB per item in DynamoDB); an unbounded\n     embedded array will hit them.\n2. **Denormalize and duplicate.**\n   - With no joins, data needed by two queries is often stored twice in two key layouts, and the\n     application updates both.\n   - DynamoDB single-table design takes this to its conclusion: every entity type in one table with\n     composite keys like `TENANT#t1#USER#u9` / `SESSION#2026-10-20T10:00`.\n   - Global secondary indexes (GSIs) re-key the same items for a second access pattern. A GSI is\n     maintained asynchronously, so reads through it are eventually consistent.\n\n### Hot partitions\n- **Per-partition ceilings.** Capacity is configured per table, but each physical partition has its own\n  throughput ceiling (in DynamoDB, documented as roughly 3,000 read units and 1,000 write units per second\n  per partition).\n- **The failure.** If one tenant produces 40% of all writes and tenant id is the partition key, that\n  partition throttles while the rest of the cluster idles.\n- **Do not design around auto-healing.** DynamoDB's adaptive capacity and split-for-heat soften this for\n  some workloads, but are not something to rely on.\n- **Fix: a high-cardinality key.** Tenant + conversation, or tenant + a shard suffix, spreads a busy tenant\n  over several partitions, at the cost of fan-out reads for \"everything for this tenant\".\n\n### Why wide-column stores write so fast\nCassandra, ScyllaDB and Bigtable use a log-structured merge tree (LSM), not an update-in-place B-tree:\n\n1. A write is appended to a commit log and inserted into an in-memory sorted table.\n2. When that fills, it is flushed to disk as an immutable sorted file.\n3. Background compaction merges files and discards overwritten and deleted versions.\n\n- **Gain:** every write is sequential I/O with no read first, which is why ingest rates are high.\n- **Cost on reads:** a read may consult several files (bloom filters skip most), and compaction consumes\n  disk bandwidth in the background.\n- **Tombstones:** deletes are written as markers, so a partition with many deletes reads slower until\n  compaction removes them, a well-known trap with queue-like access patterns.\n\n### Transactions and indexes\n- **Transactions exist but are not the idiom.** Modern document stores support multi-document\n  transactions; DynamoDB offers transactional reads and writes across a bounded set of items (up to 100\n  per transaction at the time of writing). They cost more capacity and are limited in scope.\n- **Every secondary index is a copy** maintained on every write. In DynamoDB each GSI also consumes its own\n  write capacity and storage, so \"index everything\" shows up directly on the bill.","example":"### Agent sessions and messages in DynamoDB\nDesigned from two access patterns, \"list a user's recent sessions\" and \"load the last 20 messages of a\nsession\", each a single-partition `Query`.\n\n- **Messages are referenced, not embedded,** because a session can grow without bound.\n- **Zero-padded sequence in the sort key** makes lexical order chronological: `MSG#00000010` sorts after\n  `MSG#00000009`; unpadded, `MSG#10` would sort before `MSG#9`.\n- **The caller assigns `seq`** (for example from a counter on the session item).\n- **The conditional put makes collisions visible.** It distinguishes \"my own retry already landed\" from\n  \"another writer took this sequence number\", which must not be silently swallowed.\n\n```python\nimport boto3, time\nfrom boto3.dynamodb.conditions import Key\n\ntable = boto3.resource(\"dynamodb\").Table(\"agent-app\")\nDuplicate = table.meta.client.exceptions.ConditionalCheckFailedException\n\ndef create_session(tenant, user, session_id, title):\n    table.put_item(Item={\n        \"pk\": f\"TENANT#{tenant}#USER#{user}\",\n        \"sk\": f\"SESSION#{int(time.time()*1000):013d}#{session_id}\",\n        \"title\": title, \"session_id\": session_id,\n    })\n\ndef append_message(session_id, seq, role, content):\n    key = {\"pk\": f\"SESSION#{session_id}\", \"sk\": f\"MSG#{seq:08d}\"}\n    try:\n        table.put_item(Item={**key, \"role\": role, \"content\": content},\n                       ConditionExpression=\"attribute_not_exists(sk)\")  # never overwrite\n    except Duplicate:\n        existing = table.get_item(Key=key, ConsistentRead=True)[\"Item\"]\n        if existing[\"content\"] != content:    # someone else took this seq: caller must retry\n            raise                             # with the next one, or the message is lost\n        # else: our own earlier attempt landed; the retry is a no-op\n\ndef recent_sessions(tenant, user, limit=10):\n    r = table.query(\n        KeyConditionExpression=Key(\"pk\").eq(f\"TENANT#{tenant}#USER#{user}\")\n                               & Key(\"sk\").begins_with(\"SESSION#\"),\n        ScanIndexForward=False, Limit=limit)              # newest first\n    return r[\"Items\"]\n\ndef last_messages(session_id, limit=20):\n    r = table.query(\n        KeyConditionExpression=Key(\"pk\").eq(f\"SESSION#{session_id}\")\n                               & Key(\"sk\").begins_with(\"MSG#\"),\n        ScanIndexForward=False, Limit=limit,\n        ConsistentRead=True)                              # read-your-own-write after append\n    return list(reversed(r[\"Items\"]))\n```\n\nA query the design does not support, \"all messages mentioning refunds across all tenants\", would be a full\ntable scan; if it matters, the data also goes to a search index.\n\n### Runnable: why W + R > N gives fresh reads\nPure Python. It enumerates every write set and read set for N = 3, as if replicas were picked uniformly at\nrandom, and counts reads that miss every replica holding the write.\n\n```python\nfrom itertools import combinations\n\ndef stale_read_fraction(n, w, r):\n    \"\"\"Share of (write set, read set) pairs where the read misses every replica that took the write.\"\"\"\n    nodes = range(n)\n    pairs = [(ws, rs) for ws in combinations(nodes, w) for rs in combinations(nodes, r)]\n    stale = sum(1 for ws, rs in pairs if not set(ws) & set(rs))\n    return stale / len(pairs)\n\nfor w, r in [(1, 1), (1, 2), (2, 1), (2, 2), (3, 1)]:\n    print(f\"N=3 W={w} R={r}  W+R>N={w + r > 3}  stale reads: {stale_read_fraction(3, w, r):.0%}\")\n# N=3 W=1 R=1  W+R>N=False  stale reads: 67%\n# N=3 W=1 R=2  W+R>N=False  stale reads: 33%\n# N=3 W=2 R=1  W+R>N=False  stale reads: 33%\n# N=3 W=2 R=2  W+R>N=True  stale reads: 0%\n# N=3 W=3 R=1  W+R>N=True  stale reads: 0%\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: one tenant melts one partition\nA DynamoDB table takes 5,000 writes per second overall; one tenant produces 40% of them and `tenant_id`\nis the partition key. Ceiling figure from the per-partition limit above; arithmetic only, not a benchmark.\n\n| Key design | Writes/s on hottest partition | Against 1,000 write units/s ceiling |\n|---|---|---|\n| `tenant_id` | 2,000 | throttles about half the tenant's writes |\n| `tenant_id#shard` with 4 shards | ~500 per shard | fits, with headroom |\n| Cost of the fix | \"all runs for the tenant\" queries 4 partitions and merges | keep a summary item or GSI for tenant-wide views |\n\nRaising table capacity does not help, because the limit is per partition.","patterns":["**Find hot keys before they page you.** Enable CloudWatch Contributor Insights on DynamoDB tables to see the most-accessed and most-throttled keys, and alarm on `WriteThrottleEvents` and `ReadThrottleEvents`. When one key dominates, add a shard suffix derived from a hash of the item id.","**Conditional writes as the idempotency primitive.** Use `ConditionExpression=\"attribute_not_exists(sk)\"` for inserts and a version attribute check for updates, so retries cannot overwrite. Monitor the `ConditionalCheckFailedRequests` metric; a spike means competing writers or a retry storm.","**TTL for ephemeral agent state.** Put an expiry epoch-seconds attribute on session and cache items and enable DynamoDB TTL (or a MongoDB TTL index with `expireAfterSeconds`). Deletion is asynchronous, so filter expired items on read. Monitor table size trend.","**Validate shape at the edge.** Write a `schema_version` on every document and validate with a typed model on read and write; in MongoDB add a `$jsonSchema` collection validator. Monitor validation failures by version to know when old shapes can be retired.","**Stream changes to the stores that answer other questions.** Feed DynamoDB Streams or MongoDB change streams into search, analytics or the warehouse instead of scanning. Monitor consumer lag (iterator age)."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"Client request\"] --> K{\"Has partition key?\"}\n    K -->|\"yes\"| H[\"Hash key to partition\"]\n    K -->|\"no\"| SG[\"Scatter-gather to every node\"]\n    H --> RS[(\"Replica set of N nodes\")]\n    RS --> WQ[\"Write acked by W replicas\"]\n    RS --> RQ[\"Read consults R replicas\"]\n    WQ --> Q{\"W + R greater than N?\"}\n    RQ --> Q\n    Q -->|\"yes\"| F[\"Read sees latest acked write\"]\n    Q -->|\"no\"| E[\"Read may be stale\"]\n    SG --> SL[\"Cost grows with cluster size\"]","caption":"Every request either carries the partition key and goes to one replica set, or it fans out to every node;\nthe W and R settings decide whether the read is fresh."},"interview":[{"q":"What does a NoSQL store give up compared with PostgreSQL, and what does it buy?","a":"- **Gives up:** general joins, cross-record transactions and ad-hoc queries, plus database-enforced schema.\n- **Buys:** partitioning by key so each operation touches one node, which keeps latency flat at any size\n  and lets you add nodes without coordination.\n- **So** it fits narrow, known access patterns at scale, not evolving relational questions."},{"q":"Design the keys for storing agent conversations so you can list a user's sessions and page a session's messages.","a":"- **Sessions:** partition `TENANT#t#USER#u`, sort key `SESSION#<timestamp>#<id>`, queried newest first.\n- **Messages:** separate items, partition `SESSION#<id>`, sort key with a zero-padded sequence so lexical\n  order is chronological, because a session grows without bound.\n- **Writes:** conditional puts on `attribute_not_exists(sk)` so retries are safe and collisions visible."},{"q":"A user posts a message and sometimes does not see it on refresh. What is going on?","a":"- **Eventually consistent read.** The default read hit a replica that had not yet applied the write, or the\n  read went through a GSI, which is always asynchronous.\n- **Fix:** use a strongly consistent read on the base table for the read-after-write path only (it costs\n  more and can fail when a replica is down), or return the written item to the client directly."},{"q":"When would you advise against moving a product's data to DynamoDB or MongoDB?","a":"- **Small data, changing questions:** at tens of gigabytes with evolving reporting needs, one PostgreSQL\n  instance gives joins, constraints and aggregates for free.\n- **Better move:** keep PostgreSQL as the system of record and add a specialised store beside it for the\n  one access pattern that actually needs scale."},{"q":"Why do Cassandra-style stores ingest writes so fast, and what does it cost?","a":"- **LSM tree:** writes go to a commit log and an in-memory sorted table, then flush as immutable files,\n  so each write is sequential I/O with no read first.\n- **Costs:** reads may check several files (bloom filters help), compaction uses background disk bandwidth,\n  and deletes leave tombstones that slow reads on queue-like partitions until compaction."}],"tradeoffs":["**Horizontal scale vs query freedom.** Single-partition operations scale with no ceiling in practice, but any query lacking the partition key is a scatter-gather or a scan; the trade flips when ad-hoc questions dominate.","**Flexible schema vs enforcement.** Different shapes coexist and evolve without migrations, but nothing enforces them, so validation moves into code and old shapes linger in the data forever.","**Eventual consistency vs freshness.** Low latency and availability during failures, but a client that writes then reads may see stale data; pay for consistent reads where users would notice.","**Denormalized reads vs update fan-out.** Each read is one call, but every update must touch every copy, and a missed copy is a silent inconsistency with no foreign key to catch it.","**Managed simplicity vs agility.** DynamoDB removes operations work and gives predictable per-request cost, but a new access pattern can mean a new index or a data migration, not a new query.","**No object-relational mismatch vs no joins.** Documents suit nested, read-together data but lose the database's ability to join, constrain and aggregate across entities."],"pitfalls":["**Choosing NoSQL \"for scale\" at tens of gigabytes.** Symptom: a team rebuilding joins, aggregates and constraints in code for data one PostgreSQL instance would handle easily.","**Partitioning by tenant alone.** Symptom: throttling errors on one partition while overall cluster utilisation is low.","**Embedding an unbounded array** (all messages inside the session document). Symptom: writes slow as the conversation grows, then a hard document-size error.","**Expecting read-your-own-write on default eventually consistent reads.** Symptom: a user posts a message and intermittently does not see it on the next refresh.","**Scans or non-key queries on request paths.** Symptom: latency that rises with table size and a bill dominated by read capacity.","**Letting document shapes drift** (some records with `tokens`, some with `token_count`). Symptom: code full of defensive `get` calls and analytics that undercount.","**Treating secondary indexes as free.** Symptom: write cost multiplying with each index, and GSI reads that lag the base table."],"when":"- **Use when:** you can write down the handful of access patterns up front, each keys to a single\n  partition, and write rate, data volume or availability needs exceed one relational node comfortably.\n- **Typical AI fits:** per-session agent state and message logs keyed by session, high-volume event and\n  trace ingestion, feature or cache lookups by id.\n- **Avoid when:** the data fits one machine and the questions are still changing; stay relational and add\n  a specialised store (search, vector, key-value cache) beside PostgreSQL rather than replacing it.\n- **Measure:** partition heat and consistent-read usage once live.\n- **Interviews:** expect to design keys for a given set of queries and explain what happens to the query\n  you forgot.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Designs partition and sort keys from the query list, writes conditional puts for idempotency, and chooses consistent reads only on the paths where staleness would be visible.","architect":"Decides which data leaves the relational system of record, how copies stay in sync, and what consistency each read path promises to users and downstream services.","fde":"Reads the customer's existing DynamoDB or Mongo model, spots the hot partition or the unbounded document, and plans how conversation data will be exported for evaluation and compliance."},"check":{"q":"You are storing agent runs in DynamoDB with `tenant_id` as the partition key and `run_id` as the\nsort key. One enterprise tenant generates half of all traffic. What goes wrong and how do you fix it?","a":"All of that tenant's reads and writes land on one partition, which has a fixed throughput ceiling,\nso that tenant sees throttling while the table's total capacity is mostly unused. Raising provisioned\ncapacity does not help because the limit is per partition. The fix is to spread the tenant across\npartitions by adding high-cardinality data to the key, for example `tenant_id#user_id` or\n`tenant_id#shard` where shard is derived from the run id. The cost is that \"all runs for the tenant\" now\nrequires querying each shard and merging, so you keep a GSI or a separate summary item for tenant-wide\nviews, accepting that it is eventually consistent. The general lesson is that the key must distribute\nload, not just identify ownership."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a document store make joins across collections expensive or unsupported?","options":["JSON documents cannot contain foreign keys","Data is partitioned by key across nodes, so a join would require moving matching records between machines","Document stores do not support indexes on nested fields","Joins require a fixed schema that document stores lack"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Horizontal scaling relies on each operation touching one partition. A general join must combine records that live on different nodes, which is the network-heavy operation partitioning was designed to avoid."},{"q":"A session document embeds every message and writes slow down as conversations grow. What is the correct restructuring?","options":["Compress the message array before storing it","Switch the session document to a strongly consistent write","Store messages as separate items keyed by session id with an ordered sort key, and reference them from the session","Add a secondary index on the embedded message array"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Embedding suits bounded, read-together data. An unbounded array makes every append rewrite the whole document and eventually hits the size limit; separate items keyed by session keep appends constant-cost."},{"q":"With N = 3 replicas, which setting guarantees a read sees the latest acknowledged write?","options":["W = 1, R = 1, because the write is replicated eventually","W = 1, R = 2, because two readers will outvote the stale replica","W = 2, R = 2, because the write and read replica sets must overlap","Any setting, because replication is synchronous by default"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Overlap requires W + R > N. With W = 2 and R = 2 (a strict quorum), at least one replica consulted by the read holds the write. W = 1 with R = 2 can read the two replicas that both missed it."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/redis","sources":["Official docs: Redis","Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications"],"why":"- **Backends need shared, hot state.** The cached answer to an expensive call, requests a client made this\n  minute, who holds a lock, a logged-in user's session: shared across processes and read far more than written.\n- **The primary database is the wrong place.** Rows on disk with transaction logging and MVCC mean a\n  counter increment costs a write-ahead-log append and a row version; ten thousand increments a second\n  from a rate limiter compete with real transactions for CPU and I/O.\n- **Process memory is wrong with two processes.** Each has its own copy, and a load balancer sends\n  consecutive requests to different ones.\n- **A plain key-value cache is too thin.** Memcached offers only get and set, so \"add this item to the\n  user's sorted-by-time list and trim it to 100\" becomes a read-modify-write race between two servers.\n- **Redis makes those single atomic commands** on rich structures, in memory with sub-millisecond latency,\n  plus enough persistence, replication and clustering to trust it with more than a cache. In AI backends\n  it holds response caches keyed by prompt hash, per-tenant token-rate limits, semaphores capping\n  concurrent model calls, and the broker behind job queues.","how":"### Data structures, not just values\nEach key maps to one typed value:\n\n| Type | What it is | Typical use |\n|---|---|---|\n| **string** | bytes, also integers with `INCR` | counters, cached blobs |\n| **hash** | field-to-value map | an object |\n| **list** | push/pop at either end | a queue |\n| **set** / **sorted set** | members; sorted sets keep members ordered by a score | leaderboards, sliding windows, scheduled jobs |\n| **stream** | append-only log with consumer groups | the closest thing to Kafka inside Redis |\n\n- **Commands operate in place,** so \"push onto the list and trim it\" is two commands, not a round trip to\n  fetch, modify and write back.\n- **TTLs** delete a key after a set time; they are how caches and rate-limit windows expire without a\n  cleanup job.\n- **Pub/sub stores nothing.** It broadcasts to whoever is subscribed at that instant, so a subscriber\n  disconnected for a second misses that second. It suits live notifications (push a token stream to\n  whichever web node holds the user's socket), never work that must be done.\n\n### Single-threaded execution is the point\n- **Commands run one at a time on a single thread.** Newer versions use extra threads for network I/O,\n  but command execution stays serialized.\n- **So every command is atomic with no locking,** which is why `INCR`, `SET ... NX`, `ZADD` and friends\n  are the primitives of counters, locks and schedulers across many application servers.\n- **And one slow command stalls every client:** `KEYS *`, a huge `SMEMBERS`, a long Lua script.\n- **Multi-command atomicity** comes from `MULTI`/`EXEC` transactions and Lua scripts, which run as a single\n  unit on the server.\n\n### Persistence and what \"durable\" means here\n| Mode | How | Loss on crash |\n|---|---|---|\n| **RDB** | periodic snapshots, cheap | everything since the last snapshot |\n| **AOF** | appends every write to a log | set by its `fsync` policy: every write, every second, or left to the OS |\n\nNeither makes Redis a system of record. Treat its data as rebuildable, or at least acceptable to lose a\nsecond of, and put anything that must survive in a database.\n\n### Memory limits and eviction\n`maxmemory` caps the dataset; when reached, the eviction policy decides:\n\n| Policy | Behaviour | Fits |\n|---|---|---|\n| `noeviction` | writes fail | queues and lock stores, because evicting a job silently is worse than failing loudly |\n| `allkeys-lru` / `allkeys-lfu` | evict least recently / least frequently used of any key | caches |\n| `volatile-lru`, `volatile-ttl` | evict only keys with a TTL | mixed instances (with care) |\n| random variants | evict at random | rarely |\n\n- **LRU is approximated** by sampling a few keys rather than tracking exact order.\n- **Policy is instance-wide.** `maxmemory` and the policy cover every numbered logical database, which is\n  why mixing a cache and a queue in one instance is a design error.\n\n### Replication, Sentinel and Cluster\n- **Asynchronous replication.** A primary replicates to replicas; Sentinel monitors and promotes one on\n  failure. A failover can lose the last writes, which matters for locks and queues.\n- **Cluster sharding.** Keys hash into 16,384 slots spread across nodes. Multi-key commands work only when\n  all keys hash to the same slot, controlled with hash tags such as `{tenant42}:limit`.\n- **Managed offerings** (ElastiCache, Memorystore, Upstash, Redis Cloud) run these for you.\n- **Licensing.** Redis changed its licence in 2024 (to source-available terms) and the Linux Foundation\n  fork Valkey appeared; Redis 8 in 2025 added AGPLv3 as an option. Core commands match, newer features\n  diverge, so check which one your platform ships.\n\n### The patterns that matter in AI backends\n- **Cache-aside:** look up `cache:{model}:{sha256(prompt+params)}`; on miss, call the model and `SET` with\n  a TTL. Only safe when the call is deterministic enough for your use (temperature 0, or you accept a\n  stored sample).\n- **Fixed-window rate limiting:** `INCR` a per-tenant-per-window key and set its TTL on first increment.\n  Atomic, so correct across all servers, but a client can spend its whole limit in the last second of one\n  window and again in the first second of the next: up to 2× the limit in two seconds.\n- **Sliding-window rate limiting:** when that burst matters, keep a sorted set of request timestamps per\n  client (`ZREMRANGEBYSCORE` the expired ones, `ZCARD`, `ZADD`, in one script) for an exact window, at\n  one entry of memory per request; or weight the previous window's count to approximate one in constant memory.\n- **Concurrency semaphore:** cap concurrent calls to a model provider with a counter bounded by a Lua\n  script, so a burst does not trip the provider's limits.\n- **Queues and streams:** lists for simple job queues (`LPUSH`/`BRPOP`); streams with consumer groups for\n  acknowledged, replayable delivery. BullMQ, Celery and RQ build on these.\n\n### Locks, and why they need fencing\n1. **Acquire:** `SET lock:key token NX PX 30000` succeeds only if absent and expires if the holder dies.\n2. **Release by checking the token** in a Lua script, *because* a plain `DEL` from a holder whose lease\n   already expired would delete the next holder's lock.\n3. **Fence if correctness matters.** Expiry means a slow holder can lose the lock while still working. Issue\n   a number from `INCR` with each acquisition; the protected resource stores it and rejects writes carrying\n   an older one.\n4. **Redlock is disputed.** Acquiring on a majority of independent nodes addresses failover loss, but its\n   safety under clock drift and process pauses is contested (Kleppmann's critique is the usual reference).\n   Treat Redis locks as an efficiency mechanism, not a correctness guarantee, unless fenced.","example":"### Three primitives in redis-py\nAn LLM response cache, a fixed-window rate limiter made atomic with a Lua script, and a lock with a safe\nrelease.\n\n- **Why the script:** it runs server-side so the increment and the TTL cannot be separated by a crash. With\n  two client calls, a process dying after `INCR` and before `EXPIRE` leaves a key with no TTL, and that\n  tenant is rate-limited forever once it reaches the limit.\n- **Why hash every parameter:** two requests that differ only in temperature or system prompt must not\n  share an entry.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json, redis, time, uuid\nfrom contextlib import contextmanager\n\nr = redis.Redis(host=\"localhost\", port=6379, decode_responses=True)\n\ndef cached_completion(model, prompt, call, **params):\n    spec = json.dumps({\"prompt\": prompt, **params}, sort_keys=True)   # stable across dict order\n    key = f\"cache:{model}:\" + hashlib.sha256(spec.encode()).hexdigest()\n    hit = r.get(key)\n    if hit is not None:\n        return json.loads(hit)\n    out = call(model, prompt, **params)       # the expensive provider call\n    r.set(key, json.dumps(out), ex=3600)      # 1-hour TTL; eviction handles the rest\n    return out\n\nRATE_LUA = \"\"\"\nlocal n = redis.call('INCR', KEYS[1])\nif n == 1 then redis.call('EXPIRE', KEYS[1], ARGV[1]) end\nreturn n\n\"\"\"\nrate_script = r.register_script(RATE_LUA)\n\ndef allow(tenant, limit=60, window_s=60):\n    key = f\"rl:{tenant}:{int(time.time() // window_s)}\"   # e.g. rl:t42:29361600, one key per minute\n    return rate_script(keys=[key], args=[window_s]) <= limit   # 61st call this minute -> False\n\nRELEASE_LUA = \"\"\"\nif redis.call('GET', KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then return redis.call('DEL', KEYS[1]) end\nreturn 0\n\"\"\"\nrelease_script = r.register_script(RELEASE_LUA)\n\n@contextmanager\ndef with_lock(name, ttl_ms=30000):\n    token = str(uuid.uuid4())\n    if not r.set(f\"lock:{name}\", token, nx=True, px=ttl_ms):\n        raise RuntimeError(\"busy\")\n    try:\n        yield                                  # with with_lock(\"reindex:tenant42\"): ...\n    finally:\n        release_script(keys=[f\"lock:{name}\"], args=[token])   # only deletes if still ours\n```\n\n### Runnable: the fixed-window burst, simulated\nPure Python, no Redis needed. It replays 60 calls just before and 60 just after a minute boundary against\nboth limiter designs, with a limit of 60 per minute.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict, deque\n\nLIMIT, WINDOW = 60, 60.0                       # 60 requests per 60 s\nburst = [59.5] * 60 + [60.5] * 60              # 60 calls just before and just after the minute boundary\n\ndef fixed_window(times):\n    counts, allowed = defaultdict(int), 0      # mirrors INCR on rl:{tenant}:{t // window}\n    for t in times:\n        counts[int(t // WINDOW)] += 1\n        allowed += counts[int(t // WINDOW)] <= LIMIT\n    return allowed\n\ndef sliding_log(times):\n    log, allowed = deque(), 0                  # mirrors ZREMRANGEBYSCORE + ZCARD + ZADD\n    for t in times:\n        while log and log[0] <= t - WINDOW:\n            log.popleft()\n        if len(log) < LIMIT:\n            log.append(t); allowed += 1\n    return allowed\n\nprint(\"fixed window allowed:\", fixed_window(burst))   # 120 in one second: 2x the limit\nprint(\"sliding log allowed: \", sliding_log(burst))    # 60\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: one hot key saturates one node\nA 5 MB cached response (say, a large shared document summary) is read 1,000 times a second in a cluster.\n\n| Quantity | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Bytes served per second from one key | 5 MB × 1,000 = ~5 GB/s |\n| In network terms | ~40 Gbit/s, from the single node owning that slot |\n| Other nodes | idle, because the key lives in exactly one slot |\n| Fixes | shrink the value, cache it in process memory for a few seconds, or replicate it under several keys |\n\nThe arithmetic is illustrative, but the shape is common: one node's network saturates while the cluster\nlooks underused.","patterns":["**One instance per role.** Run the cache with `maxmemory-policy allkeys-lfu` (or `allkeys-lru`) and TTLs on every key; run queues and locks on a separate instance with `noeviction` and `appendonly yes` plus `appendfsync everysec`. Monitor `evicted_keys` and `used_memory` from `INFO`.","**Watch the slow log.** Set `slowlog-log-slower-than` (microseconds) and review `SLOWLOG GET` after incidents; deny `@dangerous` commands such as `KEYS` and `FLUSHALL` to application users with an ACL. Monitor command latency percentiles.","**Find big and hot keys.** Run `redis-cli --bigkeys` periodically and `MEMORY USAGE key` on suspects; `--hotkeys` works when an LFU policy is set. Monitor per-node network throughput in a cluster.","**Stampede protection on popular cache keys.** On a miss, take a short `SET ... NX PX` lock so one caller regenerates while others wait briefly or serve a stale copy, and add jitter to TTLs so keys do not expire together. Monitor hit ratio from `keyspace_hits` and `keyspace_misses`.","**Pool connections.** Create one `redis.ConnectionPool` (or client) per process at startup, with a bounded `max_connections`, rather than a client per request. Monitor `connected_clients` and handshake latency."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Incoming LLM request\"] --> RL[\"EVAL rate script: INCR plus EXPIRE\"]\n    RL --> O{\"Over limit?\"}\n    O -->|\"yes\"| X[\"Return 429\"]\n    O -->|\"no\"| G[\"GET cache key from hash of params\"]\n    G --> H{\"Hit?\"}\n    H -->|\"yes\"| A[\"Return cached answer\"]\n    H -->|\"no\"| L[\"SET lock NX PX\"]\n    L --> M[\"Call model provider\"]\n    M --> S[\"SET cache key with TTL\"]\n    S --> U[\"Release lock if token matches\"]\n    U --> A","caption":"One request path through Redis: an atomic rate-limit check first, then cache-aside with a short lock so\nonly one caller pays for the model call on a miss."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is Redis fast, and what is the catch of its single-threaded model?","a":"- **In-memory structures plus serialized execution:** no disk on the hot path and no locks, so each\n  command is atomic and sub-millisecond.\n- **The catch:** one slow command (`KEYS *`, a huge `SMEMBERS`, a long Lua script) blocks every client, so\n  use `SCAN`, bound collection sizes and keep scripts short."},{"q":"Design a per-tenant rate limiter for an LLM API running on 20 app servers.","a":"- **Shared counter in Redis,** because in-process counters split across servers. A Lua script does `INCR`\n  and sets `EXPIRE` on the first hit so a crash cannot leave a key without a TTL.\n- **Fixed window allows 2× bursts at boundaries;** if that matters, use a sorted-set sliding log in one\n  script, or a weighted two-window approximation in constant memory.\n- **Key by tenant and window,** and return 429 with a retry hint when over the limit."},{"q":"Two workers both wrote to the same index while holding a Redis lock. How?","a":"- **The lease expired while the first worker was still busy** (a GC pause or slow model call longer than\n  `PX`), so the second worker acquired it legitimately.\n- **Fix:** issue a fencing token from `INCR` on each acquisition and have the index reject writes carrying\n  an older token; release with a token-checking script so nobody deletes another holder's lock."},{"q":"When would you not put a queue in Redis?","a":"- **When losing a job is unacceptable.** Replication is asynchronous, so a failover can drop recent\n  writes, and an evicting policy can remove idle queue keys silently.\n- **Use** a durable broker or a database-backed queue instead; if you keep Redis, give the queue its own\n  instance with `noeviction` and AOF."},{"q":"How would you size and monitor a Redis cache for LLM responses?","a":"- **Size:** average response size × distinct cached prompts within the TTL, plus overhead; set\n  `maxmemory` below the instance's RAM and an `allkeys-lfu` or `allkeys-lru` policy.\n- **Monitor:** hit ratio, evicted keys, memory usage, slow-log entries and per-node network for hot keys.\n  A falling hit ratio with rising evictions means the cache is too small or the TTL too long."}],"tradeoffs":["**Speed vs RAM-sized data.** Sub-millisecond operations in memory, but the dataset must fit in RAM; when the working set exceeds memory, eviction or a move to a disk-based store is forced.","**Durability vs throughput.** AOF with fsync every write approaches database durability but cuts throughput; fsync every second is the usual compromise, accepting up to a second of loss on a crash.","**Atomic scripts vs blocking.** Lua scripts and `MULTI` give atomicity across commands, but a long script blocks every other client; flip to application-side logic when a script would touch thousands of keys.","**Cluster scale vs multi-key ops.** Cluster mode scales memory and throughput, but multi-key operations need hash tags and cross-slot transactions are unavailable, complicating single-node scripts.","**Expiring locks vs exclusivity.** They never deadlock after a crash but a slow holder can lose them; fencing tokens restore safety at the cost of making the protected resource check the token.","**Redis queue simplicity vs loss.** Simple and fast, but asynchronous replication and eviction can drop jobs; when loss is unacceptable, a durable broker or database-backed queue is the better trade."],"pitfalls":["**Running `KEYS *` or a huge `SMEMBERS` in production.** Symptom: every client stalls for seconds while the single thread walks the keyspace. Use `SCAN`.","**Caching without a TTL or sensible eviction policy.** Symptom: memory grows until `maxmemory`, then write failures (`noeviction`) or surprising evictions of non-cache keys.","**Sharing one instance between cache and queue.** Symptom: jobs vanish under memory pressure because `allkeys-lru` evicted them.","**Setting a lock without an expiry.** Symptom: a permanent \"busy\" after one worker crashes mid-task.","**Treating an expiring lock as exclusive without fencing.** Symptom: two workers write the same resource after a GC pause or slow model call exceeded the TTL.","**Hot keys and large values** (a 5 MB cached response read 1,000 times a second). Symptom: one node saturates its network while others idle.","**A new connection per request instead of pooling.** Symptom: connection-count spikes and latency dominated by handshakes.","**Cache stampede when a popular key expires.** Symptom: a burst of identical provider calls in the same second. Use a short lock or early refresh on the miss path."],"when":"- **Use when:** state is shared across processes, read-heavy or counter-shaped, and rebuildable or\n  tolerable to lose: caches, rate limits, semaphores, sessions, short-lived coordination, and simple job\n  queues where occasional loss is acceptable.\n- **Avoid when:** it would be the system of record for conversations, billing or anything you cannot\n  recompute; use PostgreSQL for that and put Redis in front.\n- **Check first:** confirm the database genuinely cannot carry the load (a rate limit on a few hundred\n  requests per second may be fine in PostgreSQL).\n- **Measure:** memory usage, evicted keys, hit ratio and slow-log entries.\n- **Interviews:** explain why single-threaded execution makes `INCR` a correct distributed counter, and\n  where an expiring lock is unsafe.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements cache-aside, rate limiting and semaphores with atomic commands or Lua, sets TTLs on every key, and pools connections in FastAPI or Node services.","architect":"Decides what may live only in Redis versus the database, the eviction policy and persistence mode per instance, and whether the queue needs a durable broker instead.","fde":"Checks a customer's managed Redis tier, memory ceiling and eviction policy before deploying a cache or limiter, and explains what is lost on failover of their setup."},"check":{"q":"A team uses one Redis instance with `allkeys-lru` for both the LLM response cache and the Celery\njob queue. Jobs occasionally disappear under load. Explain the mechanism and the fix.","a":"Under memory pressure `allkeys-lru` evicts keys that have not been touched recently, regardless of\nwhat they are. Queue libraries keep state in many keys besides the main queue list: Celery keeps\nunacknowledged and scheduled (ETA) messages and task results in separate keys, and libraries such as RQ\nand BullMQ store one hash per job. A job that has been waiting is untouched since it was enqueued, and\na queue for a rarely used task type may be idle as a whole. The cache fills memory with fresh entries,\neviction samples and removes idle keys, and some of those are queue state, so jobs vanish without an\nerror. The fix is separation into two instances: numbered logical databases do not help, because\n`maxmemory` and the eviction policy are instance-wide. The queue instance gets `noeviction` and AOF\npersistence, so running out of memory fails enqueues loudly instead of silently discarding work; the\ncache instance gets `allkeys-lru` and TTLs on every key. If loss is still unacceptable, move the queue to\na durable broker or a database-backed queue."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can a plain INCR in Redis be used as a correct rate-limit counter across many application servers?","options":["Redis replicates the increment to every application server's memory","Commands execute one at a time on a single thread, so each INCR is atomic without client-side locking","INCR acquires a row lock like a relational UPDATE","The client library retries INCR until the value is consistent"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Serial execution means no two increments interleave, so the returned count is exact. A memcached-style get-then-set would race between servers; INCR's atomicity is a property of Redis's execution model."},{"q":"A lock is acquired with SET key token NX PX 30000. What problem remains if the holder takes longer than 30 seconds?","options":["The lock becomes permanent and other workers block forever","The token is rotated automatically and the holder cannot release it","The key expires, another worker acquires the lock, and two holders can act on the resource at once","Redis returns an error to the original holder on its next command"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Expiry protects against crashed holders but not slow ones. Once the key is gone a second worker acquires it; only a fencing token checked by the protected resource makes this safe."},{"q":"Which persistence and eviction configuration is appropriate for a Redis instance used as a job queue?","options":["RDB snapshots every hour with allkeys-lru","No persistence with volatile-lru, since jobs are short-lived","AOF persistence with noeviction, so a full instance fails writes rather than discarding jobs","AOF persistence with allkeys-lru, so old jobs make room for new ones"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A queue must fail loudly, never drop work silently. noeviction makes memory exhaustion an error the producer sees, and AOF limits what a crash loses; LRU policies would evict idle waiting jobs."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels","sources":["Official docs: PostgreSQL","Berenson et al., 1995 — A Critique of ANSI SQL Isolation Levels","Ports and Grittner, 2012 — Serializable Snapshot Isolation in PostgreSQL"],"why":"- **Crashes split related writes.** Without transactions, a crash between \"insert the message\" and\n  \"increment the tenant's token counter\" leaves the two permanently inconsistent.\n- **Concurrent read-modify-writes lose updates.** Two requests that both read a balance, compute a new one\n  and write it back overwrite each other. Budget checks before an LLM call, a counter of concurrent runs,\n  \"create the conversation if it does not exist\" and \"claim the next job\" all run concurrently in any real service.\n- **The naive fixes fail.** A global application lock serializes everything and does not survive a second\n  server. Classic two-phase locking makes readers and writers block each other, so a long report query\n  stalls every insert.\n- **MVCC lets both proceed.** PostgreSQL keeps multiple versions of each row and gives each transaction a\n  consistent snapshot, so readers and writers run concurrently.\n- **Isolation levels say which anomalies remain.** That question is where most production data-corruption\n  bugs in otherwise careful code come from; understanding the levels is cheaper than debugging a tenant\n  whose token usage no longer adds up.","how":"### ACID in brief\n| Property | Meaning in PostgreSQL |\n|---|---|\n| **Atomic** | a transaction (`BEGIN` ... `COMMIT`) makes all its writes visible together, or none via rollback |\n| **Consistency** | constraints hold at commit |\n| **Isolation** | the subject of this lesson |\n| **Durability** | a committed transaction survives a crash, because the write-ahead log is fsynced before the commit is acknowledged |\n\n- **That fsync is the floor on commit latency,** which is why batching many small writes into one\n  transaction is faster than committing each.\n- **`synchronous_commit = off`** (per transaction if you like) acknowledges before the flush, risking the\n  last few commits on a crash, but never corruption.\n\n### MVCC: versions instead of locks\n1. **Versions carry ids.** Every row version records the transaction that created it (`xmin`) and, once\n   updated or deleted, the one that ended it (`xmax`).\n2. **Updates append.** An `UPDATE` writes a new version and marks the old one as ended.\n3. **Snapshots decide visibility.** A transaction's **snapshot** is the set of transaction ids committed\n   when it started looking; it sees exactly the versions committed in that snapshot. So a long `SELECT`\n   never blocks an `UPDATE` and vice versa.\n4. **`VACUUM` reclaims dead versions** once no snapshot can see them. Long-running or idle-in-transaction\n   sessions pin old snapshots and prevent cleanup, which causes table bloat.\n\n### The isolation levels in PostgreSQL\nPostgreSQL offers three distinct behaviours. READ UNCOMMITTED is accepted but behaves as READ COMMITTED,\nbecause MVCC never exposes uncommitted data.\n\n1. **READ COMMITTED (default).**\n   - Each *statement* takes a fresh snapshot, so two consecutive selects can differ if another\n     transaction committed in between (non-repeatable read).\n   - An `UPDATE` whose target row was changed by a concurrent committed transaction waits, then\n     re-evaluates its `WHERE` on the new version and proceeds.\n   - Fast and rarely aborts, but a read-modify-write in application code is not protected at all.\n2. **REPEATABLE READ.**\n   - The snapshot is taken at the first statement and held for the whole transaction, so repeated reads\n     agree and, in PostgreSQL, phantoms do not appear either.\n   - Updating a row another transaction modified and committed since the snapshot fails with serialization\n     error `40001`, and must be retried.\n   - Still permits **write skew**: two transactions each read a condition, both see it true, each writes a\n     *different* row, and the combined result violates the condition.\n3. **SERIALIZABLE.**\n   - Serializable snapshot isolation (SSI) tracks read/write dependencies between concurrent transactions\n     and aborts one on the \"dangerous structure\" (two consecutive read-write dependencies) that any\n     non-serializable cycle must contain.\n   - The check is conservative, so it sometimes aborts transactions that would have been fine.\n   - Result: as if transactions ran one at a time, with no explicit locking, at the cost of extra\n     bookkeeping and more `40001` aborts under contention. Any code using it must retry.\n\n### The anomalies, side by side\nPostgreSQL behaviour, which is stricter than the SQL standard's minimums:\n\n| Anomaly | What it is | READ COMMITTED | REPEATABLE READ | SERIALIZABLE |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Dirty read** | seeing another transaction's uncommitted write | impossible | impossible | impossible |\n| **Non-repeatable read, phantom** | a re-read returns changed values or new rows | possible | prevented | prevented |\n| **Lost update** | two read-modify-writes, one overwrites the other | possible | second writer gets `40001` | second writer gets `40001` |\n| **Write skew** | two transactions read an overlapping condition and write *different* rows | possible | possible | prevented |\n\n### The lost update, concretely\nTenant budget 1,000,000 tokens, 999,000 used, two requests costing 800 each:\n\n1. Both run `SELECT tokens_used` and read 999,000.\n2. Both compute 999,800 ≤ 1,000,000.\n3. Both run `UPDATE tokens_used = 999800`.\n4. Final value 999,800; real usage 1,000,600. The budget was exceeded without any error.\n\nUnder READ COMMITTED this is fully allowed. The second `UPDATE` does wait for the first's row lock and\nre-checks its `WHERE` on the new version, but its `SET` assigns the constant 999,800 computed from a stale\nread, so re-evaluation changes nothing. The fixes move the read inside the protected step:\n\n- **Atomic update:** `UPDATE tenants SET tokens_used = tokens_used + 800 WHERE id = $1 AND\n  tokens_used + 800 <= budget RETURNING tokens_used`. Check and write are one statement that waits for the\n  row lock, re-evaluates, and returns zero rows if the budget is now blown.\n- **Atomic update, cost by level:** cheapest, and needs no retry at READ COMMITTED; at REPEATABLE READ or\n  SERIALIZABLE the second statement gets `40001` instead of re-evaluating, so it is still safe but must be retried.\n- **Pessimistic lock:** `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` locks the row for the transaction, so the second request\n  waits until the first commits, then reads the new value. The lock lasts the whole transaction, so never\n  hold it across a network call.\n- **Optimistic:** read a `version` column, write with `WHERE version = $old`, and retry when zero rows\n  are affected; or run at SERIALIZABLE and retry on `40001`.\n\n### Row locks, SKIP LOCKED and deadlocks\n- **Row lock strengths:** `FOR UPDATE` and the weaker `FOR NO KEY UPDATE` / `FOR SHARE` lock individual rows.\n- **`FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`** returns only rows nobody else holds: the primitive behind database-backed job\n  queues, where many workers each claim a different row with one statement.\n- **`NOWAIT`** errors instead of waiting.\n- **Deadlocks:** when two transactions each hold a lock the other wants, PostgreSQL detects the cycle after\n  `deadlock_timeout` (one second by default) and aborts one with `40P01`. The usual cause is locking in\n  different orders; the fix is a consistent ordering (sort ids before locking).\n\n### Things that surprise people\n- **An error poisons the transaction.** PostgreSQL rejects every further statement until `ROLLBACK` (or\n  rollback to a `SAVEPOINT`). Catching the exception in code and continuing does not help.\n- **Transaction-mode poolers drop session state.** PgBouncer in transaction mode reuses server connections\n  between transactions, so session state such as `SET` and session-level advisory locks leaks or vanishes.\n- **Prepared statements and PgBouncer.** These were in the same list until PgBouncer 1.21 added\n  protocol-level support (`max_prepared_statements`); check your pooler version and setting.\n- **Isolation is per transaction:** `BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE`, or `SET TRANSACTION` as the\n  first statement; `SET default_transaction_isolation` changes the session default.","example":"### The budget race in psycopg (3.x)\n- **`reserve_atomic`** is the single-statement fix.\n- **`reserve_serializable`** shows SERIALIZABLE plus retry for logic that genuinely needs several\n  statements, such as checking a budget and then inserting the run that consumes it.\n\n```python\nimport time, psycopg\nfrom psycopg.errors import SerializationFailure\n\nDSN = \"postgresql://app@localhost/app\"\n\ndef reserve_atomic(conn, tenant_id, cost):\n    row = conn.execute(\n        \"\"\"UPDATE tenants SET tokens_used = tokens_used + %s\n           WHERE id = %s AND tokens_used + %s <= budget\n           RETURNING tokens_used\"\"\", (cost, tenant_id, cost)).fetchone()\n    return row is not None            # None means the budget would be exceeded\n\ndef reserve_serializable(conn, tenant_id, cost, run_id, attempts=5):\n    for attempt in range(attempts):\n        try:\n            with conn.transaction():          # call on an idle connection: SET must come first\n                conn.execute(\"SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE\")\n                used, budget = conn.execute(\n                    \"SELECT tokens_used, budget FROM tenants WHERE id = %s\",\n                    (tenant_id,)).fetchone()\n                if used + cost > budget:\n                    return False\n                conn.execute(\"UPDATE tenants SET tokens_used = %s WHERE id = %s\",\n                             (used + cost, tenant_id))\n                conn.execute(\"INSERT INTO runs (id, tenant_id, cost) VALUES (%s, %s, %s)\",\n                             (run_id, tenant_id, cost))   # unique id makes retries idempotent\n                return True\n        except SerializationFailure:      # SQLSTATE 40001: another txn won; retry\n            time.sleep(0.01 * 2 ** attempt)\n    raise RuntimeError(\"could not reserve budget after retries\")\n\nwith psycopg.connect(DSN) as conn:\n    ok = reserve_atomic(conn, tenant_id=42, cost=800)\n    conn.commit()\n    print(\"reserved\" if ok else \"over budget\")\n```\n\nRun two copies of either function concurrently against a tenant at 999,000 of 1,000,000 with a cost of\n800: each lets exactly one through.\n- **Atomic version:** the loser's `WHERE` is re-evaluated on the new row and fails.\n- **Serializable version:** the loser aborts, retries, and then sees the budget is exhausted.\n- **Naive select-then-update at READ COMMITTED** (the same body as `reserve_serializable` without the\n  isolation level) lets both through.\n\n### Production SQL: claiming jobs and finding stuck transactions\nA job claim with `SKIP LOCKED` (each worker gets a different row in one statement), and the query to find\nsessions that hold snapshots open.\n\n```sql\n-- Claim one due job; concurrent workers skip rows another worker has locked.\nWITH next AS (\n  SELECT id FROM jobs\n  WHERE  status = 'queued' AND run_at <= now()\n  ORDER  BY run_at\n  LIMIT  1\n  FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED\n)\nUPDATE jobs SET status = 'running', locked_at = now(), attempts = attempts + 1\nFROM   next\nWHERE  jobs.id = next.id\nRETURNING jobs.id, jobs.payload;\n\n-- Sessions idle inside a transaction: they pin old row versions and block VACUUM cleanup.\nSELECT pid, usename, now() - xact_start AS open_for, left(query, 60) AS last_query\nFROM   pg_stat_activity\nWHERE  state = 'idle in transaction'\nORDER  BY xact_start;\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: write skew on \"at most one active run\"\nRule: a tenant may have at most one active run. Both transactions run at REPEATABLE READ.\n\n| Time | Transaction A | Transaction B |\n|---|---|---|\n| t1 | `SELECT count(*)` of active runs: 0 | |\n| t2 | | `SELECT count(*)` of active runs: 0 |\n| t3 | `INSERT` run a1 | |\n| t4 | | `INSERT` run b1 |\n| t5 | `COMMIT` succeeds | `COMMIT` succeeds: 2 active runs |\n\nNeither updated a row the other touched, so REPEATABLE READ sees no conflict. At SERIALIZABLE, SSI sees\nthe read-write dependencies and aborts one with `40001`.","patterns":["**Timeouts as guard rails.** Set `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout` (for example a minute) so a leaked transaction is killed instead of pinning snapshots, `lock_timeout` for migrations and request paths, and `statement_timeout` per role. Monitor `pg_stat_activity` for `idle in transaction` and lock waits.","**Reserve, commit, call, settle.** Never wrap an LLM call in a transaction: reserve budget with an atomic `UPDATE` and commit, call the provider, then record actual usage in a second short transaction. Monitor transaction duration p99, which should not track provider latency.","**A shared retry wrapper.** One helper retries on `40001` and `40P01` with exponential backoff and jitter and a small attempt cap, and requires idempotent bodies (unique ids). Monitor retries per second and `deadlocks` in `pg_stat_database`.","**Consistent lock order.** Lock multiple rows with `SELECT ... WHERE id = ANY($1) ORDER BY id FOR UPDATE`, and turn on `log_lock_waits` so waits longer than `deadlock_timeout` are logged. Monitor the log for recurring lock-wait statements.","**Transaction-scoped advisory locks behind a pooler.** Under PgBouncer transaction mode, use `pg_advisory_xact_lock` (released at commit), never session-level advisory locks. Monitor lock wait time."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    W[\"Concurrent read-modify-write\"] --> S{\"Fits one statement?\"}\n    S -->|\"yes\"| A[\"Atomic conditional UPDATE\"]\n    S -->|\"no\"| J{\"Claiming queued work?\"}\n    J -->|\"yes\"| K[\"FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED\"]\n    J -->|\"no\"| R{\"One row, short transaction?\"}\n    R -->|\"yes\"| F[\"SELECT FOR UPDATE, then write\"]\n    R -->|\"no, multi-row invariant\"| Z[\"SERIALIZABLE plus retry on 40001\"]\n    A --> C[\"COMMIT before any LLM call\"]\n    K --> C\n    F --> C\n    Z --> C","caption":"A decision path for a concurrent write: pick the cheapest mechanism that moves the read inside the\nprotected step, and keep every transaction short."},"interview":[{"q":"How does MVCC let readers and writers avoid blocking each other in PostgreSQL?","a":"- **Updates create new versions** stamped with `xmin`/`xmax` instead of overwriting, so the old version\n  stays readable.\n- **Each transaction reads from a snapshot** of committed transaction ids, so a long `SELECT` sees a\n  stable view while `UPDATE`s proceed.\n- **The cost is cleanup:** `VACUUM` must reclaim dead versions, and old open snapshots prevent it."},{"q":"Two concurrent requests both pass a budget check and overspend. Walk through your fix.","a":"- **Diagnose:** it is a lost update; both read the same committed value at READ COMMITTED and wrote a\n  constant computed from a stale read.\n- **Fix:** one conditional `UPDATE ... SET used = used + cost WHERE used + cost <= budget RETURNING`, which\n  waits for the row lock and re-evaluates, so exactly one succeeds without retries.\n- **If several statements are needed,** use `FOR UPDATE` in a short transaction or SERIALIZABLE with a retry loop."},{"q":"When would you not use SERIALIZABLE everywhere?","a":"- **On hot rows,** because aborts rise with contention and a busy counter can thrash in retries.\n- **When callers cannot retry safely,** for example non-idempotent side effects inside the transaction.\n- **Prefer** atomic statements and explicit locks for known hot paths, and reserve SERIALIZABLE for\n  multi-row invariants that are hard to lock."},{"q":"Tables are bloating and queries slowing, but write volume is unchanged. What do you look for?","a":"- **Old open snapshots:** query `pg_stat_activity` for `idle in transaction` sessions and long\n  `xact_start` ages; they hold back the cleanup horizon for the whole database.\n- **Fix the source** (a transaction wrapped around an HTTP or LLM call, a forgotten commit) and set\n  `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout` as a backstop."},{"q":"What breaks when you put PgBouncer in transaction mode in front of an app?","a":"- **Session state:** `SET`, session-level advisory locks and anything assumed to persist between\n  transactions, because each transaction may get a different server connection.\n- **Prepared statements** worked only from PgBouncer 1.21 with `max_prepared_statements` set.\n- **Fix:** move settings into each transaction (`SET LOCAL`) and use `pg_advisory_xact_lock`."}],"tradeoffs":["**READ COMMITTED throughput vs races.** High throughput and almost no aborts, but every read-modify-write in code is a race; flip to a stricter level or explicit locks once money or quotas are involved.","**SERIALIZABLE safety vs aborts.** Correctness without reasoning about every race, but aborts rise with contention and every caller must retry; on a hot counter row it can thrash.","**`FOR UPDATE` simplicity vs blocking.** Never aborts, but blocks other writers for the length of the transaction, so a transaction that also calls an LLM holds the row for seconds.","**Optimistic versions vs contention.** Version columns avoid locks and work through a pooler, but under heavy contention most attempts fail and the retry loop becomes the bottleneck.","**Long consistent reads vs bloat.** Long transactions give reports a consistent view but pin old row versions, block `VACUUM` and cause bloat; flip to a replica or a snapshot export for long analytics.","**Transaction pooling vs session features.** It multiplies the clients one database can serve, but costs session features such as advisory locks and `SET`, which must move into the transaction."],"pitfalls":["**Select, compute, update in code under the default level.** Symptom: counters and balances drift from the sum of their events, with no errors in the logs.","**Holding a transaction across an LLM or HTTP call.** Symptom: lock waits and idle-in-transaction sessions that scale with provider latency, then pool exhaustion.","**Not retrying on `40001` at REPEATABLE READ or SERIALIZABLE.** Symptom: sporadic 500s under load that disappear when you retry by hand.","**Assuming REPEATABLE READ prevents write skew.** Symptom: a two-row invariant (for example \"at most one active run per tenant\") violated by concurrent transactions that each saw it satisfied.","**Catching an exception mid-transaction and continuing.** Symptom: `current transaction is aborted, commands ignored until end of transaction block` on the next statement.","**Locking rows in different orders in two code paths.** Symptom: intermittent `40P01` deadlocks roughly one second after the conflicting requests start.","**Expecting READ UNCOMMITTED to behave as in other databases.** In PostgreSQL it is READ COMMITTED, so code relying on dirty reads silently gets committed data only.","**Forgetting a transaction-mode pooler breaks `SET` and advisory locks across statements.** Symptom: settings that seem to apply randomly to other requests."],"when":"- **Default:** start at READ COMMITTED and make every write path safe by construction.\n- **Counters and quotas:** atomic single-statement updates.\n- **Exclusive multi-statement decisions:** `FOR UPDATE`, when the transaction stays short.\n- **Job claiming:** `SKIP LOCKED`.\n- **Multi-row invariants hard to lock explicitly:** SERIALIZABLE with retries.\n- **Always:** keep transactions short and never span a network call.\n- **Measure:** `pg_stat_activity` for idle-in-transaction sessions and lock waits, and serialization\n  failures per second.\n- **Interviews:** expect a read-modify-write and a question on which level or lock fixes it, an\n  explanation of write skew, and why a long transaction slows a table it never wrote to.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the atomic update or the `FOR UPDATE` block, adds the `40001` retry loop, and keeps the transaction boundary inside the request rather than around the model call.","architect":"Sets the policy: which invariants are enforced by the database versus the application, the default isolation level, and the rule that transactions never cross service or provider calls.","fde":"Diagnoses a customer's drifting usage counters or lock pileups from `pg_stat_activity`, and explains why their pooler configuration broke session-level settings."},"check":{"q":"A service enforces \"at most three concurrent runs per tenant\" by counting active runs in a\nREPEATABLE READ transaction, then inserting a new run if the count is below three. Under load, tenants\nend up with five active runs. Why, and what are two fixes?","a":"This is write skew. Each transaction's snapshot shows two active runs, each inserts a *new* row\nrather than updating a row the other touched, so neither conflicts with the other at REPEATABLE READ and\nboth commit, giving four or five runs. Fix one: run the transaction at SERIALIZABLE and retry on `40001`;\nSSI detects the read-write dependency between the count and the inserts and aborts one. Fix two: make\nthe conflict explicit by locking something shared, for example `SELECT ... FROM tenants WHERE id = $1\nFOR UPDATE` before counting, or a per-tenant `pg_advisory_xact_lock`, and drop to READ COMMITTED, so the\nsecond transaction waits and its count statement takes a fresh snapshot after the first commits. At\nREPEATABLE READ the lock alone is not enough: the snapshot was taken by the locking statement before it\nwaited, so the recount still cannot see the other transaction's insert. A third option is to keep an `active_runs` counter on the tenant row and use a\nsingle conditional `UPDATE ... WHERE active_runs < 3`, which turns the invariant into one atomic\nstatement and avoids retries entirely."},"quiz":[{"q":"Under PostgreSQL's default READ COMMITTED level, why can two transactions both pass a \"balance is sufficient\" check and overspend?","options":["READ COMMITTED allows reading uncommitted data from the other transaction","Each transaction reads the committed balance, computes a new value, and writes it; neither read locks the row, so both writes succeed and one update is lost","The database merges both updates by adding them together","READ COMMITTED serializes writes but not reads, so the second write is silently dropped"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A plain SELECT takes no lock and the UPDATE only waits for a concurrent writer of the same row. Both transactions read the same committed value, so the later write overwrites the earlier one."},{"q":"What does SERIALIZABLE in PostgreSQL require from application code that READ COMMITTED does not?","options":["Explicit FOR UPDATE locks on every row that will be modified","A single connection per transaction without pooling","A retry loop, because the database may abort a transaction with a serialization failure when a conflict is detected","Disabling MVCC so that writers block readers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"SSI resolves dangerous dependency cycles by aborting one transaction with SQLSTATE 40001. The outcome is correct only if the caller retries; code that treats the abort as a fatal error turns safety into 500s."},{"q":"Why does an idle session that opened a transaction an hour ago slow down tables it never touched?","options":["It holds an exclusive lock on the whole database","Its snapshot keeps old row versions alive, so VACUUM cannot reclaim dead tuples and tables bloat","It consumes all available connections in the pool","PostgreSQL pauses checkpoints while any transaction is open"],"answer":1,"explanation":"MVCC keeps a row version until no snapshot can see it. An old open snapshot (or an old assigned transaction id) holds back the cleanup horizon for the whole database, so dead versions accumulate in every table, indexes grow and scans read more pages; this is why idle-in-transaction is monitored."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/message-queues","sources":["Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Official docs: Apache Kafka","Official docs: Amazon SQS","Official docs: RabbitMQ"],"why":"- **Inline fan-out fails.** A request triggering five actions (store the upload, extract text, embed\n  chunks, update the index, notify the user) would take minutes, fail entirely on any one failure, and hit\n  every downstream at the burst rate.\n- **Synchronous calls couple three things.** Availability (embedder down, uploads fail), capacity (the\n  slowest service sets the pace) and deployment (a change in one means retries across all).\n- **\"Retry in a background thread\" is not a fix.** It loses the work when the process restarts and still\n  has no buffer when the downstream is slower than the upstream.\n- **A queue removes all three couplings.** Producers write and return, the broker stores durably, consumers\n  pull at their own rate, and messages wait while a consumer is down. It also gives fan-out (several\n  consumer groups read one event), load levelling (a burst becomes a backlog that drains), and a home for\n  retries and dead-letter handling.\n- **AI systems use it constantly,** for ingestion pipelines, batch embedding, buffering provider webhooks,\n  decoupling an agent's tool calls from their executors, and events (run started, tool failed, tokens\n  consumed) for observability and billing. The price: the system becomes asynchronous and eventually\n  consistent, and every consumer must tolerate seeing the same message twice.","how":"### The core loop\n1. A producer publishes a message to a named destination (queue, topic, exchange).\n2. The broker persists it and makes it available.\n3. A consumer receives it and does the work.\n4. The consumer **acknowledges** it; only then does the broker consider it done.\n5. If the consumer crashes before acknowledging, the broker redelivers to another consumer.\n\n| Ack timing | Guarantee | Failure mode |\n|---|---|---|\n| After the work | **at-least-once** | finishing then dying before the ack causes a duplicate |\n| Before the work | **at-most-once** | no duplicates, but a crash loses the message |\n\n- **\"Exactly-once\" across arbitrary side effects is not achievable.** Systems offer at-least-once plus\n  **idempotent** consumers (deduplicate by message id or business key): effectively-once from the outside.\n- **Kafka's \"exactly-once semantics\" is real but narrower.** Idempotent producers plus transactions make a\n  read-from-Kafka, write-to-Kafka pipeline commit outputs and consumer offsets atomically, so nothing\n  inside Kafka is duplicated.\n- **The guarantee ends at Kafka's edge.** A consumer that calls an email API or writes to a database is\n  back to at-least-once plus idempotency.\n\n### Publishing reliably\n- **The dual write.** A handler commits an order, then publishes `order.created`; a crash between the two\n  leaves an order with no event, ever.\n- **Publishing first is worse:** consumers act on an order the database then rolls back. Retrying the\n  publish does not help when the process is gone.\n- **The transactional outbox.** Write the event into an `outbox` table in the same database transaction as\n  the business change; a separate relay (a poller, or change-data capture reading the database log)\n  publishes outbox rows and marks them sent.\n- **The relay is at-least-once too.** It can crash after publishing and before marking, so consumers still\n  deduplicate.\n\n### Ordering, partitions and parallelism\n- **More workers, no order.** One queue consumed by one worker is ordered; add workers and messages\n  complete out of order.\n- **Kafka partitions.** A topic is split into N ordered logs; a message's key picks its partition (same\n  key, same partition, strict order); a **consumer group** assigns each partition to exactly one consumer.\n- **So parallelism is capped by partition count,** and a skewed key (one huge tenant) makes one partition hot.\n\n| Broker | Ordering |\n|---|---|\n| Kafka | strict within a partition |\n| SQS FIFO | the same idea, per message group id |\n| SQS standard | best-effort only |\n| RabbitMQ | FIFO as stored, but competing consumers and requeued redeliveries mean messages are not *processed* in order once there is more than one consumer or any failure |\n\n### Backpressure and retries\n- **Watch the lag.** A queue only levels load if someone watches **lag** (messages waiting, or the age of\n  the oldest message); an unbounded backlog is a silent outage.\n- **The arithmetic.** With arrivals λ per second and completions μ, the backlog grows at λ − μ while λ > μ\n  and drains at μ − λ afterwards.\n\n| Scenario | Drain time |\n|---|---|\n| 120,000 waiting, consumers 500/s, steady arrivals 400/s | 120,000 ÷ 100 = 1,200 s, twenty minutes in which every new message waits |\n| Consumers doubled to 1,000/s | 200 s |\n\n- **Autoscale on lag, not CPU.** That is why consumer autoscaling keys off lag or oldest-message age.\n- **λ above max μ is debt.** If arrivals exceed the maximum service rate for long, the queue is\n  accumulating debt, and the producer must be slowed or work shed.\n- **Bound in-flight messages:** RabbitMQ `prefetch`, SQS `MaxNumberOfMessages`, Kafka `max.poll.records`.\n- **Retry, then dead-letter.** Failed messages retry with backoff and after N attempts go to a\n  **dead-letter queue** (DLQ), so one poison message cannot block the rest.\n- **Claim-check large payloads.** A PDF or transcript should not travel through the broker: store it in\n  object storage and send the reference.\n\n### Kafka: a distributed, replayable log\n- **Retention, not deletion.** Messages stay on disk for a configured retention (time or size), whether\n  consumed or not.\n- **Consumers own offsets.** Each tracks and commits its **offset** per partition; replay means resetting it.\n- **Durability:** each partition is replicated to several brokers; producers use `acks=all` plus the\n  idempotent producer setting, which deduplicates retries within a session.\n- **Log compaction** keeps the latest message per key, turning a topic into a changelog.\n- **Fit:** high throughput, many consumers of one stream, event sourcing. **Cost:** an operations burden\n  (or a managed service) and rigid partition-based parallelism; a poor fit for a small job queue needing\n  per-job retry and priority.\n- **Share groups (KIP-932)** add queue-style consumption with per-message acknowledgement beyond the\n  partition count in recent releases; check its maturity on your version before relying on it.\n\n### SQS: a managed queue without replay\n- **Fully managed:** no brokers to run, near-unlimited throughput on standard queues, pay-per-request.\n- **Visibility timeout:** a received message is hidden from others until the consumer deletes it or the\n  timeout expires, when it reappears.\n- **Long polling** waits up to 20 seconds for messages instead of returning empty.\n- **No replay:** retention is bounded (days, not forever) and messages are deleted when acknowledged.\n- **Redrive policy** moves messages to a DLQ after `maxReceiveCount` attempts.\n- **Standard vs FIFO:** standard is at-least-once with best-effort ordering; FIFO adds ordering per message\n  group and a deduplication id honoured for a five-minute window.\n- **Size limits:** 256 KiB for most of SQS's history, raised to 1 MiB in 2025 (check current limits), so\n  large payloads go to S3.\n\n### RabbitMQ: a routing broker\n- **AMQP exchanges:** producers publish to an **exchange**; bindings route to **queues** by routing key\n  (direct), pattern (topic), to all bound queues (fanout), or by headers. Complex routing and\n  per-consumer queues are easy.\n- **Flow control:** consumers ack or nack; `prefetch` bounds in-flight messages per consumer; dead-letter\n  exchanges handle rejects and TTL expiry.\n- **Durability:** quorum queues replicate via Raft; the newer streams feature adds a Kafka-like replayable log.\n- **Fit:** task distribution with rich routing and moderate throughput; it needs operating like any\n  stateful system.\n\n### Choosing\n| Need | Choose |\n|---|---|\n| Replay, many independent consumers of the same events, very high throughput | Kafka (or a managed equivalent) |\n| Simple durable buffer, minimal operations, on AWS | SQS |\n| Routing rules, per-message priorities, AMQP interop | RabbitMQ |\n| Small job queue, already on PostgreSQL | a table with `SKIP LOCKED`; Redis-backed queues sit between that and a real broker |","example":"### An SQS consumer for an embedding job\n- **Long-polls** for up to five messages at a time.\n- **Processes idempotently:** the job id is the dedup key stored with the result.\n- **Heartbeats every message in the batch,** extending visibility while they wait and while the slow model\n  call runs, so none is redelivered mid-flight.\n- **Deletes only after success;** failures expire and retry, and the redrive policy sends repeat offenders\n  to a DLQ.\n\n```python\nimport json, threading, boto3\n\nsqs = boto3.client(\"sqs\")\nQUEUE = \"https://sqs.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/123456789012/embed-jobs\"\n\ndef extend_visibility(receipt, stop, every=30, extra=60):\n    while not stop.wait(every):                        # heartbeat while work is in flight\n        sqs.change_message_visibility(QueueUrl=QUEUE, ReceiptHandle=receipt,\n                                      VisibilityTimeout=extra)\n\ndef process(job):\n    if results.exists(job[\"job_id\"]):                  # idempotent: seen this job already\n        return\n    vectors = embed(load_chunks(job[\"document_id\"]))   # the slow call\n    results.store(job[\"job_id\"], vectors)              # write result keyed by job id\n\nwhile True:\n    resp = sqs.receive_message(QueueUrl=QUEUE, MaxNumberOfMessages=5,\n                               WaitTimeSeconds=20, VisibilityTimeout=60)\n    batch = resp.get(\"Messages\", [])\n    stops = [threading.Event() for _ in batch]          # heartbeat all, not just the current one\n    for msg, stop in zip(batch, stops):\n        threading.Thread(target=extend_visibility,\n                         args=(msg[\"ReceiptHandle\"], stop), daemon=True).start()\n    for msg, stop in zip(batch, stops):\n        try:\n            process(json.loads(msg[\"Body\"]))\n            sqs.delete_message(QueueUrl=QUEUE, ReceiptHandle=msg[\"ReceiptHandle\"])\n        except Exception as e:\n            log.warning(\"job failed, will be redelivered: %s\", e)   # no delete: retry later\n        finally:\n            stop.set()\n```\n\n- **App-supplied names:** `results`, `embed`, `load_chunks` and `log` are the application's own; the shape\n  of the loop is what matters.\n- **Why the batch-wide heartbeat:** without it, messages two to five would sit unprocessed while message\n  one ran, their 60-second timeout would lapse, and another worker would receive them too.\n- **Same loop elsewhere:** with Kafka, poll, process, then commit the offset; with RabbitMQ, set\n  `basic_qos(prefetch_count=5)`, `basic_consume`, and `basic_ack` after success.\n\n### Runnable: backlog drain and consumer sizing\nPure Python for the λ/μ arithmetic above; use it to set autoscaling targets.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef drain_seconds(backlog, arrival_per_s, service_per_s):\n    \"\"\"Time to clear a backlog while new messages keep arriving (λ = arrival, μ = service).\"\"\"\n    if service_per_s <= arrival_per_s:\n        return math.inf                      # λ ≥ μ: the queue is accumulating debt, not levelling load\n    return backlog / (service_per_s - arrival_per_s)\n\ndef consumers_needed(backlog, arrival_per_s, per_consumer_per_s, target_s):\n    \"\"\"Smallest consumer count that drains the backlog within target_s.\"\"\"\n    return math.ceil((backlog / target_s + arrival_per_s) / per_consumer_per_s)\n\nprint(drain_seconds(120_000, 400, 500))      # 1200.0 s, twenty minutes\nprint(drain_seconds(120_000, 400, 1_000))    # 200.0 s after doubling consumers\nprint(drain_seconds(120_000, 600, 500))      # inf: shed load or slow the producer\nprint(consumers_needed(120_000, 400, per_consumer_per_s=50, target_s=300))   # 16\n```\n\n### Production SQL: the transactional outbox\nThe business row and the event commit together; the relay publishes and marks rows, and several relays\ncan run safely because of `SKIP LOCKED`.\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE outbox (\n  id         uuid PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),\n  topic      text NOT NULL,\n  key        text NOT NULL,                 -- partition key, e.g. document id\n  payload    jsonb NOT NULL,\n  created_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),\n  sent_at    timestamptz\n);\n\n-- In the request handler: one transaction for the change and its event.\nBEGIN;\nINSERT INTO documents (id, tenant_id, status) VALUES ($1, $2, 'uploaded');\nINSERT INTO outbox (topic, key, payload)\nVALUES ('document.uploaded', $1, jsonb_build_object('document_id', $1, 'tenant_id', $2));\nCOMMIT;\n\n-- In the relay: lock a batch, publish each row to the broker, then mark them sent.\nBEGIN;\nSELECT id, topic, key, payload FROM outbox\nWHERE  sent_at IS NULL\nORDER  BY created_at\nLIMIT  100\nFOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED;\n-- ... publish every row; a crash here means they are published again later ...\nUPDATE outbox SET sent_at = now() WHERE id = ANY($1);   -- $1: ids of the rows just published\nCOMMIT;\n```","patterns":["**Autoscale consumers on age, not CPU.** On SQS scale from CloudWatch `ApproximateAgeOfOldestMessage` and `ApproximateNumberOfMessagesVisible`; on Kafka from consumer-group lag (shown by `kafka-consumer-groups.sh --describe`). KEDA has scalers for both on Kubernetes. Alert when oldest-message age exceeds the product's freshness promise.","**DLQ with an owner and a redrive path.** Set `maxReceiveCount` to a small number (for example 5), alarm on any DLQ depth above zero, and after fixing the bug move messages back to the source queue (SQS supports DLQ redrive). Monitor DLQ inflow rate by error type.","**Durable Kafka producers.** Set `acks=all` and `enable.idempotence=true`, use replication factor 3 with `min.insync.replicas=2`, so one broker loss neither loses nor blocks writes. Monitor under-replicated partitions and producer error rate.","**Outbox via change-data capture.** Instead of polling, stream the `outbox` table from the database log (for example Debezium's outbox event router) into the broker. Monitor relay lag and the age of the oldest unsent outbox row.","**Timeouts sized to real model latency.** Set the visibility timeout or ack deadline above p99 processing time and heartbeat for long calls; key every result by message or job id. Monitor redelivery rate (SQS `ApproximateReceiveCount` per message) as a timeout-too-short signal."],"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant A as API handler\n    participant DB as Database\n    participant R as Outbox relay\n    participant B as Broker\n    participant C as Consumer\n    A->>DB: COMMIT business row plus outbox row\n    R->>DB: read unsent outbox rows\n    R->>B: publish event\n    R->>DB: mark row sent\n    B->>C: deliver message\n    Note over C: skip if message id already processed\n    C->>C: do the work, store result by id\n    C->>B: ack or delete\n    B-->>C: redeliver if no ack before timeout","caption":"The full at-least-once path: the outbox closes the producer's dual-write gap, and the ack after the work\ncloses the consumer's, so the only remaining risk is a duplicate, which the idempotency check absorbs."},"interview":[{"q":"What does at-least-once delivery mean, and why can't a broker give you exactly-once?","a":"- **At-least-once:** the broker redelivers until it sees an ack after the work, so a crash between the work\n  and the ack produces a duplicate but never a loss.\n- **No exactly-once across side effects,** because the ack and an external call cannot be made atomic.\n  Kafka's transactions cover only Kafka-to-Kafka pipelines.\n- **So consumers are idempotent,** keyed by message id or business key."},{"q":"Your service writes to the database and then publishes an event. Sometimes the event never appears. Why, and how do you fix it?","a":"- **Dual write:** the process crashed or was redeployed between commit and publish, so nothing ever tried\n  to publish again.\n- **Fix:** a transactional outbox, writing the event row in the same transaction, with a relay or CDC\n  publishing it; consumers deduplicate because the relay is at-least-once too."},{"q":"Kafka or SQS for an LLM document-ingestion pipeline?","a":"- **SQS** if it is a job buffer: per-message retries, a DLQ, visibility timeouts and no brokers to run.\n- **Kafka** if several teams consume the same document events, need replay to rebuild an index, or the\n  throughput is very high.\n- **Neither** if volume is small and you already run PostgreSQL: a `SKIP LOCKED` jobs table is enough."},{"q":"One enterprise tenant makes a Kafka partition hot and its consumer lag grows. What do you do?","a":"- **Diagnose:** the partition key is the tenant id, so all its traffic lands on one partition and one consumer.\n- **Fix:** key by a finer entity (document or conversation id) when per-tenant order is not needed, or add\n  a sub-key suffix for that tenant; raise partitions to allow more consumers.\n- **Trade-off:** order is then preserved only per finer key."},{"q":"A backlog of 120,000 messages builds while arrivals are 400/s. How many consumers do you need?","a":"- **Arithmetic:** draining in 5 minutes needs 120,000 ÷ 300 = 400/s of extra capacity on top of the 400/s\n  arrivals, so 800/s total.\n- **At 50 messages per second per consumer** (an assumed figure), that is 16 consumers, and the\n  autoscaler should key on oldest-message age to get there automatically."}],"tradeoffs":["**Decoupling vs visibility.** Producers gain availability and burst tolerance, but the caller no longer knows when (or whether) work finished, so every flow needs a status path or an event back.","**At-least-once vs at-most-once.** At-least-once never loses acknowledged work but forces idempotent consumers; at-most-once is simpler and acceptable only for data you can drop, such as metrics samples.","**Kafka replay vs weight.** The retained log allows replay and many consumer groups, but operations cost, partition planning and no per-message retry make it heavy for a plain job queue.","**SQS simplicity vs history.** No broker operations and no capacity planning, but no replay, bounded retention and a small message size push payloads to S3 and audit history to a database.","**RabbitMQ routing vs scale.** Exchanges give fine-grained routing and priorities, but one very hot queue is harder to scale than Kafka partitions, and the cluster must be run and upgraded.","**Keyed ordering vs parallelism.** Partition keys preserve per-entity sequence, but parallelism is capped by partition count and a skewed key concentrates load; flip to unordered queues when order does not matter."],"pitfalls":["**Non-idempotent consumers.** Symptom: duplicate emails, double charges or two embeddings per chunk after any consumer restart or timeout.","**A visibility timeout or ack deadline shorter than the work** (a 90-second model call under a 30-second timeout). Symptom: the same job on two workers and redelivery counts climbing under load.","**No dead-letter queue.** Symptom: one malformed message redelivered forever, consuming a worker and hiding a growing backlog behind it.","**Committing the Kafka offset (or deleting the SQS message) before processing.** Symptom: work silently disappears whenever a worker crashes.","**Nobody alerting on lag or oldest-message age.** Symptom: a backlog discovered hours later by a user asking why their document never got indexed.","**Kafka for a few messages per second of job traffic.** Symptom: the team spends its time on partition rebalances and consumer-group tuning instead of the product.","**Commit to the database, then publish as a second step.** Symptom: rows with no downstream effect (never indexed, never notified) after a deploy or crash, and no error anywhere because the publish was never attempted.","**Assuming order on SQS standard or across Kafka partitions.** Symptom: \"updated\" events processed before \"created\" events and intermittent not-found errors.","**Multi-megabyte payloads through the broker.** Symptom: size errors, slow brokers, and a broker that has become an accidental blob store."],"when":"- **Use when:** work must outlive the request, a downstream is slower or less available than the caller,\n  several consumers need the same events, or bursts must be smoothed.\n- **Avoid when:** it is a synchronous read the user is waiting on, or a database table with `SKIP LOCKED`\n  would carry the load without a new system.\n- **Measure from day one:** lag, oldest-message age, redelivery rate and DLQ depth.\n- **Day to day:** \"should this endpoint enqueue or call directly\", choosing a partition key, and tuning\n  timeouts to the real duration of an LLM call.\n- **Interviews:** explain at-least-once, design a partition key for a tenant-skewed workload, and justify\n  Kafka versus SQS for a scenario.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes idempotent consumers, sets visibility or ack timeouts to match model latency, wires the DLQ and alerts on lag before the first production backlog.","architect":"Chooses the broker per workload (log versus queue), defines topics, keys and retention, and decides where the status of asynchronous work is recorded for callers.","fde":"Integrates with the customer's existing Kafka or SQS, negotiates message schemas and ordering guarantees, and sizes consumers against their throughput and quota limits."},"check":{"q":"A document pipeline on SQS standard occasionally embeds the same chunk twice and sometimes\nprocesses a document's \"deleted\" event before its \"created\" event. What is happening and how would you\nchange the design?","a":"Both are expected properties of a standard queue. Duplicate embeddings come from at-least-once\ndelivery: a worker finished the model call but crashed or exceeded its visibility timeout before deleting\nthe message, so it was redelivered. The fix is an idempotent consumer: key each result by chunk id or\njob id and skip or overwrite when it already exists, and set the visibility timeout above the real\nprocessing time with heartbeat extension for long calls. The ordering problem comes from standard queues\noffering only best-effort order across many workers. Either move to a FIFO queue with the document id as\nthe message group id, so events for one document are serialized while different documents proceed in\nparallel, or make the consumer order-independent by storing a version per document and ignoring events\nolder than the current state. Add a DLQ so poison messages do not block either path."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is \"exactly-once delivery\" not something a broker can promise for a consumer that calls an external API?","options":["Brokers lose messages under load, so at-least-once is the best achievable","The consumer can complete the external call and then fail before acknowledging, so the broker cannot know whether the side effect happened and must redeliver","External APIs do not support message acknowledgements","Exactly-once requires FIFO queues, which most brokers lack"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The acknowledgement and the side effect are separate operations that cannot be made atomic across systems. Redelivery is the safe default, so idempotency on the consumer side is what makes the outcome effectively once."},{"q":"A Kafka topic has 4 partitions and a consumer group has 8 consumers. What happens?","options":["Each partition is read by two consumers for redundancy","Kafka automatically increases the partition count to 8","Four consumers sit idle, because each partition is assigned to at most one consumer in a group","Messages are round-robined across all 8 consumers regardless of partition"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Parallelism within a classic consumer group is bounded by partition count. Extra consumers are standby only, which is why partition count must be planned for peak consumer parallelism (newer share groups are a separate mechanism with different rules)."},{"q":"Which situation makes SQS a better choice than Kafka?","options":["Multiple independent teams need to replay the same event history","A team needs a durable buffer for background jobs with minimal operations and no need to re-read past messages","Strict ordering of every message across the whole topic is required","Messages must be retained indefinitely as the system of record"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SQS offers a managed, pay-per-use queue with retries and dead-lettering but deletes messages on acknowledgement and bounds retention. Replay, long retention and many consumer groups are Kafka's strengths."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/background-jobs-durable-workers","sources":["Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Official docs: PostgreSQL (SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED)","Official docs: Celery","Official docs: Temporal"],"why":"- **Some work outlasts any client's patience.** Embedding a 300-page PDF, running a twenty-step agent,\n  sending ten thousand notifications, re-indexing a tenant.\n- **Inline fails three ways.** Load balancers and browsers cut idle connections (commonly around a minute\n  by default; check your own), so the client errors while the server keeps working. Slow work eats the web\n  tier's capacity, queueing fast requests. A failure at minute nine leaves partial side effects with no\n  record of progress.\n- **\"Fire and forget\" loses work silently.** A thread, an `asyncio` task or a framework background task\n  lives only in one process's memory; the next deploy, out-of-memory kill or scale-in destroys every\n  in-flight and waiting job, and nobody is told.\n- **It also has no controls.** No retry, no visibility, no backpressure (a burst of uploads becomes a burst\n  of threads), and no way to scale workers separately from the web tier. Teams find out when a customer\n  asks why their document never got indexed.\n- **Make the job a record, not a call.** Once committed: the job is not lost; at most one worker runs it at\n  a time; if that worker dies, it becomes runnable again. That is a message queue's at-least-once contract\n  from the worker's side, plus state the worker owns (attempts, next run time, progress, result). It is\n  what lets you ship five deploys a day without aborting agent runs in progress.","how":"### The job is a row\n| Field | Why it exists |\n|---|---|\n| id, type | identity, and which handler runs it |\n| payload | arguments, kept small; large inputs go to object storage by reference |\n| state: `queued`, `running`, `succeeded`, `failed`, `dead` | lifecycle, and the caller's status endpoint |\n| attempt counter | makes retry policy a function of the row, not worker memory |\n| `run_at` | delayed and scheduled jobs for free |\n| lease: `locked_by`, `lease_until` | what makes crash recovery possible |\n| error or result | the row itself is the audit trail |\n\n### Enqueue is a write, not a call\n- **Same database, same transaction.** If the job table lives with the business data, insert the job in\n  the *same transaction* as the change that caused it: the job exists if and only if the change committed.\n- **Separate queue, dual-write problem.** With Redis, SQS or a broker, \"commit, then enqueue\" loses the job\n  if the process dies between the two; \"enqueue, then commit\" runs a job for data that may have been rolled back.\n- **The general cure is the transactional outbox:** write the job to a table in the business transaction\n  and let a relay forward it to the external queue.\n\n### Claim with a lease, not a lock\n1. **Claim atomically.** Flip `queued` to `running`, set `lease_until = now + T` and `locked_by = me`. The\n   PostgreSQL idiom is one `UPDATE ... WHERE id = (SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED LIMIT 1) RETURNING *`.\n2. **`SKIP LOCKED` prevents serialization.** Without it N workers block on the same hot row; with it each\n   skips rows another worker is examining and takes the next.\n3. **A lease, not a transaction-long lock,** because a dead process's lock is held until its connection\n   drops, and a transaction open for a ten-minute job pins a database connection.\n4. **Expiry is recovery.** The claim also matches `running` rows whose `lease_until` is past, which is\n   exactly how a crashed worker's orphaned job gets picked up.\n\nSQS calls the same thing a visibility timeout; Redis-based libraries call it a lock with a duration the\nworker renews.\n\n### Heartbeats and the long-job dilemma\n| Lease length | Problem |\n|---|---|\n| Shorter than the job | a healthy worker loses its job to a colleague mid-run, and both run it |\n| Very long | recovery from a real crash waits that long |\n| **Short lease plus heartbeat** | the usual answer, paired with a hard maximum duration so an alive-but-stuck worker cannot hold a job forever |\n\n### Finish, retry, dead-letter\n- **Success:** set `succeeded` and store the result.\n- **Retryable errors** (timeouts, 429s, 5xxs from a model provider): set `queued` again with `run_at = now +\n  backoff(attempts)`, exponential with jitter, because a dependency recovering from an outage would\n  otherwise be hit by every retry at the same instant.\n- **Permanent errors** (validation, 4xx, a missing document): straight to `dead`; burning five retries only\n  delays the moment a human sees the problem.\n- **After `max_attempts`** the job is `dead` and stays inspectable: the dead-letter queue in table form.\n  Nothing is ever deleted on failure.\n\n### Idempotency is the price of durability\n- **The unavoidable race.** The worker finishes the side effect, then dies before marking the row done; the\n  lease expires and the job runs again. \"Call the external API\" and \"update the row\" cannot be atomic.\n- **Tolerate a second run:** upsert keyed by job id, pass an idempotency key to payment and email\n  providers, check the store before acting.\n- **Checkpoint multi-step jobs** (a step index, or a per-chunk `done` marker) so a retry *resumes*: a\n  40-minute job failing at minute 39 should cost one more minute, not forty.\n- **Taken to its conclusion,** recording every step and replaying on failure is what durable execution\n  engines such as Temporal do, and what agent runtimes need for long tool-calling loops; the lessons on\n  durable workflow execution and checkpointing go deeper.\n\n### Where the store lives\n| Store | Gains | Costs |\n|---|---|---|\n| PostgreSQL table with `SKIP LOCKED` | no new infrastructure, transactional enqueue, trivially inspectable | polling (idle workers cost queries); finished rows must be archived or the table bloats |\n| Redis-backed (Celery with Redis, RQ, BullMQ, Sidekiq) | blocking pops, low latency | Redis's weaker durability and the dual-write problem |\n| Broker-backed (Celery with RabbitMQ, SQS consumers) | the message-queue lesson from the consumer's chair | another system to run |\n\nChoose by what losing a job costs and by throughput; a few hundred jobs per second (illustrative) is well\nwithin a Postgres table on modest hardware.\n\n### Sizing, fairness and shutdown\n- **Little's law sizes workers:** jobs in progress = arrival rate × average duration. Uploads at 0.5 per\n  second with 4-minute jobs give 0.5 × 240 = 120 jobs running at steady state; fewer than 120 worker slots\n  means the backlog grows without bound, however fast each worker is.\n- **Size for peak** arrivals you must absorb within the latency target; the model provider's rate limit,\n  not your worker count, may be the real ceiling.\n- **Run-order queues are unfair.** One tenant enqueuing 10,000 jobs puts every other tenant behind them.\n  Fixes: a per-tenant concurrency cap in the claim (skip tenants at their limit), a claim that rotates\n  across tenants, or separate queues per priority class so interactive work never waits behind backfills.\n- **Deploys are the most frequent \"crash\".** On SIGTERM a worker stops claiming, finishes or checkpoints the\n  current job within the grace period, and exits. Anything unfinished is recovered by lease expiry, which is\n  why the lease, not the shutdown handler, is the correctness mechanism.\n\n### What to measure\n- **Queue depth and age of the oldest queued job** detect a stalled worker fleet.\n- **Run duration per type, attempts histogram and dead count** detect a failing dependency.","example":"### A PostgreSQL-backed worker (psycopg 3)\n- **The claim reclaims expired leases,** and the handler must be idempotent.\n- **Retryable failures** are rescheduled with jittered backoff; **permanent failures** go to `dead`\n  without burning attempts.\n- **Every finishing update is guarded by `locked_by`,** so a worker whose lease expired and was taken over\n  cannot overwrite the new holder's outcome.\n- **Heartbeating is omitted for brevity;** for jobs longer than the five-minute lease, extend\n  `lease_until` from a timer thread.\n\n```python\nimport random, time, uuid, psycopg\n\nWORKER = str(uuid.uuid4())\nCLAIM = \"\"\"\nUPDATE jobs SET state='running', locked_by=%s, attempts=attempts+1,\n                lease_until=now() + interval '5 minutes'\nWHERE id = (SELECT id FROM jobs\n            WHERE (state='queued' AND run_at <= now())\n               OR (state='running' AND lease_until < now()    -- orphaned by a dead worker\n                   AND attempts < 5)                          -- crash-loopers left for a sweeper\n            ORDER BY run_at LIMIT 1 FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED)\nRETURNING id, type, payload, attempts\"\"\"\nDONE = \" WHERE id=%s AND locked_by=%s\"     # fence: only the current lease holder may finish\n\ndef backoff(attempt):                      # 2, 4, 8 ... s, capped, with jitter\n    return min(2 ** attempt, 300) * (0.5 + random.random())\n\ndef embed_document(job_id, payload):       # idempotent: keyed writes, safe to re-run\n    for chunk in load_chunks(payload[\"document_id\"]):\n        if not vectors.exists(chunk.id):   # resume: skip chunks a previous attempt finished\n            vectors.upsert(chunk.id, embed(chunk.text))\n\nHANDLERS = {\"embed_document\": embed_document}\n\ndef run_one(conn):\n    with conn.transaction():\n        row = conn.execute(CLAIM, (WORKER,)).fetchone()\n    if row is None:\n        return False\n    job_id, kind, payload, attempts = row\n    try:\n        HANDLERS[kind](job_id, payload)\n        conn.execute(\"UPDATE jobs SET state='succeeded', finished_at=now()\" + DONE, (job_id, WORKER))\n    except RetryableError as e:\n        if attempts >= 5:\n            conn.execute(\"UPDATE jobs SET state='dead', error=%s\" + DONE, (str(e), job_id, WORKER))\n        else:\n            conn.execute(\"UPDATE jobs SET state='queued', error=%s, \"\n                         \"run_at=now() + %s * interval '1 second'\" + DONE,\n                         (str(e), backoff(attempts), job_id, WORKER))\n    except Exception as e:                 # permanent: straight to dead, keep it inspectable\n        conn.execute(\"UPDATE jobs SET state='dead', error=%s\" + DONE, (str(e), job_id, WORKER))\n    conn.commit()\n    return True\n\nwith psycopg.connect(DSN) as conn:\n    while True:\n        if not run_one(conn):\n            time.sleep(1)                  # idle poll; LISTEN/NOTIFY can wake workers instead\n```\n\n- **App-supplied names:** `load_chunks`, `embed`, `vectors`, `RetryableError` and `DSN` belong to the application.\n- **Keep the claim fast:** a partial index on `(run_at) WHERE state IN ('queued','running')` keeps it fast\n  as the table grows.\n- **Why the `attempts < 5` guard on orphan reclaim:** a job that kills its worker (an out-of-memory crash on\n  one huge document) never reaches the `except` branches. Without the guard it is reclaimed forever, and a\n  small periodic sweeper should move such rows to `dead`.\n\n### Runnable: lease expiry and the completion fence\nPure Python, in memory. Worker A stalls past its lease, B reclaims the job, and A's late completion is\nrejected, exactly what `DONE` does in SQL.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Job:\n    id: str\n    state: str = \"queued\"\n    locked_by: str = \"\"\n    lease_until: float = 0.0\n    attempts: int = 0\n\ndef claim(job, worker, now, lease_s=300):\n    orphaned = job.state == \"running\" and job.lease_until < now\n    if job.state == \"queued\" or orphaned:\n        job.state, job.locked_by, job.lease_until = \"running\", worker, now + lease_s\n        job.attempts += 1\n        return True\n    return False\n\ndef finish(job, worker):\n    if job.locked_by != worker:            # the fence: WHERE id=%s AND locked_by=%s\n        return False\n    job.state = \"succeeded\"\n    return True\n\njob = Job(\"embed-42\")\nprint(claim(job, \"A\", now=0))              # True: A holds the lease until t=300\nprint(claim(job, \"B\", now=100))            # False: lease still valid\nprint(claim(job, \"B\", now=301))            # True: A looks dead (GC pause, slow call), B reclaims\nprint(finish(job, \"A\"))                    # False: A's late completion is rejected\nprint(finish(job, \"B\"), job.attempts)      # True 2\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: sizing workers and protecting small tenants\nUploads average 0.5 per second with 4-minute embedding jobs; then one tenant bulk-imports 10,000 documents.\n\n| Question | Arithmetic | Answer |\n|---|---|---|\n| Worker slots at steady state | 0.5/s × 240 s | 120 jobs in progress; provision more than 120 slots |\n| Bulk import on a shared run-order queue, 120 slots | 10,000 × 240 s ÷ 120 | ~20,000 s (about 5.6 hours) before other tenants' jobs start |\n| Same import with a per-tenant cap of 20 slots | the tenant uses 20, others share 100 | other tenants keep near-normal latency; the import takes longer |\n\nThe cap trades the bulk tenant's completion time for everyone else's latency, which is usually the right\nproduct call; make it explicit in the tier's terms.","patterns":["**Partial index plus archival.** Create `CREATE INDEX jobs_claimable ON jobs (run_at) WHERE state IN ('queued','running')` and move finished rows older than a retention window to an archive table nightly, in batches. Monitor claim query latency and table size.","**Wake workers with LISTEN/NOTIFY.** Issue `NOTIFY` in the enqueue transaction (it is delivered only on commit) and have idle workers `LISTEN`, keeping a slow poll as a fallback for missed notifications. Monitor idle-poll query rate and pickup latency.","**Per-tenant concurrency cap in the claim.** Add a predicate that skips tenants whose `running` count is at their limit, or rotate the claim across tenants. Monitor oldest-queued-job age per tenant, not just globally.","**Late acks in Celery.** Set `acks_late=True` and `task_reject_on_worker_lost=True` so a crash redelivers instead of losing the task, `worker_prefetch_multiplier=1` for long jobs, and with Redis a broker `visibility_timeout` above the longest job. Monitor duplicate executions per task id.","**Graceful shutdown sized to the platform.** Handle SIGTERM by stopping claims and checkpointing, and set the platform grace period (for example Kubernetes `terminationGracePeriodSeconds`) above the checkpoint interval. Monitor jobs reclaimed by lease expiry after each deploy; it should be near zero."],"diagram":{"code":"stateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> queued: insert in business transaction\n    queued --> running: claim with SKIP LOCKED and lease\n    running --> running: heartbeat extends lease\n    running --> succeeded: handler done, fenced by locked_by\n    running --> queued: retryable error, run_at plus backoff\n    running --> dead: permanent error or max attempts\n    running --> queued: lease expired, worker presumed dead\n    succeeded --> [*]\n    dead --> [*]","caption":"The job's lifecycle as stored in the row: every arrow is one guarded `UPDATE`, and lease expiry is the\nonly way a crashed worker's job returns to the pool."},"interview":[{"q":"Why isn't FastAPI's BackgroundTasks (or a thread) enough for embedding uploads?","a":"- **The job lives only in process memory,** so a deploy, OOM kill or scale-in silently destroys it with no\n  record and no retry.\n- **No controls:** no backpressure, no status for the caller, and no way to scale workers apart from the\n  web tier. A durable job row fixes all of these."},{"q":"Why does SKIP LOCKED matter in a database-backed queue?","a":"- **Without it, N workers contend for the same oldest row** and wait on each other's locks, so the queue\n  is effectively single-threaded.\n- **With it, each worker skips rows another holds** and takes the next, so a single `UPDATE ... RETURNING`\n  claims a distinct job per worker in one statement."},{"q":"A job's `attempts` climbs to 5 with no errors logged. What happened?","a":"- **Lease expiry without failure:** the lease is shorter than the job, so other workers reclaim it while the\n  first is still healthy, or the job crashes its worker (OOM) and never reaches the error handler.\n- **Fix:** heartbeat the lease with a maximum duration, fence completions on `locked_by`, and have a sweeper\n  move crash-loopers to `dead` for inspection."},{"q":"When would you move from a Postgres jobs table to a broker or Temporal?","a":"- **Broker:** at thousands of jobs per second, many idle pollers, or fan-out to several consumer services.\n- **Durable execution engine:** when workflows are long, branching and multi-step, and you need resume\n  from the last step with full history.\n- **Stay on the table** while transactional enqueue and inspectability matter more than throughput."},{"q":"Uploads arrive at 0.5/s and each embedding takes 4 minutes. How many workers do you need?","a":"- **Little's law:** 0.5 × 240 s = 120 jobs in progress, so you need more than 120 concurrent slots or the\n  backlog grows forever.\n- **Then check the real ceiling:** the provider's rate limit may cap throughput below what the worker count\n  allows, and peak arrivals, not the average, set the target."}],"tradeoffs":["**Job table vs broker.** A table in the main database gains transactional enqueue and zero new infrastructure but pays polling and row churn; flip to Redis or a broker at (as an order of magnitude) thousands of jobs per second, or when hundreds of idle workers poll.","**Redis speed vs loss.** Redis-backed queues gain blocking pops and millisecond pickup but lose jobs if persistence is off or lagging; fine for cache warming, unacceptable for billing or customer-visible work.","**Long lease vs fast recovery.** A long lease simplifies the worker but delays crash recovery; a short lease with heartbeats recovers fast but needs a maximum-duration guard so a stuck worker cannot renew forever.","**Retry everything vs classify.** Retrying every failure is easy but hides permanent errors behind five slow attempts; classifying costs code and is worthwhile the moment a model provider returns 4xx.","**Restart vs checkpoint.** Restarting a failed multi-step job is simple but repeats side effects and wasted minutes; checkpointing adds state and pays off once jobs exceed a few minutes or several steps.","**Durable execution engine vs a loop.** An engine gains resume-from-step and full history but costs a runtime to operate and a model to learn; a table and a loop suffice until workflows get long and branching."],"pitfalls":["**In-process background tasks for work that matters.** Symptom: jobs vanish after every deploy, discovered weeks later by a customer rather than an alert.","**A non-idempotent handler.** Symptom: duplicate emails, double charges or two embeddings per chunk after a worker is OOM-killed or a lease expires.","**Enqueueing outside the business transaction.** Symptom: jobs failing on \"row not found\" because the insert rolled back, or committed changes with no job because the process died first.","**A lease shorter than the job, and no heartbeat.** Symptom: the same job id on two workers and `attempts` climbing with no recorded failure.","**No maximum attempts or dead state.** Symptom: one poison job retried forever, a worker permanently busy and a backlog growing behind it.","**One shared run-order queue for all tenants.** Symptom: one customer's bulk import turns every other customer's two-second job into a two-hour wait, with workers fully busy and no errors.","**Large payloads in the job row.** Symptom: a bloated table, slow claims and a database that has become a blob store.","**Never archiving finished rows, no partial index.** Symptom: a claim query that slows every week even though the backlog is small.","**Assuming the library acks after the work.** Some default to acknowledging before execution (Celery's `acks_late` is off by default); the symptom is lost jobs on worker crash despite a \"durable\" broker."],"when":"- **Use when:** work must survive the request: anything longer than a few seconds, any external side effect\n  that must happen exactly once from the user's view, anything needing retry, scheduling or a pollable status.\n- **Avoid when:** it is a fast read the user is waiting on; use in-process tasks only for best-effort work\n  you can lose, such as a metrics sample.\n- **Start simple:** a table in the database you already run; move to a broker or execution engine when\n  throughput, fan-out or workflow length demands it.\n- **Measure from day one:** oldest-job age and dead count.\n- **Day to day:** \"should this endpoint enqueue or call\", lease length against real LLM latency, and what is retryable.\n- **Interviews:** design the job table, explain `SKIP LOCKED` and leases, and justify idempotency.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the claim query, idempotent handlers and error classification, sizes leases to model latency, and adds the partial index and archival job before the table grows.","architect":"Decides where job state lives (database, Redis, broker, execution engine), the retry and dead-letter policy, and how callers learn the status of asynchronous work.","fde":"Fits the pipeline to the customer's deploy cadence and infrastructure, proves that a mid-run deploy loses nothing, and explains duplicate-safe design when their downstream systems are not idempotent."},"check":{"q":"Your API accepts document uploads that take two to twenty minutes to chunk and embed, and the\nteam deploys five times a day. Design the job pipeline so a deploy in the middle of an embedding never\nloses or duplicates work, and explain each mechanism's role.","a":"Make the job a row inserted in the same transaction as the upload record, so a job exists exactly\nwhen a document does. Workers claim with an atomic update using `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` that sets a\nlease; on deploy, workers receive SIGTERM, stop claiming and either finish or let the lease expire, after\nwhich a new worker reclaims the row, so nothing is lost. Guard every completion update with the lease\nholder's id so a pre-empted worker cannot overwrite the new holder's result. Duplication is handled inside the handler: each\nchunk's vector is upserted by chunk id and skipped if present, so a re-run after an expired lease resumes\nrather than repeats, and the twenty-minute job costs only the unfinished chunks. Heartbeat the lease\nwhile embedding so a slow but healthy worker is not pre-empted, with a maximum duration as a guard.\nClassify provider errors: 429 and 5xx reschedule with jittered backoff, 4xx go to `dead`. Alert on the age\nof the oldest queued job and the dead count."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a durable worker claim a job with an expiring lease instead of holding a row lock for the duration of the job?","options":["Row locks are not supported for UPDATE statements in most databases","A lease expires if the worker dies, letting another worker reclaim the job, whereas a lock would require keeping a transaction and connection open for the whole job","Leases allow several workers to run the same job in parallel for speed","Locks cannot be combined with SKIP LOCKED"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Crash recovery depends on the claim timing out. A transaction-long lock either pins a database connection for minutes or, if the worker dies, disappears without any record of what was in progress."},{"q":"A worker finishes sending an email through a provider, then crashes before marking the job succeeded. What happens next in a correctly built durable system?","options":["The job is marked failed because the worker's connection dropped","The broker detects the completed side effect and marks the job done","The lease expires, another worker re-runs the job, and the handler's idempotency key stops the provider from sending a second email","The job is lost, which is why email should be sent inline"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Side effect and acknowledgement cannot be made atomic, so re-running is the only safe default. The handler must therefore be idempotent, for example by passing a key the provider deduplicates on."},{"q":"Which enqueue strategy guarantees that a job exists if and only if the business change was committed?","options":["Push to Redis first, then commit the database transaction","Commit the transaction, then push to the queue inside a try/except","Insert the job row in the same database transaction as the business change, or write it to an outbox table relayed to the queue","Enqueue from a background thread after the response is sent"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Only a write in the same transaction is atomic with the business change. Any order of two separate writes can be interrupted between them, producing either an orphan job or a change with no job."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/authentication-vs-authorization","sources":["OWASP, 2021 — Top 10: A01 Broken Access Control and A07 Identification and Authentication Failures","OWASP Cheat Sheet Series — Authentication and Authorization Cheat Sheets","Hardt, 2012 — RFC 6749: The OAuth 2.0 Authorization Framework","Pang et al., 2019 — Zanzibar: Google's Consistent, Global Authorization System"],"why":"- **The second user forces it.** With one user, \"logged in\" and \"allowed\" are the same. With two, a request\n  for `/documents/42` from a logged-in user says nothing about whether document 42 is theirs; stop asking\n  and user A reads user B's data by editing a number in the URL.\n- **It is the top web risk.** A valid identity treated as sufficient permission is what OWASP ranks first in\n  its 2021 Top 10 (broken access control), and a staple finding of penetration tests.\n- **Folding them together fails.** \"Check at login, trust everything after\" ignores that the questions have\n  different inputs and change at different rates. Identity is established once per session; permission\n  depends on resource, action, tenant, current role and sometimes time of day, and must be re-evaluated on\n  every request. Folding also pushes authorization into the UI (hide the button), which non-UI clients ignore.\n- **Separation buys three things.** Several authentication methods (password, single sign-on, API key,\n  service identity) feed one authorization model; policy changes without touching login; and audit works,\n  because *who* did *what* needs an explicit principal.\n- **Agents sharpen it.** An agent calling tools acts *on behalf of* a user, so authentication must say which\n  user, and authorization must run per tool call against that user's permissions, not the agent's broad\n  service account.","how":"### Authentication: credential to principal\n- **A credential is evidence:** something the caller knows (password), has (hardware key, phone, signed\n  token), or is (biometric).\n- **The output is a principal,** a small object such as `{user_id, tenant_id, roles, auth_method,\n  auth_time}` attached to the request context.\n- **One chokepoint.** Everything downstream trusts that object and never re-examines the credential, so\n  this layer must be a single, well-tested chokepoint (middleware or a dependency), not code repeated per handler.\n\n### The common mechanisms\n| Mechanism | What is verified | Where state lives |\n|---|---|---|\n| Password | a slow, salted hash (bcrypt, scrypt or Argon2), *because* a leaked database must not be crackable offline at GPU speed | user table |\n| Browser **session** cookie | an opaque id | server-side store: revocation is instant, but every server must reach the store |\n| **Bearer token**, opaque | looked up like a session | server-side store |\n| **Bearer token**, **JWT** | verified locally with the issuer's public key | none, so it cannot be revoked before expiry |\n| **API key** | a hash of a long random string | key table |\n| Service-to-service | mutual TLS or platform identities | platform |\n| Single sign-on | delegated to an identity provider through OIDC or SAML (the OAuth/OIDC lesson covers the flows) | identity provider |\n\n### Session cookies done right\n- **`HttpOnly`:** script cannot read it, so an XSS bug cannot exfiltrate it.\n- **`Secure`:** never sent over plain HTTP.\n- **`SameSite=Lax` or `Strict`:** not sent on most cross-site requests, *because* browsers attach cookies\n  automatically. State-changing endpoints still need CSRF protection where `SameSite` alone does not cover the case.\n- **Fresh id at login.** Issue a new session id rather than upgrading the anonymous one, or an attacker who\n  planted a known id before login inherits the authenticated session (session fixation).\n- **A second factor** (TOTP, or a phishing-resistant WebAuthn key) raises the bar from \"knows the password\"\n  to \"also holds the device\", which matters because reused and phished passwords are the common way in.\n\n### JWT verification: four checks\n1. **Signature,** with the accepted algorithm pinned rather than trusting the token's own `alg` header,\n   which is how `none`-algorithm and key-confusion attacks get in.\n2. **Issuer.**\n3. **Audience,** the one people skip; skipping it means a token minted for another service is accepted by yours.\n4. **Expiry.** Because a JWT cannot be revoked before it expires without server state, access tokens are\n   short-lived and paired with a refresh token.\n\n### API keys\n- **Store only a hash.** Unlike passwords they are long random strings, so a fast hash such as SHA-256 or an\n  HMAC is enough; a slow password hash would add latency to every request for no gain.\n- **Compare in constant time.**\n- **Give keys a recognisable prefix** so secret scanners can spot a leaked key and you can tell which key it\n  is without exposing the secret.\n\n### Authorization: (principal, action, resource) to allow or deny\nAuthorization is a function of three inputs plus context, evaluated with **default deny**. The simplest\nform is an ownership check, `resource.owner_id == principal.user_id`, and its absence is the bug above.\n\n| Family | Decides by | Fits |\n|---|---|---|\n| **RBAC** | principals map to roles, roles to permissions | what most products start with |\n| **ABAC** | policies over attributes of principal, resource and environment (\"analysts may read documents whose classification is at most internal, from the corporate network\") | attribute-driven rules |\n| **ReBAC** | relationships (\"user is an editor of this document via membership of this group\"), the Zanzibar model behind systems such as OpenFGA | deeply shared, hierarchical data |\n\n### Collections need the same care\n- **Single resource:** `GET /documents/42` is authorized by loading 42 and checking it.\n- **Collection:** `GET /documents?q=refund` must put the permission into the query itself\n  (`WHERE tenant_id = $1 AND (owner_id = $2 OR ...)`).\n- **Because filtering afterwards** returns short or empty pages, leaks totals through counts, and on a\n  vector search lets disallowed chunks crowd out allowed ones in the top-k.\n\n### Placement rules matter more than the model\n1. **Enforce on the server, close to the data, on every request.** The UI hiding a button is not\n   authorization. In multi-tenant systems every row carries a `tenant_id`, every query filters by it, and\n   database row-level security backstops the query someone forgets.\n2. **Separate enforcement from decision.** The **enforcement point** (handler or middleware) asks; the\n   **decision point** (Casbin, OPA, Cedar, OpenFGA, or your own module) answers, so policy is written once\n   and testable in isolation.\n\n### Scopes are not permissions\n- **A scope** bounds what a *client application* was allowed to request on the user's behalf.\n- **A permission** is what the *user* actually holds.\n- **Effective right = intersection:** a `documents:write` scope on a token for a user who can only read\n  grants nothing.\n\n### Order inside a request\n1. **Authenticate first, in middleware.** Invalid or missing credential: 401 with a `WWW-Authenticate`\n   header saying how to authenticate.\n2. **Then load the resource and authorize in the handler.** Denied: 403, or 404 when revealing existence\n   would itself leak (a document id belonging to another tenant).\n3. **Never reverse the order:** an authorization check cannot run without a principal, and an\n   unauthenticated caller must not learn which ids exist.\n\n### Agents are principals too\n- **A new principal acts for an old one.** The agent (or the tool server) acts for the user.\n- **Confused deputy.** If the runtime calls tools with its own powerful credentials and relies on the prompt\n  to stay within the user's permissions, the model can be talked into fetching what the user may not see.\n- **Fix:** carry the user's identity into every tool call (token exchange, or passing the principal to the\n  tool) and let the tool's authorization layer decide, which also makes retrieval permission-aware.\n- **Approval gates** for consequential actions are authorization decisions made at a different time, by a\n  different decider.","example":"### A FastAPI endpoint with the two concerns in two places\n- **`current_principal` is the authentication chokepoint.** It verifies signature, issuer, audience and\n  expiry of a JWT via the identity provider's published keys (PyJWT).\n- **`require` closes a gap:** PyJWT checks `exp` only if the claim is present, so `require` makes its absence\n  an error too.\n- **`authorize` is the decision function:** tenant match first (returning 404 so other tenants' ids are not\n  discoverable), then ownership or an explicit permission.\n\n```python\nimport jwt                                   # PyJWT\nfrom fastapi import Depends, FastAPI, HTTPException, Request\n\napp = FastAPI()\nJWKS = jwt.PyJWKClient(\"https://idp.example.com/.well-known/jwks.json\")\n\nclass Principal:\n    def __init__(self, claims):\n        self.user_id = claims[\"sub\"]\n        self.tenant_id = claims[\"tenant\"]\n        self.permissions = set(claims.get(\"permissions\", []))\n\ndef current_principal(request: Request) -> Principal:        # AUTHENTICATION\n    auth = request.headers.get(\"authorization\", \"\")\n    if not auth.startswith(\"Bearer \"):\n        raise HTTPException(401, headers={\"WWW-Authenticate\": \"Bearer\"})\n    token = auth.removeprefix(\"Bearer \")\n    try:\n        key = JWKS.get_signing_key_from_jwt(token).key\n        claims = jwt.decode(token, key, algorithms=[\"RS256\"],     # pinned, never from the header\n                            issuer=\"https://idp.example.com\", audience=\"docs-api\",\n                            options={\"require\": [\"exp\", \"sub\", \"tenant\"]})\n    except jwt.PyJWTError:                                   # bad signature, issuer, audience or expiry\n        raise HTTPException(401, headers={\"WWW-Authenticate\": \"Bearer\"})\n    return Principal(claims)\n\ndef authorize(p: Principal, action: str, doc) -> None:       # AUTHORIZATION, default deny\n    if doc.tenant_id != p.tenant_id:\n        raise HTTPException(404)                             # do not reveal cross-tenant existence\n    if doc.owner_id == p.user_id or f\"documents:{action}\" in p.permissions:\n        return\n    raise HTTPException(403)\n\n@app.delete(\"/documents/{doc_id}\")\ndef delete_document(doc_id: int, p: Principal = Depends(current_principal)):\n    doc = db.get_document(doc_id)\n    if doc is None:\n        raise HTTPException(404)\n    authorize(p, \"delete\", doc)\n    db.delete_document(doc_id, actor=p.user_id)              # audit records who, not just what\n    return {\"deleted\": doc_id}\n```\n\n- **`db` is the application's data layer.**\n- **Note what is *not* here:** no permission logic in the token parsing, no identity logic in the handler,\n  and the tenant check runs before any content-dependent decision.\n\n### Runnable: issuing and checking API keys\nStandard library only. The key is shown once, stored as a SHA-256 hash with a public key id and the\ntenant it is bound to, and checked in constant time.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, hmac, secrets\n\nSTORE = {}                                   # key_id -> record; in production, a database table\n\ndef issue_api_key(tenant_id):\n    key_id = secrets.token_hex(4)            # public part: lets you find and name the key safely\n    plaintext = f\"docs_{key_id}_{secrets.token_urlsafe(32)}\"   # prefix helps secret scanners\n    STORE[key_id] = {\"tenant_id\": tenant_id,\n                     \"sha256\": hashlib.sha256(plaintext.encode()).hexdigest()}\n    return plaintext                         # shown to the customer once, never stored\n\ndef authenticate_api_key(presented):\n    try:\n        prefix, key_id, _ = presented.split(\"_\", 2)\n    except ValueError:\n        return None\n    record = STORE.get(key_id)\n    if prefix != \"docs\" or record is None:\n        return None\n    digest = hashlib.sha256(presented.encode()).hexdigest()\n    if not hmac.compare_digest(digest, record[\"sha256\"]):   # constant-time comparison\n        return None\n    return {\"tenant_id\": record[\"tenant_id\"], \"auth_method\": \"api_key\", \"key_id\": key_id}\n\nkey = issue_api_key(\"tenant-a\")\nprint(authenticate_api_key(key))             # principal bound to tenant-a\nprint(authenticate_api_key(key[:-1] + (\"x\" if key[-1] != \"x\" else \"y\")))   # None: tampered secret\nprint(authenticate_api_key(\"garbage\"))       # None: wrong format\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: which status for which request\nThe `DELETE /documents/{doc_id}` endpoint above, called by different principals.\n\n| Request | Gate that decides | Response |\n|---|---|---|\n| No `Authorization` header | authentication | 401 with `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer` |\n| Valid signature, audience is another service | authentication | 401 |\n| Valid token, document belongs to another tenant | authorization (tenant check) | 404 |\n| Same tenant, not the owner, no `documents:delete` permission | authorization | 403 |\n| Same tenant, owner of the document | authorization | 200, audit log records the user as actor |","patterns":["**Authenticate by default, opt out explicitly.** Attach the principal dependency to the whole app or router (FastAPI supports app- and router-level `dependencies=[Depends(current_principal)]`) and mark the few public routes deliberately. Monitor 401 rate per endpoint and alert on sudden changes.","**A cross-tenant test for every route.** Seed two tenants in integration tests and assert that every endpoint returns 404 or 403 for the other tenant's ids, including list and search endpoints. Monitor 403 and 404 spikes per principal in production as an enumeration signal.","**Database backstop for tenancy.** Set the tenant per transaction (for example `SET LOCAL app.tenant_id`) and enforce PostgreSQL row-level security policies on tenant tables, so a forgotten `WHERE` returns nothing. Monitor policy-denied queries in tests and logs.","**Short tokens, fast deprovisioning.** Keep access tokens to minutes, rotate refresh tokens on use, and revoke sessions when the identity provider deprovisions a user (for example via SCIM). Monitor active sessions or tokens belonging to disabled users; the target is zero.","**User-scoped tool calls for agents.** Exchange the user's token for a narrowly scoped downstream token (OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange, RFC 8693) or pass the principal to each tool, and log the user as actor. Monitor tool calls whose actor is the agent's own service account."],"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant C as Client\n    participant M as Auth middleware\n    participant H as Handler\n    participant P as Policy decision\n    C->>M: request with Bearer token\n    Note over M: verify signature, issuer, audience, expiry\n    M-->>C: 401 plus WWW-Authenticate if invalid\n    M->>H: principal with user and tenant\n    H->>H: load resource by id\n    H->>P: principal, action, resource\n    P-->>H: allow or deny, default deny\n    H-->>C: 404 if other tenant, 403 if denied\n    H-->>C: 200 with result if allowed","caption":"One request, two separate gates: the middleware turns a token into a principal or returns 401, and only\nthen does the handler load the resource and ask the policy for allow, 403 or 404."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between authentication and authorization, and why keep them separate?","a":"- **Authentication** turns a credential into a principal (who); **authorization** decides whether that\n  principal may do an action on a resource (may they).\n- **Separate because they change at different rates:** identity once per session, permission per request,\n  resource and role. Separation lets many login methods feed one policy, and makes audit possible."},{"q":"Which JWT checks are mandatory, and what attack does each stop?","a":"- **Signature with a pinned algorithm** stops forged tokens and `none` or key-confusion tricks.\n- **Issuer** stops tokens from an untrusted identity provider; **audience** stops a token minted for another\n  service being replayed against yours.\n- **Expiry** (required, not optional) bounds how long a stolen token works, since JWTs cannot be revoked\n  without server state."},{"q":"Design authorization for a search endpoint over a multi-tenant document store with sharing.","a":"- **Filter in the query, not after it:** tenant and permission predicates in SQL, or ACL metadata filters\n  in the vector search, so pages are full and counts do not leak.\n- **Model sharing explicitly:** ownership plus RBAC to start, ReBAC (Zanzibar-style, for example OpenFGA)\n  once documents are shared through groups and folders.\n- **Backstop** with row-level security and a cross-tenant test suite."},{"q":"When would you return 404 instead of 403?","a":"- **When existence itself is sensitive,** such as another tenant's document id: 404 stops enumeration of\n  valid ids across tenants.\n- **Cost:** support and debugging get harder, so log the true reason server-side; use 403 when the caller\n  can legitimately know the resource exists but lacks the right."},{"q":"A security review finds that an employee removed yesterday can still call the API. What do you check?","a":"- **Token lifetime and revocation:** long-lived JWTs or API keys keep working until expiry, so shorten\n  access tokens and revoke refresh tokens and sessions on deprovisioning.\n- **Authorization caching:** cached decisions or role lookups outliving the change; bound cache TTLs to\n  your revocation promise.\n- **Machine credentials:** API keys the employee created must be bound to owners and rotated."}],"tradeoffs":["**Stateless JWTs vs revocation.** No store lookup per request, but no revocation before expiry; flip to server-side sessions when you must log a user out instantly, as in offboarding or compromise.","**Central policy service vs latency.** One consistent, auditable model across services, but added latency and a dependency on every request; inline checks are fine until a second service needs the same rules.","**Fine-grained permissions vs reviewability.** Precision and least privilege, but the policy surface grows and gets hard to review; coarse roles suffice until customers demand custom roles or per-resource sharing.","**404 vs 403 for cross-tenant resources.** Hides existence from attackers, but confuses your own debugging and support unless the server logs the real reason.","**Long-lived API keys vs exposure.** Simple for machine clients but never expire on their own; flip to short-lived, scoped tokens once keys are shared across customer systems or appear in logs."],"pitfalls":["**Insecure direct object reference.** Authentication present, ownership check absent; changing an id in a URL returns another user's record, and it is the first thing a pen tester tries.","**Skipping the JWT audience check.** Symptom: a token issued for one API is accepted by another, so a compromise of a low-value service unlocks a high-value one.","**Authorization only in the front end.** Symptom: `curl` with a valid token bypasses every restriction the UI shows.","**One agent service account holding the union of all users' permissions.** Symptom: a user asks the agent for a document they cannot open in the app and receives it.","**Caching authorization decisions beyond revocation.** Symptom: a removed employee or deleted share still works minutes or hours later.","**Treating \"valid API key\" as \"known tenant\"** without binding keys to tenants and filtering every query. Symptom: cross-tenant data in search or RAG results.","**Plaintext API keys or passwords, or passwords under a fast unsalted hash.** Symptom: one database dump exposes every customer's credentials.","**Returning 403 for an unauthenticated request.** Symptom: clients never retry with credentials because the status told them they were already identified."],"when":"Every multi-user system needs both, so the decisions are about shape:\n- **Browser logins where instant logout matters:** server-side sessions.\n- **APIs and mobile clients:** short-lived JWTs with refresh tokens.\n- **Machine access:** hashed, prefixed, tenant-bound API keys.\n- **Enterprise customers:** delegate login to an identity provider as soon as the first one appears.\n- **Authorization model:** start with ownership plus RBAC; add ABAC or ReBAC only when sharing rules or\n  attribute policies outgrow roles.\n- **Measure:** 401 and 403 rates per endpoint, failed-login rates per account, and authorization decision latency.\n- **Day to day:** the dependency that produces the principal and the check at the top of every mutating handler.\n- **Interviews:** explain 401 versus 403, design the checks for a multi-tenant endpoint, and describe how an\n  agent acts on behalf of a user without a confused-deputy hole.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the authentication dependency once, verifies all four JWT properties, writes a default-deny `authorize` function with tenant and ownership checks, and tests it with another tenant's ids.","architect":"Owns the identity and permission model: session versus token, where policy is decided, how tenant isolation is enforced at the data layer, and how agents carry user identity into tools.","fde":"Maps the customer's identity provider, groups and roles onto the product's principal and permission model, and proves to their security team that retrieval and tool calls respect existing ACLs."},"check":{"q":"An agent platform executes tools with one service account that can read every customer's\ndocuments, and the system prompt instructs the model to \"only access the current user's documents\". The\ncustomer's security team asks whether this is acceptable. What do you tell them and what would you change?","a":"It is not acceptable, because the prompt is doing authorization and a prompt is not a security\nboundary: the model can be induced through injection or error to request another user's document, and\nthe service account will be allowed to fetch it. Authentication is fine (the user is known at the API\nedge) but that identity is dropped before the tool call, which is the confused-deputy pattern. The fix\nis to carry the user's principal into every tool invocation, by token exchange for a user-scoped token\nor by passing the principal explicitly, and to have the document service authorize each read against\nthat user's tenant and permissions with default deny. Retrieval should filter by the user's ACLs at\nquery time, and the agent's own account should hold only the minimal rights it needs for itself. Audit\nlogs then record the user, not the agent, as the actor."},"quiz":[{"q":"A request arrives with no credential at all. Which response is correct and why?","options":["403 Forbidden, because the caller is not allowed to access the resource","401 Unauthorized with a WWW-Authenticate header, because the server cannot establish who is calling","404 Not Found, to avoid revealing that the endpoint exists","400 Bad Request, because the Authorization header is required"],"answer":1,"explanation":"401 signals an authentication problem and tells the client how to supply credentials. 403 would claim identity was established and permission denied, which is false and stops clients from retrying with a token."},{"q":"A JWT presented to your API has a valid signature from your identity provider and has not expired, but its audience claim names a different service. Should it be accepted?","options":["Yes, because the signature proves the identity provider issued it","Yes, provided the subject exists in your user table","No, because a token minted for another service must not grant access to yours, even from the same issuer","Only if it also carries the correct scopes"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Signature and expiry prove origin and freshness, not intended recipient. Accepting tokens regardless of audience lets a compromise of any service that shares the issuer become a compromise of all of them."},{"q":"A logged-in user changes the id in `/invoices/118` to `/invoices/119` and sees another customer's invoice. Which layer failed?","options":["Authentication, because the session should have been bound to the invoice","Authorization, because the handler never checked that the principal may read that resource","Transport security, because the id was visible in the URL","Rate limiting, because enumeration of ids should have been blocked"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The user was correctly identified; the server then assumed identity implied permission. This is an insecure direct object reference, the classic broken-access-control bug, fixed by an ownership or tenant check."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/oauth-2-0-oidc","sources":["Hardt (ed.), 2012 — The OAuth 2.0 Authorization Framework (RFC 6749)","Sakimura et al., 2014 — OpenID Connect Core 1.0","Sakimura, Bradley, Agarwal, 2015 — Proof Key for Code Exchange by OAuth Public Clients (RFC 7636)","Lodderstedt, Bradley, Labunets, Fett, 2025 — Best Current Practice for OAuth 2.0 Security (RFC 9700)"],"why":"- **Password sharing was the old answer.** A third-party calendar app asked for your Google password. That\n  hands over everything, not just the calendar; it cannot be revoked per app (only by changing the\n  password, which breaks every app at once); it stops working once the account has MFA; and dozens of\n  apps end up storing a credential that unlocks the whole account.\n- **Static per-user API keys only fix half.** They remove password sharing but keys are long-lived, rarely\n  scoped, hard to rotate, and the user still copies a secret between systems by hand.\n- **OAuth 2.0 (2012) split the problem into four roles.** The **resource owner** (user), the **client**\n  (app), the **authorization server** (authenticates the user, records consent, issues tokens) and the\n  **resource server** (the API, accepts tokens). The user authenticates only to the authorization server;\n  the client gets a token that is scoped (`calendar.readonly`), short-lived (minutes to an hour), revocable\n  and tied to that one client.\n- **Using OAuth for login is an inference error.** \"If the app can call the profile API, the user must be\n  who the profile says\" is wrong: an access token proves the *client was authorized*, not *who is at the\n  keyboard*. An attacker can get a valid token for a victim through a malicious app and replay it against\n  another app that trusts the profile endpoint (token substitution).\n- **OIDC (2014) closes that hole.** Its **ID token** is a JWT signed by the identity provider, with the\n  client's own id in the audience and a nonce bound to this login attempt, so authentication is verified\n  rather than inferred.","how":"### Two channels, one principle\n| Channel | What it is | Trust |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Front channel** | The user's browser | Untrusted: logged, visible in history and referrers |\n| **Back channel** | TLS from the client's server to the authorization server | Trusted |\n\n- **Rule that explains most of the protocol:** *nothing valuable travels the front channel*.\n- The browser only ever carries a short-lived, single-use authorization code; tokens move on the back\n  channel.\n\n### Authorization code flow with PKCE, step by step\n1. **Generate three random values.** `state` (binds the callback to this browser session, defeating login\n   CSRF), `nonce` (OIDC: binds the ID token to this attempt) and a PKCE pair: a `code_verifier` and\n   `code_challenge = BASE64URL(SHA256(code_verifier))`. Store all three in the user's server-side session.\n2. **Redirect to `/authorize`** with `response_type=code`, `client_id`, `redirect_uri`,\n   `scope=openid profile email …`, `state`, `nonce`, `code_challenge` and `code_challenge_method=S256`.\n3. **The authorization server authenticates the user** however it likes (password, passkey, MFA, corporate\n   SSO); the client sees none of it. It shows a consent screen listing the scopes.\n4. **Redirect back** to `redirect_uri?code=…&state=…`. The client checks `state` matches the session.\n5. **Back channel exchange.** The client POSTs `code`, `redirect_uri`, `code_verifier` and, for a\n   confidential client, its `client_secret` to `/token`. The server checks:\n   - the code is unused and unexpired;\n   - `SHA256(code_verifier)` equals the challenge from step 2;\n   - `redirect_uri` is identical to the one in step 2, which `/authorize` already matched exactly against\n     the registered list.\n6. **Response.** `access_token`, `token_type`, `expires_in`, optionally `refresh_token`, and, when the\n   `openid` scope was requested, `id_token`.\n\nWhy the checks in step 5 matter:\n- **Exact redirect matching**, *because* the redirect URI is where the code is delivered; a wildcard or\n  prefix match lets an attacker steer codes to a page they control.\n- **PKCE**, *because* public clients (SPAs, mobile apps, CLIs) cannot keep a secret and a code can be\n  intercepted (a malicious app registering the same custom URL scheme, a proxy log). Binding the code to a\n  value the interceptor never saw makes the stolen code worthless.\n- **PKCE everywhere.** The IETF security best current practice (RFC 9700) makes PKCE mandatory for public\n  clients and recommends it for confidential clients too, since it also stops code injection.\n\n### Three tokens, three audiences\n| Token | Audience | Format | Validated by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Access token | The API (resource server) | Opaque or JWT; client treats it as opaque | The API |\n| ID token | The client | Always a JWT | The client |\n| Refresh token | The authorization server | Opaque to the client | The authorization server |\n\n- **Access token.** Sent as `Authorization: Bearer …`. For a JWT, the API checks the signature against the\n  issuer's JWKS plus `iss`, `aud`, `exp` and the required scopes; for an opaque token, it calls the\n  introspection endpoint (RFC 7662).\n- **ID token claims.** `iss`, `sub`, `aud` (must equal your `client_id`), `exp`, `iat`, plus `nonce`\n  (present when the request sent one) and `auth_time` (when requested), and optionally `email`, `name`.\n- **Key users on `(iss, sub)`.** `sub` is the stable user identifier, unique only *within* that issuer;\n  email can change or be re-issued.\n- **Never send the ID token to an API as a credential**; its audience is you.\n- **Refresh token.** Gets fresh access tokens without the user present. It is the longest-lived secret in\n  the system and therefore the most valuable to steal. Mitigations: rotation (each use returns a new one\n  and reuse of an old one revokes the whole family), sender-constraining (DPoP or mTLS), and keeping it\n  server-side only.\n\n### Discovery and key rotation\n- An OIDC provider publishes `/.well-known/openid-configuration` listing its endpoints and a `jwks_uri`.\n- The JWKS holds public signing keys, each with a `kid`.\n- Clients cache the set and refetch on an unknown `kid`, which is how a provider rotates signing keys\n  without breaking anyone.\n\n### The other grants\n| Grant | Who | Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Client credentials** | No user; a service authenticates as itself (secret, private-key JWT or mTLS) | Batch jobs, agents acting as themselves |\n| **Device code** | TVs and CLIs that cannot host a browser | Device polls while the user approves on a phone |\n| **Token exchange (RFC 8693)** | A service holding a user's token | \"On-behalf-of\" calls to a downstream API |\n| **Implicit, password** | Deprecated | Removed in the OAuth 2.1 draft |\n\n- **Token exchange** trades a user's token for a new one with a different audience and usually narrower\n  scope. It is the clean way for an API to call a downstream API, or for an agent's tool runtime to get a\n  token for one tool, *because* forwarding the original token sends a credential whose audience is wrong\n  and whose scope is wider than the downstream call needs.\n- **Deprecated grants.** The implicit grant (token in the URL fragment, front channel) and the\n  resource-owner password grant (the client sees the password). Do not build anything new on them.\n\n### Scopes are not permissions\n- **A scope is an upper bound** the *user* granted the *client*.\n- **The resource server still applies the user's own authorization** underneath: `scope=repo` never lets an\n  app delete a repository the user could not delete themselves.\n- **Both checks run on every request** (see RBAC).","example":"### Confidential web client: code flow with PKCE and ID-token validation\nA server-side client doing the authorization code flow with PKCE and validating the ID token against the\nprovider's JWKS. The pieces that matter are the three random values, the back-channel exchange, and the\nclaims that are checked before trusting `sub`.\n\n```python\nimport base64, hashlib, secrets, urllib.parse, requests, jwt   # pip install requests pyjwt[crypto]\n\nISSUER, CLIENT_ID, CLIENT_SECRET = \"https://login.example.com\", \"my-web-app\", \"<from secrets manager>\"\nREDIRECT_URI = \"https://app.example.com/callback\"\ndisc = requests.get(f\"{ISSUER}/.well-known/openid-configuration\").json()   # endpoints + jwks_uri\njwks = jwt.PyJWKClient(disc[\"jwks_uri\"])   # caches keys; refetches when it meets an unknown kid\n\ndef start_login(session):            # 1. PKCE pair, state, nonce -> stored server-side, never in the URL\n    verifier = secrets.token_urlsafe(64)\n    challenge = base64.urlsafe_b64encode(hashlib.sha256(verifier.encode()).digest()).rstrip(b\"=\").decode()\n    session.update(verifier=verifier, state=secrets.token_urlsafe(16), nonce=secrets.token_urlsafe(16))\n    return disc[\"authorization_endpoint\"] + \"?\" + urllib.parse.urlencode({\n        \"response_type\": \"code\", \"client_id\": CLIENT_ID, \"redirect_uri\": REDIRECT_URI,\n        \"scope\": \"openid profile email\", \"state\": session[\"state\"], \"nonce\": session[\"nonce\"],\n        \"code_challenge\": challenge, \"code_challenge_method\": \"S256\"})\n\ndef handle_callback(code, returned_state, session):   # 2. browser returns ?code=...&state=...\n    if returned_state != session[\"state\"]:\n        raise PermissionError(\"state mismatch: login CSRF or stale session\")\n    tok = requests.post(disc[\"token_endpoint\"], data={             # back channel, TLS, server-to-server\n        \"grant_type\": \"authorization_code\", \"code\": code, \"redirect_uri\": REDIRECT_URI,\n        \"client_id\": CLIENT_ID, \"client_secret\": CLIENT_SECRET,\n        \"code_verifier\": session[\"verifier\"]})\n    tok.raise_for_status(); tok = tok.json()                   # error responses carry no tokens\n    # 3. Validate the ID token: signature via JWKS, pinned algorithm, issuer, audience = our client_id, expiry\n    key = jwks.get_signing_key_from_jwt(tok[\"id_token\"])\n    claims = jwt.decode(tok[\"id_token\"], key.key, algorithms=[\"RS256\"],\n                        audience=CLIENT_ID, issuer=disc[\"issuer\"])\n    if claims.get(\"nonce\") != session[\"nonce\"]:\n        raise PermissionError(\"nonce mismatch: token not minted for this login\")\n    user_key = (claims[\"iss\"], claims[\"sub\"])     # sub is unique per issuer; email is a display attribute\n    return user_key, tok[\"access_token\"], tok.get(\"refresh_token\")   # keep tokens server-side\n```\n\n- **After `handle_callback`** the app issues its own HttpOnly session cookie.\n- **The access token** is used only when the backend calls the provider's APIs on the user's behalf.\n- **The refresh token** never leaves the server.\n\n### Why a stolen code is useless: PKCE in 15 lines\nRunnable with the standard library. The authorization server stores only the challenge; an interceptor\nwho has the code but not the verifier cannot redeem it.\n\n```python\nimport base64, hashlib, secrets\n\ndef challenge_for(verifier: str) -> str:\n    digest = hashlib.sha256(verifier.encode()).digest()\n    return base64.urlsafe_b64encode(digest).rstrip(b\"=\").decode()\n\nverifier = secrets.token_urlsafe(64)        # stays in the client's server-side session\nstored_challenge = challenge_for(verifier)   # sent on the front channel at /authorize\n\ndef token_endpoint_accepts(presented_verifier: str) -> bool:\n    return secrets.compare_digest(challenge_for(presented_verifier), stored_challenge)\n\nprint(token_endpoint_accepts(verifier))                     # True: the legitimate client\nprint(token_endpoint_accepts(secrets.token_urlsafe(64)))    # False: attacker with the code only\n```\n\n### Resource server: bearer middleware for JWT access tokens\nWhat the API, not the client, runs on every request. It pins issuer, audience and algorithm, then checks\nscope. The `scope` claim is a space-separated string in the JWT access-token profile; some providers use\na different claim name, so confirm yours.\n\n```python\nimport jwt   # pyjwt[crypto]\n\nAPI_AUDIENCE, ISSUER = \"https://api.example.com\", \"https://login.example.com\"\njwks = jwt.PyJWKClient(f\"{ISSUER}/.well-known/jwks.json\")   # use the jwks_uri from discovery\n\ndef authorize(auth_header: str, needed_scope: str) -> dict:\n    if not auth_header.startswith(\"Bearer \"):\n        raise PermissionError(\"401: missing bearer token\")\n    token = auth_header.removeprefix(\"Bearer \")\n    key = jwks.get_signing_key_from_jwt(token)\n    claims = jwt.decode(token, key.key, algorithms=[\"RS256\"],\n                        audience=API_AUDIENCE, issuer=ISSUER, leeway=30)  # 30 s clock skew\n    if needed_scope not in claims.get(\"scope\", \"\").split():\n        raise PermissionError(\"403: insufficient scope\")\n    return claims   # next: the user's own role check (RBAC) on the resource\n```","patterns":["**Backend-for-frontend for SPAs.** When a browser app needs login, run the code flow on a small backend and give the browser only an HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite session cookie; tokens stay server-side. Monitor session-store size and refresh failures per provider.","**Refresh-token rotation with reuse detection.** Turn on rotation in the identity provider so each refresh returns a new token and a replayed old one revokes the family. Alert on reuse-detected events, because each one is either a bug (two workers racing) or a theft.","**JWKS cache with refetch on unknown `kid`.** Cache the key set (PyJWKClient does this) and refetch only when a token names a `kid` you lack, so key rotation needs no deploy. Monitor signature-validation failures; a spike right after provider rotation means a stale or pinned key.","**Token exchange for downstream and agent tool calls.** Instead of forwarding the user's token, POST to `/token` with `grant_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:token-exchange`, the user's token as `subject_token`, and the target audience. Log audience and scope per issued token to spot over-broad grants.","**Exact redirect URIs per environment.** Register `https://app.example.com/callback` and the staging and localhost equivalents separately, never a wildcard. Review the registered list in the provider console as part of each release that touches login."],"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant B as Browser\n    participant C as Client backend\n    participant AS as Authorization server\n    participant API as Resource server\n    B->>C: GET /login\n    Note over C: make state, nonce, PKCE pair\n    C->>B: 302 to /authorize with code_challenge\n    B->>AS: authenticate and consent\n    AS->>B: 302 to redirect_uri with code and state\n    B->>C: GET /callback, code, state\n    C->>AS: POST /token with code and code_verifier\n    AS->>C: access_token, id_token, refresh_token\n    Note over C: validate id_token, key user on iss and sub\n    C->>API: Bearer access_token","caption":"Follow the code: it is the only thing that crosses the browser, and it is worthless without the verifier\nthat stays on the server."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect, and why does it matter?","a":"- **OAuth is delegated authorization.** It issues an access token whose audience is an API and which proves\n  a client was allowed to act, not who the user is.\n- **OIDC adds authentication.** The ID token is a signed JWT with `aud` equal to your `client_id` and a\n  `nonce` for this login, so identity is verified rather than inferred.\n- **It matters because** using an access token as identity allows token substitution between apps."},{"q":"Users report being logged in as someone else after an IdP migration. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Check the user key first.** If accounts are matched on email, two issuers or a re-issued address can\n  map different people to one row; key on `(iss, sub)`.\n- **Check validation pins.** Confirm `iss` and `aud` are pinned and the new issuer's JWKS is used, so tokens\n  for another tenant or app are rejected.\n- **Then check sessions.** Make sure the session cookie is reissued on login, not reused across users."},{"q":"Would you use JWT or opaque access tokens for an internal API that handles payments?","a":"- **JWT** gives local validation with no network hop, but a stolen token lives until `exp`.\n- **Opaque with introspection** revokes instantly at the cost of a call or short cache per request.\n- **For payments** I would favour opaque tokens or very short JWT lifetimes plus a denylist, because the\n  cost of a long-valid stolen token outweighs one extra lookup."},{"q":"An AI agent needs to call GitHub and a CRM on a user's behalf. How do you design its credentials?","a":"- **Delegated user tokens per tool,** obtained by the code flow with minimal scopes, so the agent can never\n  exceed what the user can do.\n- **Token exchange per downstream call** to narrow audience and scope rather than forwarding one broad token.\n- **Refresh tokens server-side with rotation,** and client credentials only for work the agent does as\n  itself, so one compromised worker cannot act as every user."},{"q":"Why is PKCE now recommended even for confidential clients that have a secret?","a":"- **PKCE binds the code to one login attempt.** The verifier never crosses the front channel, so an\n  intercepted code cannot be redeemed.\n- **It also stops code injection,** where an attacker plants their own code in a victim's callback; a client\n  secret alone does not tie the code to the session that started the flow.\n- **RFC 9700** makes it mandatory for public clients and recommends it for all."}],"tradeoffs":["**Delegation vs moving parts.** No password sharing, paid for with redirect complexity, more moving parts and an authorization server on your login path. It flips to \"a session cookie is enough\" for an internal tool that talks only to its own backend and never to third parties.","**JWT vs opaque access tokens.** JWTs let a resource server validate locally with no network call, but cannot be revoked before `exp` without adding a denylist lookup, which gives back the network call. Opaque tokens with introspection revoke instantly at the cost of a call (or short cache) per request. Choose by how long a stolen token may stay valid.","**Short-lived access tokens vs refresh risk.** They shrink the damage from a leak, but move the risk onto the refresh token and the session store; without rotation and server-side storage you have only relocated the problem.","**External identity provider vs vendor dependency.** Okta, Entra ID, Google or Auth0 take passwords, MFA and recovery out of your codebase, but add a vendor on the critical path and provider-specific quirks in claim names and refresh policies. For B2B customers SSO is a requirement, so this is rarely a real choice."],"pitfalls":["**Treating an access token as proof of identity** by calling `/userinfo` and trusting the result. Symptom: a token issued to app A logs the same user into app B. Fix: require an ID token and check `aud` and `nonce`.","**Omitting or not checking `state`.** Symptom: login CSRF, where a victim's browser completes the attacker's login and the victim then uploads data into the attacker's account.","**Keying users on `email` instead of `(iss, sub)`.** Symptom: duplicate accounts after a rename, account takeover at providers that re-issue addresses, or a second, multi-tenant issuer letting anyone assert an email they do not own.","**Registering wildcard or prefix-matched redirect URIs** (\"any path under app.example.com\"). Symptom: codes delivered to an open-redirect or user-content page on your own domain, from where an attacker reads them out of the URL. Fix: register exact URIs only.","**Decoding the JWT without pinning `algorithms`, `audience` and `issuer`.** Symptom: any token from the same provider for any other app is accepted, or an `alg: none` token passes.","**Storing tokens in browser `localStorage`.** Symptom: one XSS exfiltrates every user's tokens. Fix: a backend-for-frontend with an HttpOnly session cookie, tokens server-side.","**Handing an agent or integration a broad, long-lived refresh token \"to be safe\".** Symptom: one compromised worker can act as every user. Fix: request minimal scopes, and use client credentials for work the agent does as itself."],"when":"- **Use OIDC when humans log in** and you do not want to own passwords, MFA and account recovery. For B2B\n  products that is nearly always: \"do you support SSO\" appears in the first security questionnaire.\n- **Use the authorization code grant** when your product must call APIs as the user (Google Drive, GitHub,\n  Slack, a CRM for an agent); **use client credentials** when a service calls another service.\n- **Avoid when** it is a single internal app on a session cookie, and never use it as a substitute for \"the\n  cron job needs a credential\".\n- **Day to day:** token validation middleware, mapping provider claims to your roles, token storage and\n  rotation, and provider quirks.\n- **Interviews:** draw the code flow, explain why the exchange happens on the back channel, what PKCE\n  prevents, and what each of the three tokens is for and who validates it.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the callback, validates ID tokens against the JWKS, stores refresh tokens server-side, and writes the bearer middleware that checks issuer, audience, expiry and scope on every API request.","architect":"Decides client types and grants per surface (backend-for-frontend for SPAs, client credentials for workers, delegated user tokens for agents), picks JWT versus opaque access tokens by revocation needs, and owns the identity-provider boundary and the claim-to-role mapping.","fde":"Wires the product into the customer's Okta or Entra tenant, debugs redirect-URI and claim mismatches, gets the agent's tool integrations consented with minimal scopes, and explains why the agent acts with the user's token rather than a shared admin key."},"check":{"q":"A single-page app logs users in with Google by obtaining an access token in the browser, calling\nthe userinfo endpoint, and posting the returned email to the backend, which trusts it. What is wrong,\nand how would you redesign it?","a":"Three independent failures. First, identity is inferred from an access token: a token minted for\nany other app for the same user, or an attacker's own token, is accepted, because nothing ties it to this\nclient; the fix is to require an ID token and validate signature, `iss`, `aud` equal to your client id,\n`exp` and `nonce`. Second, tokens live in JavaScript, so a single XSS steals them. Third, the backend\ntrusts a client-asserted email. Redesign as a backend-for-frontend: the browser hits `/login`, the server\nbuilds the authorize URL with `state`, `nonce` and PKCE, handles the callback on the back channel,\nvalidates the ID token, keys the user on `(iss, sub)`, and sets an HttpOnly session cookie. Access and refresh\ntokens stay server-side and are used only for calls to Google on the user's behalf, with refresh-token\nrotation enabled."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the authorization code flow send a code through the browser and exchange it server-side instead of returning the access token directly?","options":["Codes are shorter than tokens and fit more reliably in a redirect URL","The browser is an untrusted channel; a single-use code bound to PKCE or a client secret is useless if intercepted, whereas a token would be directly usable","Access tokens can only be issued over a connection authenticated with mTLS","It lets the authorization server rate-limit how many tokens a client receives"],"answer":1,"explanation":"URLs leak through history, logs and referrers. A code that can only be redeemed with the verifier or secret the interceptor lacks limits the damage; the implicit grant that put tokens in the URL was deprecated for precisely this reason."},{"q":"A mobile app calls its own backend API and also needs to know who the user is. Which token goes where?","options":["The ID token to the API, and the access token to establish identity","The access token to the API, and the ID token to establish identity; each has a different audience","The refresh token to the API, and the ID token to establish identity","Either token works for both purposes because both are signed JWTs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The ID token's audience is the client, the access token's audience is the API. Sending the ID token to an API or using an access token as identity proof breaks the audience check that prevents token substitution between applications."},{"q":"What does PKCE protect against that a client secret alone cannot?","options":["Brute-force guessing of the user's password at the authorization server","Replay of an access token after it has expired","Interception of the authorization code by or on behalf of a public client that cannot hold a secret, and code injection generally","Forging the signature of an ID token"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A public client ships its code to users, so any embedded secret is public. PKCE binds the code to a per-request verifier that never travels the front channel, so an intercepted code cannot be redeemed; the IETF best-practice now recommends it for all clients."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/rbac","sources":["Sandhu, Coyne, Feinstein, Youman, 1996 — Role-Based Access Control Models","Ferraiolo, Sandhu, Gavrila, Kuhn, Chandramouli, 2001 — Proposed NIST Standard for Role-Based Access Control","Pang et al., 2019 — Zanzibar: Google's Consistent, Global Authorization System","Official docs: Kubernetes (Using RBAC Authorization)"],"why":"- **Every request after login asks \"may they do this\".** Authentication answers \"who is calling\". The\n  obvious answer is a direct grant: a table of (user, permission) rows, or an access-control list on each\n  resource. It works for ten users and dies at a few hundred.\n- **Direct grants explode.** 2,000 employees and 300 distinct permissions (\"read invoices\", \"delete\n  customer\", \"run eval job\") can produce up to 600,000 direct grants. Onboarding a support engineer means\n  deciding, grant by grant, which 40 of the 300 they need; a team move means revoking those 40 and adding\n  35 others.\n- **Reviews become impossible.** \"Who can delete customer records?\" needs a scan of the whole table, and\n  \"is this person's access appropriate?\" has no reference. Each grant is bespoke, so the set drifts toward\n  \"whatever was needed once\" and never shrinks. This is how a departed contractor keeps production access.\n- **Roles compress the problem.** Organisations are made of jobs, and jobs need bundles of permissions.\n  With twelve roles averaging forty permissions, assignments drop to 2,000 + 480 rows instead of up to\n  600,000. \"Is the editor role right?\" is one owner's question; \"is Alice in the right roles?\" is her\n  manager's.\n- **AI raises the stakes.** An agent must not do more than its user; retrieval must not surface chunks from\n  documents the user cannot open; a tool call must be checked when it runs, not at login. All three need a\n  decision cheap enough for every request and every retrieved item, which a role lookup gives you.","how":"### The model\nFour sets and two relations:\n\n| Element | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Subjects | Human users, service accounts, agents |\n| Roles | Named bundles of permissions |\n| Permissions | An operation on an object type, written `documents:read` |\n| Sessions | A subject activates some of its roles |\n| User assignment | `UA ⊆ subjects × roles` |\n| Permission assignment | `PA ⊆ roles × permissions` |\n\n```\nallow (u, p)  iff  there exists a role r with (u, r) ∈ UA and (r, p) ∈ PA\n```\n\n- **Default deny.** Anything not explicitly allowed is denied, *because* a default-allow system cannot be\n  reasoned about: every missing rule is a hole rather than a lock.\n- **Decision rule in words.** Assignments are `UA ⊆ subjects × roles` and `PA ⊆ roles × permissions`. The decision is: allow `(u, p)` iff some role `r` has `(u, r) ∈ UA` and `(r, p) ∈ PA`.\n\n### NIST refinements\n- **Hierarchical RBAC.** A senior role inherits a junior role's permissions (`admin ⊇ editor ⊇ viewer`),\n  because without inheritance the admin's list duplicates the editor's and the two drift apart.\n- **Constrained RBAC (separation of duty).** Nobody may hold both \"submit payment\" and \"approve payment\",\n  because fraud should require collusion.\n  - *Static* separation forbids assigning the two roles to one person at all.\n  - *Dynamic* separation allows both assignments but forbids activating them in the same session, which is\n    where the session set earns its place.\n- **Why these are named levels:** they are the two extensions every real deployment reinvents.\n\n### Least privilege in time, and explicit deny\n- **Elevate, do not stand.** An operator assigned `admin` should normally act as `viewer` and elevate for a\n  bounded window, with a reason and an audit record, *because* standing privilege is what a stolen session\n  or a prompt-injected agent inherits.\n- **Explicit deny (cloud IAM).** A deny statement overrides any allow, so a guardrail (\"nobody outside the\n  security team may delete audit logs\") holds regardless of which roles are later granted.\n- **Evaluation order:** deny, then allow, then the implicit default deny.\n\n### Scope: where a role applies\n- **The production question is scoped.** Not \"can this user read documents?\" but \"can this user read *this\n  tenant's* documents?\".\n- **Make assignments three-part:** (subject, role, scope), where scope is a node in a resource tree such as\n  `org → project → resource`.\n- **Roles apply downward.** A role granted at a scope applies to everything beneath it, because that is how\n  delegation works: the org admin administers every project.\n- **Kubernetes implements exactly this split.** `Role` and `RoleBinding` are namespaced; `ClusterRole` and\n  `ClusterRoleBinding` apply cluster-wide; binding a ClusterRole inside a namespace restricts it to that\n  namespace.\n\n### Where the decision runs\nSeparate the *decision* (given subject, permission, scope, return allow/deny) from *enforcement*\n(middleware at the API boundary, a filter in a database query, a guard in front of a tool call). The\ndecision needs the caller's role assignments, from one of two places:\n\n1. **From the token.** OIDC providers can put `groups` or `roles` claims in the ID or access token, so the\n   check is a local set lookup with no I/O. Cost: staleness, because the token says what was true when it\n   was issued, so a revoked role stays usable until expiry. Acceptable for coarse, rarely-changing org\n   roles with short token lifetimes.\n2. **From the authorization store.** Look assignments up per request (cached with a short TTL). One extra\n   read, but revocation takes effect in seconds, and scoped assignments at project or resource level, which\n   would bloat a token, live naturally here.\n\nMost systems do both: coarse roles in the token, scoped assignments resolved server-side.\n\n### Enforce at every boundary, not just HTTP\n- **The handler check does not cover the rest.** It does nothing for the retrieval query inside it, the\n  background job that re-indexes documents, or the tool the agent calls.\n- **Apply the same decision function** as a row or chunk filter at data access and as a guard at tool\n  invocation.\n- **Run as the *end user's* identity, not the service's.** An agent under a privileged service account has\n  effectively every role at once; a prompt injection then inherits all of them.\n\n### When roles are not enough\nRoles describe job functions, not relationships. \"The owner of a document can edit it\" or \"share this with\nBob\" needs a role per document, which is role explosion by another name.\n\n| Model | Decides on | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| RBAC | Role at scope | Organisation-level capabilities |\n| ABAC | Attributes of subject, resource, context | `resource.classification <= subject.clearance`, `request.time in business_hours` |\n| ReBAC (Zanzibar) | Graph of relationship tuples | `doc:7#viewer@group:eng#member`; sharing in Google Docs and Drive |\n\n- **In practice you layer:** RBAC for organisation-level capabilities, ownership or relationship checks for\n  per-object access, and ABAC conditions where the customer's policy demands them.","example":"### Scoped, hierarchical check with permission-named guards\nRoles inherit downward, assignments carry a scope, and a role at any ancestor scope applies to the\nresource. `current_user` stands in for the authentication dependency from the OAuth lesson.\n\n- **Handlers name *permissions*, never roles,** so adding a role touches one table instead of every\n  endpoint.\n- **Scope comes from the resource's stored owner, never from the URL,** because checking `project:{id}`\n  from the path while deleting `doc_id` lets an editor of project 42 delete project 7's documents by\n  sending `/projects/42/documents/<a project-7 doc>` (an insecure direct object reference).\n\n```python\nfrom fastapi import Depends, FastAPI, HTTPException\n\napp = FastAPI()\ndef current_user() -> str: return \"alice\"   # stub for the bearer-token auth dependency\nPERMS = {                                   # role -> permissions granted directly\n    \"viewer\": {\"documents:read\"},\n    \"editor\": {\"documents:write\"},\n    \"admin\":  {\"documents:delete\", \"members:manage\"},\n}\nINHERITS = {\"admin\": \"editor\", \"editor\": \"viewer\"}      # senior -> junior\n\ndef permissions_of(role: str) -> set[str]:\n    out: set[str] = set()\n    while role:                             # walk down the hierarchy\n        out |= PERMS[role]; role = INHERITS.get(role, \"\")\n    return out\n\n# (subject, scope) -> roles. Scopes form a tree: project -> org.\nASSIGNMENTS = {(\"alice\", \"org:acme\"): {\"viewer\"}, (\"alice\", \"project:42\"): {\"editor\"}}\nPARENT = {\"project:42\": \"org:acme\", \"project:7\": \"org:acme\"}\nDOC_SCOPE = {101: \"project:42\", 202: \"project:7\"}      # each document's real owning scope\n\ndef allowed(subject: str, perm: str, scope: str) -> bool:\n    while scope:                            # a role at any ancestor scope applies here\n        if any(perm in permissions_of(r) for r in ASSIGNMENTS.get((subject, scope), ())):\n            return True\n        scope = PARENT.get(scope, \"\")\n    return False                            # deny by default\n\ndef require_on_doc(perm: str):\n    def guard(doc_id: int, user: str = Depends(current_user)) -> str:\n        scope = DOC_SCOPE.get(doc_id)       # scope from the resource, not from the path\n        if scope is None or not allowed(user, perm, scope):\n            raise HTTPException(404)        # 404, not 403: do not confirm the doc exists\n        return user\n    return guard\n\n@app.delete(\"/documents/{doc_id}\")\ndef delete_doc(doc_id: int, user: str = Depends(require_on_doc(\"documents:delete\"))):\n    return {\"deleted_by\": user}\n\nassert allowed(\"alice\", \"documents:write\", \"project:42\")       # editor at the project\nassert allowed(\"alice\", \"documents:read\", \"project:7\")         # viewer inherited from the org\nassert not allowed(\"alice\", \"documents:delete\", \"project:42\")  # admin nowhere\nassert not allowed(\"alice\", \"documents:write\", DOC_SCOPE[202]) # doc 202 lives in project 7\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: the assignment arithmetic\nThe same 2,000-person company before and after introducing roles.\n\n| Quantity | Direct grants | RBAC |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rows to manage | up to 600,000 | 2,000 user-role + 480 role-permission |\n| Onboard a support engineer | pick 40 of 300 grants | assign 1 role |\n| Team move | revoke 40, add 35 | swap 1 role |\n| \"Who can delete customers?\" | scan every row | list roles with that permission, then their members |\n\n### Kubernetes: the same model in a manifest\nA namespaced `Role` plus `RoleBinding` gives a group read-only access to pods in one namespace only.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Role\nmetadata:\n  namespace: team-a\n  name: pod-reader\nrules:\n  - apiGroups: [\"\"]\n    resources: [\"pods\"]\n    verbs: [\"get\", \"list\", \"watch\"]\n---\napiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\nkind: RoleBinding\nmetadata:\n  namespace: team-a\n  name: team-a-devs-read-pods\nsubjects:\n  - kind: Group\n    name: team-a-devs\n    apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io\nroleRef:\n  kind: Role\n  name: pod-reader\n  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io\n```\n\nVerify a decision without guessing: `kubectl auth can-i list pods --as=alice -n team-a`.","patterns":["**Permission-named guards, enforced in review.** Every endpoint declares `require(\"documents:delete\")`; a CI grep fails the build on role-string comparisons such as `role == \"admin\"`. Monitor the share of handlers with a declared permission, aiming for all of them.","**Just-in-time elevation.** Operators hold `viewer` by default and request `admin` for a bounded window with a reason; the grant expires on its own and is written to the audit log. Monitor how many people hold standing admin and how often elevation is used.","**Coarse roles in the token, scoped grants in the store.** Keep access-token lifetimes short and resolve project-level assignments server-side with a short cache TTL, so revocation bites in seconds. Monitor time-to-revoke in offboarding drills.","**Permission-aware retrieval.** At ingestion, write each chunk's allowed users and groups as metadata; at query time pass the caller's principals as a filter inside the vector or keyword search. Run a standing leakage test: a test user must never retrieve a document they cannot open.","**Group-to-role mapping via SCIM.** Map customer IdP groups to product roles and let SCIM deprovision users, so offboarding happens in their directory. Monitor the ratio of roles to users and run access reviews per role owner."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Request: subject, permission, resource\"] --> S[\"Look up resource's stored scope\"]\n    S --> D{\"Explicit deny matches?\"}\n    D -->|\"yes\"| X[\"Deny\"]\n    D -->|\"no\"| A[\"Roles at this scope\"]\n    A --> G{\"Any role grants permission?\"}\n    G -->|\"yes\"| OK[\"Allow\"]\n    G -->|\"no\"| P{\"Parent scope exists?\"}\n    P -->|\"yes, move up\"| A\n    P -->|\"no\"| X","caption":"Read it top to bottom: explicit deny wins, then a role at the resource's own scope or any ancestor, and\neverything else falls through to default deny."},"interview":[{"q":"What problem does RBAC solve that a per-user permission list does not?","a":"- **It adds one indirection, the role,** so assignments go from users × permissions to users → roles plus\n  roles → permissions; 2,000 users and 300 permissions shrink from up to 600,000 rows to a few thousand.\n- **It makes reviews tractable,** because a role owner audits one bundle and a manager audits a person's\n  roles, instead of anyone auditing hundreds of bespoke grants."},{"q":"A pentest finds that an editor in tenant A can edit tenant B's documents. Where do you look?","a":"- **Scope on assignments:** check that roles are granted at a scope and that the check passes the resource's\n  scope, not a global \"editor\".\n- **Scope source:** check whether the scope is read from the URL or body instead of the stored resource, the\n  classic insecure direct object reference.\n- **Other boundaries:** confirm background jobs and retrieval queries apply the same tenant filter."},{"q":"When would you not use RBAC alone?","a":"- **Per-object sharing.** \"Share this doc with Bob\" needs a role per document; use relationship tuples\n  (ReBAC, Zanzibar-style) instead.\n- **Attribute-driven rules.** Classification, region or time-of-day conditions belong in ABAC.\n- **Keep RBAC** for organisation-level capabilities and layer the others on top."},{"q":"How fast does access revocation take effect in your design, and how would you shorten it?","a":"- **Roles in the token** lag by the token lifetime: with one-hour tokens, up to an hour.\n- **Server-side lookup with a short cache** brings it down to the cache TTL, at the cost of one read per\n  request.\n- **Shorten further** by shortening access-token lifetimes for sensitive surfaces and revoking sessions on\n  offboarding events from SCIM."},{"q":"How do you stop an AI agent from exceeding its user's permissions?","a":"- **Run tools as the end user's delegated identity,** never as a privileged service account that holds\n  every role at once.\n- **Check at tool invocation time,** with the same decision function the API uses, not just at login.\n- **Filter retrieval inside the query** by the user's principals, so unauthorized chunks never reach the\n  prompt where an injection could exfiltrate them."}],"tradeoffs":["**Indirection vs catalogue ownership.** Assignments shrink from users × permissions to user-role plus role-permission rows, but the role catalogue needs an owner; once roles are minted per team per exception (\"role explosion\") you are back to per-user lists with an extra hop.","**Roles in the token vs revocation speed.** Every check is a local set lookup with no database round trip, but revocation lags by the token lifetime. The moment removal must bite within seconds (offboarding, a compromised account) the balance flips to a server-side lookup.","**Hierarchies vs silent inheritance.** They remove duplication and mirror the org chart, but a senior role silently inherits every new junior permission, which is how admins accumulate access nobody reviewed. For high-impact permissions (payments, deletion, key management) flat, explicitly granted roles are safer.","**Coarse roles vs expressiveness.** Simple to audit, but cannot express ownership or sharing; as soon as the product needs \"share this document with Bob\" you add a relationship layer and keep RBAC only for organisation-level capabilities."],"pitfalls":["**Comparing role strings in code** (`if user.role == \"admin\"`) instead of checking permissions. Symptom: a new \"support\" role works nowhere until twenty endpoints are edited, and some are missed.","**Forgetting scope.** Symptom: an editor in one tenant can edit in every tenant, found by a penetration test or, worse, a customer.","**Taking the scope from the request** (a path or body `project_id`) instead of the stored resource. Symptom: changing one ID in a URL reaches another project's object while every permission check still passes.","**Enforcing only at the HTTP layer** while the retrieval query or background job reads the index unfiltered. Symptom: the assistant quotes a document the user cannot open in the source system.","**Running the agent under a privileged service account** instead of the user's delegated roles. Symptom: a prompt-injected agent deletes or reads what the asking user never could.","**Role explosion:** hundreds of near-duplicate roles. Symptom: \"who can do X?\" needs a script and quarterly access reviews are rubber-stamped.","**Stale claims after a role change.** Symptom: \"I removed their access an hour ago and they are still deleting things\", because the token has not expired."],"when":"- **Use when** a multi-user system has a stable set of job functions: admin panels, SaaS workspaces,\n  Kubernetes, cloud IAM. RBAC is the default.\n- **Add ABAC** when decisions depend on attributes of the resource or context (classification, region, time\n  of day), and **ReBAC** when the product has per-object sharing.\n- **Measure:** the ratio of roles to users (creeping up signals explosion), time-to-revoke, the share of\n  checks that are permission-named rather than role-string comparisons, and whether every retrieval and\n  tool call carries the end user's identity.\n- **Day to day:** write `require(\"x:y\")` guards, map identity-provider groups to roles and filter queries by\n  scope.\n- **Interviews:** explain users → roles → permissions with scope, the token-versus-store freshness trade,\n  and name the case RBAC cannot express.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements permission-named guards as middleware or dependencies, stores scoped assignments, and applies the same decision as a chunk filter in retrieval and a check before each tool call.","architect":"Owns the role catalogue and scope hierarchy, decides what rides in the token versus the authorization service, and whether a central policy engine (OPA or a Zanzibar-style system) is justified.","fde":"Maps the customer's IdP groups from Okta or Entra ID to product roles, wires SCIM-driven assignment, and proves to their security team that the assistant cannot see what the user cannot."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants an AI assistant over a shared document store where every document has its own\nsharing list, and they also have org-level roles (admin, member, guest). Design authorization so the\nassistant never leaks a document.","a":"Separate two layers. Org-level RBAC handles coarse capabilities: who may use the assistant, who\nmanages members, which features guests get; carry these as roles in the token and check them with\npermission-named guards at the API boundary. Document access is per-object sharing, which is\nrelationship-based, so do not model it as roles. Record each document's allowed principals (users and\ngroups) as metadata on every indexed chunk at ingestion, resolve the caller's identity and group\nmemberships at request time, and apply them as a filter *inside* the vector or keyword query so\nunauthorized chunks never reach the prompt. Sync sharing changes incrementally and treat a stale ACL\nas a leak, so re-check the top-k hits against the source system when freshness matters. Run the\nassistant under the user's delegated identity, never a privileged service account, so a prompt\ninjection cannot widen its reach."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do permissions attach to roles rather than directly to users in RBAC?","options":["Because users cannot be identified reliably until after authorization has run","Because it replaces a users × permissions assignment problem with users → roles plus roles → permissions, which is far smaller to manage, review and revoke","Because roles are stored in the identity provider while permissions are stored in the application","Because direct permission grants cannot be represented as JWT claims"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The indirection collapses the number of assignments and makes reviews about a handful of roles. Where roles are stored is an implementation choice, and JWTs can carry any claim, so neither explains the design."},{"q":"You removed a user's only role in your application's authorization service ten minutes ago, but they can still call a protected endpoint. Access tokens live for an hour. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The role hierarchy still grants the permission through a junior role","Roles are carried in the access token, which has not expired, so the check is using stale claims","The permission check is scoped to the wrong tenant","The identity provider has not propagated the change through SCIM"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Token claims are a snapshot at issue time, so revocation lags by the token lifetime. With no other role left there is no junior role to inherit from, and SCIM carries changes from the identity provider into the app, whereas this change was made in the app's own store; neither touches a token that is already issued and valid."},{"q":"Which requirement is RBAC alone poorly suited to express?","options":["Administrators can manage the members of their own organisation","Viewers can read documents in the projects they are assigned to","A document's owner can edit it and share it with specific colleagues","Only staff in the billing role can issue refunds"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Ownership and sharing are relationships between a subject and one object; expressing them with roles needs a role per document. The other three are role-at-scope statements that core or hierarchical RBAC handles directly."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/secrets-management","sources":["Official docs: HashiCorp Vault","Official docs: AWS Secrets Manager","Official docs: Kubernetes Secrets","OWASP — Secrets Management Cheat Sheet"],"why":"- **Secrets leak through boring paths.** A `.env` committed \"just once\", an `ENV API_KEY=` line in a\n  Dockerfile, a build argument captured in image history, a key pasted into a ticket or a chat with an LLM,\n  an exception trace that logs request headers, a notebook checked into git, a CI log that echoed the\n  environment.\n- **For AI teams a provider key is a billing key.** Keys pushed to public repositories are routinely found\n  and abused by scanners within minutes (illustrative; the exact window varies), turning into unexpected\n  bills, exhausted quotas and rate limits for your real traffic. Unless you create narrower project- or\n  workspace-scoped keys, one key often reaches a whole organisation's quota and data.\n- **Environment variables are delivery, not storage.** They are inherited by every child process, readable\n  from `/proc/<pid>/environ`, and dumped into crash reports.\n- **Encrypted repo files are only a middle ground.** sops or git-crypt work, but the decryption key is itself\n  a secret to bootstrap, and rotation means re-encrypting in every environment. \"We will be careful\" fails\n  at the first contractor laptop.\n- **Rotation is the deeper problem.** A secret copied into fourteen places cannot be rotated without an\n  outage, so nobody does, and a three-year-old key sits on a dozen laptops. Goal one: one source of truth\n  referenced by name. Goal two: as few long-lived secrets as possible.","how":"### 1. Inventory and minimise\nList every credential and ask whether it needs to exist. Every secret you do not have is one you never\nrotate or leak.\n\n| Static secret | Replace with |\n|---|---|\n| Cloud access key on a VM or pod | Platform identity: IAM role, managed identity |\n| Cloud key stored in CI | OIDC federation |\n| Static API key | Short-lived tokens, where the provider supports them |\n\n### 2. Store in a manager backed by a KMS\nVault, AWS Secrets Manager or Parameter Store, GCP Secret Manager and Azure Key Vault do the same core job:\n- **Envelope encryption.** Values are encrypted with a data key; the data key is encrypted by a root key the\n  service never exposes. Cloud services hold it in an HSM-backed KMS; Vault protects it through its seal,\n  which can itself be a cloud KMS or HSM.\n- **Access is an IAM policy** per identity per secret path.\n- **Recent versions are kept** for rollback, and **every read is audited**.\n\nKubernetes `Secret` objects are *not* this:\n- base64-encoded, stored in etcd unencrypted unless an `EncryptionConfiguration` with a KMS provider is\n  enabled;\n- readable by anyone with `get secrets` in the namespace, and in effect by anyone who can create a Pod\n  there, since a Pod can mount any Secret in its namespace;\n- so treat them as the last hop, populated from a real manager by the External Secrets Operator, the\n  Secrets Store CSI driver or a Vault agent sidecar.\n\n### Secrets store vs KMS\n| | Secrets store | KMS (AWS KMS, Cloud KMS, Key Vault keys, Vault transit) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Returns the value? | Yes, to the caller | Never; key is non-exportable |\n| Caller sends | A name | Data to sign, encrypt or decrypt |\n| Use for | Passwords, API keys | JWT signing keys, webhook signing, field-level encryption |\n\n- **Why keys belong in a KMS:** a key that never enters application memory cannot leak through a heap dump,\n  a log line or a compromised dependency.\n- **Limit:** the attacker who owns the process can still call `sign` while they hold it, but loses that\n  power the moment access is revoked.\n\n### 3. Authenticate the workload: the secret-zero problem\nTo fetch secrets a workload needs a credential; if that is a static key you have gained nothing. The way\nout is identity the platform attests without a stored secret:\n\n| Platform | Identity mechanism |\n|---|---|\n| EC2 | Instance profile |\n| EKS | IAM Roles for Service Accounts (projected service-account token exchanged for a role via OIDC federation) or EKS Pod Identity (a node agent exchanges the same token through the EKS Auth API) |\n| GKE | Workload Identity |\n| Azure | Managed identity |\n| Vault | Kubernetes and JWT auth methods |\n| GitHub Actions | `id-token: write`: a short-lived OIDC token a cloud role trusts for a specific repository and branch |\n\n- **Trust now lives in configuration** (`sub = repo:org/app:ref:refs/heads/main`) instead of in a key, so\n  keep those conditions narrow.\n\n### 4. Deliver to the process\n| Option | Pros | Cons |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fetch through the SDK at startup | Simple | Needs network and identity; cache with a TTL |\n| Mount as files on a tmpfs volume | Preferred on Kubernetes: the manager rewrites the file, the app re-reads without a restart | App must re-read |\n| Inject as environment variables | Universal | Frozen for the process life and leaky as above |\n\n- **Never bake into the image.** `docker build --secret` mounts a value during one build step without\n  writing it to any layer.\n\n### 5. Rotate, and prefer dynamic\nStatic secrets rotate on a schedule and immediately after any suspected exposure, using two-key overlap,\n*because* an atomic swap across many consumers is impossible. For a database password:\n1. Create the new credential at the database while the old one still works.\n2. Store it in the manager as the pending version.\n3. Verify a login with it.\n4. Promote it to current, so consumers pick it up on their next refresh.\n5. Wait at least one consumer refresh interval (the cache TTL, plus restart time for anything that reads\n   only at boot).\n6. Revoke the old credential.\n\n- **AWS Secrets Manager models exactly this** with staging labels `AWSPENDING`, `AWSCURRENT` and\n  `AWSPREVIOUS`.\n- **Revoking at step 4 instead of 6** is the classic rotation outage.\n- **Dynamic secrets remove the schedule.** Vault's database engine creates a per-workload database user with\n  a TTL and drops it on expiry; cloud STS tokens expire within hours.\n- **Rotation only works if consumers re-read,** so cache with a TTL and refetch on an authentication failure.\n\n### 6. Detect and contain\n- **Catch before it lands:** pre-commit and CI scanning (gitleaks, trufflehog) and GitHub push protection.\n- **Git history is permanent exposure.** A secret that reached history is compromised even after the commit\n  is removed: rotate it.\n- **Limit logs:** redact known secret values, allowlist logged fields, and alert when a secret is read by an\n  identity that never read it before.\n\n### 7. Rules specific to AI systems\n- **One model-provider key per service and environment, with a spend cap,** kept server-side only and ideally\n  behind a gateway, so rotation happens in one place and spend is attributable.\n- **Never place a secret in the prompt or a tool result,** because a model can be induced to repeat its\n  context, and traces in your observability tool would store it.\n- **Tools receive opaque references** and resolve credentials in the tool runtime, outside the model's\n  context.\n- **Per-customer credentials isolated by tenant** for agents that reach into customer systems; the agent's\n  own identity is never the customer's key.","example":"### Resolver: file mount or manager, TTL refresh and log redaction\nA small resolver that reads a secret from a mounted file when one exists (Vault agent or CSI driver),\notherwise from AWS Secrets Manager using the pod's or instance's ambient IAM identity. It re-reads after a\nTTL so rotations take effect without restarts, and installs a logging filter that redacts the value.\n\n```python\nimport logging, os, time, boto3\nfrom requests import post\n\nclass Secrets:\n    \"\"\"No static credential anywhere: boto3 picks up the IAM role from the environment (IRSA / instance profile).\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, mount=\"/var/run/secrets/app\", ttl_s=300):\n        self.mount, self.ttl_s, self._cache = mount, ttl_s, {}\n        self._sm = boto3.client(\"secretsmanager\")\n\n    def get(self, name: str, force=False) -> str:\n        hit = self._cache.get(name)\n        if hit and not force and time.monotonic() - hit[1] < self.ttl_s:\n            return hit[0]\n        path = os.path.join(self.mount, name.replace(\"/\", \"_\"))\n        if os.path.exists(path):                                   # file mount rotates in place\n            value = open(path).read().strip()\n        else:                                                      # manager call, audited per identity\n            value = self._sm.get_secret_value(SecretId=name)[\"SecretString\"]\n        self._cache[name] = (value, time.monotonic())\n        return value\n\nclass Redact(logging.Filter):\n    def __init__(self, values): super().__init__(); self.values = [v for v in values if v]\n    def filter(self, record):\n        msg = record.getMessage()\n        if any(v in msg for v in self.values):\n            for v in self.values: msg = msg.replace(v, \"[REDACTED]\")\n            record.msg, record.args = msg, ()\n        return True\n\nsecrets = Secrets()\nLLM_KEY = secrets.get(\"prod/llm-gateway/api-key\")        # per-service key with a spend cap\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO)\nfor h in logging.getLogger().handlers:                   # on handlers: logger filters skip propagated records\n    h.addFilter(Redact([LLM_KEY]))                       # re-register with the new value after a rotation\n\ndef call_provider(payload):\n    key = secrets.get(\"prod/llm-gateway/api-key\")        # cheap: served from cache until ttl\n    r = post(\"https://llm-gateway.internal/v1/chat\", json=payload, headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {key}\"})\n    if r.status_code == 401:                             # rotated under us: refetch once, then retry\n        key = secrets.get(\"prod/llm-gateway/api-key\", force=True)\n        r = post(\"https://llm-gateway.internal/v1/chat\", json=payload, headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {key}\"})\n    return r\n```\n\n- **On rotation** the file mount updates or the next TTL expiry refetches; the 401 branch covers the window\n  in between.\n- **Nothing** in the repository, image or environment contains the value.\n\n### Worked scenario: a rotation that caused an outage, and the fixed plan\nTwelve services share one database password; the resolver TTL is 300 s and a few batch jobs read only at\nboot. The figures are illustrative.\n\n| Plan | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| Old: write new password, revoke old at once (step 4) | Every consumer still holding the cached old value fails auth for up to 300 s; boot-only jobs fail until restarted |\n| Fixed: create, store as pending, verify, promote, wait, revoke (steps 1 to 6) | Consumers move over on their next refresh; revoke only after TTL plus the longest restart time |\n| Better: per-service credentials | One service's rotation cannot break the other eleven |\n| Best where possible: dynamic database users | Each workload gets its own user with a TTL; no shared password to rotate |\n\n### Build secrets and CI federation instead of stored keys\nA BuildKit secret mount for a private package index, and a GitHub Actions job that gets cloud credentials\nfrom OIDC instead of a stored key.\n\n```dockerfile\n# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1\n# build with: docker build --secret id=pip_token,src=./pip_token.txt .\nFROM python:3.12-slim\nCOPY requirements.txt .\n# the token exists only during this RUN step and is never written to a layer\nRUN --mount=type=secret,id=pip_token \\\n    PIP_INDEX_URL=\"https://__token__:$(cat /run/secrets/pip_token)@pypi.internal/simple\" \\\n    pip install -r requirements.txt\n```\n\n```yaml\npermissions:\n  id-token: write        # lets the job request an OIDC token\n  contents: read\njobs:\n  deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v4\n        with:\n          role-to-assume: arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/deploy-main   # trusts only repo:org/app:ref:refs/heads/main\n          aws-region: us-east-1\n```","patterns":["**Per-service provider keys behind a gateway.** Issue one model-provider key per service and environment with a spend cap, and route calls through an internal gateway that holds it. Monitor spend per key and 401 rates; a key whose spend jumps without a deploy is a likely leak.","**Scan on every commit and push.** Run gitleaks or trufflehog as a pre-commit hook and a CI step, and turn on GitHub push protection. Track findings per month and time from finding to rotation.","**Managed rotation with overlap.** Use the manager's rotation workflow (Secrets Manager rotation with the `AWSPENDING` and `AWSCURRENT` labels, or Vault dynamic credentials) instead of hand edits. Monitor secret age and auth failures in the minutes after each rotation.","**Kubernetes Secrets synced from a manager.** Let the External Secrets Operator or the Secrets Store CSI driver populate Secrets with a refresh interval, enable etcd encryption with a KMS provider, and restrict `get secrets` in RBAC. Audit who can create Pods in each namespace, since they can mount any Secret there.","**Alert on unusual reads.** Send manager read events (CloudTrail for Secrets Manager, Vault audit device) to your SIEM and alert when an identity reads a secret it has never read before."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    W[\"Workload: pod, VM or CI job\"] -->|\"platform identity token\"| ID[\"Cloud IAM or Vault auth\"]\n    ID -->|\"short-lived role\"| SM[(\"Secrets manager\")]\n    K[\"KMS root key\"] -.->|\"envelope encryption\"| SM\n    SM -->|\"SDK read, cached with TTL\"| APP[\"Application process\"]\n    SM -->|\"sync\"| F[\"tmpfs file mount\"]\n    F -->|\"re-read on change\"| APP\n    R[\"Rotation: pending to current\"] --> SM\n    SM -.->|\"every read\"| AU[\"Audit log\"]","caption":"The workload proves who it is with a platform-issued identity, so no stored secret is needed to reach the\nmanager; the value then arrives as a file or SDK read that refreshes on rotation."},"interview":[{"q":"Why are environment variables not a secrets store?","a":"- **They are a delivery mechanism.** They are inherited by every child process, readable from\n  `/proc/<pid>/environ`, and dumped into crash reports.\n- **They are frozen for the process lifetime,** so a rotation needs a restart.\n- **A real store adds** encryption at rest, per-identity access policy, versions and an audit trail."},{"q":"A model-provider key was pushed to a public repo ten minutes ago. What do you do, in order?","a":"- **Rotate first:** revoke the key at the provider and issue a new one, because scanners find public keys\n  within minutes and deleting the commit does not un-expose it.\n- **Contain:** check provider usage and spend for the exposure window and set or lower the spend cap.\n- **Prevent:** add push protection and pre-commit scanning, and move the key into a manager with\n  per-service scoping."},{"q":"Static secrets with rotation, or dynamic credentials: which would you choose?","a":"- **Dynamic** (Vault database engine, STS) shrinks blast radius and removes the rotation schedule, but every\n  consumer must handle expiry and database users churn.\n- **Static with scheduled rotation** is simpler where consumers cannot refresh, for example a legacy job that\n  reads only at boot.\n- **Rule of thumb:** dynamic for services you control, static plus overlap rotation for the rest."},{"q":"How do you rotate a shared database password without downtime?","a":"- **Overlap two credentials:** create the new one while the old still works, store it as pending, verify,\n  then promote to current.\n- **Wait one refresh interval,** the cache TTL plus the longest restart time for boot-only readers, before\n  revoking the old one.\n- **Make consumers re-read** on a TTL and on authentication failure, otherwise rotation always breaks\n  something."},{"q":"How should an agent's tools get credentials for customer systems?","a":"- **Resolve in the tool runtime,** keyed by tenant and tool, from the secrets store; the model only sees an\n  opaque reference.\n- **Never in the prompt or tool results,** because prompt injection can make the model repeat its context and\n  traces would store it.\n- **Isolate per customer,** so a compromised tenant credential cannot reach another tenant."}],"tradeoffs":["**Central manager vs startup dependency.** One place to rotate and audit, but a runtime dependency on startup; cache with a TTL and fail fast at boot. For a two-person project without infrastructure, encrypted files in the repo are an acceptable step up from nothing.","**Dynamic credentials vs consumer complexity.** Short-lived credentials shrink the blast radius but every consumer must handle expiry and refresh, and database users churn. Static secrets with scheduled rotation are simpler where consumers cannot refresh.","**File mounts vs environment variables.** File mounts allow hot rotation but need the application to re-read; environment variables work with every runtime but are frozen for the process lifetime and inherited by children.","**Platform identity vs portability.** It removes secret zero but ties you to that platform's IAM model; in air-gapped or on-premises deployments you may need Vault with AppRole and a bootstrap token delivered out of band."],"pitfalls":["**`ENV` or `ARG` with a secret in a Dockerfile.** Symptom: `docker history` or any pull of the image reveals it. Fix: use build secrets.","**A `.env` deleted from the working tree but still in git history.** Symptom: a scanner alert or a surprise provider bill weeks later. Fix: rotate, do not just rewrite history.","**Logging whole request objects or headers.** Symptom: `Authorization: Bearer …` lines in the log store. Fix: allowlist fields and add redaction filters.","**One shared production key used by twelve services.** Symptom: rotation causes an outage and spend cannot be attributed. Fix: per-service keys.","**Secrets placed in prompts, tool arguments or tool outputs.** Symptom: the model echoes a key in chat or a trace contains it. Fix: resolve credentials inside the tool runtime.","**Consumers that read secrets only at startup.** Symptom: authentication failures after every rotation until a restart. Fix: re-read on a TTL and on 401.","**Assuming Kubernetes Secrets are encrypted.** Symptom: any namespace reader dumps them. Fix: enable encryption at rest, restrict RBAC on secrets, and source them from a manager.","**Mounting a Kubernetes Secret with `subPath`, or reading it through `env`.** Symptom: the manager and the Secret show the new value but the Pod keeps the old one until it restarts, because neither path receives in-place updates (whole-volume mounts do, after a short sync delay).","**Revoking the old credential as soon as the new one is written.** Symptom: a burst of authentication failures across every consumer whose cache has not yet refreshed."],"when":"- **Always.** There is no setting where this does not apply; what varies is the tooling.\n- **Day to day:** adding a secret means creating it in the manager, referencing it by name in configuration,\n  granting one identity access, and never pasting it anywhere.\n- **Measure:** the number of static long-lived secrets, the age of the oldest, the time to rotate one end to\n  end, and scanner findings per month.\n- **Interviews:** explain the secret-zero problem and how workload identity solves it, how to rotate without\n  downtime, and, for AI roles, where model-provider keys live, why they never reach the browser or the\n  prompt, and how a prompt-injected agent could exfiltrate a credential left in its context.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Reads secrets through a resolver with TTL and refetch-on-401, adds log redaction, uses build secrets in Dockerfiles, and keeps provider keys out of tool arguments and traces.","architect":"Chooses the manager and the identity model per environment, defines the rotation policy and key-per-service boundaries, and sets the rule that credentials resolve in the tool runtime rather than in the model's context.","fde":"Fits the product into the customer's Vault or cloud KMS, gets workload identity granted in their cluster, and answers the security questionnaire's questions on storage, rotation and audit."},"check":{"q":"A deployment on Kubernetes stores the model-provider key in a Kubernetes Secret created by hand,\nreads it into an environment variable, and the same key is used by the API, the batch evaluator and a\ndeveloper's notebook. What are the risks, and how would you fix it in order of impact?","a":"First, the key is shared, so a leak from the notebook exposes production and rotation would\nbreak all three consumers at once; issue a per-service key with its own spend cap, kept behind a\ngateway. Second, the Kubernetes Secret is base64 in etcd, unencrypted unless at-rest encryption is\nconfigured, and readable by anyone with namespace access; source it from a managed secrets store through\nthe External Secrets Operator or CSI driver, enable etcd encryption, and restrict RBAC on secrets. Third,\nthe environment variable is frozen and inherited by child processes; mount it as a file and re-read on a\nTTL so rotation needs no restart. Fourth, no identity story exists for the workload; use IRSA or workload\nidentity so the pod authenticates to the manager without a stored credential. Finally, add scanning,\nredaction and a read-audit alert, and rotate the current key now because it has lived in a notebook."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the \"secret zero\" problem and what solves it?","options":["The first secret created in a vault is unencrypted until the vault is sealed; sealing solves it","A workload needs a credential to fetch its credentials; platform-attested identity (IAM roles, workload identity, OIDC federation) solves it without a stored secret","Environment variables are numbered from zero and the first one is visible to all users","The root token of a secrets manager cannot be rotated; a hardware module solves it"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If the bootstrap credential is a static key, the manager only relocates the problem. Identity that the platform asserts about the running workload breaks the chain, because nothing durable exists to steal."},{"q":"Why are Kubernetes Secret objects not a sufficient secrets store on their own?","options":["They can hold at most one value per object","They are only available to pods on the control-plane nodes","They are base64-encoded, unencrypted in etcd unless at-rest encryption is configured, and readable by any identity with namespace secret access","They cannot be mounted as files, only as environment variables"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Base64 is an encoding, not encryption. Without an EncryptionConfiguration and tight RBAC, a namespace reader or an etcd backup exposes every value; a managed store feeding Secrets fixes the storage and audit gap."},{"q":"An agent's tool for querying a customer's CRM needs an API token. Where should the token be handled?","options":["Passed to the model in the system prompt so it can include it in tool calls","Returned to the model inside the tool's JSON result for subsequent calls","Resolved inside the tool runtime from the secrets store, keyed by tenant, so it never enters the model's context","Stored in the conversation memory so multi-turn sessions can reuse it"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Anything in the model's context can be echoed back through prompt injection or captured in traces. The tool runtime looks the credential up by the tenant and the tool, uses it, and returns only data."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/docker","sources":["Official docs: Docker","Open Container Initiative — Image Format Specification","Official docs: NVIDIA Container Toolkit"],"why":"- **A service depends on far more than `requirements.txt`.** The interpreter version, system libraries such\n  as glibc and OpenSSL, compiled wheels built against a specific CUDA version, locale settings, environment\n  variables.\n- **Hand or scripted installs drift.** Two servers provisioned three months apart differ in ways nobody\n  recorded, \"works on my machine\" is a literal description of the problem, and rolling back means\n  uninstalling.\n- **VMs fix isolation at a high price.** A VM image is gigabytes with its own kernel, boots in tens of\n  seconds to minutes, and one per service wastes memory and makes dev/prod parity painful, so people share\n  VMs and the drift returns.\n- **A process does not need its own kernel to be isolated.** Linux namespaces control what a process can\n  *see* (process tree, network interfaces, mount table); cgroups control what it can *use* (CPU, memory,\n  I/O). Add an immutable, content-hashed filesystem and a registry, and the artifact becomes the deployment\n  unit: no install step, no drift, rollback is pointing at the previous image.\n- **For AI it ends \"which torch wheel matches which CUDA\".** A virtualenv plus setup script pins Python\n  packages but nothing beneath them, and CUDA libraries, system codecs and build toolchains are exactly\n  what breaks. One image runs identically on a laptop GPU, a CI runner and a Kubernetes GPU node.","how":"### Images: layers and content addressing\n- **Each `RUN`, `COPY` and `ADD` produces a layer:** a tarball of filesystem changes relative to the previous\n  layer, identified by the hash of its contents.\n- **An image is an ordered list of layer digests plus a config** (entrypoint, environment, exposed ports,\n  user).\n- **Layers are immutable and shared.** Twenty services built on the same `python:3.12-slim` base hold one copy\n  of those layers on disk and in the registry, and a push uploads only layers the registry lacks.\n- **At run time a union filesystem** (overlay2 on most hosts) stacks the layers read-only and adds a thin\n  writable layer per container. Writes there disappear with the container, which is *why* durable state goes\n  in volumes, not the container filesystem.\n\n### The build cache\n1. Each instruction's result is cached, keyed on the instruction and its inputs (for `COPY`, the content of\n   the copied files).\n2. The first instruction whose inputs changed invalidates every later layer.\n3. So ordering is a performance decision: copy the dependency manifest, install, then copy source,\n   *because* source changes every commit and dependencies every few weeks. Get it backwards and every commit\n   reinstalls torch.\n\n### Containers: namespaces and cgroups\n| Mechanism | What `docker run` gives the process |\n|---|---|\n| PID namespace | Sees itself as PID 1 |\n| Network namespace | Own interfaces and port space |\n| Mount namespace | Its root is the image |\n| UTS, IPC, optional user namespace | Own hostname, IPC objects, optionally remapped users |\n| cgroup | CPU and memory caps; exceeding `--memory` triggers the kernel OOM killer: exit code 137, no traceback |\n| Capabilities and seccomp | Most Linux capabilities dropped; a seccomp profile filters syscalls |\n\n- **It remains a normal process.** `ps` on the host shows it, a kernel vulnerability escapes it, and it\n  starts in milliseconds.\n- **Good enough for your own services, not for untrusted code,** which is why agent sandboxes use stronger\n  boundaries such as gVisor or microVMs.\n\n### PID 1 and signals\n- **The kernel treats PID 1 specially:** default signal dispositions do not apply, so a process without an\n  explicit SIGTERM handler ignores `docker stop` and is SIGKILLed when the grace period (10 seconds by\n  default) expires.\n- **Use the exec form** `CMD [\"uvicorn\", ...]`, not the shell form `CMD uvicorn ...`, *because* the shell\n  form wraps the command in `/bin/sh -c`; unless that shell happens to `exec` the command (behaviour varies by\n  shell and command), the shell is PID 1 and does not forward the signal.\n- **If the app spawns children,** run under `--init` or `tini` so zombies are reaped.\n- **Why it matters later:** this plumbing is what makes graceful draining of an in-flight streaming response\n  possible in Kubernetes.\n\n### Tags, digests and registries\n- **A tag** such as `:1.4.2` or `:latest` is a mutable pointer; **the digest** `sha256:…` is the immutable\n  identity.\n- **Deploy by digest or an immutable tag like the git SHA,** because `:latest` on two nodes can mean two\n  different images.\n\n### Multi-stage builds\nA build stage with compilers, headers and dev tools produces artifacts; the final stage copies only those\ninto a slim runtime base. The toolchain never ships, the image shrinks, and the attack surface with it.\n\n### GPUs\n- **Containers see no GPU by default.** The NVIDIA Container Toolkit injects device nodes and the host\n  driver's user-space libraries when you run with `--gpus all`.\n- **The split:** the image carries the CUDA runtime and torch; the host carries the kernel driver, which must\n  be new enough for the image's CUDA version.\n- **Size is cold-start time.** ML images are large (several gigabytes is typical, as an order of magnitude),\n  so base-image choice and layer ordering set pull time, which becomes cold-start time when autoscaling.\n\n### Compose\n`docker compose` runs several containers (API, Redis, Postgres, a vector database) on one network with named\nvolumes from a single file: the local development stack that mirrors production topology.\n\n### What \"Docker\" actually is, and the alternatives\n\"Docker\" names a stack whose pieces are separable *because* the Open Container Initiative standardised them.\n\n| Piece | Role |\n|---|---|\n| `docker` CLI → `dockerd` daemon | User interface and API |\n| BuildKit | Builds |\n| containerd → runc | Runs containers; runc sets up namespaces and cgroups |\n| OCI image and runtime specs | Any OCI runtime runs a Docker-built image |\n| containerd or CRI-O via the Container Runtime Interface | What Kubernetes nodes use; Docker not the runtime since dockershim was removed (v1.24) |\n| Podman | Largely compatible CLI, no central root daemon; common where rootless is required |\n| Buildah, kaniko | Daemonless builds, suited to CI runners that must not expose a privileged Docker socket |\n\n- **On a Kubernetes node** Docker-built images run unchanged, but `docker ps` shows nothing.\n- **The choice rarely changes the Dockerfile;** it changes who needs root on the build machine.","example":"### Multi-stage Dockerfile for a FastAPI inference service\nBuilt with `uv`:\n- **Dependencies are installed from the lockfile in their own layer** before the source is copied.\n- **The runtime stage contains only the virtualenv and code,** and the process runs as a non-root user.\n- **The exec-form `CMD` makes uvicorn PID 1** so it receives SIGTERM.\n- **Pin the `uv` image** to a specific version in a real repository.\n\n```dockerfile\n# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1\nFROM python:3.12-slim AS builder\nCOPY --from=ghcr.io/astral-sh/uv:latest /uv /bin/uv\nWORKDIR /app\n# Dependencies first: this layer is rebuilt only when the lockfile changes.\nCOPY pyproject.toml uv.lock ./\nRUN --mount=type=cache,target=/root/.cache/uv \\\n    uv sync --frozen --no-dev --no-install-project\nCOPY src/ ./src/\nRUN --mount=type=cache,target=/root/.cache/uv uv sync --frozen --no-dev\n\nFROM python:3.12-slim AS runtime\nRUN useradd --create-home --uid 10001 app\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY --from=builder /app/.venv /app/.venv\nCOPY --from=builder /app/src /app/src\nENV PATH=\"/app/.venv/bin:$PATH\" PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1\nUSER app\nEXPOSE 8000\nHEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=3s \\\n  CMD python -c \"import urllib.request as u; u.urlopen('http://127.0.0.1:8000/health')\" || exit 1\n# Exec form: uvicorn is PID 1 and receives SIGTERM from `docker stop`.\nCMD [\"uvicorn\", \"src.app:app\", \"--host\", \"0.0.0.0\", \"--port\", \"8000\"]\n```\n\nBuild and run it with an immutable tag and a memory cap. A `.dockerignore` listing `.git`, `.venv`, `data/`\nand model weights keeps the build context small and the `COPY` cache stable.\n\n```bash\ndocker build -t llm-api:$(git rev-parse --short HEAD) .\ndocker run --rm -p 8000:8000 --memory=2g -e LLM_SECRET_ID=prod/llm llm-api:$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\ndocker history llm-api:$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)   # where did the bytes go?\n```\n\n### A PID 1 that drains on SIGTERM\nRunnable with the standard library. As PID 1 this process gets no default SIGTERM behaviour, so it installs\na handler, finishes in-flight work and exits 0 well inside the 10-second grace period.\n\n```python\nimport signal, sys, threading, time\n\nstop = threading.Event()\n\ndef on_term(signum, frame):\n    print(\"SIGTERM received: stop accepting work, finish in-flight\", flush=True)\n    stop.set()\n\nsignal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, on_term)\nprint(\"serving\", flush=True)\nwhile not stop.is_set():\n    time.sleep(0.2)            # stand-in for serving requests\ntime.sleep(1)                  # stand-in for draining one in-flight stream\nprint(\"drained, exiting 0\", flush=True)\nsys.exit(0)\n# Try it: python drain.py & sleep 1; kill -TERM $!   (in a container: docker stop <name>)\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: reading exit codes after a restart\nContainer exit codes above 128 mean \"killed by signal (code minus 128)\". That turns a silent restart into a\ndiagnosis.\n\n| Exit code | Signal | Usual cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0 | none | Clean exit after handling SIGTERM | Nothing to fix |\n| 137 | 9 (SIGKILL) | Memory over `--memory` (OOM killer), or SIGTERM ignored until the grace period ended | Raise the limit above real peak, or add a handler and exec-form `CMD` |\n| 143 | 15 (SIGTERM) | Process died from SIGTERM with default handling | Add a handler that drains before exiting |\n| 1 | none | Application error | Read `docker logs` for the traceback |\n\n- **Telling the two 137 cases apart:** `docker inspect` reports `OOMKilled: true` for memory kills; a stop\n  that took exactly ten seconds points at signal handling.","patterns":["**Deploy by digest.** Tag images with the git SHA, then record the pushed digest (shown by `docker push`, or `docker inspect --format '{{index .RepoDigests 0}}' <image>`) in the deploy manifest. Monitor which digest runs in each environment so \"what is in prod\" has one answer.","**Shared build cache in CI.** Export BuildKit cache to the registry with `docker buildx build --cache-to type=registry,ref=<repo>:buildcache,mode=max --cache-from type=registry,ref=<repo>:buildcache`, so fresh runners skip unchanged dependency layers. Track cached versus uncached build time.","**Scan and gate images.** Run an image scanner such as Trivy (`trivy image --severity CRITICAL,HIGH --exit-code 1 <image>`) in CI and fail on fixable critical findings. Monitor open critical CVEs per base image and rebuild on base-image updates.","**Harden the runtime.** Run as a non-root `USER`, add `--read-only` with a `--tmpfs /tmp`, and `--cap-drop ALL`, adding back only what the service needs. Watch for permission errors in logs after enabling, which reveal unexpected writes.","**Keep weights out of the image.** Ship code and runtime only; load weights from object storage or a persistent volume with a node-local cache, and pre-pull large images on GPU nodes. Measure pull time and model-load time separately."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    DF[\"Dockerfile\"] --> BK[\"BuildKit: one layer per step\"]\n    BK --> IMG[\"Image: layer digests plus config\"]\n    IMG -->|\"push missing layers\"| REG[(\"Registry\")]\n    REG -->|\"pull by digest\"| N[\"Node: containerd and runc\"]\n    N --> C[\"Container process, PID 1\"]\n    C --- NS[\"Namespaces: what it sees\"]\n    C --- CG[\"cgroups: what it uses\"]\n    C --> WL[\"Thin writable layer, lost on exit\"]\n    C -.->|\"durable state\"| V[(\"Volume\")]","caption":"Follow an image from recipe to running process: build once, address by digest, and every node runs the same\nbytes with only a thin writable layer on top."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between a container and a virtual machine?","a":"- **A container is a host process** isolated by namespaces (what it sees) and cgroups (what it uses), sharing\n  the host kernel, so it starts in milliseconds.\n- **A VM boots its own kernel** on a hypervisor: stronger isolation, but gigabytes per image and boot times\n  of tens of seconds or more.\n- **So** containers suit your own services; untrusted code needs gVisor or a microVM."},{"q":"A container restarts every few hours with exit code 137 and no traceback. How do you debug it?","a":"- **137 means SIGKILL.** Check `docker inspect` (or the pod status) for `OOMKilled`; if true, the cgroup\n  memory limit was exceeded.\n- **Correlate with workload:** model load or large batches usually drive the peak; measure real peak memory\n  and set the limit above it, or bound batch size.\n- **If not OOM,** look for a stop that hit the grace period because SIGTERM was ignored."},{"q":"Why does instruction order in a Dockerfile change build time but not image size?","a":"- **The cache invalidates every layer after the first changed input,** so installing dependencies before\n  copying source lets a code change rebuild only the final cheap layers.\n- **Size is the sum of layers either way,** so swapping the two steps leaves the image essentially the same\n  size; only rebuild time changes."},{"q":"When would you not bake model weights into the image?","a":"- **When images get large,** because multi-gigabyte layers make every new node pull for minutes and turn\n  autoscaling into an image-size problem.\n- **When weights change on a different cadence** from code, so a model update does not need a code release.\n- **Instead** load from object storage or a persistent volume with a node-local cache, keeping the image to\n  code and runtime."},{"q":"Why is mounting the Docker socket into a CI job dangerous, and what do you use instead?","a":"- **The socket is root on the host:** anything that can talk to it can start a privileged container that\n  mounts the host filesystem.\n- **Use a daemonless or rootless builder** such as kaniko, Buildah or rootless BuildKit, which build OCI\n  images without a privileged daemon."}],"tradeoffs":["**Reproducible images vs pull time.** Deploys are reproducible and rollback is a pointer change, but a multi-gigabyte CUDA image takes minutes to pull onto a fresh node, so autoscaling latency becomes an image-size problem. The fix flips toward runtime bases, pre-pulled nodes and weights outside the image.","**Shared kernel vs isolation.** Near-native performance and millisecond startup compared with a VM, but weaker isolation; for code you did not write, such as agent-generated scripts, the balance flips to gVisor or microVM sandboxes.","**Layer caching vs hidden inputs.** Rebuilds are fast when instructions are ordered well, but the cache only knows the inputs the Dockerfile shows it; an `apt-get update` cached from last month or a `pip install` without a lockfile yields different images on different days.","**One container vs split model server.** API plus model in one container is simple to run; splitting the model server from the API adds a network hop in exchange for scaling and restarting the two independently."],"pitfalls":["**Shell-form `CMD` or no SIGTERM handler.** Symptom: `docker stop` takes exactly ten seconds and in-flight requests die; later, rolling updates drop streams.","**Running as root.** Symptom: every security review flags it, and any code-execution bug becomes root inside the container. Fix: add a `USER`.","**No `.dockerignore`.** Symptom: builds send gigabytes of context (`.git`, `.venv`, datasets, weights) and miss the cache every time because a `COPY` input changed.","**Copying source before installing dependencies.** Symptom: every one-line change reinstalls torch for several minutes.","**A `:latest` base image or unpinned dependencies.** Symptom: a rebuild of the \"same\" Dockerfile produces a different image that fails, usually on the day of an incident.","**Writing state inside the container.** Symptom: data vanishes on restart, plus slow writes through the overlay filesystem.","**CUDA runtime newer than the host driver supports.** Symptom: a \"driver version is insufficient for CUDA runtime version\" error on one node pool and not another.","**A memory limit below real peak usage.** Symptom: silent restarts with exit code 137 and no Python traceback, typically during model load or a large batch.","**Mounting `/var/run/docker.sock` into a CI or agent container \"so it can build\".** Symptom: anything in that container can start a privileged container and own the host. Fix: a rootless or daemonless builder."],"when":"- **Use when** you deploy anything more than once or onto more than one machine: inference APIs, workers,\n  eval jobs, fine-tuning runs.\n- **Skip it** for a notebook on your laptop; serverless platforms that accept source or a zip build the image\n  for you.\n- **Measure:** image size, cached and uncached build time, pull time, and time from container start to ready;\n  watch each when a deploy feels slow.\n- **Day to day:** write Dockerfiles, debug with `docker exec`, `docker logs` and `docker history`, and run\n  the local stack with Compose.\n- **Interviews:** explain layers versus containers, namespaces versus cgroups, why PID 1 matters, and why\n  instruction order changes build time.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the multi-stage Dockerfile, pins the base image and lockfile, handles signals for graceful shutdown, keeps the CUDA and torch versions consistent, and keeps the image small.","architect":"Decides what lives in the image versus mounted at runtime (weights, config), sets the base-image and registry-scanning policy, and chooses the isolation level for untrusted execution.","fde":"Ships the product as images into the customer's registry or air-gapped environment, and works around their driver versions, proxies and approved-base-image restrictions."},"check":{"q":"Your inference image is about 9 GB and each new node takes six minutes to become ready during\nautoscaling. Walk through what you would investigate and change.","a":"Start with `docker history` to see where the bytes are; it is usually CUDA toolkit layers, torch\nwheels and model weights copied into the image. In order of payoff: move the weights out of the image\nonto object storage or a persistent volume with a node-local cache, so the image is only code and\nruntime; switch to a CUDA runtime base instead of a devel base and use a multi-stage build so compilers\nnever ship; drop unused dependencies; and order layers so the heavy, stable ones (base plus\ndependencies) are shared across versions, so a node that pulled last week's image already has most of\nthis week's. Then attack the platform side: pre-pull images with a DaemonSet, keep a warm node pool,\nand use a registry in the same region. Finally separate pull time from model-load time in the\nmeasurements, because the second needs a startup probe and a weight cache rather than a smaller image."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should a Dockerfile copy the dependency manifest and install before copying the application source?","options":["Because the source files need the installed packages present at `COPY` time","Because the first changed layer invalidates every later cached layer, so putting rarely-changing dependency installation first lets most rebuilds skip it","Because Docker requires all `COPY` instructions to appear at the end of a build","Because installing dependencies after the source makes the final image larger"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The cache is keyed on each instruction and its inputs, in order. Source changes on every commit, dependencies rarely, so installing first means a code change rebuilds only the cheap final layers. Swapping these two steps leaves the final image essentially the same size; only rebuild time changes."},{"q":"A container ignores `docker stop` and is killed after ten seconds. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The container's memory limit is set too low","The image was built without a `HEALTHCHECK`","The main process is PID 1 and does not handle SIGTERM, for example because `CMD` used shell form so `/bin/sh` received the signal","The container is running as root"],"answer":2,"explanation":"PID 1 has no default signal handlers, so SIGTERM is dropped unless the process installs one, and the shell form typically leaves a shell as PID 1 that does not forward it. Memory limits produce OOM kills, not stop timeouts, and health checks do not affect signal delivery."},{"q":"What does a container share with its host that a virtual machine does not?","options":["The root filesystem","The environment variables of the host shell","The kernel","The network namespace"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A container is a process isolated by namespaces and cgroups on the host's kernel, which is why it starts in milliseconds and why a kernel exploit escapes it. Its root filesystem comes from the image and it gets its own network namespace by default."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/kubernetes","sources":["Official docs: Kubernetes","Burns, Grant, Oppenheimer, Brewer, Wilkes, 2016 — Borg, Omega, and Kubernetes","Verma et al., 2015 — Large-scale cluster management at Google with Borg"],"why":"- **Docker answers \"what to run\", not \"where and how\".** Which machine fits its CPU, memory and GPU needs;\n  what happens when it crashes at 3 a.m. or its node dies; how a new version replaces the old without\n  downtime; how service A finds B's current IP as replicas come and go; how to go from two replicas to\n  twenty; how configuration and secrets reach the process.\n- **SSH plus `docker run` fails in a predictable order.** Nothing reconciles: a dead container stays dead\n  unless you wrote the watchdog. The real state lives in people's heads and shell history, so no one can say\n  what runs where. Compose on a few large hosts has the same gaps.\n- **Humans do not bin-pack.** Manual placement leaves expensive GPU nodes half empty while others are\n  oversubscribed; bin-packing is what makes GPUs affordable. Every scale, update and rollback becomes a\n  bespoke procedure that differs the second time it runs.\n- **Borg's idea, generalised.** Google's Borg had operators declare desired state and controllers converge\n  the cluster toward it, forever. Kubernetes is the open-source generalisation.\n- **Declarations beat commands** (\"this should exist\" vs \"start this here\"), *because* declarations can be\n  reconciled after failures, versioned in git, and reasoned about without knowing every machine's state.","how":"### Control plane and reconciliation\n| Component | Job |\n|---|---|\n| API server | The only component that reads and writes etcd, where every object lives |\n| Controllers | Each watches one kind of object and acts to make reality match its spec |\n| Scheduler | Assigns Pods with no node to a node whose free resources satisfy their requests |\n| kubelet (every node) | Starts containers through the container runtime and reports status |\n| kube-proxy and CNI plugin | Networking |\n\n- **`kubectl apply` executes nothing.** It changes desired state in the API server, and controllers converge\n  toward it, repeatedly. That is why a deleted Pod comes back and why a `docker run` on a node is invisible to\n  the cluster.\n\nEvery controller is the same loop, run on every change event and on a periodic resync:\n\n```\nloop:\n    desired = read spec from API server        # e.g. replicas: 3, image: v2\n    actual  = observe status                   # e.g. 2 Pods running v1, 1 Pending\n    diff    = desired - actual\n    act on diff (create, delete, update)       # one step toward desired, not a script to the end\n    write status back\n```\n\n- **Level-triggered:** it acts on the current difference, not on the event that caused it, *because* events\n  are lost when components restart; a controller that wakes after a crash simply recomputes the diff.\n- **No \"done\" signal from `apply`;** watch status (`kubectl rollout status`) to learn when reality caught up.\n\n### The objects you actually use\n| Object | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| **Pod** | One or more containers sharing a network namespace and volumes; the unit of scheduling; ephemeral, almost never created directly in production (a controller owns it) |\n| **Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pods** | Replica count, Pod template and rollout strategy |\n| **Service** | Stable virtual IP and DNS name load-balancing to Pods matching a label selector, membership gated by readiness; an Ingress or Gateway brings HTTP in from outside |\n| **ConfigMap**, **Secret** | Configuration injected as environment variables or mounted files |\n| **Job**, **CronJob** | Run-to-completion work: evals, batch embedding, fine-tuning |\n| **StatefulSet** | Stable identity and storage (vector databases) |\n| **DaemonSet** | One Pod per node (the GPU device plugin, a log shipper) |\n| **Namespace**, RBAC, **ResourceQuota** | Sharing a cluster across teams |\n\nHow a Deployment rolls out:\n1. Changing the image creates a new ReplicaSet, scaled up while the old one scales down, bounded by\n   `maxSurge` and `maxUnavailable`.\n2. Old ReplicaSets are kept at zero replicas, which is what makes `kubectl rollout undo` fast.\n3. **Kubernetes does not roll back on its own.** A rollout whose new Pods never become ready simply stalls\n   (with `maxUnavailable: 0`, harmlessly) and is marked failed after `progressDeadlineSeconds`.\n4. Reverting is a human, pipeline or progressive-delivery tool (Argo Rollouts, Flagger) decision.\n\n### Scheduling: requests, limits and GPUs\n- **Requests are what the scheduler reserves** on a node; **limits are what the cgroup enforces** at run time.\n- **Over a CPU limit** the container is throttled; **over a memory limit** it is OOM-killed (exit code 137).\n- **GPUs** are exposed by the NVIDIA device plugin as the extended resource `nvidia.com/gpu`: whole integers,\n  request equal to limit, no overcommit, *because* a GPU cannot be time-shared transparently the way CPU can.\n- **GPU node pools are usually tainted** so only Pods with a matching toleration land there, keeping CPU-only\n  work off expensive hardware.\n- **Fractional sharing** needs MIG partitions or time-slicing configured on the node.\n\n### Quality-of-service classes\nRequests and limits also set who dies first when a node runs short of memory.\n\n| QoS class | Rule | Evicted under memory pressure |\n|---|---|---|\n| **BestEffort** | No requests or limits at all | First |\n| **Burstable** | Anything in between | Next, those using more than they requested |\n| **Guaranteed** | Every container has CPU and memory requests equal to limits | Last |\n\n- **Why Guaranteed goes last:** a Pod that reserved exactly what it uses has made no claim on anyone else's\n  memory.\n- **The catch:** Guaranteed requires a CPU limit, which reintroduces throttling; the example below is\n  Burstable on purpose.\n\n### Probes: the contract that makes rollouts safe\n| Probe | Question | On failure | Rule |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Startup** | Has it finished starting (loading weights)? | Other probes stay paused | Needed, *because* otherwise liveness kills the Pod mid-load: CrashLoopBackOff |\n| **Readiness** | Should it get traffic? | Removed from Service endpoints, not restarted | Overload or a dependency outage should shed traffic, not restart the process |\n| **Liveness** | Should it be restarted? | Container restarted | Cheap; never depends on downstream systems, or a database incident becomes an API restart storm |\n\n### Termination\n1. The Pod is marked Terminating.\n2. It is removed from Service endpoints, asynchronously, so traffic can still arrive for a moment.\n3. Its `preStop` hook runs.\n4. PID 1 receives SIGTERM.\n5. After `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` (30 by default; the clock starts before `preStop`, so the hook's time\n   counts against it) it receives SIGKILL.\n\n- **The server must** stop accepting, drain in-flight requests including streams, and exit.\n- **For LLM responses that run for minutes,** raise the grace period and add a short `preStop` sleep so\n  endpoint removal propagates first.\n\n### Scaling\n- **The Horizontal Pod Autoscaler** changes replica counts on CPU, memory or custom metrics.\n- **CPU is a poor signal for GPU-bound services;** scale on queue depth, in-flight requests or tokens waiting,\n  through KEDA or custom metrics.\n- **Node autoscaling is slow for GPUs.** The Cluster Autoscaler or Karpenter adds nodes, but a GPU node takes\n  minutes to boot and pull a multi-gigabyte image, so reactive scaling needs headroom or a warm pool.\n\n### AI workloads specifically\n| Where weights live | Trade |\n|---|---|\n| Baked into the image | Simple, slow to pull |\n| Shared read-only volume | Fast start, shared dependency |\n| Init container fetches from object storage into an `emptyDir` with a node-local cache | Small image, cache warms per node |\n\n- **Serving stacks** such as vLLM or KServe run as Deployments with the pattern above.\n- **Distributed training or inference** runs on Ray via KubeRay.\n- **A PodDisruptionBudget** stops node maintenance from evicting every replica at once.","example":"### Deployment and Service for a GPU-backed inference API\n- **Capacity:** the rollout never drops below three ready replicas.\n- **Startup:** the startup probe allows up to ten minutes of model loading before liveness checks begin.\n- **Traffic:** the readiness endpoint gates it.\n- **Shutdown:** the grace period plus `preStop` sleep let long streaming responses finish before SIGKILL.\n- **Image by digest,** so every node runs the same bytes.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata: {name: llm-api}\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  strategy: {rollingUpdate: {maxSurge: 1, maxUnavailable: 0}}   # never below 3 ready\n  selector: {matchLabels: {app: llm-api}}\n  template:\n    metadata: {labels: {app: llm-api}}\n    spec:\n      terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 120       # let long streaming responses finish\n      nodeSelector: {accelerator: nvidia-l4}\n      tolerations: [{key: nvidia.com/gpu, operator: Exists, effect: NoSchedule}]\n      containers:\n      - name: api\n        image: registry.example.com/llm-api@sha256:3f9c...   # immutable digest, not :latest\n        ports: [{containerPort: 8000}]\n        envFrom: [{secretRef: {name: llm-api-secrets}}]   # frozen at start; mount as files for hot rotation\n        resources:\n          requests: {cpu: \"2\", memory: 8Gi, nvidia.com/gpu: 1}\n          limits:   {memory: 8Gi, nvidia.com/gpu: 1}          # no CPU limit: avoids throttling\n        startupProbe:                            # model load may take minutes\n          httpGet: {path: /health, port: 8000}\n          periodSeconds: 10\n          failureThreshold: 60                   # up to 10 min before giving up\n        readinessProbe:\n          httpGet: {path: /ready, port: 8000}    # 200 only after the model is loaded and warm\n          periodSeconds: 5\n        livenessProbe:\n          httpGet: {path: /health, port: 8000}   # cheap, no downstream calls\n          periodSeconds: 30\n        lifecycle:\n          preStop: {exec: {command: [\"sleep\", \"5\"]}}   # let endpoint removal propagate\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata: {name: llm-api}\nspec:\n  selector: {app: llm-api}\n  ports: [{port: 80, targetPort: 8000}]\n```\n\n### A level-triggered reconcile loop you can run\nA toy Deployment controller with `maxSurge` 1. Each step looks only at the current state, so when a node\ndies mid-rollout it does not need to replay the lost event: the next diff simply includes the missing Pod.\n\n```python\nMAX_SURGE = 1\ndesired = {\"replicas\": 3, \"image\": \"v2\"}\npods = [\"v1\", \"v1\", \"v1\"]                      # observed: 3 Pods on the old image\n\ndef reconcile(pods):\n    \"\"\"One level-triggered step: compute the current diff and take ONE action.\"\"\"\n    new = [p for p in pods if p == desired[\"image\"]]\n    old = [p for p in pods if p != desired[\"image\"]]\n    if len(new) < desired[\"replicas\"] and len(pods) < desired[\"replicas\"] + MAX_SURGE:\n        return pods + [desired[\"image\"]], \"create new Pod\"\n    if old:\n        pods = pods.copy(); pods.remove(old[0])\n        return pods, \"delete one old Pod\"\n    if len(new) > desired[\"replicas\"]:\n        return pods[1:], \"scale down extra Pod\"\n    return pods, \"in sync\"\n\nfor step in range(10):\n    if step == 3:\n        pods = pods[1:]                        # a node dies: one Pod vanishes, no event replayed\n        print(\"   !! lost a Pod:\", pods)\n    pods, action = reconcile(pods)\n    print(f\"{step}: {action:22} {pods}\")\n    if action == \"in sync\":\n        break\n# Converges to ['v2', 'v2', 'v2'] in six steps despite the lost Pod.\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: the termination and startup timelines of the manifest above\nNumbers come straight from the manifest and the defaults.\n\n| Moment | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| t = 0 s | Pod marked Terminating; endpoint removal starts propagating; `preStop` `sleep 5` begins |\n| t = 5 s | `preStop` ends; uvicorn (PID 1) gets SIGTERM, stops accepting, drains streams |\n| t = 120 s | SIGKILL if still running; the budget includes the 5 s `preStop` |\n| Startup budget | `periodSeconds: 10` × `failureThreshold: 60` = 600 s, the \"up to 10 min\" in the comment |\n| Default liveness, no startup probe | about 30 seconds of failed checks before a restart, so a 4-minute model load loops forever |\n| Rollout with 3 replicas, `maxSurge: 1` | Needs a 4th GPU free during the rollout; capacity never dips below 3 ready |","patterns":["**PodDisruptionBudget for every serving Deployment.** Add a `policy/v1` PodDisruptionBudget with `minAvailable: 2` and the same label selector, so node drains and upgrades evict one replica at a time. Watch `kubectl get pdb` for \"allowed disruptions\" stuck at 0, which blocks maintenance.","**Autoscale GPU services on queue depth.** Use KEDA or the HPA with a custom metric (requests waiting or in flight per replica) instead of CPU, and keep a warm node or spare replica because GPU nodes take minutes. Monitor queue depth, Pending Pods and time from scale-up to ready.","**Dedicated, tainted GPU node pools.** Taint GPU nodes `nvidia.com/gpu:NoSchedule`, give GPU Pods the matching toleration and a `nodeSelector`, and set a ResourceQuota on `nvidia.com/gpu` per namespace. Compare GPU allocation with actual utilization (for example from NVIDIA's DCGM exporter).","**Progressive delivery with automatic abort.** Run rollouts through Argo Rollouts or Flagger as a canary with an analysis step on 5xx rate and latency, so the tool reverts instead of a human at 3 a.m. Track rollout duration and aborted rollouts.","**Event-first debugging.** Start every incident with `kubectl describe pod <name>` and `kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp`, which show `Insufficient nvidia.com/gpu`, OOMKilled and probe failures before any log does. Alert on restart counts and Pods Pending beyond a few minutes."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    U[\"kubectl apply\"] -->|\"write desired spec\"| API[\"API server\"]\n    API <--> E[(\"etcd\")]\n    API -->|\"watch\"| CTL[\"Deployment and ReplicaSet controllers\"]\n    CTL -->|\"create Pods\"| API\n    API -->|\"unscheduled Pods\"| SCH[\"Scheduler: fit requests\"]\n    SCH -->|\"bind to node\"| API\n    API -->|\"Pods for this node\"| KL[\"kubelet and runtime\"]\n    KL -->|\"report status\"| API\n    CTL -.->|\"diff desired vs actual, repeat\"| CTL","caption":"Nothing in the cluster runs your command: `apply` only edits stored state, and the loop on the right keeps\npulling reality toward it, including after failures."},"interview":[{"q":"What does \"level-triggered reconciliation\" mean, and why does Kubernetes use it?","a":"- **Definition:** each controller compares desired spec with observed state and acts on the current\n  difference, not on the event that caused it.\n- **Why:** events are lost when components restart; a controller that wakes after a crash recomputes the diff\n  and carries on, which is why deleted Pods come back.\n- **Consequence:** `apply` gives no \"done\"; you watch `kubectl rollout status`."},{"q":"A GPU Pod has been Pending for 20 minutes. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Read the events** with `kubectl describe pod`: `Insufficient nvidia.com/gpu` means no node has a free\n  whole GPU; a taint message means the toleration or `nodeSelector` is wrong.\n- **Check supply:** is the device plugin DaemonSet running on GPU nodes, is the node pool at its max, is the\n  node autoscaler able to add a node?\n- **Check quotas:** a namespace ResourceQuota on `nvidia.com/gpu` can block it too."},{"q":"When would you set CPU limits, and when would you not?","a":"- **Without CPU limits,** latency-sensitive Pods avoid CFS throttling, and requests still guarantee their\n  share under contention.\n- **Set them** when you need Guaranteed QoS (last to be evicted) or must contain a noisy neighbour on shared\n  nodes.\n- **Always set** requests and memory limits; memory cannot be throttled, only OOM-killed."},{"q":"How do you deploy an LLM service whose responses stream for up to two minutes without cutting them?","a":"- **Readiness** returns 200 only after the model is loaded and warm, with a startup probe covering load time.\n- **Termination:** handle SIGTERM by draining, set `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` above the longest stream\n  plus the `preStop` sleep, for example 120 with a 5-second sleep.\n- **Rollout:** `maxUnavailable: 0`, `maxSurge: 1` and a PodDisruptionBudget so capacity never dips."},{"q":"Why is CPU a bad autoscaling signal for a GPU inference service?","a":"- **The work is on the GPU,** so CPU can stay low while requests queue; the HPA never scales.\n- **Scale on demand directly:** queue depth, in-flight requests or tokens waiting, via KEDA or custom metrics.\n- **Plan for lag:** GPU nodes take minutes to boot and pull, so keep headroom or a warm pool."}],"tradeoffs":["**Declarative reconciliation vs operating cost.** Self-healing and repeatable deploys, paid for with a large object model and a control plane to operate. For one service on one box a managed container service is less to learn; the balance flips once you have several services, GPUs, batch jobs and more than one team.","**`maxUnavailable: 0` vs spare GPUs.** It keeps capacity during rollouts but needs a spare GPU for the surge Pod; when GPUs are scarce you choose between a brief capacity dip and permanently reserved headroom.","**CPU limits vs latency.** They protect neighbours from a runaway process but throttle latency-sensitive Pods through the CFS quota. The common compromise: always set requests and memory limits, rarely CPU limits.","**Queue-depth autoscaling vs idle cost.** It tracks load correctly, but GPU node provisioning takes minutes, so reactive scaling needs idle headroom that costs money every hour it is not used."],"pitfalls":["**No resource requests.** Symptom: nodes overloaded and Pods evicted, or, with requests set too high, Pods stuck Pending with `Insufficient nvidia.com/gpu` in the events.","**A liveness probe that calls the model or the database.** Symptom: a restart storm during an incident that turns a slow dependency into a full outage.","**No startup probe on a service that loads weights for minutes.** Symptom: CrashLoopBackOff. Liveness kills the container mid-load (with default settings after about 30 seconds of failed checks), and the restart backoff grows toward its five-minute cap while the model never finishes loading.","**Readiness returning 200 before the model is loaded.** Symptom: a burst of 5xx responses right after every deploy.","**The default 30-second grace period with no drain logic.** Symptom: streaming responses cut mid-token on every rollout and node drain.","**Deploying a `:latest` tag.** Symptom: Pods on different nodes run different code depending on when each node last pulled.","**OOMKilled with no application log.** Symptom: restarts under load because the memory limit was set below the real peak of tokenizer plus model plus batch.","**Treating `emptyDir` or local disk as durable for an index.** Symptom: data gone after the Pod is rescheduled.","**HPA on CPU for a GPU-bound service.** Symptom: a growing queue while the autoscaler sees low CPU and never adds replicas."],"when":"- **Use when** you run several services, need GPU scheduling, run batch jobs beside serving, share\n  infrastructure across teams, or deploy into enterprises that already run it, which is most of them.\n- **Avoid when** it is a single container with modest load: a managed serverless container platform (Cloud\n  Run, AWS App Runner) or a simpler orchestrator (ECS, Nomad) costs less to operate. Those give scheduling and\n  restarts but a smaller object model, less portability and, for serverless, limited GPU and long-request\n  support.\n- **Managed is not free.** Even on EKS, GKE or AKS you own node pools, upgrades, add-ons and every manifest,\n  which is where most of the operating cost lives.\n- **Measure:** time-to-ready for a new Pod, rollout duration, restart counts, Pending Pods, and GPU allocation\n  versus actual utilization.\n- **Day to day:** `kubectl get`, `describe`, `logs` and `exec`, reading events, writing Deployments, Jobs and\n  autoscalers, packaged with Helm or Kustomize.\n- **Interviews:** explain reconciliation, how a Deployment rolls out, the three probes, requests versus\n  limits, GPU scheduling and graceful termination.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes manifests or Helm charts, sets probes, requests and grace periods correctly, and debugs CrashLoopBackOff, OOMKilled and Pending Pods from `describe` output and events.","architect":"Designs the cluster topology (GPU node pools, taints, namespaces, quotas), chooses autoscaling signals, decides where weights live, and picks managed versus self-run control planes.","fde":"Deploys into the customer's cluster with their CNI, ingress, Pod Security Standards, registry mirrors, GPU operator version and restricted RBAC, and sizes node pools with their platform team."},"check":{"q":"After each deploy of your vLLM-based service, users see a burst of errors and some streaming\nresponses are cut off, even though the Deployment uses a rolling update. What is happening and how do\nyou fix it?","a":"Two separate failures. The errors at the start come from new Pods reporting ready before the\nengine has loaded, so the Service routes to them; fix it by making `/ready` return 200 only after the\nmodel is loaded and a warm-up request succeeded, and add a startup probe with a generous\n`failureThreshold` so liveness does not kill Pods during load. The cut-off streams come from old Pods\nreceiving SIGTERM and either exiting immediately or hitting the 30-second default grace period\nmid-stream; fix it by handling SIGTERM to stop accepting and drain in-flight requests, raising\n`terminationGracePeriodSeconds` to cover the longest stream, and adding a `preStop` sleep so endpoint\nremoval propagates before shutdown begins. Set `maxUnavailable: 0` with `maxSurge: 1` so capacity never\ndips, accepting one spare GPU during rollout, and verify by watching 5xx and stream-abort metrics\nacross a rollout."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does `kubectl apply` of a Deployment manifest actually do?","options":["Starts the containers on the node your kubeconfig points at","Records desired state in the API server; controllers then create ReplicaSets and Pods and keep converging reality toward that spec","Builds the image and pushes it to the cluster's registry","Sends the manifest to every kubelet, which executes it once"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Kubernetes is declarative: `apply` changes the stored spec and the Deployment and ReplicaSet controllers do the work, repeatedly, which is why Pods are recreated after failures. Nothing builds images, and kubelets only run Pods the scheduler has bound to their node."},{"q":"Which probe should decide whether a Pod receives traffic, without restarting it?","options":["livenessProbe","readinessProbe","startupProbe","the preStop hook"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A failing readiness probe removes the Pod from Service endpoints and nothing else, which is the right response to overload or a dependency outage. Liveness failure restarts the container, the startup probe only gates the other probes during initialisation, and preStop runs at termination."},{"q":"Why do GPU Pods often sit Pending while CPU Pods on the same cluster schedule immediately?","options":["GPU Pods must be created with `kubectl run` rather than a Deployment","The scheduler only honours GPU requests in the kube-system namespace","`nvidia.com/gpu` is an integer, non-overcommittable resource exposed by the device plugin, and GPU nodes are usually tainted, so the Pod needs a free whole GPU plus a matching toleration","GPU nodes do not run the kubelet"],"answer":2,"explanation":"CPU is requested in millicores on plentiful, cheap nodes, so a CPU Pod usually fits somewhere. A GPU request needs an entire unallocated device on a node whose taint the Pod tolerates; if none is free the Pod waits until one is released or a new node is provisioned."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/ci-cd","sources":["Humble, Farley, 2010 — Continuous Delivery","Forsgren, Humble, Kim, 2018 — Accelerate","Official docs: GitHub Actions"],"why":"- **Late integration is where bugs live.** Weeks of divergent branches meet at release time, and the merge\n  is the defect.\n- **\"Passes locally\" carries no information.** Tests run on developer machines with whatever happens to be\n  installed.\n- **Manual deploys are not repeatable.** A runbook followed by a person under time pressure differs the\n  second time in some detail nobody wrote down. Nobody can say which commit is in production, and rollback\n  (\"redeploy the old one\") fails because dependency resolution has moved on.\n- **Each is a feedback-delay problem.** A defect's cost grows with the distance between the change and its\n  detection, *because* context is lost and other changes pile on top. CI shrinks that distance by integrating\n  at least daily against a build that must stay green; CD extends it to production with stages that give\n  fast, cheap feedback first and slow, expensive feedback later.\n- **Small and frequent is safer.** The DORA research in *Accelerate* found teams deploying more often with\n  shorter lead times also had *lower* change failure rates, the opposite of \"test everything manually before\n  the big release\".\n- **AI raises the stakes.** A prompt edit is a behaviour change with no type checker; a model version bump can\n  silently regress an untested task; an index rebuild changes answers without touching code. Unverified,\n  quality drift ships and users find it.","how":"### The stages of a deployment pipeline\n1. **Commit stage, minutes.** Clean runner, install from the lockfile, lint, type-check, unit tests with the\n   model client mocked or recorded, secret scan. It runs on every push, so it must be cheap and\n   deterministic; it exists to fail fast on the majority of mistakes.\n2. **Build.** Build the container image once, tag it with the git SHA, push it to a registry. The image digest\n   is now the artifact. \"Build once, promote many\" is a rule *because* rebuilding per environment can resolve\n   different dependencies or base layers, so production would run something never tested.\n3. **Acceptance.** Start real dependencies (Postgres, Redis) as service containers, run API tests against the\n   built image, contract tests against provider APIs, and a small, fixed evaluation set against the prompts\n   and model with pass thresholds.\n4. **Supply chain.** Audit dependencies, scan the image, produce a software bill of materials, and sign the\n   image so later stages can verify they deploy what this stage produced.\n5. **Staging.** Deploy automatically; run smoke tests against a production-shaped environment.\n6. **Production.** Promote the same digest, gated by an approval (delivery) or automatically (deployment),\n   progressively: a canary takes a slice of traffic while error rate, latency and online quality metrics are\n   compared with the baseline, then full rollout or automatic rollback.\n\n### Production rollout strategies\n| Strategy | How | Cost | What it detects |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Rolling** | Replace replicas a few at a time | No extra capacity beyond the surge | Little: old and new serve side by side and a bad version reaches every user before anyone compares metrics |\n| **Blue-green** | Full second environment, switch traffic at once | Double capacity during the release | Nothing that only shows under real load until all users are on it; rollback is an instant switch back |\n| **Canary** | Small share of traffic to the new version; promote only if metrics hold | Needs enough traffic for a statistically meaningful comparison | The only one that limits the blast radius of a bad release |\n\n- **Canary metrics must reflect quality:** for LLM features that means online evaluation signals, not just\n  HTTP 5xx.\n- **GitOps.** The pipeline's last step commits the new digest to a configuration repository, and a controller\n  (Argo CD, Flux) reconciles the cluster to it. The config repo becomes the audit log of what ran when, and\n  rollback is `git revert`.\n\n### Principles that make the stages work\n- **Trunk-based development with feature flags.** Small pull requests into an always-deployable main branch;\n  flags separate *deploying* code from *releasing* behaviour.\n- **Pipeline as code,** versioned next to the application, so a pipeline change is reviewed like any other.\n- **Reproducibility.** Lockfiles for dependencies, base images pinned by digest, third-party pipeline actions\n  pinned to a commit SHA rather than a mutable tag.\n- **Fast feedback.** Cache dependencies keyed on the lockfile hash, parallelise test shards, and move slow\n  suites to a nightly or on-demand lane rather than letting the commit stage grow to thirty minutes,\n  *because* a slow pipeline makes developers batch changes, the large, risky merge the pipeline exists to\n  prevent.\n- **Secrets through identity.** The CI job presents an OIDC token the cloud exchanges for a short-lived role\n  credential instead of a stored long-lived key; secrets are masked in logs and never exposed to jobs\n  triggered by untrusted pull requests.\n- **Environment protection.** Production deploys require a reviewer, a branch rule, or both.\n- **Backward-compatible changes across a deploy.** During any rolling or canary release, old and new versions\n  run at once against the same database, queue and caches, and a rollback puts the old version back on data\n  the new one wrote. Message and API formats follow the same rule.\n\n### Expand-contract for schema changes\n1. **Expand:** add the new column or table first; old code ignores it.\n2. **Migrate code:** deploy code that writes both and reads the new one.\n3. **Backfill** existing rows.\n4. **Contract:** only in a later release, drop the old shape.\n\n### What changes for AI systems\n| Concern | Practice |\n|---|---|\n| **Non-determinism** | Unit tests mock or record the client and assert on structure and control flow; quality is measured in an evaluation stage with thresholds over many cases, never with exact-match asserts on generated text |\n| **Cost and quota** | Real-model tests spend money and hit rate limits: a small smoke evaluation on every pull request, the full set on main or nightly, under a CI-only key with a spend limit |\n| **Artifacts beyond code** | Weights and indexes live in a registry or object store by version; a change to a prompt template, model version or retrieval index triggers the evaluation gate exactly as code triggers tests |\n| **GPU work** | Self-hosted runners or cluster Jobs, never the default runner |\n| **Rollback scope** | The deployable unit pins code, prompt and model version together, so a rollback reverts all three |\n\n### How you know it is working\n- **The four DORA metrics:** deployment frequency, lead time for changes, change failure rate, and time to\n  restore service.\n- **Plus pipeline duration and flake rate;** a pipeline nobody trusts is one nobody waits for.","example":"### GitHub Actions: test on every PR, build once and deploy the digest on main\n- **Every pull request** runs lint, types and mocked unit tests, plus a small smoke evaluation that only runs\n  when the secret is available (pushes and same-repository pull requests).\n- **On `main`,** the image is built once and pushed with the commit SHA as its tag, and the exact digest is\n  rolled out using short-lived credentials obtained through OIDC rather than a stored cloud key.\n- **The `production` environment** carries required-reviewer protection.\n\n```yaml\nname: ci\non:\n  pull_request:\n  push: {branches: [main]}\npermissions: {contents: read}                # least privilege by default; jobs widen it\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4             # pin to a commit SHA in a real repository\n      - uses: astral-sh/setup-uv@v5\n      - run: uv sync --frozen\n      - run: uv run ruff check . && uv run mypy src\n      - run: uv run pytest -q tests/unit      # model client mocked: deterministic, free, fast\n      - run: uv run pytest -q -m smoke tests/eval   # ~20 golden cases against a cheap model\n        if: github.event_name == 'push' || github.event.pull_request.head.repo.full_name == github.repository\n        env: {LLM_API_KEY: \"${{ secrets.CI_LLM_KEY }}\"}\n  build-and-deploy:\n    needs: test\n    if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    environment: production                  # protection rule: required reviewers\n    permissions: {contents: read, packages: write, id-token: write}   # id-token: OIDC to the cloud\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: docker/login-action@v3\n        with: {registry: ghcr.io, username: \"${{ github.actor }}\", password: \"${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}\"}\n      - id: build\n        uses: docker/build-push-action@v6\n        with: {push: true, tags: \"ghcr.io/${{ github.repository }}:${{ github.sha }}\"}   # repo name must be lowercase\n      - uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v4\n        with: {role-to-assume: \"arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/ci-deploy\", aws-region: us-east-1}\n      - run: |                                 # promote the exact digest that was built and tested\n          aws eks update-kubeconfig --name prod\n          kubectl set image deployment/llm-api api=ghcr.io/${{ github.repository }}@${{ steps.build.outputs.digest }}\n          kubectl rollout status deployment/llm-api --timeout=10m\n```\n\n### An evaluation gate as a CI step\nRunnable with the standard library. It compares this pull request's eval scores with the baseline from main\nand exits non-zero on a regression beyond tolerance, which fails the job. The scores are illustrative; in a\nreal pipeline they come from the eval run's output file.\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\nBASELINE  = {\"groundedness\": 0.91, \"format_ok\": 0.99, \"refusal_rate\": 0.04}   # from the last main run\nCANDIDATE = {\"groundedness\": 0.88, \"format_ok\": 0.99, \"refusal_rate\": 0.05}   # this pull request\nHIGHER_IS_BETTER = {\"groundedness\": True, \"format_ok\": True, \"refusal_rate\": False}\nTOLERANCE = {\"groundedness\": 0.02, \"format_ok\": 0.01, \"refusal_rate\": 0.02}   # allowed regression\n\ndef regressions(base, cand):\n    out = []\n    for m, b in base.items():\n        delta = cand[m] - b if HIGHER_IS_BETTER[m] else b - cand[m]   # negative = worse\n        if delta < -TOLERANCE[m]:\n            out.append(f\"{m}: {b:.2f} -> {cand[m]:.2f} (tolerance {TOLERANCE[m]:.2f})\")\n    return out\n\nbad = regressions(BASELINE, CANDIDATE)\nfor line in bad:\n    print(\"REGRESSION\", line)\nprint(\"eval gate:\", \"FAIL\" if bad else \"PASS\")\nsys.exit(1 if bad else 0)    # a non-zero exit fails the CI job\n# Prints: REGRESSION groundedness: 0.91 -> 0.88 (tolerance 0.02) / eval gate: FAIL\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: renaming a column without breaking a rollout\nA team wants to rename `user_name` to `display_name` on a service that deploys by rolling update.\n\n| Release | Change | Why it is safe |\n|---|---|---|\n| Wrong: one release | Rename the column and ship code that reads `display_name` | Old replicas still running mid-rollout query `user_name` and error; rollback fails because old code cannot read the new schema |\n| 1 (expand) | Add `display_name`; old code ignores it | Both versions work |\n| 2 | Code writes both columns, reads `display_name` | Rollback to release 1 still finds `user_name` populated |\n| 3 | Backfill `display_name` for old rows | No code change |\n| 4 (contract) | Drop `user_name` once nothing reads it | No running version depends on it |","patterns":["**Path-filtered evaluation gate.** Trigger the full eval job only when prompts, model config or retrieval settings change, using `on: pull_request: paths:` with entries such as `prompts/**`, and run the smoke set on everything else. Track eval-gate pass rate and the cost per run.","**Pinned actions with automated updates.** Reference third-party actions by full commit SHA and let Dependabot's `github-actions` ecosystem open pull requests to bump them. Review those bumps like code; monitor how many workflows still use mutable tags.","**One deploy at a time per environment.** Add a workflow `concurrency` group (for example `group: deploy-production` with `cancel-in-progress: false`) so two merges cannot roll out over each other. Watch queued deploy time as merge frequency grows.","**Flaky-test quarantine.** Mark a flaky test with a pytest marker that moves it to a non-blocking lane the day it flakes, with an owner and a deadline. Monitor flake rate per suite and the size of the quarantine.","**GitOps promotion by digest.** The pipeline commits the new digest to the config repository and Argo CD or Flux syncs it, so `git log` answers \"what ran when\" and `git revert` is the rollback. Alert on applications that stay out of sync."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    PR[\"Push or pull request\"] --> CS[\"Commit stage: lint, types, mocked unit tests\"]\n    CS --> B[\"Build image once, tag git SHA\"]\n    B --> AC[\"Acceptance: API tests, smoke eval gate\"]\n    AC --> SC[\"Scan, SBOM, sign digest\"]\n    SC --> ST[\"Staging deploy and smoke tests\"]\n    ST --> AP{\"Approval or auto?\"}\n    AP --> CN[\"Canary: compare to baseline\"]\n    CN -->|\"metrics hold\"| FULL[\"Full rollout\"]\n    CN -->|\"regression\"| RB[\"Automatic rollback\"]\n    CS -.->|\"fail fast\"| PR","caption":"Fast, cheap checks run first and every later stage reuses the one digest built in stage two, so what reaches\nproduction is exactly what was tested."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between continuous integration, delivery and deployment?","a":"- **Integration:** every change merges to main often and is verified by an automated build and tests on a\n  clean machine, so integration bugs surface within hours, not at release time.\n- **Delivery:** every green build becomes one immutable artifact with an automated path to production, but a\n  human presses the final button.\n- **Deployment:** the button is removed; every green build ships, usually through a canary."},{"q":"Production behaves differently from staging for the same commit. How do you investigate?","a":"- **Check the artifact:** compare the deployed image digests; if they differ, something rebuilt per\n  environment and dependency resolution drifted.\n- **Check configuration:** with identical digests the difference is runtime config, secrets, model version or\n  data, so diff those next.\n- **Fix the process:** build once, promote the digest, and pin prompt and model versions in the artifact."},{"q":"Rolling, blue-green or canary for an LLM chat feature: which would you choose?","a":"- **Canary,** because it is the only strategy that limits the blast radius of a bad release, and LLM\n  regressions are often quality changes that only real traffic reveals.\n- **It needs** enough traffic for a meaningful comparison and online quality signals, not just 5xx.\n- **Blue-green** fits when you need instant switch-back and can afford double capacity; rolling is the cheap\n  default with the least protection."},{"q":"How do you test code that calls an LLM without making CI flaky or expensive?","a":"- **Unit tests mock or record the client** and assert structure and control flow, so they are deterministic,\n  free and fast.\n- **Quality is an evaluation stage** with thresholds over many cases: a roughly 20-case smoke set per pull\n  request, the full set nightly or when prompt and model paths change.\n- **Use a CI-only key with a spend limit,** and never assert exact generated strings."},{"q":"How does a GitHub Actions job deploy to AWS without a stored access key?","a":"- **The job requests an OIDC token** (`id-token: write`), signed by GitHub, carrying claims such as repository,\n  branch and environment.\n- **An IAM role trusts that issuer** only for matching claims, and the cloud exchanges the token for\n  short-lived credentials.\n- **So nothing long-lived exists to leak;** keep the trust conditions narrow, for example main branch of one\n  repository."}],"tradeoffs":["**Eval on every PR vs cost and noise.** It catches prompt regressions early but costs money, time and attention when the eval is noisy. The balance flips to a small smoke set per pull request plus a full nightly run once the per-pull-request eval bill or wait time pushes people to batch prompt changes or skip the gate.","**Continuous deployment vs approval gates.** Removing the human gate maximises flow, but for regulated customers or high-blast-radius changes continuous delivery with an approval and a canary is the right stopping point.","**Build-once-promote-many vs config discipline.** It guarantees production runs what was tested, but forces every environment difference into runtime configuration rather than a rebuild, which demands discipline about config injection.","**GitOps vs simplicity.** An auditable, declarative rollback path, but a reconciliation delay and another controller to operate; direct `kubectl` from the pipeline is simpler for a single team and few services.","**Aggressive caching vs staleness.** Pipelines stay fast, but a stale cache produces \"green in CI, broken in prod\"; key caches on lockfile hashes and expire them on base-image changes."],"pitfalls":["**Tolerating flaky tests.** Symptom: a \"just re-run it\" culture in which a real failure is merged because red no longer means anything. Fix: quarantine flaky tests immediately and fix or delete them.","**Unit tests that call the real model and assert exact strings.** Symptom: intermittent failures that start the day the provider updates the model.","**Rebuilding the image at deploy time.** Symptom: production behaves differently from staging with the \"same\" commit because dependency resolution drifted between builds.","**Long-lived cloud keys stored as CI secrets.** Symptom: a leaked runner log or compromised action yields permanent credentials. Fix: OIDC federation.","**Third-party actions referenced by a mutable tag or branch.** Symptom: a supply-chain compromise of that action silently runs in your pipeline. Fix: pin to a commit SHA.","**Only code is versioned and rolled back.** Symptom: a bad prompt needs a hotfix pull request under pressure instead of a one-click revert.","**`pull_request_target` workflows that check out and execute the fork's code.** They run with secrets in the base repository's context. Symptom: a malicious pull request exfiltrates secrets through a build script or log line.","**A commit stage that grows past half an hour.** Symptom: rising pull-request size and lead time, because developers batch changes to avoid waiting.","**Shipping a destructive migration (rename or drop a column) in the same release as the code that needs it.** Symptom: errors from still-running old replicas during rollout, and a rollback that fails because the old code cannot read the new schema."],"when":"- **Use from day one** for any codebase with more than one contributor or more than one deploy; a five-minute\n  lint-and-test workflow is enough to start.\n- **Add stages as risk grows:** image build and scan once you deploy containers, an evaluation gate once\n  prompt or model changes affect users, canaries once traffic is large enough for a comparison to mean\n  something.\n- **Use path filters** so documentation changes skip the heavy stages.\n- **Measure:** the four DORA metrics plus pipeline duration, flake rate and eval-gate pass rate.\n- **Interviews:** explain build-once-promote-many, why small frequent deploys are safer, how you test\n  non-deterministic model code, and how a CI job authenticates to the cloud without a stored key.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the workflow, mocks the model client in unit tests, maintains the smoke evaluation set, pins actions and base images, and reads failed job logs before anyone else does.","architect":"Defines the promotion path, environments and gates (eval thresholds, approvals, canary criteria), the artifact and config repositories, and the rollback policy that covers prompt and model versions.","fde":"Fits delivery into the customer's tooling (GitLab, Jenkins, Azure DevOps), their change windows and approval requirements, and builds the evaluation gate on their data."},"check":{"q":"A pull request changes the system prompt of your RAG assistant and bumps the model version.\nDescribe what the pipeline should do before this reaches users, and how you keep it affordable.","a":"Treat it as code first: the commit stage runs lint, types and unit tests with the model mocked,\nwhich costs nothing. Because files under the prompt and model paths changed, the evaluation gate\ntriggers: a fixed golden set of a few hundred questions with graded references runs against the new\nconfiguration, scored by deterministic checks and an LLM judge, and compared with the baseline from\nmain using thresholds on groundedness, refusal rate and output format; the pull request fails if it\nregresses beyond tolerance. Keep cost down by running a twenty-case smoke subset on every push and the\nfull set only when those paths change or a label is applied, under a CI-only key with a spend limit.\nThen build the image once, deploy to staging, smoke-test, and canary a small share of production\ntraffic while watching the same metrics online, with the prompt and model version pinned in the same\nartifact so a rollback reverts both."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why build the image once and promote the same digest through environments rather than rebuilding per environment?","options":["Because container registries charge per build","Because a rebuild can resolve different dependencies or base layers, so production would run an artifact that was never tested; promoting one digest guarantees identity","Because Kubernetes can only pull a given image once per cluster","Because tests only execute on the first build of a commit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The artifact that passed acceptance tests is the only thing known to work; any rebuild is a new, untested artifact. Registries do not limit pulls this way and test execution is a pipeline choice, not a constraint."},{"q":"Unit tests for code that calls an LLM fail intermittently with slightly different wording in the output. What is the best fix?","options":["Raise the temperature so outputs become more consistent","Automatically retry each failing test three times","Mock or record the model client in unit tests, and move quality checks to an evaluation stage with thresholds rather than exact-match asserts","Pin the tests to one model version and assert the exact string"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Generated text is non-deterministic by nature, so exact matches will keep breaking. Unit tests should verify control flow against a stubbed client, while quality is measured statistically over many cases. Retries hide real failures and higher temperature increases variance."},{"q":"Why is OIDC federation preferred over a stored cloud access key in a CI pipeline?","options":["OIDC tokens never expire, so the pipeline never needs to re-authenticate","The job presents a short-lived signed identity token that the cloud exchanges for temporary credentials, so there is no long-lived secret to leak or rotate","Kubernetes requires OIDC for any `kubectl` access from a pipeline","Stored keys cannot be restricted to a single repository"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The credential is short-lived, and the cloud's trust policy accepts the token only when its claims (repository, branch or environment) match, which removes the single most common CI leak. OIDC tokens are deliberately short-lived, and `kubectl` access works with many credential types."}]}]});
